        {
          "type": "tool",
          "id": "call_01a12ef01d11701b81e0f2aaaf825ea3",
          "name": "read",
          "executed": false,
          "providerState": {
            "itemId": "fc_01a12ef01d11701b81e0f2aaaf825ea3"
          },
          "state": {
            "status": "completed",
            "input": {
              "path": "/Users/gillesstenger/Documents/esus/kernel/extraction/prompts.py"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Read file /Users/gillesstenger/Documents/esus/kernel/extraction/prompts.py, lines 1-376\n1: \"\"\"The extraction step prompts, each carrying an authoritative name block.\n2: \n3: The block is produced from the previous step's output by\n4: `kernel.extraction.vocabulary`, and the same vocabulary builds that step's\n5: grammar, so the prompt and the grammar always agree about which names exist.\n6: \"\"\"\n7: \n8: NAMES_HEADING = (\n9:     \"Authoritative names produced by the previous extraction step. \"\n10:     \"Use only these names, copied character for character. \"\n11:     \"Do not invent, abbreviate, rename or translate any of them.\"\n12: )\n13: \n14: # Shown to step B, the only step nothing constrains — see `intentions_block`.\n15: # The closing warning matters as much as the list: step A's phrases name\n16: # services, repositories and interfaces too, and B must not read those as\n17: # entities.\n18: INTENTIONS_HEADING = (\n19:     \"The use cases extracted from the requirements. They are the program's own \"\n20:     \"words for what it does: read them for the things it keeps, and for the \"\n21:     \"things it only passes through. They are NOT a data model — do not turn a \"\n22:     \"use case, a service, a repository or an interface named here into an entity.\"\n23: )\n24: \n25: INTENTIONS_SYSTEM = (\n26:     \"Extract core intentions (use cases/services). Do not list details. \"\n27:     'Output in English as JSON: {\"intentions\": []}.'\n28: )\n29: ERRORS_SYSTEM = (\n30:     \"List the application error types the requirements need. Each entry is an exception class \"\n31:     \"the program will raise: give it a 'name' ending in 'Error', a 'base' naming the built-in it \"\n32:     \"derives from, and a one-sentence 'description' saying when it is raised. \"\n33:     \"When the requirements NAME a type, use that exact name — a requirement that says \"\n34:     \"'CategoryNotFoundError' means a class of exactly that name, character for character. When \"\n35:     \"the requirements only DESCRIBE a failure without naming it — 'error handling for missing \"\n36:     \"files', 'malformed CSV', 'invalid input' — give it a clear name of your own in the same \"\n37:     \"style. \"\n38:     \"Use only these bases: LookupError (a value that does not exist), ValueError (a value of the \"\n39:     \"wrong kind or shape), KeyError (a missing key), PermissionError, OSError (a file or the \"\n40:     \"system), RuntimeError (a state the program cannot proceed from), TypeError, Exception. \"\n41:     \"If the requirements look a record up by its key, a name or another identifier, register a \"\n42:     \"not-found error for that entity — one per entity that is looked up — so the method that \"\n43:     \"reads it has a name for the case where it is missing. This is the commonest gap: a registry \"\n44:     \"holding an error for a missing order but none for a missing invoice leaves the invoice \"\n45:     \"lookup naming an error that no document declares. \"\n46:     \"Do not invent an error for a failure the requirements never mention, and do not turn a \"\n47:     \"validation rule into an error class: a row that is malformed and skipped is not an \"\n48:     \"exception unless the requirements say the program must fail on it. \"\n49:     \"A program whose requirements describe no failure at all has no errors: emit an empty list. \"\n50:     'Output strictly in English as JSON: {\"errors\": [{\"name\": \"...\", \"base\": \"LookupError\", '\n51:     '\"description\": \"...\"}]}.'\n52: )\n53: INVARIANTS_SYSTEM = (\n54:     \"Identify the business and data invariants explicitly stated or directly implied by the \"\n55:     \"requirements. Each invariant must be a single, unique, concrete statement. Do not repeat, \"\n56:     \"paraphrase or generalize the same statement. Do not invent any not grounded in the \"\n57:     \"requirements. Stop as soon as all distinct statements are listed. \"\n58:     \"Give every invariant a 'kind', which is exactly one of 'schema' or 'rule'. \"\n59:     \"Use 'schema' when the invariant only restates how a single field is declared — its type, \"\n60:     \"whether it is nullable or optional, whether it is unique, whether it is the primary key or \"\n61:     \"a foreign key, or the set of values it may take. ALL of the following are 'schema', however \"\n62:     \"they are worded: a field is optional, nullable or required; a field must be a boolean, an \"\n63:     \"integer, a string, a date or a timestamp; a field must be a non-null date or a non-null \"\n64:     \"timestamp; a field must be a non-negative or positive integer, or must be >= 0, or must be \"\n65:     \">= 1; a field must be unique; a field is a primary key or an auto-incremented key; a field \"\n66:     \"must be one of a fixed set of values; a field's value must exist in another entity, whether \"\n67:     \"written as 'must reference an existing X', 'must match Y in Z' or 'must be valid (exists in \"\n68:     \"X)'. A condition attached to a type, nullability or numeric bound does not change this: \"\n69:     \"'reorder_threshold must be a non-negative integer if provided' is 'schema', and \"\n70:     \"'price_cents must be a positive integer' is 'schema'. \"\n71:     \"Use 'rule' when the invariant constrains behaviour rather than one field: it relates two \"\n72:     \"different fields to each other, it makes a claim that holds only in a named state, or it \"\n73:     \"bounds an aggregate or a total. Examples of 'rule': 'wine.selling_price must be strictly \"\n74:     \"greater than wine.cost_price', 'the total number of bottles assigned to a StorageLocation \"\n75:     \"must not exceed its max_bottle_capacity', 'loan.return_date must be null if loan.status is \"\n76:     \"active', 'loan.return_date must be after loan.due_date', 'stock_quantity must be >= 0 when \"\n77:     \"the order is confirmed', 'book.available_copies must not exceed the number of copies added \"\n78:     \"for that book'. A comparison between two different fields, or a limit on a sum or a total, \"\n79:     \"makes an invariant a 'rule'. A conditional clause (if/when) makes it a 'rule' ONLY when the \"\n80:     \"claim itself is about another field, an aggregate, or a state named in the data model — a \"\n81:     \"bare type, nullability or numeric bound stays 'schema' even when a condition is attached. \"\n82:     \"The data model listed above is authoritative: an invariant must agree with it. Never call a \"\n83:     \"field unique or nullable when the model declares otherwise, and never state a rule about an \"\n84:     \"entity or field the model does not define — a rule about a field that is not there cannot be \"\n85:     \"enforced. Where an invariant and a declared attribute would disagree, the attribute is what \"\n86:     \"the model says and the invariant must not contradict it. \"\n87:     'Output strictly in English as JSON: {\"invariants\": [{\"text\": \"...\", \"kind\": \"schema\"}, '\n88:     '{\"text\": \"...\", \"kind\": \"rule\"}]}.'\n89: )\n90: DATA_MODELS_SYSTEM = (\n91:     \"Extract the data model. The FIRST field of every entity must be its primary key: set \"\n92:     \"'primary_key' to true for it and false for every other field, set its 'unique' to true \"\n93:     \"because a primary key is unique by definition, and never mark the primary key nullable. Set \"\n94:     \"'unique' to true for every other field the requirements say must not repeat — a code, a \"\n95:     \"number, an email, an ISBN or a reference that identifies one record — and false for the \"\n96:     \"rest. A field a business rule calls unique is unique in the model too: the two are the same \"\n97:     \"fact, and a model that says false while the rule says true contradicts itself. Use \"\n98:     \"ONLY these types: 'int', 'string', 'boolean', 'decimal', 'datetime', 'date', \"\n99:     \"'text', 'float'. For 'foreign_key' use null, or an object \"\n100:     '{\"target_entity\": \"...\", \"target_field\": \"...\", \"relationship\": \"many_to_one\"|\"one_to_one\"|'\n101:     '\"one_to_many\"}. Give every field a status of \"explicit\" or \"deduced\". '\n102:     \"An entity is a thing the program KEEPS: it has a lifetime beyond one operation, because \"\n103:     \"the program stores it, looks it up by an identifier, lists it, updates it, or reports \"\n104:     \"totals over it. Data that is read from an input and written straight to an output is NOT \"\n105:     \"an entity: a row that is parsed and re-emitted, a line that is transformed and passed on, \"\n106:     \"a value that is only computed and printed — nothing stores these and nothing looks them \"\n107:     \"up. If nothing in the requirements is kept, looked up or aggregated over, there is no \"\n108:     \"entity to model and the correct answer is an empty list: \"\n109:     '{\"data_models\": []}. '\n110:     \"Never invent an entity the requirements do not name or describe: it must be possible to \"\n111:     \"point at the requirements' own words for it, in whatever language they are written, and \"\n112:     \"to say which stored thing those words describe. \"\n113:     \"Output strictly in English as JSON.\"\n114: )\n115: COMPONENTS_SYSTEM = (\n116:     \"Classify the technology components. Facade type is one of cli, api, gui. \"\n117:     \"For a cli facade you must describe the command-line surface the requirements ask for in \"\n118:     \"'invocation', and then choose 'technology' to match that surface — the surface decides the \"\n119:     \"technology, never the other way round. \"\n120:     \"Set 'invocation' to null when the program accepts no arguments at all: a script whose whole \"\n121:     \"job is to print or compute something from nothing takes nothing in, and inventing a parser \"\n122:     \"for it is a defect rather than a default. \"\n123:     \"Otherwise 'invocation' is an object with three lists. 'positional' holds arguments given by \"\n124:     \"position, each with a name, a type and whether it is required. 'options' holds the flags a \"\n125:     \"user passes; write a long option name with two dashes ('--output') and its short form with \"\n126:     \"one ('-o'), and give each option a name, an optional short form, a type, whether it is \"\n127:     \"required, and a default or null. 'commands' holds subcommands, each with a name, its own \"\n128:     \"options, and its own 'subcommands'. \"\n129:     \"A flat CLI — 'tool --opt value --opt2 value' — leaves 'commands' empty and lists its \"\n130:     \"options directly. A hierarchical CLI — 'tool command --opt value', or \"\n131:     \"'tool command subcommand --opt value' — names each command in 'commands' and any second \"\n132:     \"level in that command's 'subcommands'. \"\n133:     \"A command is the thing the program acts on — 'product', 'category' — and a subcommand is \"\n134:     \"the verb it performs — 'add', 'list', 'update', 'delete'. When the requirements write \"\n135:     \"'<thing> <verb>', the thing becomes a command and the verb becomes one of that command's \"\n136:     \"subcommands; the verb is NEVER also an option of the command. \"\n137:     \"Every command line the requirements list must appear in the surface, and each distinct \"\n138:     \"thing the requirements act on is a command of its own: if they name 'book add', 'book \"\n139:     \"list', 'book search', 'member add', 'member list' and 'borrow', the surface declares both \"\n140:     \"'book' and 'member' as commands, and 'borrow' as a command in its own right — never as a \"\n141:     \"subcommand of whichever thing was written first, and never omitted. A line whose form is \"\n142:     \"'<program> <verb> --flag', with a single word between the program name and the first flag, \"\n143:     \"makes that word a top-level command carrying its own options. A command that has \"\n144:     \"subcommands carries no options of its own except the global ones ('-help', '-version'): \"\n145:     \"the flags a verb needs ('--sku', '--name', '--id') belong to that verb's subcommand, not \"\n146:     \"to the command that holds it. The same word must never appear both in a command's \"\n147:     \"'options' and in that command's 'subcommands' — 'product add --sku' and 'product -add' \"\n148:     \"cannot both be how a product is added. An option is a flag carrying one value, so its type \"\n149:     \"is that value's shape ('str', 'int', 'bool', 'float', 'list'); 'dict' or 'object' is not \"\n150:     \"an option type. Name each command once. \"\n151:     \"Technology follows the surface: no arguments at all means 'none', because a plain main() \"\n152:     \"needs no parser; positional arguments and no options means 'sys.argv'; any option means \"\n153:     \"'argparse'; nested commands mean 'click'. If the requirements name a technology, use the \"\n154:     \"name they give. If an api is requested but no technology is named, use flask; a gui \"\n155:     \"without one, pyimgui. If the requirements store data but name no database, use sqlite. If \"\n156:     \"the requirements store nothing at all — a program that only computes or prints, with no \"\n157:     \"records to keep — then set required=false, technology=none, and leave crud_services empty; \"\n158:     \"never add a database, a repository or an entity that the requirements do not ask for. \"\n159:     \"external_access lists only the cloud services the requirements name. crud_services lists \"\n160:     \"each bare domain entity the requirements ask to store (e.g. 'Book', 'Expense') at most \"\n161:     \"once, each with a subset of \"\n162:     '[\"create\", \"read_one\", \"read_all\", \"update\", \"delete\"]. Output strictly in English as JSON.'\n163: )\n164: CONTRACTS_SYSTEM = (\n165:     \"Extract high-level business service method signatures with parameters and return types. \"\n166:     \"Include domain services and repository custom aggregates (e.g. \"\n167:     \"get_total_by_category_and_month). Name each service exactly as the authoritative list does. \"\n168:     \"Declare each method once, and give every method the parameters the requirements imply — a \"\n169:     \"method that reads or writes an entity needs that entity's key and the fields it acts on. \"\n170:     \"Where the operations block below names operations for an entity, the interface that owns \"\n171:     \"that entity must declare a method for every one of them: a 'delete' needs a delete method \"\n172:     \"that takes the entity's key, an 'update' needs an update method that takes the key and the \"\n173:     \"fields it writes, and a 'create' needs a create or add method that takes the fields it \"\n174:     \"writes. Do not declare an operation as supported and then omit the method that provides it. \"\n175:     \"Those operations are a MINIMUM and not a maximum: having covered them, also declare the \"\n176:     \"other methods the requirements imply for that entity — reading one record by its key, \"\n177:     \"listing, and the custom queries, counts and aggregates the requirements describe. Do not \"\n178:     \"stop at the operations list if the requirements ask for more. \"\n179:     \"When the requirements describe a capability in prose rather than listing it, declare the \"\n180:     \"method that provides it: 'customers should be able to search their orders and invoices' \"\n181:     \"needs a search method that takes what is searched for and returns the matches, and \"\n182:     \"'administrators can filter products by category' needs a filter method. A capability the \"\n183:     \"CLI exposes but no method provides cannot be implemented. \"\n184:     \"A state change the requirements describe needs the method that performs the transition, \"\n185:     \"named for it: 'confirming an order reserves stock' needs a 'confirm_order', and \"\n186:     \"'cancelling a confirmed order releases the reserved stock' needs a 'cancel_order'. A \"\n187:     \"generic status setter is not a substitute — it does not say which transition it performs, \"\n188:     \"so nothing the CLI names is implemented by a method of that name. \"\n189:     \"Never emit a placeholder such as 'none', 'None', 'void', 'NoneType' or ':' as a parameter \"\n190:     \"name or type. \"\n191:     \"Where the requirements describe a failure a method can produce, name it in that method's \"\n192:     \"'raises' list, copied exactly from the registered error types listed below; use an empty \"\n193:     \"list for a method that raises none. Never name an error that is not registered, and never \"\n194:     \"invent one here — the registry is the authoritative list. \"\n195:     \"A method that creates a record cannot fail to find it: a create or an add performs no \"\n196:     \"lookup, so never give a create-like method a not-found error. That error belongs to the \"\n197:     \"read, update or delete method that really does look the record up. \"\n198:     \"If the authoritative list is empty, or the requirements describe no domain entities at all, \"\n199:     'emit {\"service_contracts\": []} — never invent a service just to have something to write. '\n200:     'Output strictly in English as JSON: {\"service_contracts\": [{\"service_name\": \"...\", \"methods\": '\n201:     '[{\"name\": \"...\", \"description\": \"...\", \"params\": [{\"name\": \"...\", \"type\": \"...\"}], '\n202:     '\"return_type\": \"...\"}]}]}.'\n203: )\n204: ADAPTERS_SYSTEM = (\n205:     \"Extract abstract interaction adapters mapping external input actions onto the domain service \"\n206:     \"methods listed above. target_service and target_method MUST be copied exactly from that list, \"\n207:     \"and target_param MUST be one of the parameters of the method you chose. When the method \"\n208:     \"declares a parameter whose name is the input field's own name, map the field to THAT \"\n209:     \"parameter: a field named 'category_id' goes to a parameter named 'category_id', never into \"\n210:     \"an entity object beside it, because the method has already said where the value goes. \"\n211:     \"Transforms: 'DIRECT', 'LOOKUP_ID', 'DECIMAL_TO_CENTS', 'ISO_DATE', 'CSV_LIST', 'ENUM_LOOKUP'. \"\n212:     \"Choose the transform that matches the field's own name and type: 'DECIMAL_TO_CENTS' is for a \"\n213:     \"money amount ('price', 'cost', 'amount', 'total', 'budget', 'limit'), never for a count, a \"\n214:     \"quantity, a boolean or a description; 'CSV_LIST' is for a field that holds several values \"\n215:     \"separated by commas, never for a single name, a description or a flag; 'ISO_DATE' is for a \"\n216:     \"date or timestamp; 'LOOKUP_ID' is for a foreign-key identifier that must be resolved to an \"\n217:     \"entity. Set 'lookup_entity' \"\n218:     \"to an entity name when the transform is 'LOOKUP_ID', and to null for every other transform. \"\n219:     'Output strictly in English as JSON: {\"interaction_adapters\": [{\"action_name\": \"...\", '\n220:     '\"target_service\": \"...\", \"target_method\": \"...\", \"input_fields\": [{\"field_name\": \"...\", '\n221:     '\"target_param\": \"...\", \"transform\": \"...\", \"lookup_entity\": null, \"required\": true}]}]}.'\n222: )\n223: PERSISTENCE_SYSTEM = (\n224:     \"You are a software architect. Produce the persistence ports for the entities listed above. \"\n225:     \"The repository list and the entity mappings are already fixed: emit one entry for each listed \"\n226:     \"entity, in the order given, using exactly those entity names and, for each mapping, exactly \"\n227:     \"the primary key shown. You choose the interface_name, the table name and the method bodies. \"\n228:     \"A repository holds the methods for its own entity only — never a method named after, or \"\n229:     \"belonging to, any other entity in the list, and never an entity name used as a method name. \"\n230:     \"The operations listed for an entity below 