                "type": "text",
                "text": "Read file /Users/gillesstenger/Documents/esus/kernel/extraction/repairs.py, lines 1-352\n1: \"\"\"The two contradictions a grammar cannot express, removed deterministically.\n2: \n3: The project's rule is consistency *by construction*: a value that must not exist\n4: is made unrepresentable in the GBNF, and a prompt sentence is the second line of\n5: defence, not the first. Three defects stay outside both, because they are global\n6: properties of the document rather than the shape of any one field:\n7: \n8: - **Two declared errors for one missing thing.** `CategoryNotFoundError` and\n9:   `MissingCategoryError` are the same failure; whether two names mean one thing is\n10:   a comparison *between* entries, and GBNF cannot express \"distinct subjects over\n11:   an unbounded alphabet\" any more than it can express \"distinct method names\"\n12:   (§9.7). The prompt says one name per failure and the model wrote two.\n13: - **A create that reports a not-found of what it creates.** `create_product`\n14:   raising `ProductNotFoundError` needs the method's *verb* and its *noun* to be\n15:   compared against the error's noun; the contract grammar leaves both names free,\n16:   so it cannot.\n17: - **A transform that does not fit the field's name.** `DECIMAL_TO_CENTS` on\n18:   `copies` needs the field's *name* and its *transform* to agree on a kind of\n19:   value; the adapter grammar closes `transform` to an enum and `field_name` to\n20:   free text, so the pair is not a regular property either.\n21: \n22: This is the same move as `dedupe.py` (§9.7): a *removal* of something that\n23: contradicts the rest of the document, never the invention or rewriting of a\n24: value. Every decision is logged, and the predicates are imported from\n25: `kernel.consistency` — the checker is the single definition of each\n26: contradiction, so a repair and the rule that reports it can never disagree. Run\n27: after every step that produces the documents it touches, and before writing.\n28: \"\"\"\n29: \n30: import re\n31: \n32: from kernel.consistency import checks_adapters, checks_errors\n33: \n34: \n35: def repair_errors(errors, services, log):\n36:     \"\"\"Remove the two error-registry contradictions; return `(errors, services)`.\n37: \n38:     The order matters: duplicates are resolved (and their `raises` references\n39:     redirected) *before* the create/not-found raises are dropped, so a reference\n40:     that becomes contradictory only after a redirect is still caught.\n41: \n42:     Doing nothing to a document that cannot hold the defect is deliberate: a\n43:     program with no services and a clean registry passes through untouched.\n44:     \"\"\"\n45:     errors, services = _resolve_duplicate_subjects(errors, services, log)\n46:     services = _drop_contradictory_raises(services, log)\n47:     return errors, services\n48: \n49: \n50: # --- a declared error nothing raises and the requirements never name ---------------\n51: \n52: # Too common to prove an error is grounded on their own: every name ends in one,\n53: # and \"invalid\" is the shape this repair exists to remove, not evidence for it.\n54: _GROUNDING_STOPWORDS = frozenset(\n55:     {\"error\", \"exception\", \"found\", \"not\", \"the\", \"invalid\", \"missing\"}\n56: )\n57: \n58: # The validation shapes `ERRORS_SYSTEM` forbids: a class named for an input check\n59: # rather than a failure the program must handle.\n60: _VALIDATION_PREFIXES = (\"invalid\", \"malformed\", \"bad\", \"unrecognized\", \"unexpected\")\n61: _VALIDATION_MARKERS = (\"validation\", \"parse\", \"format\")\n62: \n63: \n64: def repair_unraised_errors(errors, services, requirements, log):\n65:     \"\"\"Drop an error no method raises and the requirements never name.\n66: \n67:     An error type that no method raises is a class the generator would emit and\n68:     never use. Whether it belongs is decided by the requirements, the only\n69:     authority on the program's failures:\n70: \n71:     - **Kept when a method raises it** — nothing to repair.\n72:     - **Kept when the requirements spell it out** — `CategoryNotFoundError` in\n73:       inventory is named character for character, and the registry must carry it.\n74:     - **Kept when a distinctive word of its name is grounded** — cli_tool's\n75:       `MalformedCSVError` is warranted by \"malformed CSV\", and library's\n76:       `OverdueLoanFoundError` by \"overdue\"; their being unraised is a gap in the\n77:       contracts, not in the registry, so the registry stays faithful and the\n78:       checker reports the gap.\n79:     - **Dropped otherwise** — and always dropped when the name is a validation\n80:       shape (`Invalid*`, `Malformed*`), which `ERRORS_SYSTEM` forbids outright\n81:       unless the requirements name it.\n82: \n83:     Measured on library: `InvalidInputError` and `MalformedCSVError` (neither\n84:     grounded, both validation-shaped) are dropped, `OverdueLoanFoundError`\n85:     (grounded) is kept.\n86:     \"\"\"\n87:     # With no requirements text there is no authority to judge against, so the\n88:     # registry is left exactly as generated.\n89:     if not isinstance(errors, list) or not errors or not requirements:\n90:         return errors\n91:     lowered = requirements.lower()\n92:     raised = _raised_names(services)\n93:     kept = []\n94:     for item in errors:\n95:         name = item.get(\"name\") if isinstance(item, dict) else None\n96:         if not isinstance(name, str) or not name.strip():\n97:             kept.append(item)\n98:             continue\n99:         if _should_keep_error(name, raised, lowered):\n100:             kept.append(item)\n101:             continue\n102:         if log is not None:\n103:             why = \"a validation shape the requirements never name\" if _is_validation_error(\n104:                 name\n105:             ) else \"named nowhere in the requirements\"\n106:             log.write(\n107:                 f\"[ok] step errors: no method raises '{name}' and it is {why} — dropped\"\n108:             )\n109:     return kept\n110: \n111: \n112: def _should_keep_error(name, raised, lowered_requirements):\n113:     \"\"\"True when an error must survive the unraised-error repair.\"\"\"\n114:     if name.strip().lower() in raised:\n115:         return True\n116:     if name.strip().lower() in lowered_requirements:\n117:         return True\n118:     if _is_validation_error(name):\n119:         # A validation class is warranted only when the requirements describe\n120:         # that failure — cli_tool's \"malformed CSV\" grounds `MalformedCSVError`,\n121:         # while nothing in library or expenses grounds `InvalidInputError` or\n122:         # `InvalidBudgetAmountError`. Its own validation word must be present.\n123:         return _validation_word_grounded(name, lowered_requirements)\n124:     return _is_grounded(name, lowered_requirements)\n125: \n126: \n127: def _is_validation_error(name):\n128:     \"\"\"True when the name is a validation shape rather than a failure.\"\"\"\n129:     lowered = name.strip().lower()\n130:     if lowered.startswith(_VALIDATION_PREFIXES):\n131:         return True\n132:     return any(marker in lowered for marker in _VALIDATION_MARKERS)\n133: \n134: \n135: def _validation_word_grounded(name, lowered_requirements):\n136:     \"\"\"True when the requirements use the validation word the name is built on.\n137: \n138:     Only the leading validation word is consulted: `MalformedCSVError` is kept\n139:     when the requirements say \"malformed\", `InvalidInputError` is dropped when\n140:     they never say \"invalid\". A marker such as \"format\" is deliberately not used\n141:     as grounding — it appears in ordinary prose about dates and rarely describes\n142:     a failure.\n143:     \"\"\"\n144:     lowered = name.strip().lower()\n145:     return any(\n146:         lowered.startswith(prefix) and prefix in lowered_requirements\n147:         for prefix in _VALIDATION_PREFIXES\n148:     )\n149: \n150: \n151: def _is_grounded(name, lowered_requirements):\n152:     \"\"\"True when a distinctive word of the error's name appears in the requirements.\n153: \n154:     The name is a CamelCase identifier, so its words are recovered by separating a\n155:     capital that follows a lowercase before lowering — `OverdueLoanFoundError` is\n156:     `overdue`, `loan`, `found`, `error`, not one token. Only a distinctive word\n157:     can prove grounding: the stopwords every name shares (`error`, `found`, `not`)\n158:     carry no subject.\n159:     \"\"\"\n160:     spaced = re.sub(r\"([a-z])([A-Z])\", r\"\\1 \\2\", name)\n161:     for word in re.findall(r\"[a-z]+\", spaced.lower()):\n162:         if word in _GROUNDING_STOPWORDS or len(word) < 4:\n163:             continue\n164:         if word in lowered_requirements:\n165:             return True\n166:     return False\n167: \n168: \n169: # --- one name per missing subject -------------------------------------------------\n170: \n171: \n172: def _resolve_duplicate_subjects(errors, services, log):\n173:     \"\"\"Keep one error per missing subject; redirect the others' references to it.\n174: \n175:     The kept name is the one a method actually raises, so the registry follows\n176:     the code rather than the code following the registry. When neither is raised\n177:     the first declared wins — a deterministic tie-break, logged, so the choice is\n178:     visible rather than silent.\n179:     \"\"\"\n180:     if not isinstance(errors, list):\n181:         return errors, services\n182: \n183:     subjects = {}\n184:     for index, item in enumerate(errors):\n185:         if not isinstance(item, dict):\n186:             continue\n187:         name = item.get(\"name\")\n188:         subject = checks_errors._missing_subject(name)\n189:         if subject:\n190:             subjects.setdefault(subject, []).append(index)\n191: \n192:     raised = _raised_names(services)\n193:     redirect = {}\n194:     dropped_indexes = set()\n195:     for subject, indexes in sorted(subjects.items()):\n196:         if len(indexes) < 2:\n197:             continue\n198:         names = [errors[index].get(\"name\") for index in indexes]\n199:         keep_name = next(\n200:             (name for name in names if isinstance(name, str) and name.strip().lower() in raised),\n201:             names[0],\n202:         )\n203:         kept_index = next(\n204:             index for index in indexes\n205:             if errors[index].get(\"name\") == keep_name\n206:         )\n207:         for index in indexes:\n208:             if index == kept_index:\n209:                 continue\n210:             drop_name = errors[index].get(\"name\")\n211:             dropped_indexes.add(index)\n212:             if isinstance(drop_name, str):\n213:                 redirect[drop_name.strip().lower()] = keep_name\n214:             if log is not None:\n215:                 log.write(\n216:                     f\"[ok] step errors: '{drop_name}' and '{keep_name}' name one missing \"\n217:                     f\"record ({subject}) — dropped '{drop_name}', kept '{keep_name}'\"\n218:                 )\n219: \n220:     if not dropped_indexes:\n221:         return errors, services\n222: \n223:     kept_errors = [item for index, item in enumerate(errors) if index not in dropped_indexes]\n224:     _redirect_raises(services, redirect)\n225:     return kept_errors, services\n226: \n227: \n228: def _raised_names(services):\n229:     \"\"\"Every error name any method raises, lowercased.\"\"\"\n230:     raised = set()\n231:     if not isinstance(services, list):\n232:         return raised\n233:     for service in services:\n234:         if not isinstance(service, dict):\n235:             continue\n236:         for method in service.get(\"methods\") or []:\n237:             if isinstance(method, dict):\n238:                 for target in method.get(\"raises\") or []:\n239:                     if isinstance(target, str):\n240:                         raised.add(target.strip().lower())\n241:     return raised\n242: \n243: \n244: def _redirect_raises(services, redirect):\n245:     \"\"\"Point every `raises` entry at the kept spelling of its subject.\"\"\"\n246:     if not redirect or not isinstance(services, list):\n247:         return services\n248:     for service in services:\n249:         if not isinstance(service, dict):\n250:             continue\n251:         for method in service.get(\"methods\") or []:\n252:             if not isinstance(method, dict) or not isinstance(method.get(\"raises\"), list):\n253:                 continue\n254:             method[\"raises\"] = [\n255:                 redirect.get(target.strip().lower(), target)\n256:                 if isinstance(target, str) else target\n257:                 for target in method[\"raises\"]\n258:             ]\n259:     return services\n260: \n261: \n262: # --- a create cannot fail to find what it creates ---------------------------------\n263: \n264: \n265: def _drop_contradictory_raises(services, log):\n266:     \"\"\"Remove a not-found raise from the create-like method that contradicts it.\n267: \n268:     The predicate is the checker's: the created noun and the absent noun must be\n269:     the same, so `add_stock(product_id, ...)` keeping `ProductNotFoundError` is\n270:     left alone while `add_product` raising it is not.\n271:     \"\"\"\n272:     if not isinstance(services, list):\n273:         return services\n274:     for service in services:\n275:         if not isinstance(service, dict):\n276:             continue\n277:         owner = service.get(\"service_name\")\n278:         for method in service.get(\"methods\") or []:\n279:             if not isinstance(method, dict) or not isinstance(method.get(\"raises\"), list):\n280:                 continue\n281:             name = method.get(\"name\")\n282:             kept = [\n283:                 target for target in method[\"raises\"]\n284:                 if not (isinstance(target, str)\n285:                         and checks_errors.is_contradictory_raise(name, target))\n286:             ]\n287:             if len(kept) != len(method[\"raises\"]):\n288:                 for target in method[\"raises\"]:\n289:                     if target not in kept and log is not None:\n290:                         log.write(\n291:                             f\"[ok] step contracts: {owner}.{name} is a create and cannot fail to \"\n292:                             f\"find what it creates — dropped raises '{target}'\"\n293:                         )\n294:                 method[\"raises\"] = kept\n295:     return services\n296: \n297: \n298: # --- a transform that does not fit the field's name -------------------------------\n299: \n300: \n301: def repair_adapter_transforms(adapters, log):\n302:     \"\"\"Replace a transform whose kind contradicts the field's name with DIRECT.\n303: \n304:     A transform states how a value's representation changes; the field's name is\n305:     the only evidence of what kind of value it is. When the two disagree — a\n306:     count converted to cents, a single name read as a list, a description parsed\n307:     as a date — the transform is wrong for the field, and `DIRECT` is the honest\n308:     replacement: copy the value unchanged. The hint sets and the comparison come\n309:     from the checker, so the repair and `mismatched_transform` share one\n310:     definition of \"fits\".\n311:     \"\"\"\n312:     if not isinstance(adapters, list):\n313:         return adapters\n314:     for adapter in adapters:\n315:         if not isinstance(adapter, dict):\n316:             continue\n317:         action = adapter.get(\"action_name\")\n318:         for field_entry in adapter.get(\"input_fields\") or []:\n319:             if not isinstance(field_entry, dict):\n320:                 continue\n321:             replacement = _fitter_transform(field_entry)\n322:             if replacement is None:\n323:                 continue\n324:             field_name = field_entry.get(\"field_name\")\n325:             if log is not None:\n326:                 log.write(\n327:                     f\"[ok] step adapters: {action}.{field_name} was '{field_entry.get('transform')}' \"\n328:                     f\"but its name describes no such value — replaced with DIRECT\"\n329:                 )\n330:             field_entry[\"transform\"] = replacement\n331:     return adapters\n332: \n333: \n334: # Which transform claims a kind of value, and the hints its field name must carry.\n335: _TRANSFORM_HINTS = (\n336:     (\"DECIMAL_TO_CENTS\", checks_adapters.MONEY_FIELD_HINTS),\n337:     (\"CSV_LIST\", checks_adapters.CSV_FIELD_HINTS),\n338:     (\"ISO_DATE\", checks_adapters.DATE_FIELD_HINTS),\n339: )\n340: \n341: \n342: def _fitter_transform(field_entry):\n343:     \"\"\"`DIRECT` when the field's transform contradicts its name, else None.\"\"\"\n344:     transform = field_entry.get(\"transform\")\n345:     field_name = field_entry.get(\"field_name\")\n346:     if not isinstance(field_name, str):\n347:         return None\n348:     lowered = field_name.strip().lower()\n349:     for claimed, hints in _TRANSFORM_HINTS:\n350:         if transform == claimed and not checks_adapters._mentions(lowered, hints):\n351:             return \"DIRECT\"\n352:     return None"
