                "type": "text",
                "text": "Read file /Users/gillesstenger/Documents/esus/kernel/extraction/classification.py, lines 1-80\n1: \"\"\"Step F: classify invariants and contract methods into kernel patterns.\n2: \n3: This step used to be silent and dominated run time. It now reports one line per\n4: item, which both removes the appearance of a hang and makes the per-item cost\n5: visible in the run log.\n6: \n7: **Nothing is discarded here.** Until this change the stage-1 \"no domain fits\"\n8: answer was filtered out before the document was written, so 62 of the 191 items\n9: across the seven prompts were classified and then thrown away — every one of them\n10: ordinary CRUD, which the classifier had identified correctly. The answers are all\n11: kept now. The gap ids (`NO_MATCH`, `NO_PATTERN_IN_DOMAIN`) are the coverage\n12: report: their count and their texts say which pattern the vocabulary is missing.\n13: `STANDARD_CRUD` is a legitimate answer recording what no named pattern needs to\n14: describe, and is never a finding.\n15: \"\"\"\n16: \n17: from kernel.classifier.hierarchical_classifier import classify_requirement_2step, load_catalog\n18: from kernel.pattern_catalog import GAP_PATTERN_IDS, NO_PATTERN_IN_DOMAIN, STANDARD_CRUD\n19: from kernel.pattern_shapes import rejection_reason, returns_records\n20: \n21: # Step C's tag for an invariant that states a behaviour, and so may map onto a\n22: # kernel pattern. Its counterpart, `SCHEMA_KIND`, restates a field declaration\n23: # and is not classified — see `classification_items`.\n24: RULE_KIND = \"rule\"\n25: \n26: # How close the top two answers may be before the classification is reported as\n27: # undecided. The absolute confidence alone cannot say this: a 0.70 that beat a\n28: # 0.69 is a coin-flip and a 0.70 that beat a 0.05 is a decision, and both report\n29: # 0.70. Calibrated against the corpus once the read-shape vocabulary was\n30: # completed; re-derive it whenever the option list changes, for the same reason\n31: # as the checker's confidence floor.\n32: NARROW_MARGIN = 0.15\n33: \n34: # The same question asked of **step 1**, which is the more consequential of the\n35: # two: the domain decides which pattern list step 2 even sees, so a near-tied\n36: # domain can put the match in the wrong list entirely, while a near-tied pattern\n37: # keeps it in the right one. Kept as its own constant because the two margins are\n38: # on different scales — the pattern margin is the domain probability times the\n39: # gap between two pattern probabilities, the domain margin is a raw softmax gap —\n40: # so calibrating one does not calibrate the other. 0.15 is a starting point from\n41: # the pattern stage, **not** a measured value for this stage: the first run that\n42: # uses it should be read for how many items it flags before it is trusted.\n43: DOMAIN_NARROW_MARGIN = 0.15\n44: \n45: # The three decisions a match carries, so a generator switches on one value\n46: # instead of re-deriving a threshold:\n47: #   \"undefined\" — the kernel has no answer (NO_MATCH, NO_PATTERN_IN_DOMAIN).\n48: #   \"undecided\" — the kernel has two answers too close to call; `undecided_stage`\n49: #                 says which stage, and the alternatives and margin are recorded.\n50: #   \"decided\"   — neither.\n51: DECIDED = \"decided\"\n52: UNDECIDED = \"undecided\"\n53: UNDEFINED = \"undefined\"\n54: \n55: STAGE_DOMAIN = \"domain\"\n56: STAGE_PATTERN = \"pattern\"\n57: \n58: \n59: def classify_documents(classifier, documents, log):\n60:     \"\"\"Classify every invariant and contract method; return every match.\"\"\"\n61:     catalog = load_catalog()\n62:     items = classification_items(documents)\n63: \n64:     if not items:\n65:         log.write(\"[ok] step patterns: nothing to classify\")\n66:         return []\n67: \n68:     matches = []\n69:     gaps = []\n70:     standard_crud = 0\n71:     total = len(items)\n72:     signatures = _method_signatures(documents)\n73: \n74:     for index, (source, text) in enumerate(items, start=1):\n75:         result = classify_requirement_2step(classifier, text, catalog)\n76:         claimed = result[\"pattern_id\"]\n77:         return_type = signatures.get(source)\n78:         # The classifier's own total describes the pattern it *claimed*, and that\n79:         # is not the recorded one when the claim is corrected. Recording the\n80:         # claim's figure beside the corrected pattern made the two disagree, and\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 81]"
