        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "All three come from step F (classification), defined in `kernel/consistency/checks_patterns.py` and `kernel/extraction/classification.py`. Each match carries a `decision`: `decided`, `undecided`, or `undefined` (gap).\n\n**`low_confidence`** — confidence below the 0.2 floor (`checks_patterns.py:50`). Confidence is the product of two softmaxes (domain × pattern), so 0.2 means *both* stages were unsure. It's a note because the checker can't tell \"unsure but right\" from \"wrong\". Example this run: none currently above floor in library, but previously `list_budgets → FILTER_BY_FIELD` at 0.07. For the generator: a weak basis for the implementation choice — the LLM should weigh the method signature more than the pattern id.\n\n**`narrow_margin`** — the winner beat the runner-up by less than 0.15 (`NARROW_MARGIN`, `DOMAIN_NARROW_MARGIN`). A 0.70 beating 0.69 is a coin flip; a rerun can land on the other answer, so the JSON — and code generated from it — may differ between runs. That's why it matters for your consistency requirement. Two stages: *domain* (worse — it decides which pattern list step 2 even sees) and *pattern*. Live examples in library just now: `borrow_book` — `STANDARD_CRUD` beat `REPO_QUERY` by 0.02 in the domain stage; invariant #5 — `BUDGET_OVERFLOW_GUARD` (0.38) vs `QUOTA_CAPACITY_GUARD` (0.37). For the generator: treat `undecided` matches as genuinely ambiguous; either implementation is defensible, so the LLM should decide from the method text, not the recorded winner.\n\n**`corrected_claim`** — the classifier claimed pattern X, but X is *impossible* for the method's return type (`pattern_shapes.rejection_reason`), so the pipeline deterministically recorded the next shape-compatible candidate instead. Example: `get_budgets_by_month` claimed `AGGREGATE_BY_FK_AND_DATE` (returns scalar/mapping) but returns `List[Budget]`, recorded as `FILTER_BY_FIELD`. This one is good news: the recorded pattern is already the right one. A high count just signals the classifier systematically reaches for aggregates on record-returning methods — i.e. method descriptions oversell aggregation, which the generator LLM should read with skepticism.\n\nIn short: `corrected_claim` = already fixed, trust the recorded id; `low_confidence`/`narrow_margin` = uncertainty flags, let the generator LLM arbitrate from the raw method/invariant text.",
