        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Yes — for the ties that change the generated body, the requirements author is the only source of ground truth. No classifier can resolve *\"available_copies must not exceed...\"* between budget-overflow and capacity-quota (margin 0.0023), because the sentence itself doesn't say. The classifier's real job there is *detecting* the ambiguity, which it now does.\n\nBut ask selectively, or the cure is worse than the disease:\n\n* **Worth asking (~3 of 14 ties):** guard-vs-guard and semantic ties — library's `BUDGET_OVERFLOW` vs `QUOTA_CAPACITY`, gui's `STATE_TRANSITION` vs `QUOTA_CAPACITY`, 60's `cancel_order` guards. These change the emitted check, and only the author knows which failure the requirement means.\n* **Not worth asking:** `STANDARD_CRUD` vs `REPO_QUERY` (identical code either way) and `FILTER` vs `FILTER_WITH_PAGINATION` when the signature has no pagination params (signature already decides). Auto-resolve with the documented rule.\n\nDesign I'd recommend — opt-in, batch-safe:\n\n1. Pipeline stays non-interactive by default (CI-safe, current behavior).\n2. New review step after F: for body-changing ties only, present text + signature + both candidates with margins and a signature-based recommendation. Per prompt that's 0–3 questions, not 14.\n3. Answers persist in `out/<prompt>/decisions.json` (text → pattern) and are re-applied after classification on every rerun — so the decision survives reruns and the JSON stays stable. Entry becomes `decision: decided, decided_by: user`.\n4. Generator rule simplifies to: trust `decided`, reason from text only on the remaining harmless `undecided`.\n\nThis keeps your \"leave it to the LLM\" plan for everything the signature can settle, and puts a human exactly where neither classifier nor generator has the information. Implement it?",
          "state": {
            "itemId": "msg_01a12f46d4417138a4ce89a310709700"
          }
        }
