        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Auto-resolve them with documented equivalence rules — and enforce that the generator doesn't branch on the coin flip. The 11 all share one property: both candidates compile to the same body.\n\nThe pairs and their rules:\n\n* `STANDARD_CRUD` vs `REPO_QUERY` (confirm_order, borrow_book, create_budget, update_budget…) → plain method, no guard. The domain tie never changes a body.\n* `FILTER_BY_FIELD` vs `FILTER_WITH_PAGINATION` (detect_recurring, get_product_by_category_and_month) → signature decides: pagination params present → paginated, else plain filter. Both cases have no pagination params, so deterministic from the signature alone.\n* `AGGREGATE_BY_FK` vs `AGGREGATE_BY_FK_AND_DATE`, `EXISTS_BY_UNIQUE_COMPOSITE` vs `AGGREGATE_BY_FK` (all the *\"X must reference an existing Y\"* invariants) → FK existence check. Identical code.\n* `READ_ALL` vs `THRESHOLD_SCAN` (get_overdue_loans, no threshold param) → return matching records. Same body.\n\nThe mechanism that makes this sound rather than hand-waving:\n\n1. An equivalence table (candidate-pair → resolution rule) lives with the generator spec. Known pair → deterministic rule, no LLM judgment needed.\n2. The generator is then *tested* on it: swap winner/runner-up on an undecided entry, assert byte-identical output. A real test we write when building the generator — it pins \"the recorded winner is just a label\".\n3. Any future tie whose pair is **not** in the table escalates to the ask queue automatically. The system is self-maintaining: novel ambiguity → human, known-harmless → rule, and the table grows only when a genuinely new ambiguity appears.\n\nSo the split is: 3 ties go to you (novel, body-changing, persisted in `decisions.json`), 11 go to rules (stable, tested), and the table is the boundary between them.",
          "state": {
            "itemId": "msg_01a12f48e447704cb671f02d79bdc0f4"
          }
        }
      ],
