        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "By treating generalizability as a loop, not a state. Most of the pipeline's smarts are corpus-tuned content — synonym stems, money/date hint lists, the 0.2/0.15 thresholds, the `<noun> <verb> --flag` notation assumption — and tuning those harder on 7 prompts just overfits. What generalizes is the machinery around them:\n\n1. **Grow the corpus adversarially.** The 7 prompts are the happy path. Add prompts designed to break assumptions: commands written without backticks or flags, non-English requirements (the prompts already claim language-independence — untested), single-command tools, 15-entity domains, contradictory requirements. Each new prompt's `out/` becomes a fixture; the bar is probe-0-blockers after triage, same as now.\n2. **Perturbation testing for determinism.** Paraphrase each prompt (or rerun at varied seeds) and assert structural stability: same commands, entities, operations; `pattern_id`s may differ only within the equivalence table. This measures the margin fragility directly instead of hoping 0.15 holds. Cheap to run, and it converts \"may differ between runs\" from a note into a number.\n3. **Separate content from mechanism, and version the content.** The hand lists (stems, hints) and thresholds already carry \"re-derive when X changes\" comments — make that a script, not a comment: a calibration check that recomputes floors/margins from the corpus and fails when the constants drift from measured values. Thresholds become derived artifacts, not magic numbers.\n4. **Property-test the gate against silent passes.** The worst failure isn't a wrong repair, it's the probe saying 0 while the generator invents code (that's what `library/member` was). Fuzz with synthetic documents: random placeholder names always stripped or reported, unresolvable references always surfaced, never absorbed. Every check gets its \"stays silent when it should\" test — the suite already follows this pattern (`test_generator_readiness_rules.py` pins silences).\n5. **Keep the documented loop as the process:** new defect → checker rule + shared predicate → pipeline repair → test → human decision if body-changing. The 4→0 history is the template, and `review_ties.py` + `decisions.json` already make novel ambiguity survivable without code changes.\n\nThe honest ceiling: closed-vocabulary extraction assumes requirements name things consistently, and true ambiguities will always need you. A generalizable pipeline isn't one that never fails on prompt 8 — it's one where prompt 8's failure arrives as a probe finding with a known fix procedure, not as silently generated wrong code.",
          "state": {
            "itemId": "msg_01a12f5532e172b49782b6e967a203e0"
          }
        }
