          "text": "Yes — fix `extract_arch` first. The generator must not salvage any of this, and here's the boundary:\n\n**Salvaging would mean inventing information**, and each variant fails differently:\n* Inventing `--sku`/`--amount` flags from requirements text inside the generator → two definitions of the surface (step D's job), guaranteed to drift.\n* Filtering `author_id` out of `Book(**kwargs)` → books silently stored without authors. Data corruption with exit 0.\n* Guessing `--output` means `file_path` → name-matching gamble baked into shipped code.\n\nThe generator's refusals are not failures — they're the bug reports extraction needs. Degradation is only acceptable when nothing is invented or silently lost (keeping the long flag on `-a` collisions). Everything else refuses.\n\nThe upstream fixes, both following the established loop (checker rule sharing its predicate → pipeline retry → probe blocker → test):\n\n1. **Flag-level surface coverage.** The D-retry only compares verbs; inventory's `product/add` has the verb with zero data flags while requirements say `--sku --name ...`. New rule: every required flag token in the requirements' backtick lines must bind (via `codegen.bind_field`, aliases included) to a declared option on the owning leaf. Retry D with the miss named; probe blocks on it.\n2. **Adapter↔model agreement.** `unknown_model_fields` already exists as a probe blocker — wire it as a G-step retry (\"assemble only declared fields\"), so the model either renames the target or the run visibly fails instead of shipping a dead path. The `Book.author_id` linkage itself is a step-B miss (requirements name `--author-id`, model lacks the FK) — that one may need a human look at whether the rerun heals it or the prompt needs sharpening.\n\nImplement both loops, then rerun the four blocked prompts?",
