              "command": "cd /Users/gillesstenger/Documents/esus && echo \"=== library data model section ===\"; sed -n '1,16p' prompts/prompt_library_system.txt; echo; echo \"=== expenses data: amount ===\"; grep -n -i \"amount\\|cents\" prompts/prompt_expenses.txt | head; echo; echo \"=== 60 format ===\"; head -n 40 prompts/prompt_60.txt"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "=== library data model section ===\nCreate a multi-module Python library management system with the following features:\n\n1. **Book model** — id, title, isbn (unique), published_year, available_copies (default 1)\n2. **Author model** — id, name, birth_year, biography\n3. **Member model** — id, name, email (unique), membership_date, is_active (default True)\n4. **Loan model** — id, book_id (FK), member_id (FK), loan_date, due_date, return_date (nullable), status (active/returned/overdue)\n5. **BookRepository** — SQLite storage with CRUD + search by title/author/isbn + filtering by available copies\n6. **AuthorRepository** — SQLite storage with CRUD + find books by author\n7. **MemberRepository** — SQLite storage with CRUD + find active/inactive members\n8. **LoanRepository** — SQLite storage with CRUD + find active loans, overdue loans, member loan history, book loan history\n9. **LibraryService** — business logic:\n   - borrow_book(member_id, book_id): checks availability, creates loan, decrements copies\n   - return_book(loan_id): sets return_date, updates status, increments copies\n   - get_overdue_loans(): returns loans past due_date\n   - renew_membership(member_id): toggles is_active\n   - search_books(query): searches across title/author/isbn\n\n=== expenses data: amount ===\n3:1. **Category model** — id, name (unique), description, monthly_budget (optional int, stored as cents), icon (optional str)\n4:2. **Expense model** — id, amount_cents (int), description, expense_date (str, ISO date), category_id (FK), payment_method (cash/card/transfer), is_recurring (bool)\n5:3. **Budget model** — id, category_id (FK), month (str like \"2024-01\"), amount_limit_cents (int), UNIQUE(category_id, month)\n20:   - detect_recurring(): finds expenses with same amount/category recurring monthly and marks them\n27:   - `budget add --category-id --month --amount`\n28:   - `budget update --category-id --month [--amount]`\n30:   - `expense add --amount --description --category [--expense-date] [--method] [--recurring]`\n37:Use type hints throughout. Use SQLite with foreign keys enabled. All monetary values must be stored as integers (cents) to avoid floating-point precision issues — convert to/from decimal for display. All IDs must be auto-incremented primary keys that are optional (default None) in the domain models. Implement proper error handling with custom exception classes: CategoryNotFoundError, ExpenseNotFoundError, BudgetExceededException.\n\n=== 60 format ===\nBuild a Python 3.12 application for a small business that manages customers, products, orders and invoices. Customers can place orders containing multiple products. Products belong to categories and have stock quantities. Orders can be pending, confirmed, shipped or cancelled. Confirming an order reserves stock. Cancelling a confirmed order releases the reserved stock. An invoice is created when an order is confirmed. Customers should be able to search their orders and invoices. Administrators should be able to manage products and categories. The application must persist all data in SQLite, expose the main operations through a CLI, validate input, handle errors cleanly, keep business logic separate from persistence and remain reasonably modular.\n"
              }
            ],
