            "input": {
              "command": "cd /Users/gillesstenger/Documents/esus && python3 - <<'PYEOF'\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n# 1. inventory: drop the duplicated unbracketed --stock\np = Path('prompts/prompt_inventory.txt')\nt = p.read_text()\nold = \"`product add --sku --name --category --price --stock [--stock]`\"\nassert old in t, \"inventory line not found\"\nt = t.replace(old, \"`product add --sku --name --category --price [--stock]`\", 1)\np.write_text(t)\n\n# 2. expenses: flag-to-field mapping sentence before the type-hints paragraph\np = Path('prompts/prompt_expenses.txt')\nt = p.read_text()\nanchor = \"Use type hints throughout.\"\nassert anchor in t\nnote = (\"Field mappings: CLI --amount fills amount_cents (Expense) and amount_limit_cents \"\n        \"(Budget); CLI --output fills file_path; CLI --category and --method fill \"\n        \"category_id and payment_method.\\n\\n\")\nt = t.replace(anchor, note + anchor, 1)\np.write_text(t)\n\n# 3. library: author_id FK + copies semantics on the Book model line\np = Path('prompts/prompt_library_system.txt')\nt = p.read_text()\nold = \"1. **Book model** — id, title, isbn (unique), published_year, available_copies (default 1)\"\nassert old in t, \"library book line not found\"\nnew = (\"1. **Book model** — id, title, isbn (unique), published_year, available_copies \"\n       \"(default 1), author_id (FK to Author); --copies sets the initial available_copies \"\n       \"(a count of books, never a monetary value)\")\nt = t.replace(old, new, 1)\np.write_text(t)\n\n# 4. 60: pin invoice search flags\np = Path('prompts/prompt_60.txt')\nt = p.read_text()\nassert not t.endswith(\"\\n\\n\")\nt = t.rstrip(\"\\n\") + (\" Invoice search accepts --customer-id and --order-id filters \"\n                      \"to narrow results by customer and order.\\n\")\np.write_text(t)\nprint('all four prompts updated')\nPYEOF\ngit diff --stat prompts/ 2>/dev/null; git diff prompts/ 2>/dev/null | head -n 60"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "all four prompts updated\n prompts/prompt_60.txt             | 2 +-\n prompts/prompt_expenses.txt       | 2 ++\n prompts/prompt_inventory.txt      | 2 +-\n prompts/prompt_library_system.txt | 2 +-\n 4 files changed, 5 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)\ndiff --git a/prompts/prompt_60.txt b/prompts/prompt_60.txt\nindex d076bfc..c839ddd 100644\n--- a/prompts/prompt_60.txt\n+++ b/prompts/prompt_60.txt\n@@ -1 +1 @@\n-Build a Python 3.12 application for a small business that manages customers, products, orders and invoices. Customers can place orders containing multiple products. Products belong to categories and have stock quantities. Orders can be pending, confirmed, shipped or cancelled. Confirming an order reserves stock. Cancelling a confirmed order releases the reserved stock. An invoice is created when an order is confirmed. Customers should be able to search their orders and invoices. Administrators should be able to manage products and categories. The application must persist all data in SQLite, expose the main operations through a CLI, validate input, handle errors cleanly, keep business logic separate from persistence and remain reasonably modular.\n+Build a Python 3.12 application for a small business that manages customers, products, orders and invoices. Customers can place orders containing multiple products. Products belong to categories and have stock quantities. Orders can be pending, confirmed, shipped or cancelled. Confirming an order reserves stock. Cancelling a confirmed order releases the reserved stock. An invoice is created when an order is confirmed. Customers should be able to search their orders and invoices. Administrators should be able to manage products and categories. The application must persist all data in SQLite, expose the main operations through a CLI, validate input, handle errors cleanly, keep business logic separate from persistence and remain reasonably modular. Invoice search accepts --customer-id and --order-id filters to narrow results by customer and order.\ndiff --git a/prompts/prompt_expenses.txt b/prompts/prompt_expenses.txt\nindex 897a112..7edc55a 100644\n--- a/prompts/prompt_expenses.txt\n+++ b/prompts/prompt_expenses.txt\n@@ -34,4 +34,6 @@ Create a multi-module Python expense tracking system with the following features\n    - `expense export --from-date --to-date --output`\n    - `expense recurring detect`\n \n+Field mappings: CLI --amount fills amount_cents (Expense) and amount_limit_cents (Budget); CLI --output fills file_path; CLI --category and --method fill category_id and payment_method.\n+\n Use type hints throughout. Use SQLite with foreign keys enabled. All monetary values must be stored as integers (cents) to avoid floating-point precision issues — convert to/from decimal for display. All IDs must be auto-incremented primary keys that are optional (default None) in the domain models. Implement proper error handling with custom exception classes: CategoryNotFoundError, ExpenseNotFoundError, BudgetExceededException.\ndiff --git a/prompts/prompt_inventory.txt b/prompts/prompt_inventory.txt\nindex 9773acd..7f3d505 100644\n--- a/prompts/prompt_inventory.txt\n+++ b/prompts/prompt_inventory.txt\n@@ -14,7 +14,7 @@ Create a multi-module Python inventory management system with the following feat\n    - low_stock_report(): returns products whose stock_qty is below their category's reorder_threshold\n    - stock_value_by_category(): returns the total price_cents * stock_qty per category\n 6. **CLI interface using click** with nested command groups:\n-   - `product add --sku --name --category --price --stock [--stock]`\n+   - `product add --sku --name --category --price [--stock]`\n    - `product list [--category] [--low-only]`\n    - `product update --id [--name] [--price] [---category]`\n    - `product delete --id`\ndiff --git a/prompts/prompt_library_system.txt b/prompts/prompt_library_system.txt\nindex 0afa7c4..1db95be 100644\n--- a/prompts/prompt_library_system.txt\n+++ b/prompts/prompt_library_system.txt\n@@ -1,6 +1,6 @@\n Create a multi-module Python library management system with the following features:\n \n-1. **Book model** — id, title, isbn (unique), published_year, available_copies (default 1)\n+1. **Book model** — id, title, isbn (unique), published_year, available_copies (default 1), author_id (FK to Author); --copies sets the initial available_copies (a count of books, never a monetary value)\n 2. **Author model** — id, name, birth_year, biography\n 3. **Member model** — id, name, email (unique), membership_date, is_active (default True)\n 4. **Loan model** — id, book_id (FK), member_id (FK), loan_date, due_date, return_date (nullable), status (active/returned/overdue)\n"
