        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Valid concern — but re-asking every time kills automation for no benefit when the prompt *didn't* change. The precise fix: stamp each decision with the prompt hash, invalidate on mismatch.",
          "state": {
            "itemId": "rs_01a1347e6f8a74b990477af2288e0e32",
            "phase": "commentary"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "tool",
          "id": "call_01a1347e7208704d9bcac28cd1efc42d",
          "name": "edit",
          "executed": false,
          "providerState": {
            "itemId": "fc_01a1347e7208704d9bcac28cd1efc42d"
          },
          "state": {
            "status": "error",
            "input": {
              "newString": "\"\"\"User-recorded answers to near-tied classifications.\n\n`review_ties.py` asks about the ties `kernel.tie_rules` cannot resolve and\nstores each answer here, in the output directory beside the documents. The\nextractor re-applies them after classification on every run, so one review\nsettles each tie permanently; the review also patches the on-disk document,\nso the current output is fixed without a rerun.\n\nA decision record carries the classified text (the exact match key), the\nchosen pattern id, and the candidates it was chosen from, so a stale answer —\nfor a text no run produces any more — is visible rather than silently applied.\nIt also carries the sha of the requirements it was decided under: answers are\nonly re-applied when the prompt is unchanged, because a reworded prompt can\nmove the meaning a text had. A record predating a prompt change is dropped\nwith a log line (the tie reopens and is re-asked); records from before hashes\nexisted keep applying, since there is nothing to compare them against.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport hashlib\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nDECISIONS_NAME = \"decisions.json\"\nDECISIONS_KEY = \"decisions\"\nDECIDED = \"decided\"\nDECIDED_BY_USER = \"user\"",
