        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Verdict, with one correction owed first: my \"impossible\" evidence was my own misreading. `run.log` concatenates two runs (my history-seeding feature) — the borrow-repair lines belong to the *full* run, the book-only document to the *resume*. No contradiction ever existed in the code; the keep-best path is exactly as designed and tested. Sorry for the long detour around my own log format.\n\n**Hallucination or deterministic bug? Neither, precisely:**\n\n* **Not a pipeline bug.** Grammar allows 20 commands, the prompt explicitly demands `member`/`borrow`, repairs/retries/keep-best all behaved per spec, probe reports honestly. Verified by code reading plus 475 tests.\n* **Not sampling noise either** — temp 0 reproduces this omission faithfully for these inputs (the retry's hot attempt missed it too).\n* **It is model-side omission**: dropped required content despite explicit instruction, systematic *for a given input state*, variable *across* states (other rolls kept `member`). Reproducible enough to debug, fixable only by changing inputs — which right now means hot rerolls, since prompts are frozen.\n\n**The hunt paid for itself anyway**: it exposed a genuine deterministic blind spot — single-word `<program> <verb> --flag` lines were invisible to *both* surface rules (pair needs two words, unbacked only checks declared verbs), so a vanished `borrow` could never be reported. Closed with a structural test (frequency-independent, so inventory can't false-positive), 4 new tests green. Library probe reads 3, all true: `member` group, `borrow`, `return`.",
