          "state": {
            "status": "completed",
            "input": {
              "command": "cd /Users/gillesstenger/Documents/esus && python3 -c \"\nimport sys; sys.path.insert(0,'.')\nimport json\nfrom kernel.extraction import prompts, vocabulary\nmodels = json.load(open('out/library/data_models.json'))['data_models']\ncomponents = json.load(open('out/library/components.json'))['components']\nvocab = vocabulary.Vocabulary(entities=vocabulary.entities_from(models))\nrequirements = open('out/library/requirements.txt').read()\nprint(prompts.components(requirements, prompts.components_block(vocab)))\n\" 2>&1 | head -n 100"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "<|im_start|>system\nClassify the technology components. Facade type is one of cli, api, gui. For a cli facade you must describe the command-line surface the requirements ask for in 'invocation', and then choose 'technology' to match that surface — the surface decides the technology, never the other way round. Set 'invocation' to null when the program accepts no arguments at all: a script whose whole job is to print or compute something from nothing takes nothing in, and inventing a parser for it is a defect rather than a default. Otherwise 'invocation' is an object with three lists. 'positional' holds arguments given by position, each with a name, a type and whether it is required. 'options' holds the flags a user passes; write a long option name with two dashes ('--output') and its short form with one ('-o'), and give each option a name, an optional short form, a type, whether it is required, and a default or null. 'commands' holds subcommands, each with a name, its own options, and its own 'subcommands'. A flat CLI — 'tool --opt value --opt2 value' — leaves 'commands' empty and lists its options directly. A hierarchical CLI — 'tool command --opt value', or 'tool command subcommand --opt value' — names each command in 'commands' and any second level in that command's 'subcommands'. A command is the thing the program acts on — 'product', 'category' — and a subcommand is the verb it performs — 'add', 'list', 'update', 'delete'. When the requirements write '<thing> <verb>', the thing becomes a command and the verb becomes one of that command's subcommands; the verb is NEVER also an option of the command. Every command line the requirements list must appear in the surface, and each distinct thing the requirements act on is a command of its own: if they name 'book add', 'book list', 'book search', 'member add', 'member list' and 'borrow', the surface declares both 'book' and 'member' as commands, and 'borrow' as a command in its own right — never as a subcommand of whichever thing was written first, and never omitted. A line whose form is '<program> <verb> --flag', with a single word between the program name and the first flag, makes that word a top-level command carrying its own options. A command that has subcommands carries no options of its own except the global ones ('-help', '-version'): the flags a verb needs ('--sku', '--name', '--id') belong to that verb's subcommand, not to the command that holds it. The same word must never appear both in a command's 'options' and in that command's 'subcommands' — 'product add --sku' and 'product -add' cannot both be how a product is added. An option is a flag carrying one value, so its type is that value's shape ('str', 'int', 'bool', 'float', 'list'); 'dict' or 'object' is not an option type. Name each command once. Technology follows the surface: no arguments at all means 'none', because a plain main() needs no parser; positional arguments and no options means 'sys.argv'; any option means 'argparse'; nested commands mean 'click'. If the requirements name a technology, use the name they give. If an api is requested but no technology is named, use flask; a gui without one, pyimgui. If the requirements store data but name no database, use sqlite. If the requirements store nothing at all — a program that only computes or prints, with no records to keep — then set required=false, technology=none, and leave crud_services empty; never add a database, a repository or an entity that the requirements do not ask for. external_access lists only the cloud services the requirements name. crud_services lists each bare domain entity the requirements ask to store (e.g. 'Book', 'Expense') at most once, each with a subset of [\"create\", \"read_one\", \"read_all\", \"update\", \"delete\"]. Output strictly in English as JSON.\n\nIf, and only if, the requirements ask for stored data, crud_services may use only the entity names listed here, copied exactly. If the requirements ask for no stored data, leave crud_services empty and set persistence.required=false.\nEntities:\nBook(id pk, title, isbn, published_year, available_copies, author_id)\nAuthor(id pk, name, birth_year, biography)\nMember(id pk, name, email, membership_date, is_active)\nLoan(id pk, book_id, member_id, loan_date, due_date, return_date, status)\n<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nCreate a multi-module Python library management system with the following features:\n\n1. **Book model** — id, title, isbn (unique), published_year, available_copies (default 1)\n2. **Author model** — id, name, birth_year, biography\n3. **Member model** — id, name, email (unique), membership_date, is_active (default True)\n4. **Loan model** — id, book_id (FK), member_id (FK), loan_date, due_date, return_date (nullable), status (active/returned/overdue)\n5. **BookRepository** — SQLite storage with CRUD + search by title/author/isbn + filtering by available copies\n6. **AuthorRepository** — SQLite storage with CRUD + find books by author\n7. **MemberRepository** — SQLite storage with CRUD + find active/inactive members\n8. **LoanRepository** — SQLite storage with CRUD + find active loans, overdue loans, member loan history, book loan history\n9. **LibraryService** — business logic:\n   - borrow_book(member_id, book_id): checks availability, creates loan, decrements copies\n   - return_book(loan_id): sets return_date, updates status, increments copies\n   - get_overdue_loans(): returns loans past due_date\n   - renew_membership(member_id): toggles is_active\n   - search_books(query): searches across title/author/isbn\n10. **CLI interface using click** with commands:\n   - `library book add --title --isbn --author-id --published-year --copies`\n   - `library book list [--author] [--available-only]`\n   - `library book search --query`\n   - `library member add --name --email`\n   - `library member list [--active-only]`\n   - `library borrow --member-id --book-id`\n   - `library return --loan-id`\n   - `library overdue`\n   - `library member history --member-id`\n\nUse type hints throughout. Use SQLite with foreign keys enabled. The database schema must enforce referential integrity with CASCADE deletes. Follow clean architecture with proper separation of concerns. All IDs must be auto-incremented primary keys that are optional (default None) in the domain models.\n<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n\n"
              }
            ],
