          "type": "text",
          "text": "That's exactly the right question, and the answer reframes the whole step. **We already know ~90% of the surface deterministically today** — the checker proves it, since it parses every command line without a model:\n\n| Surface element | Status |\n|---|---|\n| Command lines present | **Known** — backtick parse (the checker's own regexes) |\n| Thing→command, verb→subcommand, bare `<prog> <verb> --flag` mapping | **Known** — stated rules, mechanical |\n| Required vs optional | **Known** — brackets |\n| Flag types | **Known when** uniquely bound to a model field (your repair already does this) |\n| Short flags (`-n`, `-o`) | **Unknown** — pure convention, model must invent |\n| `crud_services`, persistence, technology | **Judgment** — stays with the model |\n| Prose-only mentions (60, no backticks) | **Judgment** — stays with the model |\n\nSo today the model is asked to *discover* (parse prose), *transcribe*, *invent shorts*, and *judge* — four jobs, and discovery is what's failing. Your proposal lifts exactly the failing job out, and crucially it needs **no edit to `prompts/*.txt`**: the pipeline already injects authoritative blocks (the entity list works this way). We'd parse the requirements into an enumerated command-lines block — *\"The surface MUST contain exactly these N command lines. For each one: …\"* — using the checker's own span/flag parsers as the single definition, so prompt and verifier can never disagree about what a line says. Grammar unchanged, checker unchanged, graceful fallback to discovery when a prompt has no parseable lines.\n\nHonest calibration: this upgrades omission from \"model didn't notice\" to \"model refused an explicit checklist item\" — a much rarer failure, well-established prompting practice — but it does not *guarantee* compliance the way closing the grammar would. If enumerated lines still get dropped, the fallback is synthesizing the mechanical 90% deterministically and leaving the model only shorts and judgment calls. But that's the bigger build; the enumerated block is small, reversible, and measurable (member survives or it doesn't).\n\nShall I build the enumerated command-lines block?",
