s\\' and any second level in that command\\'s \\'subcommands\\'. A command is the thing the program acts on — \\'product\\', \\'category\\' — and a subcommand is the verb it performs — \\'add\\', \\'list\\', \\'update\\', \\'delete\\'. When the requirements write \\'<thing> <verb>\\', the thing becomes a command and the verb becomes one of that command\\'s subcommands; the verb is NEVER also an option of the command. Every command line the requirements list must appear in the surface, and each distinct thing the requirements act on is a command of its own: if they name \\'book add\\', \\'book list\\', \\'book search\\', \\'member add\\', \\'member list\\' and \\'borrow\\', the surface declares both \\'book\\' and \\'member\\' as commands, and \\'borrow\\' as a command in its own right — never as a subcommand of whichever thing was written first, and never omitted. A line whose form is \\'<program> <verb> --flag\\', with a single word between the program name and the first flag, makes that word a top-level command carrying its own options. A command that has subcommands carries no options of its own except the global ones (\\'-help\\', \\'-version\\'): the flags a verb needs (\\'--sku\\', \\'--name\\', \\'--id\\') belong to that verb\\'s subcommand, not to the command that holds it. The same word must never appear both in a command\\'s \\'options\\' and in that command\\'s \\'subcommands\\' — \\'product add --sku\\' and \\'product -add\\' cannot both be how a product is added. An option is a flag carrying one value, so its type is that value\\'s shape (\\'str\\', \\'int\\', \\'bool\\', \\'float\\', \\'list\\'); \\'dict\\' or \\'object\\' is not an option type. Name each command once. Technology follows the surface: no arguments at all means \\'none\\', because a plain main() needs no parser; positional arguments and no options means \\'sys.argv\\'; any option means \\'argparse\\'; nested commands mean \\'click\\'. If the requirements name a technology, use the name they give. If an api is requested but no technology is named, use flask; a gui without one, pyimgui. If the requirements store data but name no database, use sqlite. If the requirements store nothing at all — a program that only computes or prints, with no records to keep — then set required=false, technology=none, and leave crud_services empty; never add a database, a repository or an entity that the requirements do not ask for. external_access lists only the cloud services the requirements name. crud_services lists each bare domain entity the requirements ask to store (e.g. \\'Book\\', \\'Expense\\') at most once, each with a subset of [\"create\", \"read_one\", \"read_all\", \"update\", \"delete\"]. Output strictly in English as JSON.\\n<|im_end|>\\n<|im_start|>user\\nSearch orders by customer.<|im_end|>\\n<|im_start|>assistant\\n'\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------------\nRan 13 tests in 0.080s\n\nFAILED (failures=1)\n"
