ng: the room the prompt left behind. `MAX_NEW_TOKENS`\n149:     is only a guard against filling the whole context on the first attempt.\n150:     \"\"\"\n151:     if llm is None or not prompt or llm.n_ctx() <= 0:\n152:         return MAX_NEW_TOKENS\n153:     return max(MIN_BUDGET, llm.n_ctx() - token_count(llm, prompt) - CONTEXT_RESERVE)\n154: \n155: \n156: def generation_budget(llm, prompt, max_new_tokens=None):\n157:     \"\"\"The response ceiling: the one asked for, clamped to the room available.\n158: \n159:     The retry after a truncation passes `max_new_tokens=available_tokens(...)`,\n160:     which is how a step that hit `MAX_NEW_TOKENS` gets the rest of the context\n161:     on the second attempt instead of the same ceiling that cut it off.\n162:     \"\"\"\n163:     ceiling = MAX_NEW_TOKENS if max_new_tokens is None else max_new_tokens\n164:     return max(MIN_BUDGET, min(ceiling, available_tokens(llm, prompt)))\n165: \n166: \n167: def generate(llm, prompt, grammar_text, label, temperature=0.0, max_new_tokens=None, seed=None):\n168:     \"\"\"Generate one document under `grammar_text`, honouring the context budget.\n169: \n170:     The call is **not** streamed. Streaming yields one chunk per token and\n171:     rebuilds a response dict for each, which is pure Python overhead on top of\n172:     the sampler that already runs per token — it buys nothing here, because the\n173:     document is only ever parsed once the whole step has finished. A single\n174:     non-streaming call returns the same text in one dict, and its `usage` gives\n175:     the exact completion-token count instead of the one-per-chunk estimate the\n176:     stream forced.\n177: \n178:     The caller is responsible for having validated `grammar_text` first: this\n179:     function assumes it is well formed and would otherwise fail on the first\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 180]"
