          "type": "text",
          "text": "Yes — that's sound, with a precise trigger. The generator's refusal contract stays intact because this isn't invention: in these cases the requirements *fully* describe the program and there's simply no architecture to be faithful to.\n\nProposed rule — LLM fallback when **all** hold:\n* 0 blockers, 0 open ties (docs are clean, just empty),\n* no commands, no services, no entities.\n\nHello and cli_tool both qualify; no domain prompt does (any entity/service/command disables it).\n\nBehavior in that case: skip the 7 mechanical files, write a single `generated/app.py` via one model call grounded on `requirements.txt` (+ the error registry for the declared errors), with a header marking it LLM-generated from requirements. Stdout says so explicitly — no silent switching. temp=0 + seed keeps it reproducible like the rest.\n\nCaveat: it's still an LLM writing code, so no probe can verify it — I'd smoke-test by running it (`hello` prints, `cli_tool` converts a sample CSV), which is the only verification that means anything there.\n\nWant me to implement it?",
