                "text": "Build a Python 3.12 application for a local wine shop owner to manage their premium wine inventory and tracks bottle storage locations.\n\nThe system must satisfy the following criteria:\n\n1. Core Features & Architecture:\n   - Provide a responsive GUI (Graphical User Interface) for the shop owner to visually manage the bottles, update stock levels, and assign bottles to physical locations (e.g., Rack A, Shelf 3).\n   - The application must persist all data locally across sessions.\n   - Enforce a strict separation between the visual presentation layer, the data persistence access layer, and the core domain logic.\n\n2. Expected Data Structure:\n   - Wine Model: unique identifier, name, vintage year (int), wine type (red, white, rosé, sparkling), country of origin, cost price, selling price, and total current stock quantity.\n   - Storage Location Model: unique identifier, zone name (e.g., \"Main Cellar\", \"Front Display\"), row number, shelf number, and maximum bottle capacity.\n   - Each wine bottle entry must point to exactly one primary storage location.\n\n3. Key Constraints & Rules:\n   - The selling price of a wine bottle must always be strictly greater than its cost price.\n   - A wine's stock quantity can never drop below zero.\n   - The total number of bottles assigned to a specific storage location cannot exceed that location's defined maximum bottle capacity.\n   - All wine names must be unique in the system.\n   - Prevent the removal of a storage location if wine bottles are currently assigned to it.\n\n4. Robustness:\n   - Implement clean user validation with intuitive error handling.\n   - Raise custom domain exceptions when business constraints are violated, such as NegativeStockError, LocationOverCapacityException, or InvalidPricingException.\n\nzsh:1: == not found\n"
