          "type": "text",
          "text": "Not quite — the pattern *is* implemented, but a service method must pass two gates and `list_expenses` fails the second:\n\n1. **Mechanical reading** (`codegen.plan_method`, `kernel/codegen.py:139`): `FILTER_BY_FIELD` is in `MECHANICAL_PATTERNS`, and `list_expenses` classifies as `read_all` by name — so the plan succeeds. Repositories would run this body (`_select_body`: criteria + select).\n2. **Same-name delegation** (`generator.py:emit_services`): services implement *only* 1:1 delegation to a same-name repository method. No `list_expenses` on any repo → documented stub, pattern notwithstanding. That's the `[no same-name repository method to delegate to]` in the docstring.\n\nSo what's unimplemented is **service-level composition**, not the pattern: the service can't delegate to a *differently-named* repo method (`read_expenses_by_category` et al. — themselves stubs, since `read_*_by_*` doesn't match the `filter` name prefixes, `classify_crud` returns `None`).\n\nTwo honest enhancements, different sizes:\n* **(a) Param-shape filter classification** — a repo method returning `List[…]` with scalar params gets `crud:filter` regardless of name prefixes (`_select_body` already handles arbitrary params via criteria; unmatched ones raise loudly). Bounded, deterministic, would implement `read_expenses_by_category` mechanically.\n* **(b) Service composition** — plan a service body from non-same-name repo methods (e.g. `list_expenses` → category/date/payment reads + merge). That's a planner, not a rule — properly the stub-filling LLM pass, or a real design task.\n\n(a) I can do now; (b) is the deferred workstream wearing a new hat. Your call.",
