eturn_type\": \"List[Member]\"},\n                                  {\"name\": \"find_by_name\",\n                                   \"params\": [{\"name\": \"name\", \"type\": \"str\"}],\n                                   \"return_type\": \"List[Member]\"}]}],\n            \"services\": [],\n            \"models\": [{\"name\": \"Member\", \"fields\": [\n                {\"name\": \"id\", \"type\": \"int\", \"primary_key\": True}]}],\n            \"adapters\": adapters, \"entity_names\": [\"Member\"],\n            \"components\": {\"facade\": {\"invocation\": invocation}},\n        }\n        stats = {\"prog\": \"member\", \"unmapped\": []}\n        text = generator.emit_cli(ctx, bindings, stats)\n        self.assertIn(\"'active-only'\", text)   # the surface keeps declaring it\n        self.assertEqual(stats[\"dead_options\"],\n                         [\"member list: -active-only — no adapter field reads it\"])\n```\n\nHmm — bind_commands signature: `codegen.bind_commands(adapters, invocation, COMMAND_VERB_STEMS)`. verb stem matching: command \"member\" + sub \"list\" → verb \"list\" → action \"list_members\"? The scoring: action_name \"list_members\" target read_all_members. Will bind_commands bind (\"member\",\"list\") → \"list_members\" [score]? In the real library it did. Should be fine with the same names.\n\nFor (\"member\",\"find\") → \"find_members\"? The action name \"find_members\" vs command/sub... plausible. But I don't strictly need the find binding; I could keep the test minimal: ONE action list_members and one subcommand list with the dead flag; then a second subcommand \"search\" bound to find_members with -name consumed. If bind_commands doesn't bind one of them, `bound` is None → no dead report (skips) → my assertion of exact equality might fail. Safer: assert `stats[\"dead_options\"] == [exact one]` for the active_only, and simply assert `\"-name\"` not flagged. If the find binding mismatches my expectation, the assertion `stats[\"dead_options\