NO — honesty: report. I'll add `\"dropped_routes\": dropped_routes` and main prints a line combined: existing print for slots; add routes line.\n\nHmm wait — review_surface.apply_surface_decisions handles sha-drop with log lines during review. For generation-time route consumption, mirror with the print.\n\n**(2) Helpers:**\n\n```python\ndef _service_method(ctx, service_ref, method_name):\n    \"\"\"The documented method object for a service/repo reference, or None.\"\"\"\n    wanted = normalize(service_ref or \"\")\n    for repo in ctx[\"repositories\"]:\n        if normalize(repo.get(\"interface_name\") or \"\") == wanted or normalize(repo.get(\"entity\") or \"\") == wanted:\n            for method in repo.get(\"methods\") or []:\n                if isinstance(method, dict) and method.get(\"name\") == method_name:\n                    return method\n    for service in ctx[\"services\"]:\n        if normalize(service.get(\"service_name\") or \"\") == wanted:\n            for method in service.get(\"methods\") or []:\n                if isinstance(method, dict) and method.get(\"name\") == method_name:\n                    return method\n    return None\n```\n\n```python\ndef _return_shape(ctx, returns):\n    \"\"\"'collection', 'entity' or 'scalar' — how a method's result displays.\"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(returns, str):\n        return \"scalar\"\n    words = {normalize(token) for token in re.findall(r\"[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*\", returns)}\n    if \"list\" in words:\n        return \"collection\"\n    if words & {normalize(name) for name in ctx[\"entity_names\"] if isinstance(name, str)}:\n        return \"entity\"\n    return \"scalar\"\n```\n\n```python\ndef _alternate_actions(ctx, base_action, dests, actions):\n    \"\"\"Adapter actions whose fields this leaf's flags feed end-to-end.\n\n    An alternate counts only when every declared field binds to one of the\n    leaf's own flags AND the same names are actual parameters of the\n    action's target method — the second half is what refuses a mis-targeted\n    action (`list_books_by_author` pointing at the availability filter).\n\n    Returns [(action_name, [(field_name, dest)])] sorted for determinism.\n    \"\"\"\n    base = actions.get(base_action) or {}\n    service = base.get(\"target_service\")\n    alternates = []\n    for name in sorted(actions):\n        spec = actions.get(name)\n        if not isinstance(spec, dict) or name == base_action:\n            continue\n        if spec.get(\"target_service\") != service:\n            continue\n        fields = [f for f in spec.get(\"input_fields\") or []\n                  if isinstance(f, dict) and f.get(\"field_name\")]\n        if not fields:\n            continue\n        method = _service_method(ctx, service, spec.get(\"target_method\"))\n        if method is None:\n            continue\n        params = {p.get(\"name\") for p in method.get(\"params\") or []\n                  if isinstance(p, dict) and isinstance(p.get(\"name\"), str)}\n        if not all(f[\"field_name\"] in params for f in fields):\n            continue\n        if _return_shape(ctx, method.get(\"return_type\")) not in (\"collection\", \"entity\"):\n            continue\n        bindings = []\n        used = set()\n        ok = True\n        for field in fields:\n            dest = codegen.bind_field(dests, field[\"field_name\"])\n            if dest is None or dest in used:\n                ok = False\n                break\n            used.add(dest)\n            bindings.append((field[\"field_name\"], dest))\n        if not ok:\n            continue\n        alternates.append((name, bindings))\n    return alternates\n```\n\nHmm wait — for the budget case: fields category_id (bind → category) and month (→ month); params {category_id, month} — field \"category_id\" in params ✓ (exact); field \"month\" ✓. shape: read_budget_by_category_and_month returns \"Budget\" → entity ✓. \n\nFor book list: base action = list_books (target read_all_books). Alternate list_books_by_author: target filter_books_by_available_copies — method params? Let me check that method's params — if it has e.g. (min_copies) or similar, field author_id not in params → skipped ✓. If it takes NO params and the field author_id... then `all(f in params)` fails ✓. Good.\n\nAlso — are there OTHER alternates in expenses that would fire unexpectedly? Let me list actions per service and check. E.g. ExpenseService actions: list_expenses (base for expense list), get_monthly_report, get_yearly_summary, add_expense... For (expense, list): dests [category, from_date, to_date, method]. Alternate candidates among ExpenseService actions: get_monthly_report (fields [month] — month binds? dests have no \"month\" → bind_field None → skip ✓). Fine.\n\nFor (budget, list): base read_all_budgets; alternates: read_budget_by_category_and_month (fields category_id+month ✓ → fires); check_budget_status?? target_service BudgetRepository, fields category_id+month? The action check_budget_status — let me check its fields: if it has category_id+month, it binds too! But its return is bool → scalar → skipped ✓ (shape rule saves us). read_budget_by_id (fields budget_id/id?) binds to... dests [category, month]: field \"budget_id\" → bind_field([\"category\",\"month\"], \"budget_id\")? flat \"budgetid\" vs \"category\"/\"month\" no containment → None → skip ✓. \n\nFor (member, list): base list_members; alternates: none (other member actions? list_members is the only MemberRepository-family one... maybe renew_membership (fills member_id?) not binding to active_only dest. ✓ none.\n\nFor (book, list): base list_books; alternates: list_books_by_author (skipped by consistency) ✓; search_books? target LibraryService (different service) → skipped by service rule ✓.\n\nFor (expense, report): base get_monthly_report (target ExpenseService); alternates: get_yearly_summary (fields [year]— dests [monthly, yearly]: bind_field for \"year\"... dest \"yearly\": flat \"yearly\" vs wanted \"year\": containment — wanted \"year\" in \"yearly\" ✓ unique → binds! params of get_yearly_summary: (year)? ✓; return: dict? scalar? Let me check its return_type — if dict → scalar → skipped ✓; if it returns a summary entity/dict... get_yearly_summary in docs — returns dict probably → skipped. Hmm — but the -yearly flag SHOULD route to it! Shape rule would block because reports return dicts (scalar by my classification). Hmm!! Do I want report -yearly wired? Earlier I decided to leave it as a finding (ambiguity: -monthly AND -yearly both always passed; which wins?). With both always passed, BOTH alternates' needs are satisfied → first sorted wins... get_yearly_summary comes before get_monthly_report? \"get_monthly_report\" < \"get_yearly_summary\" alphabetically (\"m\" < \"y\")... wait but get_monthly_report is the BASE (not alternate). Base is last. So yearly would win always?! That flips report to yearly always — wrong. So: report's required-both flags make routing ambiguous — leave as finding. The shape rule (dict=scalar) already skips it ✓ no action needed. But wait — is that a happy accident? The report action targets get_monthly_report whose return is a dict (scalar); alternates must match collection/entity — so report stays as-is ✓. I'll note the shape rule rationale in the docstring: \"scalar-returning (aggregates, reports) are refused — a list flag selecting a report would print a different shape than the surface promises.\" Hmm, wait — that rationale fits list commands. For a report command, scalar→scalar would be right-shape... My rule allows collection/entity ONLY, so scalar base + scalar alternate never routes. Acceptable conservative scope; the report case remains a finding. Good.\n\n**(3) Routes (decisions)** — record shape:\n```json\n{\"kind\": \"route\", \"command\": \"member\", \"subcommand\": \"list\", \"flag\": \"active_only\",\n \"service\": \"MemberRepository\", \"method\": \"find_active_members\",\n \"decided_by\": \"user\", \"requirements_sha\": \"...\"}\n```\nload_context: keep records with kind==\"route\" and valid command/flag/service/method, sha-check, store in `ctx[\"routes\"]` as list.\n\n**(4) `_leaf_choices`:** compute per leaf:\n```python\ndef _leaf_choices(ctx, cname, sname, destinations, actions, base_bound, usage):\n    \"\"\"[(action, score, need)]: recorded routes, mechanical alternates, base.\n\n    `need` items are `(dest, kind, flag)` — kind `value` for valued flags,\n    `truthy` for boolean selectors. The base entry is the empty-need\n    fallback; dispatch takes the first satisfied entry, so more specific\n    flags win whenever they are passed.\n    \"\"\"\n    base_action, base_score = base_bound\n    flag_of = dict(destinations)\n    choices = []\n    # routes\n    for record in ctx.get(\"routes\") or []:\n        if (record.get(\"command\"), record.get(\"subcommand\")) != (cname, sname):\n            continue\n        flag = record.get(\"flag\")\n        if flag not in flag_of:\n            continue\n        service = record.get(\"service\")\n        method = record.get(\"method\")\n        if _service_method(ctx, service, method) is None:\n            continue\n        key = _route_action_key(actions, usage, service, method)\n        choices.append((key, base_score, ((flag, \"truthy\", flag_of[flag]),)))\n    # alternates\n    alternates = _alternate_actions(ctx, base_action, [d for d,_ in destinations], actions)\n    for name, bindings in alternates:\n        usage.setdefault(name, []).extend... dests\n        need = tuple((dest, \"value\", flag_of[dest]) for _field, dest in bindings)\n        choices.append((name, base_score, need))\n    choices.append((base_action, base_score, ()))\n    return choices\n```\nHmm — `_route_action_key`: synthesize a virtual action in the actions dict if missing:\n```python\ndef _route_action_key(ctx, actions, service, method, virtual):\n    for name, spec in actions.items():\n        if isinstance(spec, dict) and spec.get(\"target_service\") == service and spec.get(\"target_method\") == method:\n            return name\n    key = codegen.sanitize(method, \"route\")\n    while key in actions:\n        key += \"_route\"\n    virtual[key] = {\"action_name\": key, \"target_service\": service,\n                    \"target_method\": method, \"input_fields\": []}\n    actions[key] = virtual[key]  # so ADAPTERS emission includes it\n    return key\n```\nSince `actions` is a plain dict built in emit_cli, mutating it there is fine (local). But careful — emit_cli has `actions = {a[\"action_name\"]: a for a in ctx[\"adapters\"]}`; I'll copy: `actions = dict(...)` to avoid mutating ctx. ✓\n\nSorting determinism: alternates sorted by name ✓; routes in record order ✓; base last ✓.\n\nDuplicate choices: if a route's method equals an alternate's action? Different shapes (route = paramless, alternate = fieldful); both could match... e.g. hypothetical. Not now.\n\nAlso the route flag kind: could a route target a VALUED flag? Candidate methods are paramless, so truthy bools only. But the record could name a valued flag (future); emit kind by the flag's declared type! I have the leaf options' types in... destinations only carry (dest, flag). I should carry type: destinations items = (dest, flag, is_bool). Update _emit_options destinations append → (dest, flags[0], otype == \"bool\" or default bool?) For a bool flag: otype \"bool\". For valued: else. So kind = \"truthy\" if bool else \"value\". For routes: kind from the flag's type. ✓ more robust.\n\nWait — `_emit_options` currently appends (dest, flags[0]). I'll extend to (dest, flags[0], otype). Adjust consumers (report_dead_options iterates (dest, flag) → now 3-tuples; update).\n\n**(5) emit_cli restructure:** \n\nCurrent flow order in the function: repo_classes/services/model_names → actions → invocation → argv → targets → assembled → lines=[header...] → ADAPTERS block → BINDINGS block → helpers → build_parser → commands loop → _call... → main/dispatch.\n\nNew flow:\n1. compute actions (copy), invocation, argv, targets, assembled.\n2. **Phase 0**: walk invocation commands → leaves: for each leaf, plans = _plan_options(leaf_options(...), stats); destinations[(dest,flag,isbool)]; store leaf_info[(c,s)] = {\"plans\":..., \"dests\":..., \"var\": parser var name...}. Hmm — the parser var names are computed in the commands loop; I can precompute or recompute deterministically (sanitize is pure). I'll store plans only, and the commands loop recomputes var names (pure) and renders.\n3. **Phase 1**: choices per leaf (needs base bound + routes + alternates + usage).\n4. Virtual route actions added to actions dict (mutating the local copy) — must happen BEFORE ADAPTERS emission.\n5. Alternates' argv augmentation: for action, dests_used in usage: ensure argv[action][field] = bind_field(dests_used, field) when None. Usage dests: collect ALL dests from the leaves using it as alternate (union like action_argv).\n6. Emit ADAPTERS (loop over sorted(actions)) — unchanged shape (virtual entries have input_fields [] ✓).\n7. Emit BINDINGS from choices:\n```python\n    lines.append(\"BINDINGS = {  # (command, subcommand) -> [(action, score, need)]\")\n    for (command, sub), bound in sorted(bindings.items()...):\n        if bound is None: ... None\n        else:\n            choices = leaf_choices[(command, sub)]\n            if len(choices) == 1 and not choices[0][2]:  # base only → compact tuple? \n```\nHmm — uniform lists vs compact: Let me decide the emitted runtime shape: ALWAYS a list. Rewrite the dispatch/choose/_call accordingly. Single-choice leaves: `[('read_all_budgets', 4, ())]`. Fine.\n8. helpers `_normalize`, `_choose`, `_needs_met`, `_incomplete`, `_call` (bound[0]), `build_cli`, `main`, `dispatch`.\n9. build_parser: commands loop renders from stored plans (no _plan_options call → no double stats) using a render function. Wait — _emit_options takes options and re-plans. I'll add a low-level `_render_plans(indent, parser_var, plans)` returning lines; _emit_options = _plan_options + _render_plans (kept for tests/back-compat).\n\nAlso `stats[\"choice_routes\"]` for main display: list of f\"cli {c} {s}: {flags} -> {action}\" for non-base choices.\n\nAlso the `unbound_fields` stats logic (line ~1890 in ADAPTERS emission): iterates adapter fields and `argv.get(name, {}).get(field) is None` → for alternates augmented ✓ fine; virtual actions have no fields ✓.\n\n**(6) emitted dispatch runtime:**\n```python\ndef _needs_met(present, need):\n    for dest, kind, _flag in need:\n        value = present.get(_normalize(dest))\n        if kind == \"truthy\":\n            if not value:\n                return False\n        elif value is None:\n            return False\n    return True\n\n\ndef _choose(bound, values):\n    \"\"\"The first satisfied choice: routes and filters before the fallback.\"\"\"\n    present = {_normalize(k): v for k, v in values.items()}\n    for choice in bound:\n        if _needs_met(present, choice[2]):\n            return choice, present\n    return bound[-1], present\n\n\ndef _incomplete(chosen, bound, present):\n    \"\"\"Flags that select a choice also needing companions, e.g. `-category`\n    without `-month`: naming the missing flag beats ignoring the one passed.\"\"\"\n    for action, _score, need in bound:\n        if action == chosen[0]:\n            continue\n        passed = [item for item in need if _needs_met(present, (item,))]\n        if passed and len(passed) != len(need):\n            missing = [item[2] for item in need if item not in passed]\n            return f\"{action!r} needs \" + \" and \".join(missing) + \" with \" + \", \".join(item[2] for item in passed)\n    return None\n```\nHmm — `_incomplete` semantics: chosen is the first SATISFIED choice. Any other choice with partial flags → error. But careful: a choice with need partially passed while ANOTHER fully-passed choice was chosen earlier — e.g. two alternates A (dest x,y) and B (dest y,z), user passes x,y → A chosen; B partially passed (y) → my check would ERROR even though A was satisfied! Is that wrong? User passed y which B needs plus z... but A consumed y too. Hmm — the check should only fire when the chosen choice doesn't consume the partially-passed flags... Complex. Let me define: fire only if the passed-flag subset that is \"unconsumed by chosen\" is non-empty. I.e. for each OTHER choice with partial pass: passed_partial = dests passed; if any passed dest ∉ consumed(chosen) → error. For A chosen consuming {x,y}: B's passed dest y ∈ consumed(A) → no error ✓. Good refinement:\n```python\ndef _incomplete(chosen, bound, present):\n    consumed = {dest for dest, _kind, _flag in chosen[2]}\n    for action, _score, need in bound:\n        if action == chosen[0]:\n            continue\n        passed = [item for item in need if _needs_met(present, (item,))]\n        if not passed or len(passed) == len(need):\n            continue\n        if any(item[0] in consumed for item in passed):\n            continue\n        missing = [item[2] for item in need if item not in passed]\n        return (f\"{action!r} also needs \" + \" and \".join(missing))\n    return None\n```\nAlso the FALLBACK choice (need ()) — chosen with consumed=∅: any partial alternate → error ✓ (budget list -category alone) ✓.\n\nAnd \"value\" kind present-check for partially passed: `_needs_met(present,(item,))` checks one item ✓.\n\ndispatch:\n```python\ndef dispatch(parser, args, deps):\n    \"\"\"Run one parsed command against shared dependencies.\"\"\"\n    binding = getattr(args, '_binding', None)\n    if binding is None:\n        parser.print_usage()\n        return 2\n    bound = BINDINGS.get(tuple(binding))\n    if bound is None:\n        print(f\"no adapter bound for {' '.join(binding)} — not implemented\")\n        return 1\n    values = {key: value for key, value in vars(args).items()\n              if key not in ('command', 'subcommand', '_binding', 'db')}\n    choice, present = _choose(bound, values)\n    trouble = _incomplete(choice, bound, present)\n    if trouble:\n        print(f\"error: {' '.join(binding)}: {trouble}\")\n        return 1\n    try:\n        _show(_call(choice, values, deps))\n    except Exception as error:\n        print(f\"error: {error}\")\n        return 1\n    return 0\n```\n`_call` update: `action = bound[0]`.\n\n**(7) docstring of emit_cli** line: \"Every declared verb parses; bound verbs dispatch...\" + mention routes.\n\nHmm — one subtlety: `_show` for the budget single-record alternate ✓ prints one record line.\n\n**(8) `_report_dead_options` update:** consumed = union over choices of: need dests + alternate field dests... wait alternates' need dests are the bound dests ✓ (need built from bindings). Route flags ✓. Base consumed: the base action's field dests via argv (existing logic) — but ALSO the base may be chosen... The union covers everything: choices' need dests + base consumed(via argv). Let me just compute per leaf: consumed = {dest for _d,_k,_f in union_of_needs} ∪ base_consumed(argv of base action). Pass to _report_dead_options. Note: base_consumed needs actions/assembled/argv of the base action.\n\nWhere do I call _report_dead_options now? In phase 0 per leaf (I have everything): `_report_dead_options(where, destinations, consumed, stats)` — new signature. The parser loop no longer calls it.\n\n**Wait** — one more important check: `alternates` need dests are exactly leaf dests, but what if the leaf ALSO passes a flag consumed by neither base nor alternates (book -author) → reported dead ✓ (book: alternates empty because consistency check fails; consumed = base's {} ∪ ∅ → author dead ✓).\n\n**(9) routes in load_context** need SURFACE_DECISIONS_NAME constant. review_surface owns it; generator defines its own const? Duplicate strings risk drift. generator could `from review_surface import SURFACE_DECISIONS_NAME`? circular? review_surface imports: json, os, re, sys, codegen, load_documents, normalize, Report, requirements_sha. NOT generator ✓. But importing a top-level script module from generator is a bit odd... precedent: review_slots imports generator. generator importing review_surface would make review_surface importable... it's a script in root; import works. Hmm, but load_surface_decisions is the loader — reuse it! `from review_surface import load_surface_decisions, SURFACE_DECISIONS_NAME`? Wait — would that create an import cycle: review_surface imports kernel stuff only ✓; review_slots imports generator (not review_surface) ✓. OK but hmm, \"generator.py imports review_surface\" — the dependency direction generator→review tool feels inverted but pragmatic: the decisions file's schema belongs to review_surface. Alternatively define the route records OWN file `route_decisions.json` in review_slots? No...\n\nActually WAIT. Let me reconsider: maybe routes belong in `method_slots.json`?! It's \"method slots\" — human answers about methods/services... A route = \"flag selects method\". Hmm, different domain (CLI). But the FILE is loaded by generator already and sha-stamped... Naming: method_slots is for repo pattern slots. Mixing CLI routes muddies it. \n\nDecision: extend surface_decisions.json with kind:\"route\" records, owned by review_surface.py (the CLI-surface decisions file — semantically right: it's a surface decision \"what this flag does\"), and generator reads via a tiny local loader (not importing review_surface) to keep generator self-contained? Or import. Let me check if other generator code duplicates review loaders: generator loads method_slots inline (not via review_slots.load_method_slots) ✓ precedent: INLINE loading in load_context. I'll inline the surface decisions read in load_context (same as slots), filtering kind==\"route\". Duplicated const: define SURFACE_DECISIONS_NAME in generator too? review_surface defines it; generator inline-reading the same filename literal \"surface_decisions.json\" with a comment. Acceptable (precedent: METHOD_SLOTS_NAME define