lly loads documents. Simpler: `ctx = generator.load_context(output_dir)` and `ctx[\"components\"]` etc.\n\nBut careful: generator.load_context loads documents via load_documents — let me check load_context signature: `def load_context(output_dir):` — it internally does load_documents. ✓.\n\nRoute questions:\n```python\n    decided = {(r.get(\"command\"), r.get(\"subcommand\"), r.get(\"flag\"))\n               for r in ctx.get(\"routes\") or []}\n    questions = []\n    for leaf in leaves:\n        where = (leaf[\"command\"], leaf[\"subcommand\"])\n        if leaf[\"choices\"] is None:\n            continue\n        for dest, flag, kind in leaf[\"dead\"]:\n            if kind != \"truthy\":\n                continue  # a valued flag selects nothing by itself\n            if (where[0], where[1], dest) in decided:\n                continue\n            base_action = None\n            for choice in leaf[\"choices\"]:\n                if not choice[2]:\n                    base_action = choice  # fallback: needs nothing\n            ...\n```\nHmm — base action is the last choice with empty need. Let me get from leaf: choices[-1]? The fallback is appended last, and it's the only one with empty need except... routes/alternates always have non-empty need ✓. So base = choices[-1].\n\n```python\n            base_action = leaf[\"choices\"][-1][0]\n            spec = actions.get(base_action) or {}\n            service = spec.get(\"target_service\")\n            base_method = generator._service_method(ctx, service, spec.get(\"target_method\"))\n            if base_method is None or base_method.get(\"params\"):\n                continue\n            candidates = _route_candidates(ctx, service, spec.get(\"target_method\"))\n            if not candidates:\n                continue\n            questions.append({\n                \"command\": where[0], \"subcommand\": where[1],\n                \"flag\": dest, \"flag_name\": flag,\n                \"service\": service,\n                \"reads\": base_method.get(\"name\"),\n                \"candidates\": candidates,\n            })\n    context = {\"prompt_sha\": requirements_sha(load_requirements(output_dir))}\n    return questions, context\n```\n\n`_route_candidates(ctx, service, base_method_name)`:\n```python\ndef _route_candidates(ctx, service, base_method_name):\n    \"\"\"Parameterless collection reads on the leaf's own service, usable ones\n    only: routing a flag to a stub moves the stub, it does not fix it.\"\"\"\n    wanted = normalize(service or \"\")\n    candidates = []\n    for repo in ctx[\"repositories\"]:\n        names = {normalize(repo.get(\"interface_name\") or \"\"),\n                 normalize(repo.get(\"entity\") or \"\")}\n        if wanted not in names:\n            continue\n        holder = repo.get(\"interface_name\") or repo.get(\"entity\")\n        for method in repo.get(\"methods\") or []:\n            if not isinstance(method, dict) or not method.get(\"name\"):\n                continue\n            if method[\"name\"] == base_method_name or method.get(\"params\"):\n                continue\n            if generator._return_shape(ctx, method.get(\"return_type\")) not in (\"collection\", \"entity\"):\n                continue\n            if not _usable(ctx, holder, method):\n                continue\n            candidates.append((service, method[\"name\"]))\n        break\n```\nHmm — candidates from the repo only (the member case). Should I include service methods too? A route to a paramless service read (e.g. LibraryService.something paramless) — possible but services methods often have params. Keep repos for now? Hmm — the base's service could be a SERVICE (e.g. expense list base list_expenses is a service method; candidates from ExpenseService paramless reads...). For expense report -yearly the base isn't paramless so no question anyway. For generality, include both repos and services: search ctx[\"services\"] similarly with holder = service_name. The `_usable` slots check uses holder for match index; service methods might not be in match_index → _slot_pattern returns None → not usable via slots; but plan_method strategy may be crud mechanical → usable ✓.\n\nLet me write `_usable(ctx, holder, method)`:\n```python\ndef _usable(ctx, holder, method):\n    \"\"\"Mechanical, or fully answered by slots: the routed read must run.\"\"\"\n    entry = generator._match_entry(ctx, holder, method.get(\"name\"))\n    strategy, _detail = codegen.plan_method(method, entry, ctx[\"entity_names\"])\n    if strategy.startswith(\"crud:\"):\n        return True\n    pattern, slots = generator._slot_pattern(ctx, holder, method)\n    return bool(pattern) and generator._slots_usable(pattern, slots, method, True)\n```\nCheck generator._match_entry exists and signature: `_match_entry(ctx, holder, name)` ✓ (used in _slot_pattern).\n\nFor services' methods (holder = service_name), _slot_pattern looks at match entries \"contract:<service>.<method>\" — services do have match entries; slots usable if answered. plan_method for service methods with SLOT patterns... _slots_usable(pattern, slots, method, True) treats repo-shaped; fine as approximation. This review is for repos in practice.\n\nNow describe_route + ask_route:\n\n```python\ndef describe_route(index, total, question):\n    \"\"\"The stranded flag, the unfiltered read it rides on, and the finite\n    candidates: the human names the selected method, or skips.\"\"\"\n    lines = [\n        f\"--- question {index}/{total} ---\",\n        f\"FLAG SELECTING A READ: '{question['command']}\"\n        f\"{' ' + question['subcommand'] if question['subcommand'] else ''} \"\n        f\"{question['flag_name']}' parses today and does nothing.\",\n        f\"  the command reads {question['service']}.{question['reads']} \"\n        f\"(no arguments) when the flag is passed.\",\n        \"  candidate methods:\",\n    ]\n    for number, (service, method) in enumerate(question[\"candidates\"], start=1):\n        lines.append(f\"    {number}) {service}.{method}\")\n    lines += [\n        \"  The documents link the flag to none of them; only you can say\",\n        \"  which one it selects. skip leaves the flag reported.\",\n    ]\n    return lines\n\n\ndef ask_route(question, input_fn=input):\n    \"\"\"One candidate number (or name); None on skip.\"\"\"\n    shown = \", \".join(str(n) for n in range(1, len(question[\"candidates\"]) + 1))\n    try:\n        answer = input_fn(f\"choose {shown}, or skip: \").strip().lower()\n    except (EOFError, KeyboardInterrupt):\n        return None\n    if answer.isdigit():\n        index = int(answer) - 1\n        if 0 <= index < len(question[\"candidates\"]):\n            return question[\"candidates\"][index]\n    for service, method in question[\"candidates\"]:\n        if answer in (method.lower(), f\"{service.lower()}.{method.lower()}\"):\n            return (service, method)\n    return None\n```\n\nrun_review merged:\n```python\ndef run_review(output_dir, input_fn=input, out_fn=print):\n    \"\"\"Ask every open duplicate and every stranded flag; return (asked, answered).\"\"\"\n    duplicates, context = collect_duplicates(output_dir)\n    routes, _route_context = collect_routes(output_dir)\n    questions = ([(\"duplicate\", q) for q in duplicates]\n                 + [(\"route\", q) for q in routes])\n    if not questions:\n        out_fn(\"no duplicated surface nodes or stranded flags — nothing to decide\")\n        return 0, 0\n    records = load_surface_decisions(output_dir)\n    answered = 0\n    for number, (kind, question) in enumerate(questions, start=1):\n        if kind == \"duplicate\":\n            for line in describe_question(number, len(questions), question):\n                out_fn(line)\n            keep = ask(question, input_fn)\n            if keep is None:\n                out_fn(\"skipped — left open\")\n                continue\n            record = {\n                \"command\": question[\"command\"],\n                \"subcommand\": question[\"subcommand\"],\n                \"keep\": keep,\n                \"decided_by\": \"user\",\n            }\n        else:\n            for line in describe_route(number, len(questions), question):\n                out_fn(line)\n            picked = ask_route(question, input_fn)\n            if picked is None:\n                out_fn(\"skipped — left open\")\n                continue\n            service, method = picked\n            record = {\n                \"kind\": \"route\",\n                \"command\": question[\"command\"],\n                \"subcommand\": question[\"subcommand\"],\n                \"flag\": question[\"flag\"],\n                \"service\": service,\n                \"method\": method,\n                \"decided_by\": \"user\",\n            }\n        if context[\"prompt_sha\"] is not None:\n            record[\"requirements_sha\"] = context[\"prompt_sha\"]\n        records.append(record)\n        answered += 1\n    save_surface_decisions(output_dir, records)\n    ...components apply as before...\n```\nWait — the components apply part reads components.json and applies duplicates; if there are no duplicate records, apply_surface_decisions returns components unchanged (kept empty → return components) and rewrites the file — writing components.json with no change is a bit noisy but harmless. Current behavior writes after review always. Keep — but careful: components.json rewrite re-serializes the whole file (indent 2) — it already does that on every review run. Fine. Hmm — but if a run only answers ROUTE questions, must not lose anything: apply_surface_decisions(payload[\"components\"], records) → kept includes only keep-records (routes skipped) → if no keep records, returns components unchanged → file rewritten identically ✓.\n\nActually — to be safe about not rewriting components.json when there are no keep-records... it's identical content, fine.\n\nNote `context[\"prompt_sha\"]` is from collect_du