          "text": "Now I'll write the LLM stub-fill machinery. I'll insert it after `strip_code_fences` (line 2806) and before `generate_fallback`.\n\nCode to add:\n\n```python\n# --- the stub pass: the model writes what the emitter refused -------------\n\nSTUB_FILES = (\"repositories.py\", \"services.py\")\nSTUB_PROMPT_BUDGET = 24000   # chars of module text a prompt may carry\n# Modules a filled body may reach for; `datetime` and the store are usually\n# already imported, the rest are added when the body actually uses them.\nBODY_IMPORTS = (\"csv\", \"json\", \"os\", \"re\", \"math\", \"time\", \"io\", \"random\",\n                \"string\", \"collections\", \"itertools\", \"functools\", \"decimal\",\n                \"uuid\", \"datetime\", \"pathlib\", \"sqlite3\")\n\n\ndef find_stub_spans(text):\n    \"\"\"`[(start, end, info)]` for every `raise NotImplementedError` stub.\n\n    `start` is the docstring line when the emitted method has one (the\n    filler rewrites its `[...]` annotation), `end` the closing line of\n    the raise. `info` carries the class, the method's `def` line and the\n    work order the emitter wrote, so the prompt can state the exact hole.\n    \"\"\"\n    lines = text.splitlines()\n    spans = []\n    cls = None\n    def_line = None\n    for index, line in enumerate(lines):\n        if re.match(r\"^class (\\w+)\", line):\n            cls = line.split()[1].rstrip(\":\")\n            def_line = None\n        elif re.match(r\"^    def (\\w+)\", line):\n            def_line = line.strip()\n        elif line == \"        raise NotImplementedError(\":\n            if def_line is None:\n                continue\n            end = None\n            reason = \"\"\n            for offset in (1, 2):\n                probe = lines[index + offset] if index + offset < len(lines) else \"\"\n                if probe.strip().startswith('\"') and probe.strip().endswith('\")'):\n                    reason = probe.strip()[1:-2]\n                    end = index + offset\n                    break\n            if end is None:\n                continue\n            start = index\n            docstring = None\n            if index and lines[index - 1].startswith('        \"\"\"'):\n                start = index - 1\n                docstring = lines[index - 1]\n            spans.append((start, end, {\"cls\": cls, \"def\": def_line,\n                                       \"reason\": reason,\n                                       \"docstring\": docstring}))\n    return spans\n```\nHmm — careful: `for index, line in enumerate(lines)` with `continue` is fine.\n\nMethod name: from def_line: `re.match(r\"def (\\w+)\", def_line.strip())`.\n\n```python\ndef _stub_method(info):\n    match = re.match(r\"def (\\w+)\", info[\"def\"] or \"\")\n    return match.group(1) if match else \"?\"\n\n\ndef _clip_module(text, cls, budget):\n    \"\"\"The module, or its header plus the stub's own class when too big.\"\"\"\n    if len(text) <= budget:\n        return text\n    lines = text.splitlines()\n    header = []\n    for line in lines:\n        if re.match(r\"^class \", line):\n            break\n        header.append(line)\n    block, inside = [], False\n    for line in lines:\n        if re.match(r\"^class \", line):\n            inside = line.split()[1].rstrip(\":\") == cls\n        if inside:\n            block.append(line)\n    clipped = \"\\n\".join(header + [\"\"] + block)\n    return clipped[:budget]\n\n\ndef stub_prompt(requirements, module_text, info, references=(), budget=STUB_PROMPT_BUDGET):\n    \"\"\"One ask: the emitted module is the API reference, fill exactly one hole.\n\n    Nothing is rewritten into a summary: the module shows the real store\n    calls, model constructors and error raises the body must imitate, and a\n    model told a simplified API invents one that does not exist. When the\n    text would not fit `budget`, it is clipped to the module header and the\n    stub's own class; `references` lists callable signatures a service body\n    may need from another module.\n    \"\"\"\n    method = _stub_method(info)\n    lines = [\n        \"A Python module was generated mechanically from the architecture\",\n        \"documents below. Exactly one method could not be derived\",\n        \"mechanically and raises NotImplementedError; write its body.\",\n        \"\",\n        \"Rules:\",\n        \"- Reply with ONLY the replacement for the raise statement: the body\",\n        \"  lines of the method, indented with 8 spaces. No `def` line, no\",\n        \"  docstring, no markdown fences, no explanation.\",\n        f\"- The method is `{info['cls']}.{method}`; it was refused with:\",\n        f\"  {info['reason']}\",\n        \"- Call only what the module shows: `self._store` methods used in\",\n        \"  sibling bodies, `self._repos['Name']` repositories, `models.*`,\",\n        \"  `errors.*`, module helpers. `_store.select` criteria support only\",\n        \"  `eq`, `ge` and `le` — anything else (substring search, several\",\n        \"  fields) reads `self._store.all(self.ENTITY)` and filters in Python.\",\n        \"- If the requirements do not determine the behavior, reply with\",\n        '  `raise NotImplementedError(\"...\")` and nothing else.',\n    ]\n    if references:\n        lines += [\"\", \"Callable methods this module may lean on:\"]\n        lines += [f\"  {signature}\" for signature in references]\n    lines += [\"\", \"requirements:\", (requirements or \"\").strip(), \"\",\n              f\"module ({info.get('file', 'module')}), cut to the stub:\",\n              \"```python\",\n              _clip_module(module_text, info[\"cls\"], budget).rstrip(),\n              \"```\",\n              \"\",\n              \"Now write the body of \"\n              f\"`{info['cls']}.{method}`, replacing `raise NotImplementedError(...)`:\",\n              \"```python\"]\n    return \"\\n\".join(lines) + \"\\n\"\n```\nHmm — \"module ({...file...})\" — I'll pass file in info. But info dict from find_stub_spans doesn't include file; fill_stubs adds it.\n\nContinue:\n\n```python\ndef _clean_body(text):\n    \"\"\"The model's answer as unindented body lines, fences and `def` gone.\"\"\"\n    code = extract_code(text)\n    lines = [line.rstrip() for line in code.splitlines()]\n    while lines and not lines[0].strip():\n        lines.pop(0)\n    while lines and not lines[-1].strip():\n        lines.pop()\n    if lines and re.match(r\"^\\s*def \\w+\", lines[0]):\n        lines.pop(0)  # the whole method, not just its body; keep the rest\n    while lines and not lines[0].strip():\n        lines.pop(0)\n    indents = [len(line) - len(line.lstrip(\" \")) for line in lines if line.strip()]\n    if not indents:\n        return []\n    trim = min(indents)\n    return [line[trim:] if line.strip() else \"\" for line in lines]\n\n\ndef _module_api(text):\n    \"\"\"`(self-able names, store method names)`: what a flat body may touch.\"\"\"\n    selfable = {\"_store\", \"_repos\", \"ENTITY\", \"PK\"}\n    store = set()\n    for line in text.splitlines():\n        match = re.match(r\"^    (?:def )?(\\w+)\", line)\n        if not match:\n            continue\n        selfable.add(match.group(1))\n    ...\n```\nHmm — the store method names must come from store.py, and module defs from the module text. Let me split:\n\n```python\ndef _module_names(text):\n    \"\"\"Class attributes and methods: the `self.<name>` a body may touch.\"\"\"\n    names = {\"_store\", \"_repos\"}\n    for line in text.splitlines():\n        match = re.match(r\"^    (?:def )?(\\w+)\", line)\n        if match:\n            names.add(match.group(1))\n    return names\n```\nThis collects class-level names across ALL classes (fine — generous but harmless: calling self.other_entity_class_method would be wrong but compile-valid; manual review; hmm, \"generous\" weakens the check but keeps false refusals near zero. Refusing only undefined names is the real win.)\n\n```python\ndef _body_refusal(body, module_names, store_names):\n    \"\"\"Why this answer may not replace the stub, or None when it may.\"\"\"\n    if not body:\n        return \"the model returned nothing\"\n    text = \"\\n\".join(body)\n    if \"NotImplementedError\" in text:\n        return \"the model kept it unimplemented\"\n    if all(not line.strip() for line in body):\n        return \"the model returned nothing\"\n    unknown = sorted({name for name in re.findall(r\"self\\.(\\w+)\", text)\n                      if name not in module_names})\n    if unknown:\n        return f\"calls self.{unknown[0]}, which the module does not define\"\n    bad = sorted({name for name in re.findall(r\"self\\._store\\.(\\w+)\", text)\n                  if name not in store_names})\n    if bad:\n        return f\"calls self._store.{bad[0]}, which the store does not define\"\n    return None\n\n\ndef _add_imports(text, body_text):\n    \"\"\"Module imports the body uses but the file never imported.\"\"\"\n    added = []\n    for module in BODY_IMPORTS:\n        if not re.search(rf\"\\b{re.escape(module)}\\.\", body_text):\n            continue\n        if re.search(rf\"^import {re.escape(module)}\\b\", text, re.M):\n            continue\n        if re.search(rf\"^from {re.escape(module)}\\b\", text, re.M):\n            continue\n        lines = text.splitlines()\n        anchor = 0\n        for index, line in enumerate(lines):\n            if re.match(r\"^(import|from) \\w\", line):\n                anchor = index\n        lines.insert(anchor + 1, f\"import {module}\")\n        text = \"\\n\".join(lines) + (\"\\n\" if text.endswith(\"\\n\") else \"\")\n        added.append(module)\n    return text, added\n```\nHmm wait — inserting into `text` iteratively + rebuilding; the `text.endswith(\"\\n\")` handling: original text has trailing \"\\n\"? render_program files: emit_repositories returns \"\\n\".join(lines) + \"\\n\" ✓ trailing newline. splitlines() drops it, join + re-add \"\\n\" ✓ (always add since our inputs have it; keep the conditional for safety).\n\nNow the driver:\n\n```python\ndef fill_stubs(files, ctx, model_path, requirements, out=print):\n    \"\"\"Ask the model to write the bodies the mechanical emitter refused.\n\n    Best-effort by design: the mechanical method set is already final and\n    honest, so a missing model, a refused answer or a body that does not\n    compile leaves the stub in place and says so. Every accepted body\n    passes the module's `compile`, may not call `self.<name>` or\n    `self._store.<name>` the module never defines, and is tagged in its\n    docstring as LLM-written, because nothing here verifies behavior.\n    Returns `[(label, verdict)]`, one row per stub — written or kept.\n    \"\"\"\n    targets = [(name, find_stub_spans(files[name])) for name in STUB_FILES\n               if isinstance(files.get(name), str)]\n    todo = sum(len(spans) for _name, spans in targets)\n    if not todo:\n        return []\n    model_name = os.path.basename(model_path)\n    rows = []\n    try:\n        llm = runner.load_model(model_path, logits_all=False)\n    except Exception as error:  # missing file, missing runtime: stubs stand\n        return [(f\"{todo} stub(s)\", f\"kept — the model would not load ({error})\")]\n    store_names = {match.group(1) for match in\n                   re.finditer(r\"^    def (\\w+)\", files.get(\"store.py\") or \"\")}\n    for filename, spans in targets:\n        text = files[filename]\n        references = ()\n        if filename == \"services.py\":\n            references = _method_signatures(files.get(\"repositories.py\") or \"\")\n        for start, end, info in reversed(spans):\n            info[\"file\"] = filename\n            method = _stub_method(info)\n            label = f\"{info['cls']}.{method}\"\n            prompt = stub_prompt(requirements, text, info, references)\n            result = runner.generate_free(llm, prompt, f\"stub:{label}\",\n                                          temperature=FALLBACK_TEMPERATURE,\n                                          seed=FALLBACK_SEED)\n            body = _clean_body(result.text)\n            refusal = _body_refusal(body, _module_names(text), store_names)\n            if refusal is not None:\n                rows.append((label, f\"kept stub — {refusal}\"))\n                continue\n            lines = text.splitlines()\n            replacement = []\n            docstring = info.get(\"docstring\")\n            if docstring:\n                rewritten = re.sub(r\"\\[[^\\]]*\\](\\\"\\\"\\\")$\",\n                                   f\"[llm-written by {model_name} — review]\\\"\\\"\\\"\",\n                                   docstring) if ... \n```\nHmm the regex: docstring line = `        \"\"\"Description. [rule x: y]\"\"\"`. Pattern: replace the LAST bracket group before the closing quotes:\n`re.sub(r\"\\[[^\\]]*\\]\\s*(\\\"\\\"\\\")$\", lambda m: f\"[llm-written by {model_name} — review]{m.group(1)}\", docstring)` — but `$` applies to the string; docstring has no trailing spaces (we saw `]\"\"\"`) ✓. If no bracket at the end (shouldn't happen for stubs), leave as-is. Also what if the docstring contains an EARLIER bracket? The regex anchors at the end ✓.\n\n```python\n                if rewritten != docstring:\n                    replacement.append(rewritten)\n                else:\n                    replacement.append(docstring)\n            else:\n                replacement = []\n            replacement.extend(\"        \" + line if line.strip() else \"\"\n                               for line in body)\n            candidate = \"\\n\".join(lines[:start] + replacement + lines[end + 1:])\n            if text.endswith(\"\\n\"):\n                candidate += \"\\n\"\n            try:\n                compile(candidate, filename, \"exec\")\n            except SyntaxError as error:\n                rows.append((label, f\"kept stub — the body does not compile ({error.msg})\"))\n                continue\n            text, added = _add_imports(candidate, \"\\n\".join(body))\n            hmm — _add_imports returns new text; but compile should be re-checked after adding imports (inserting import lines can't break; skip re-compile? cheap to compile again... do it: compile(text...) — can't fail from adding an import line. Skip.\n            files[filename] = text  # store for the next (earlier) stub in the same file\n            note = \"written\" + (f\"; added import {' '.join(added)}\" if added else \"\")\n            rows.append((label, note))\n    return rows\n```\nWait — bug: iterating `reversed(spans)`, each replacement uses `text` captured at loop start; I mutate `text` inside: `files[filename] = text` and `text = ...`? I need to keep `text` updated for the next span. Since spans are reversed, replacements at earlier positions don't shift later-processed (earlier) spans ✓. So update `text` variable + files at the end of each iteration. Let me restructure: `text = files[filename]` before inner loop; inside: `text = candidate...` after success; after inner loop `files[filename] = text`.\n\nBut careful: `_module_names(text)` and store_names computed per span from current text ✓ fine.\n\nAlso `reversed(spans)`: spans sorted ascending from scan ✓.\n\nAlso `max_new_tokens`: default None → generation_budget gives room minus prompt, capped MAX_NEW_TOKENS=3584; a chatty model could output a lot; whatever.\n\nAlso the compile of the module: candidate compiles only if the BODY is syntactically valid at module level ✓.\n\n`_method_signatures`:\n```python\ndef _method_signatures(text):\n    \"\"\"`Class.method(signature) -> returns` for the prompt's cross-module list.\"\"\"\n    signatures = []\n    cls = None\n    for line in text.splitlines():\n        match = re.match(r\"^class (\\w+)\", line)\n        if match:\n            cls = match.group(1)\n            continue\n        match = re.match(r\"^    def (\\w+\\(.*\\)) -> \", line)\n        if match and cls:\n            signatures.append(f\"{cls}.{match.group(1)}\")\n    return signatures\n```\nEmits \"BookRepository.create_book(book: models.Book = None, isbn: str = None)\" — includes params but not returns; fine. Let me capture to the colon: `r\"^    def (\\w+\\(.*\\))\\s*(->.*)?:$\"` → f\"{cls}.{name}{ret}\". Keep it simple: take the whole def line stripped of leading spaces and trailing \":\" → `BookRepository.def create...` no. Let me do:\n```python\nmatch = re.match(r\"^    def (\\w+\\(.*?\\)(?:\\s*->\\s*[^:]+)?):$\", line)\nif match:\n    signatures.append(f\"{cls}.{match.group(1)}\")\n```\nGood enough.\n\nNow main() wiring:\n\n```python\n    if args.model:\n        rows = fill_stubs(files, ctx, args.model, load_requirements(args.out_dir))\n        stats[\"llm_stubs\"] = rows\n```\nHmm — but I want per-stub prints at the time... fill_stubs returns rows; main prints them after writing? Print after write with the coverage section:\n\n```python\n    if stats.get(\"llm_stubs\"):\n        written = sum(1 for _label, verdict in stats[\"llm_stubs\"] if verdict.startswith(\"written\"))\n        print(f\"llm: {written} of {len(stats['llm_stubs'])} stub(s) written by \"\n              f\"{os.path.basename(args.model)} — bodies marked 'llm-written'; read them\")\n        for label, verdict in stats[\"llm_stubs\"]:\n            if not verdict.startswith(\"written\"):\n                print(f\"  llm kept: {label} — {verdict}\")\n```\nHmm, verdict strings: \"written\" / \"written; added import json\" / \"kept stub — ...\". Let me standardize: fill_stubs rows = (label, \"written\", note) ? Simpler: rows as dicts? Keep tuples (label, verdict) with verdict in {\"written\", f\"written (+import json)\"} ... Let me use explicit tuples (label, status, note): status \"written\"/\"kept\". Then printing:\n```python\n    for label, status, note in stats.get(\"llm_stubs\") or []:\n        marker = \"llm written\" if status == \"written\" else \"llm kept stub\"\n        print(f\"  {marker}: {label}{' — ' + note if note else ''}\")\n```\nand summary line: `llm: N of M refused stubs written by <model> — not mechanically verified; review`.\n\nAnd progress during the pass: print a line per stub BEFORE generating? Generation takes seconds→minute each; printing the plan first is nice: `fill_stubs(..., out=print)` prints `llm: writing BookRepository.search_books...`. But then rows printed again in main — duplicate. Let me have fill_stubs PRINT progress (stub queued) via print inside the loop: `print(f\"llm: {label}: asking the model ({i}/{todo})...\")`, and main prints the final rows. That's acceptable verbosity for a long-running pass.\n\nWait — the loader messages: load_model prints? Let me check runner.load_model signature body briefly (it may print \"[model] ...\" lines when quiet=False). Let me read lines 96-135 of runner.py.\n\nAlso one more design point: `--model` with the DEGENERATE path: main returns earlier ✓.\n\nAlso: existing tests that call generator.main([out]) without --model → fill_stubs not called ✓. A test with patched runner + --model on the shop fixture: are there stubs in the shop fixture? Yes: `export_products` (NO_PATTERN_IN_DOMAIN) and `price_report` (custom aggregate in service) — 2 stubs? and `find_products_by_category` is undecided (a tie → gate may refuse! The fixture's pattern_matches has an undecided FILTER_BY_FIELD → probe blocker → gate refuses generation!). Hmm — but GenerationTests setUpClass asserts main([out]) == 0, so the fixture passes the gate... meaning ties resolved somewhere (maybe low margin auto-decides or the tie is \"undecided\" but the probe treats it as blocker? The test says generation succeeds, so fine).\n\nFor the LLM-pass test I'll use a hand-built small out dir (like the shop fixture but simpler) OR use the shop fixture and check that fill_stubs with a fake runner replaces `export_products`/`price_report` stubs. Better: unit-test fill_stubs directly on rendered files from a small ctx via render_program (no disk): \n```python\nctx = ... small (like CliChoiceTests.product_ctx + a custom method) ...\nfiles = generator.render_program(ctx, stats)  # includes stub for export_products\nrows = generator.fill_stubs(files, ctx, \"fake.gguf\", requirements, ...) with patched generator.runner\n```\nBut render_program reads the store template from disk ✓ fine; no llama unless runner used — patch `generator.runner.load_model`/`generate_free` — but `import runner` contains llama_cpp import at module import; tests already import generator (which imports runner) — so llama_cpp must be installed in the test env (it is, since generator imports fine in tests).\n\nFake runner setup:\n```python\nfrom unittest import mock\nfake_llm = object()\nwith mock.patch.object(generator.runner, \"load_model\", return_value=fake_llm), \\\n     mock.patch.object(generator.runner, \"generate_free\") as gen:\n    gen.return_value = types.SimpleNamespace(text=\"...body...\", tokens=42, seconds=0.1)\n    rows = generator.fill_stubs(files, ctx, \"Ministral-3-3B-Instruct-2512-Q4_K_M.gguf\", \"requirements text\")\n```\nWait — mock.patch.object(generate_free) replaces the function; fill_stubs calls runner.generate_free ✓. But `generator.runner` is the SAME module object as `kernel.extraction.runner` ✓ patching affects both.\n\nTest cases:\n1. `test_the_model_fills_a_stub_and_the_body_lands_marked`: body returns e.g.\n```\nvalues = self._store.all(self.ENTITY)\nreturn [models.Product(**record) for record in values]\n```\n→ module contains the body (indented 8), docstring rewritten `[llm-written by Ministar...]`, compile-ok, rows == [(\"ProductRepository.export_products\", \"written\", \"\")]. Hmm — wait, export_products's real stub: let me check the shop fixture: ProductRepository.export_products(file_path: str) -> None refused NO_PATTERN_IN_DOMAIN → stub in repositories.py. In my hand ctx I should mirror a similar stub. Let me build the ctx minimal (like the service composition ctx) and add a custom method with an entry t