          "text": "The replay reproduced the Mac's five \"written\" rows exactly ✓. Smoke results:\n\n1. `member add` (mechanical) ✓ — note is_active/membership_date are null (mechanical default; the prompt says defaults True — the emitter doesn't apply defaults; that's a pre-existing mechanical gap, known from run 1? Possibly a standing finding. Interesting: find_active_members uses is_active=True — CLI-added members are NULL → never active! That's a mechanical finding (defaults not applied on add). Worth flagging — hmm, was that already known? The summary mentions run-1 review verified `member add` mechanically — but maybe didn't check is_active. Actually this matters for the route `member list -active-only`: a freshly added member (via CLI) won't appear active. Let me flag it as a finding (mechanical-level: prompt says default True / Book available_copies default 1 — neither applied by create_* bodies). Let me verify book add via CLI: -copies required=True so user passes it; but member is_active has no flag → default not applied → NULL. This is a mechanical emitter gap (defaults from prompt not implemented) — a \"findings\" item. Hmm — but careful: maybe there IS a mechanism (data_models.json lists defaults; the create_* body uses _record_values with all fields; no default application). So yes: **mechanical finding: model defaults (is_active=True, available_copies=1) are documented but never applied on create**. Also membership_date null (created without date).\n\nWait — but is that in scope? The user's pipeline goal: honest stubs; this is a mechanical gap in emitted create bodies. It's a legit finding to report. Let me check data_models.json to see if defaults are recorded there — if the doc records \"default\": true/1, the emitter ignoring them is a mechanical gap worth a fix (but that's the emitter's / kernel's territory — a catalog/codegen fix). Report it; let user decide.\n\n2. `book search hobbit` → CRASH (int.lower) — even the title-matching query, because