ii) models type as str. This is a DECISION + mechanical change (store template) most likely.\n\nWait — actually, let me think about (i) more precisely. `_persist` does json.dumps(record). Options: `json.dumps(..., default=lambda value: value.isoformat() if hasattr(value, \"isoformat\") else str(value))`. And on load, strings come back as strings (models then carry str though annotated datetime). Date COMPARISONS: get_overdue_loans does str(...)[:10] ✓ works either way. So a `default=` hook would make return_book/borrow_book work.\n\nAlso note: with the hook, `update_loan` patch contains a datetime → stored as ISO string ✓; subsequent reads give str ✓; `loan.return_date is not None` ✓ (str). Round-trip: second return attempt check works (return_date not None) ✓.\n\nSo fix (i) = one-line in kernel/codegen_store_template.py's _persist + a regression test. But it changes ALL generated store.py (byte-diffs on next regeneration) — the user cares about byte-identity? \"regenerations of out/library + out/expenses without --model remain byte-identical\" was a property, not a goal per se; a deliberate template change re-baselines it. Still: user decision.\n\nB. **Dataclass API habit (.copy(update=…)).** The model used pydantic's copy. In a dataclass world, that's `dataclasses.replace(book, available_copies=...)`. The prompt's API section could state: \"models are plain dataclasses: copy with `dataclasses.replace`, never `.copy(update=…)`\". OR — alternatively — keep the body as the model wrote and let it fail... no. A prompt line is cheap and precise. But careful: is it right for the model to mutate and update? `book.available_copies -= 1; update_book(book_id, book)` — simpler, uses only shown API (run-2's borrow answer? we don't have it). Actually the guard could... no. A prompt fact line is the fix: \"dataclasses have no `.copy`; mutate the read object and pass it back.\" Hmm — the API section already says \"read their fields as attributes, never .get\". Add: \"and no `.copy(update=...)`: mutate and pass the dataclass back to the repository.\" Good.\n\nC. **Search semantics + int/str field blindness.** `record.get(\"author_id\").lower()` — the model treats unknown fields as strings. The fields line lists ONLY names, not types! Improvement: include TYPES in the API fields list: `Book(id: int, title: str, isbn: str, published_year: int|None, available_copies: int, author_id: int)`. The emitter KNOWS the types (data_models/annotations) — `_model_fields` just prints names today; it can read the annotation text after `name:`. That would stop int.lower instantly. And \"never call str methods on ids / types are as listed\". Cheap and mechanical.\n\nAlso the author NAME search: `search by title/author/isbn` — architecturally the repo can't join. But the SERVICE could orchestrate: authors via AuthorRepository (`find_books_by_author` returns books for an author id, not name...). Honestly the pragmatic scope: search title/isbn here; author name search is a documented gap (finding). OR — the service could do: for each author matching name substring (AuthorRepository.read_all_authors) collect book ids? No method for \"books by author id\" — wait, AuthorRepository.find_books_by_author(author_id) EXISTS ✓. So the SERVICE could: `author_matches = [a for a in read_all_authors() if query in a.name]` then `books += find_books_by_author(a.id)` etc. That's the clean path for the service's `search_books` — but the REPO's search_books_by_title_author_isbn still can't do author properly. The doc attributes search to BookRepository (\"search by title/author/isbn\") — structurally impossible in the repo layer; it belongs in the service. That's a FINDING: doc/codegen layer mismatch — the emitter placed the method in BookRepository per the architecture docs; realizing it needs author data → the service layer. Options: keep repo search = title/isbn only + service adds author matches via AuthorRepository (composite), or accept a documented limitation. Flag for decision.\n\nSo my next-step proposal (for user approval, in the spirit of previous iterations):\n1. `_persist` serializes date/time values (store template) — makes annotated datetimes work on file DBs. [mechanical fix]\n2. API section prints field TYPES, not just names. [prompt fix]\n3. API section states: plain dataclasses, no `.copy(update=…)` — mutate and pass back; and dates: once (1) lands, no prompt text needed... Hmm — with (1) the model's datetime usage is correct as-is; but reads return strings (annotations lie). Eh — with default=str the WRITE works; the annotation semantics still off but pragmatically OK. So (1) alone suffices; no date prompt text. And (3) dataclass-copy note.\n4. Optional: service search_books gets author-name capability? Leave as finding; the prompt can't decide design. Actually — maybe leave entirely to the user's call.\n\nBut first — the EXPENSES dump is still missing! The user hasn't pasted it. Before doing more code work, maybe I should ask for expenses + present library findings + await decisions? The user's rhythm: they paste, I analyze, then we decide edits. I have three decisions pending now (dates convention, prompt fact lines, search design) — and expenses review pending.\n\nHmm — but wait. Fixes (2) and (3) are unambiguous prompt improvements I could implement immediately (like the previous round). The dates fix (1) is a template decision. Let me propose all, ask which to implement... Actually the user's style in this session: I proposed, they said \"yes\", I implemented. Here I could: (a) report library findings, (b) ask for expenses dump, (c) present the three-item proposal for approval. Keep the report CRISP. Better to not implement before their yes (esp. the store template change).\n\nAlso should I quickly check the expenses side mechanically for the same classes of bug? I can't — no dump yet. Ask.\n\nOne more important thing to verify before reporting: does the CURRENT `borrow_book` failure happen before or after the avail