tly, but dies with its dependency (same crash).\n- `return_book` ✗ `Object of type datetime is not JSON serializable` — the store persists records with `json.dumps`; the model assigned `datetime.now()` (consistent with the models' annotations!) and the file-backed store can't hold it. In-memory runs would pass; file DBs can't.\n- `borrow_book` ✗ `'Book' object has no attribute 'copy'` — pydantic habit (`.copy(update=…)`) on a plain dataclass; also invents the 14-day due date (prompt says nothing). Its guard paths work (0 copies → clean domain error).\n\n**Three findings with teeth:**\n1. Date convention: models say `datetime.datetime`, store demands JSON → nobody can be right. Options: teach the store template to serialize dates (`_persist`'s json.dumps default → isoformat), or declare ISO-string convention.\n2. Mechanical defaults dropped: prompt defaults (`copies=1`, `is_active=True`) absent from data_models.json → `member add` makes `is_active: null` → `member list -active-only`/`find_active_members` never see CLI-added members. Extraction finding + emitter would need to apply defaults.\n3. Author-name search is architecturally homeless (repo can't join; the service has the tools via `AuthorRepository`).\n\n**Proposal** (for approval, small and separate):\n- (A) store template: `json.dumps(..., default=…)` that isoformats date/datetime — makes the annotated types true; regression test.\n- (B) prompt API section: field list gains types (`Book(id: int, title: str, …)`) and one fact line: models are plain dataclasses — mutate and pass back, no `.copy(update=…)`.\n- (C) whatever the user decides about defaults + author search (their call; can also be left as findings).\n\nThen: paste the expenses dump and I'll do the same review there.\n\nWait — should I present (A) as recommended? Yes: making the store serialize what the models annotate is the honest fix (it fixes ALL programs' date handling; e.g. expenses dates). And keep the prompt free of a lie. But it's a template change → user approves.\n\nAlso — I should be careful in the report to distinguish \"LLM body quality\" vs \"mechanical/infrastructure findings\". The user's process cares about which layer is at fault. Let me write it compactly. Also mention the replay at /tmp/esus_llm_check2 and that the run's files matched exactly.\n\nOne more check — I claimed \"the prompt never states a loan period\". Confirmed by reading prompt_library_system.txt (no due date rule). ✓ 14 days is invented (a standard-library default-ish guess; flag, maybe acceptable — the user may decide to keep 14 as a documented assumption).\n\nAlso — hmm, before writing: the return_book crash nuance \"in-memory would work\" — let me be careful: with `--db :memory:`... the CLI default DB is None → DEFAULT_DB maybe \":memory\"? `build_cli(args.db or DEFAULT_DB)` — DEFAULT_DB probably \":memory:\"? Hmm, earlier the shop usage said \"default: shop.db in the working directory; use :memory: to stay in memory\" — so default is a FILE (library.db?) — so default runs are file-backed → crash. Right: file-backed is the NORM, so the datetime bug bites by default. Let me check DEFAULT_DB quickly... not critical; the finding stands either way (a file DB is the normal case — my smoke used one; the Mac's smoke.db too). Skip.\n\nActually one more nuance for finding 1: are there OTHER programs already storing dates successfully? The mechanical bodies that store dates: `create_member` with membership_date (CLI member add passes no date → null). Expenses: create_expense(expense) with date fields — CLI `expense add` passes a date STRING from args (adapter → str) → works. So today, dates only work because adapters pass strings; an LLM body naturally constructs datetimes from annotations. The store fix (default=str-ish) covers both. ✓.\n\nLet me also note in the report that the harness (fill pass) itself is now clean: 5/5 written, no refusals, guard didn't false-positive — the remaining failures are value/API knowledge (what to