          "text": "More CLI mechanics to accommodate:\n\n1. `-amount` is `type=int` — the CLI's parse type is int, NOT the DECIMAL_TO_CENTS transform at the argparse level. So `-amount 25.00` → \"invalid int value\". So amounts are passed as integer CENTS directly on the CLI?? Wait — the transform DECIMAL_TO_CENTS would multiply by 100... but argparse says `-amount AMOUNT` with type=int. Then the transform would take the int (e.g. 25) and do int(round(float(25) * 100)) = 2500?? Hmm — argparse int + DECIMAL_TO_CENTS transform → passing `-amount 25` → transform → 2500 cents. Let me check: `_transform(\"DECIMAL_TO_CENTS\", value=25, field)`: `int(round(float(25) * 100))` = 2500 ✓. And passing \"25.00\" fails at argparse int. So the CLI wants `-amount 25` (dollars as int) → stored 2500. OK — use integer dollars: -amount 25 → 2500 ✓. Or maybe the intended semantics is cents if no decimal. Whatever — use `-amount 25` and check stored amount_cents = 2500.\n\nHmm wait, is that right? If argparse type=int and transform DECIMAL_TO_CENTS, values like `-amount 25` → 2500 cents ✓. And my expectation \"25.00 dollars\" → pass 25. Fine.\n\nHmm — but what about the DECIMAL_TO_CENTS transform at the adapter level... the transform already ran? The CLI: parse (int) → dispatch applies transforms → 2500 ✓. Good.\n\n2. `expense report -monthly 2026-10 -yearly 2026` → `error: Invalid isoformat string: '2026-10'` — interesting! The ISO_DATE transform on month \"2026-10\" runs `datetime.date.fromisoformat(\"2026-10\")` → ValueError → \"error: Invalid isoformat string\" — the KNOWN Budget.month-class issue, but here it's MONTHLY REPORT's month (\"YYYY-MM\" per prompt) being transformed as ISO_DATE. So `expense report` can't be invoked with the prompt's own \"YYYY-MM\" format! It never even reaches the LLM body (get_monthly_report). So the monthly report is INACCESSIBLE via CLI due to the adapter's ISO_DATE month. That's ANOTHER instance of the month-format extraction issue (beyond budget list