          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "Now I have the full picture. Let me design the changes.\n\n## Fix A: store template serializes date/datetime as ISO\n\nIn `kernel/codegen_store_template.py`:\n- `_persist` does `json.dumps(self._records[entity][key], sort_keys=True)` — fails on `datetime.date`/`datetime.datetime` objects.\n- Add a `_json_default` function that converts date/datetime to ISO strings:\n\n```python\ndef _json_default(value):\n    \"\"\"Dates and datetimes persist as ISO strings (times keep seconds).\"\"\"\n    if isinstance(value, (datetime.date, datetime.datetime)):\n        return value.isoformat()\n    raise TypeError(...)\n```\n\nWait — careful: `datetime.datetime` is a subclass of `datetime.date`, so `isinstance(value, datetime.date)` covers both. `datetime.isoformat()` gives \"2026-10-19T12:34:56.789\" — fine.\n\nBut hold on: should the serialization convert, or should it fail loudly? The docstring says \"values must stay JSON-shaped (str, int, float, bool, None — everything the CLI parses), and anything else fails loudly instead of persisting wrong.\" Hmm — the design intent was to fail loudly on non-JSON values. But the LLM bodies (and potentially future hand-written code) construct date objects. The decision (fix A, approved) is: dates serialize to ISO strings.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: the in-memory record keeps the actual date object; only the persisted doc is converted. Then on reload, the value comes back as a string. So memory mode ≠ file mode for date-valued fields (memory keeps the date object; file keeps the string). Hmm — the store's charter says \"file-backed behavior is identical to memory by construction\". Converting at dump time breaks that equality slightly: a record added with `datetime.date(2026, 10, 5)` in memory mode returns the date object from `get`; in file mode (after reopen) it returns the ISO string.\n\nOptions:\n1. `default=` in json.dumps only (dump-time conversion): memory keeps date object, file stores string, reload returns string. Divergence.\n2. Normalize at `add`/`update` time: convert date/datetime to ISO strings when the record enters the store. Then memory and file are identical (both strings), comparisons with strings work, `str(record.get('due_date'))[:10]` works, and the model's `expense_date[:7]` works even if the model stored a date. This is more consistent, but it mutates the caller's data semantics — hmm, is that OK? The store already says \"records are plain dicts ... values must stay JSON-shaped\". Normalizing date objects to ISO strings is exactly \"making values JSON-shaped\" at the door. And it preserves the \"identical by construction\" property. \n\nBut wait — would normalizing break anything? `update` merges non-None values: `{k: v for k, v in values.items() if v is not None}` — normalization there too. `add` stores `dict(values)`. Also nested values? Records are flat dicts keyed by field name; keep it flat — only top-level values normalized. Nested date objects would still fail at dump (loudly) — fine.\n\nWhich does the user expect? The approved fix A description from my earlier proposal: \"store template `json.dumps(..., default=…)` ISO-formatting dates (`kernel/codegen_store_template.py` + regression test)\". So the APPROVED form is the `default=` parameter. Hmm. But then memory/file divergence: in file mode, a date passed to `add` is json-dumped with default → stored as ISO string in the db; `get` in the SAME session (before close) returns the in-memory record — with the date object still in it! Because `space[key] = record` keeps the original. So within one process, `get` returns a date object; after reopen, returns a string. That's a real behavioral trap: `get_overdue_loans` in library does `str(record.get('due_date'))[:10]` — works for both (str() of date gives \"2026-10-05\"). But equality checks against strings would differ...\n\nHmm. Which is more faithful to what the user approved? The checkpoint says: \"Proposed fixes awaiting user go/no-go: A store template `json.dumps(..., default=…)` ISO-formatting dates (`kernel/codegen_store_template.py` + regression test)\". And the user just said \"Yes — A and B together\". So implement A as described: `default=`. But I can make it MORE correct without stretching: normalize the value into the record at add/update time so both modes agree? That changes more code paths and could be seen as scope creep beyond what was approved.\n\nLet me think about what actually fixes the observed failures:\n- `return_book` failure: body sets `return_date` (or loan `return_date`) to a datetime/date object, then store.update/add → `_persist` json.dumps fails. With `default=`: persists fine as ISO string. In-process record keeps the object until reload; after reload it's a string. The body's later logic (e.g., checking overdue) — within the same process, might compare date objects vs strings weirdly. But the immediate crash is gone and persisted data is right.\n- `borrow_book` failure: `.copy(update=...)` — that's fix B's domain (prompt fact line). Its secondary issue: invents 14-day due date — C decision.\n\nAlso the store docstring says \"anything else fails loudly instead of persisting wrong\" — with `default=`, dates no longer fail; they persist as ISO. Update the docstring accordingly: \"date and datetime values persist as ISO strings; anything else fails loudly.\"\n\nNow — should I ALSO normalize into memory so file/memory are identical? The store's design statement \"file-backed behavior is identical to memory by construction\" is important. With dump-time-only conversion, that identity is broken for date values (memory: date; reopened file: str). Within-session file mode returns the date (same as memory). So the divergence appears only across reopen — which is a normal persistence boundary; values round-trip through JSON, so ints stay ints, strings stay strings... and dates become strings. Honestly, any store has this boundary; the doc can state it.\n\nAlternatively normalize at add/update: