her. Then json.dumps everywhere works.\n\nLet me check the cli template's dumping.\n\nAlso check `_record_values` in services/repos templates: \"passes dataclass attrs raw\". Dataclass attrs could be date objects from LLM code... but normalization at store.add/update is the choke point for anything that gets persisted. But records also flow back out of the store (get/select/all) and get compared. If normalization happens on entry, all subsequent flows see strings — consistent.\n\nI think the best fix A implementation:\n1. Add `_json_shaped(value)` helper (or inline) that converts `datetime.date`/`datetime.datetime` → ISO string.\n2. Apply normalization in `add` (on the copied dict) and in `update` (on merged non-None values).\n3. ALSO add `default=` to json.dumps in `_persist` as a safety net for values that bypass (there are none today, but belt-and-braces for any nested/unanticipated path — hmm, nested dates would produce strings at dump but the in-memory copy stays a date → divergence again; but nested values aren't a documented shape anyway; keep the default= as backstop).\n4. Update docstrings: values are JSON-shaped; dates/datetimes are normalized to ISO strings on entry.\n5. Regression tests in StorePersistenceTests: add a date value via store.add with file backend, close, reopen, assert ISO string and that json.dumps works; also assert in-memory store returns the string immediately (equality memory/file).\n\nWait, but the approved fix said `json.dumps(..., default=…)`. Should I stick exactly to `default=` only? Let me weigh: The user approved my proposal as pitched, but the pitch was one line; the user trusts me to implement the right thing (\"A: store template serializes date/datetime as ISO in the JSON column\"). The intent: storing date objects must work and produce ISO in the DB. Normalizing at entry satisfies the intent MORE completely (in-memory records also become JSON-safe, CLI printing works, memory==file holds). The `default=` alone does not fix CLI printing of returned records and creates memory/reopen divergence. I'll implement normalization-at-entry + `default=` backstop, and explain in the commit message + response. I think that's within the spirit and clearly better engineering. Actually hmm, let me check the CLI print path first to confirm the in-memory date would crash the CLI — that would justify the stronger fix strongly.\n\nLet me check cli.py print code.\n\n## Fix B: prompt API section improvements\n\nCurrent `_model_fields` produces `Widget(widget_id, title)`. Proposed: fields WITH types: `Book(id: int, title: str, ...)`. The source models module text has lines like:\n```\n    id: int = None\n    title: str = None\n    loan_date: datetime.datetime = None\n    is_active: bool = True\n```\n(really it's `id: int = None`, etc.)\n\nSo parse `^    (\\w+): ([^=]+?)\\s*=` → name, type. Keep it robust: `^    (\\w+):\\s*(.+?)\\s*=` might miss fields without default... emitted models always have `= None` default (per the models template docstring \"Every field defaults to None\"). But defaults could be `= True` etc. Let me use regex `^    (\\w+)(?::\\s*([^=]+?))?\\s*=` and fall back to name-only if no type. Also fields might use `Optional[...]`. Fine.\n\nNew entry format: `Book(id: int, title: str, is_active: bool)`.\n\nPlus TWO fact lines:\n1. \"Models are plain dataclasses: to change one, set its fields and pass the object back — there is no `.copy(...)` (pydantic-style) method.\" — something like:\n   \"- The dataclasses are plain: mutate the attributes and pass the object back; there is no `copy(...)` with keyword updates.\"\n2. Dates are ISO strings fact: \"record values are JSON-shaped: fields annotated `datetime.date`/`datetime.datetime` hold ISO strings like `2026-10-05` (slice/compare them as strings; use `datetime.date.fromisoformat` only when doing date arithmetic).\" Hmm — but careful: in-memory, after a body stores a date object... with normalization they're always strings. So the claim \"hold I