          "text": "Replay reproduced the Mac's five rows exactly. Run-4 verdicts:\n\n## Library run 4 — smoke results\n\n| Body | Run 3 | Run 4 now | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `renew_membership` | ✅ | ✅ | toggle True→False→True persisted; missing id → clean `MemberNotFoundError` |\n| `search_books` (service) | delegate | delegate | fine itself once the repo works |\n| `BookRepository.search_…` | ❌ `int.lower` | ❌ `'Book' object has no attribute 'author'` | crashes on every query once any book misses on title |\n| `return_book` | ❌ loud datetime crash | ❌ **silent corruption** | `return` succeeds, but the loan is never updated: DB keeps `return_date: null, status: \"active\"`; a second `return` succeeds again and inflates copies 1→2 |\n| `borrow_book` | ❌ `.copy` crash | ✅ **works** | `.copy` gone (B), datetimes persist ISO (A); second borrow → clean `No available copies` |\n\nSo A+B did exactly what they targeted: the `.copy` habit is gone, and the datetime wall is gone — `borrow` went crash → working. But this run's `return_body` has a new logic hole (it sets the loan fields in memory and never calls `update_loan`), and the repo search still can't invent its way to an author name.\n\n**Correction on author search (C2):** I'd said service-side join was the only workable spot. Reading the mechanical tree again, `AuthorRepository.find_books_by_author` already reads the **`Book` table cross-entity**, so cross-table reads are the established pattern here — and the prompt's own line 7 puts \"search by title/author/isbn\" in `BookRepository`. A repo-side join is both workable and more faithful.\n\n## Proposed hand fixes (demonstrated working)\n\nI patched a copy of the run-4 tree with these three bodies and re-smoked: title, ISBN, *and author-name* queries all return the right books; `borrow` returns the created loan; `return` persists the loan, guards a second return (`error: Book already returned`), and adds exactly one copy back.\n\n**`repositories.py` → `BookRepository.search_books_by_title_author_isbn`:**\n```python\n    def search_books_by_title_author_isbn(self, query: str = None) -> List[models.Book]:\n        \"\"\"Searches books by title, ISBN or author name.\"\"\"\n        needle = (query or \"\").lower()\n        authors = {author.get(\"id\"): (author.get(\"name\") or \"\").lower()\n                   for author in self._store.all(\"Author\")}\n        found = []\n        for record in self._store.all(self.ENTITY):\n            text = f\"{record.get('title') or ''} {record.get('isbn') or ''}\"\n            if needle in text.lower() or needle in authors.get(record.get(\"author_id\"), \"\"):\n                found.append(models.Book(**record))\n        return found\n```\n\n**`services.py` → `return_book`:**\n```python\n    def return_book(self, loan_id: int = None) -> models.Loan:\n        \"\"\"Marks the loan returned and puts the copy back on the shelf.\"\"\"\n        loan = self._repos['LoanRepository'].read_one_loan(loan_id)\n        if loan.return_date is not None:\n            raise errors.InvalidReturnDateError(\"Book already returned\")\n        book = self._repos['BookRepository'].read_one_book(loan.book_id)\n        loan.return_date = datetime.datetime.now()\n        loan.status = \"returned\"\n        updated_loan = self._repos['LoanRepository'].update_loan(loan_id, loan)\n        book.available_copies = (book.available_copies or 0) + 1\n        self._repos['BookRepository'].update_book(book.id, book)\n        return updated_loan\n```\n\n**`services.py` → `borrow_book`** (only two changes: return the loan `create_loan` hands back, and the 14-day line gets an honest comment):\n```python\n    def borrow_book(self, member_id: int = None, book_id: int = None) -> models.Loan:\n        \"\"\"Checks a copy is on the shelf, creates the loan, takes the copy.\"\"\"\n        member = self._repos['MemberRepository'].read_one_member(member_id)\n        book = self._repos['BookRepository'].read_one_book(book_id)\n        if (book.available_copies or 0) <= 0:\n            raise errors.InsufficientCopeiesError(\"No available copies of this book\")\n        loan = models.Loan(\n            book_id=book.id,\n            member_id=member.id,\n            loan_date=datetime.datetime.now(),\n            due_date=datetime.datetime.now() + datetime.timedelta(days=14),  # loan period: not stated by the prompt\n            status=\"active\",\n        )\n        created = self._repos['LoanRepository'].create_loan(loan)\n        book.available_copies = (book.available_copies or 0) - 1\n        self._repos['BookRepository'].update_book(book.id, book)\n        return created\n```\n(Typo above in the pasted block — it's `InsufficientCopiesError`, as in the tested copy.)\n\nWith these three in place, all five library methods pass, and the replaced bodies drop their `[llm-written …]` tags. They'll be lost if `out/library` is ever regenerated again — same as any replaced body."
