          "text": "The user is pushing on a real question: \"but that's a problem for the generator. do we say we have an LLM quality problem?\"\n\nI need to think carefully about this. The user is challenging my framing that \"hand fixes are not a generator problem.\" They're saying: isn't this a problem for the generator? Is the issue LLM quality?\n\nLet me think about the actual situation from first principles.\n\nThe project's goal: prompts → JSON docs → runnable Python. The generator mechanical emitter covers ~85% of methods mechanically. The rest become documented stubs. Then the LLM pass (`--model`) attempts to fill stubs. The user's acceptance criteria for the LLM pass: module must compile + no invented names, bodies tagged, checked manually.\n\nThe empirical results across two runs (library run 4, expenses run 3):\n- Library run 4: 5 bodies; 2 pass review (renew_membership, search_books delegation), 3 defective (repo search architecture, return silent, borrow wrong return).\n- Expenses run 3: 5 bodies; 2 pass (both CSVs), 3 defective (yearly ignores year, monthly NameError, recurring dict_keys).\n- Library run 3 (before A+B): 2-3 defective of 5 as well.\n\nSo the LLM pass roughly produces ~40% usable bodies. Is that \"an LLM quality problem\"? Yes and no. Let me think about what frames are accurate and useful for the user.\n\nFacts:\n1. The model is Ministral-3-3B-Instruct (a 3B model, Q4 quantized). It's small. The bodies it writes are syntactically valid, mostly use the right APIs (the API section helped), but make semantic/Python mistakes: `.copy(update=...)` pydantic habit, `.keys()[i]`, undefined variables, ignoring parameters, inventing cross-entity attributes, missing persistence calls.\n\n2. Is this a \"generator problem\"? The generator's contract:\n   - Mechanical: refuse what it can't derive; document stubs. It does that 100%.\n   - LLM pass: best-effort fill, tagged, verified only for compilation + name guard. It does that.\n   - Review: manual,