1) bigger model, (2) prompt nudges for the specific recurring classes (we did two), (3) review/accept.\n\nAlso worth noting: the user's requirement \"no catalog additions (no mechanical pattern → documented stub or LLM pass)\" means the LLM pass IS the designed solution for unroutable stubs. So when the LLM pass underdelivers, the acceptable outcomes are: stub (documented) or hand-fixed body. The pipeline never promised automatic correctness. The \"zero generator blockers\" goal was about the mechanical emitter + docs, and that's achieved (gate passes, blockers documented).\n\nHmm, let me re-read the objective from the checkpoint: \"Drive the LLM architecture-extraction pipeline (prompts → JSON docs → runnable Python) to zero generator blockers; latest stages: the LLM stub pass (`generator.py --model`) is filling 5/5 stubs in out/library and out/expenses, and the resulting bodies are being manually reviewed at runtime.\"\n\nSo \"generator blockers\" = things that block the generator from producing a program: gate failures, uncovered fields, doc inconsistencies. The LLM pass filling stubs \"solves\" the runtime NotImplementedError blockers — but quality then becomes the question. So the user is now at the point: the last mile is LLM quality.\n\nSo my answer should be:\n1. Yes — honestly: with the mechanical layer at its design limit, the residual failure rate is LLM quality. But be precise about what that does and doesn't mean:\n   - It's not a generator bug: the generator's contracts are met; the LLM pass is best-effort by design.\n   - It IS gating the \"runnable program without review\" goal: 6/10 filled bodies had defects, mostly of a class that signals model reasoning limits (undefined variable, dict-view indexing, ignored parameter, pydantic `.copy`).\n2. Quantify: library run 4: 2/5 clean; expenses run 3: 2/5 clean (well, 2 CSV exports worked; let me double check expenses tally: export repo ✓, service export ✓, yearly ❌, recurring ❌, monthly ❌ = 2/5). Combined