ned book not loan.\nSo 3 crash, 2 silent, 1 contract. That's a quality problem, plainly.\n\nAnd what about \"do we say we have an LLM quality problem?\" — I think the honest answer is: yes, say it exactly like that, with two qualifiers:\n(a) it's the LLM-as-stub-filler's quality, not the mechanical generator's;\n(b) it's bounded and tagged by design: compilation + name guards pass; semantic verification is manual; the corpus of failure modes is small and now documented (6 bodies, ~6 classes).\n\nMaybe also frame: the LLM pass has a measurable acceptance rate; run the same programs on a stronger model to see if the problem is model-scale or prompt-scale. This is a scientific-ish framing the user seems to like.\n\nOne more consideration: should I propose specific error-catching guards the generator could add? E.g.:\n- Guard: reject bodies that index `.keys()` as a list? Too specific.\n- Guard: attribute access on dataclass instances — check `record.<attr>` where record is `models.X(**...)`? Complex.\n- The user banned catalog additions; guards are not catalog additions though (guards = refusing bodies). We extended guards before (models.*, errors.*, self.*). Could extend to catch `.copy(` on models? Hmm: guard for pydantic-isms: reject `.copy(update=`? That's very targeted. Or reject `.model_dump`, `.dict()`? Could be worthwhile but ad hoc.\n- Undefined local variable detection: we could `ast`-parse the body and detect loads of names that aren't defined/params/builtins? Actually we could compile with `compile()` which doesn't catch undefined names (they're runtime). We could use `symtable` or a simple AST walk to catch obviously-undefined *local* variable LOADS (e.g., `category_id` where it's not a parameter/assigned). That's a real guard: reject bodies that reference names not bound anywhere (params, assignments, module imports/globals). That would catch the monthly NameError at fill time. Hmm — risk of false positives: names imported in module scope (datetime, models...), globals defined in file (`_ho...` helpers — e.g., `_holds` helper in file). We know module names via regex of the module text: top-level defs, imports, class names, method names... The guard already computes `_module_names` (self.*) but not module-level globals. AST walk could collect: body params, assigned names, comprehension vars, imports, and module-level names (functions/classes/imports/constants from the file text). Then flag Loads not in that set + builtins. That's a meaningful generator-side improvement — reject-and-retry? There's no retry; reject → stub stays (honest).\n\nIs that worth proposing? It addresses 1 of 6 classes (undefined variable). The `.keys()[i]` and `.author` classes need heavier analysis (type inference — not feasible). So guard improvements have limited ROI. The dominant fix is a better model. I should be honest: guards can catch a subset; but the user configures acceptance already (manual review); adding speculative static checks has diminishing returns. If they want, I can add a \"suspicious patterns\" report (not blocking) — but that's machinery again.\n\nHmm, actually — the user's question \"do we say we have an llm quality problem?\" might be a question about *messaging/reporting* — like, in their notes/README, do we characterize this as LLM quality? I think they want an honest assessment to decide next steps. My answer: yes, with precision about what it is and what it isn't; and propose measuring with a bigger model to confirm model-scale vs prompt-scale.\n\nLet me also recall: constraints say \"Do not run the model on this server; tests that need the model are handed to the user as Mac commands.\" So the bigger-model experiment = hand to user as Mac commands. Good.\n\nPlan for the reply:\n1. Direct answer: Yes — if the goal is \"fresh run produces a correct program without a human\", the bottleneck is LLM quality. State it precisely: the mechanical layer has no unresolved blockers; the stub-filling layer is where the residual defects live. 6/10 filled bodies needed fixes; 3 crashed, 2 silently misbehaved, 1 returned the wrong object.\n2. Why it's fair to call it LLM quality, and why it's not a generator bug (contracts met: compile + name guards + tags + review; failures are semantic/Python-reasoning classes).\n3. What the evidence says about fixability: list the error classes; classify which are addressable by prompts (we already fixed 2 classes: .copy habit, datetime storage) vs model reasoning (undefined var, dict-keys, ignoring a parameter, cross-entity confusion). The latter class doesn't respond to better prompts reliably.\n4. Recommended experiment: run the same two programs with a stronger instruct model available in `../models` (ask them to `ls ../models`), same conditions; I replay/review and we get a second data point: if defects drop sharply → model-scale problem, keep the bigger model for the stub pass; if not → prompt/architecture-level work (or accept review).\n5. State the decision framing: if \"zero hand fixes\" is the bar, options are only (a) bigger model, (b) manual review (current), (c) new mechanical patterns (ruled out). There's no fourth option.\n6. Keep it tight.\n\nShould I use tools? I could compute a small stats summary from my review data — but I have it all. Maybe check `../models` on the server? The model directory is on the Mac, not the server. The server's parent dir might not have models. Don't guess — ask them to `ls ../models`.\n\nHold on — is there anything on the server that tracks the runs' review outcomes? No machinery. Fine.\n\nOne more angle to include: the acceptance criteria for the LLM pass (from their decisions): \"module must compile, plus no invented names; bodies tagged; still counted as stubs; every raw answer dumped.\" Under THOSE criteria, the pass is 10/10 compliant — the quality problem lives one level above the pass's contract, in the review. So \"LLM quality problem\" = yes, but it's exactly what the tagging/review design anticipates; the question is whether it's acceptable or we need a better model. Good framing.\n\nAlso note: the user earlier chose \"Rerun after A+B, then hand-fix stragglers\" — they've been okay with hand-fixing stragglers. Now they're questioning whether that's acceptable (\"that's a problem for the generator\"). So maybe they're rethinking: they don't want hand fixes; they want the generator to produce correct code. I should mirror: the levers are model quality or acceptance; mechanical patterns are ruled out; prompts have diminishing returns. And measure with a bigger model.\n\nAlso, one more honest note: 5 stubs per program is small N; one more run each with a bigger model gives N=20 bodies (two models) — still small but directionally useful. Also variance: same model/settings temp=0 seed fixed → deterministic, so rerun with same model gives same answers; different model = different sample. Actually temp=0 + fixed seed → deterministic; so re-running the same model won't fix anything. Important point: rerunning the same 3B model is pointless (deterministic); only prompt changes or a different model change the output. Wait — is that exactly true? FALLBACK_TEMPERATURE and seed are fixed (temp=0, seed 20261012). So yes, same inputs → same outputs (deterministic decoding). Actually the model could be non-deterministic due to backend, but with temp 0 seed fixed, essentially same. Also prompt changed between runs — that's why run 3→4 differ. So: with the current prompt, rerunning the 3B yields the same run-4 bodies. To improve: change prompt (again) or change model. That's a strong point for the \"model quality\" argument: we've now seen what this model does with the improved prompt; further same-model reruns add nothing.\n\nHmm wait — is that fully true? Run 4 was with the NEW prompt (A+B). Any further rerun with the same prompt/model reproduces run 4 deterministically. So to get better library/expenses bodies without hand fixes, either prompt changes or a stronger model. Given diminishing prompt returns, the model is the lever. \n\nAlso, the expenses review: we haven't done the run-4 expenses (still run 3 bodies, pre-A+B). The user still owes the expenses rerun. With the same model (3B), the expenses rerun with the A+B prompt would produce new bodies (prompt changed), possibly better on the classes B addresses (e.g., agent may fix... expenses defects: yearly ignores year — not addressed by A+B; monthly undefined var — not addressed; recurring dict_keys — not addressed; CSVs were fine). Actually none of the expenses defects are directly targeted by A+B except maybe... nothing really. So the expenses rerun with 3B may not improve much. Worth noting: expected benefit for expenses is low; the main value is consistency (reviewing once with current prompt).\n\nOK so my recommendation: (1) finish the expenses rerun+review for the record (cheap), (2) then decide on model upgrade — ask what other models are in ../models; if nothing bigger, consider obtaining one (e.g., Qwen2.5-Coder-7B/14B Instruct GGUF is an obvious candidate; or Mistral-Nemo-12B; but I shouldn't over-prescribe with my knowledge cutoff—maybe suggest \"a 7B+ code-capable instruct model\"), (3) keep the current 3B as the fast default; the `--model` flag makes swapping zero-code.\n\nAlso, the user asked \"do we say we have an llm quality problem?\" — answer literally: yes, with receipts (error classes), and with the nuance that the pipeline's design already anticipates it (tagging + review), so it's not a hidden failure — it's the measured residual gap between \"best-effort fill\" and \"correct program\". If they need a label for notes: \"stub-filling quality is model-limited at 3B; residual semantic defects require review; upgrade path is a larger instruct model.\"\n\nLet me also double-check the tallies I'll cite:\nLibrary run 4: 5 filled. Clean as-is: renew_membership ✓, search_books (delegation) ✓ → 2. Defective: repo search, return_book, borrow_book → 3.\nLibrary run 3: renew ✓, search_books delegate ✓ (inherits broken dep), repo search ❌, return ❌ (datetime), borrow ❌ (.copy) → same 2/5.\nExpenses run 3: repo export ✓, service export ✓, yearly ❌, monthly ❌, recurring ❌ → 2/5.\nTotal review: 6 clean of 15 filled bodies = 40%. Let me double check \"service export\" count: yes it worked. So across three reviews (library r3, library r4, expenses r3): 15 bodies, 6 pass as-written, 9 defective. Hmm: library r3 2/5 + library r4 2/5 + expenses r3 2/5 = 6/15 = 40% pass rate. That's a compelling number.\n\nBut careful: are those independent? library r3 and r4 share 2 clean (renew, search_books) — same two methods clean in both runs. So distinct methods: library 5 methods → 2 clean, 3 never clean; expenses 5 methods → 2 clean, 3 never clean. So per-method: 4/10 methods ever passed unreviewed. The 40% aggregate is fine to cite but the per-method view is starker: half the problematic methods failed in both attempts (well, library failed twice with 2 prompts; expenses once).\n\nGood. Also mention which error classes we already killed with system fixes: `.copy` habit (run4 borrow clean) — wait, borrow in run 4 was still defective? Run 4 borrow: no `.copy` (B worked), created loan with dates (A made persistable), guard works, second borrow clean — but returns book not loan, and 14-day invention. So borrow's *crash* class was fixed; its residual defect is contract-mismatch (returns book) — which is arguably minor and now hand-fixed. Anyway, A+B removed the datetime-crash class and the `.copy` class. Let me not over-claim: .copy removed from borrow run-4; datetime storage fixed globally. The remaining six defect classes: cross-entity hallucination (`.author`), undefined variable, dict-view indexing, ignored parameter, missing persistence call, wrong return object.\n\nActually — hmm, \"wrong return object\" (borrow returning book, return returning book) might relate to the prompt/API: the stub signature is shown (`-> models.Loan`), so returning the book is model sloppiness. Fine.\n\nNow, format: The user asked a yes/no-ish question with a challenge. Answer directly:\n\n**Yes — call it what it is: a stub-filling (LLM) quality problem.** Then: receipts; then why it's not a generator bug; then what it means for next steps; then the move: check available models, try a bigger one on the same two programs; note determinism (same model+prompt = same bodies, so rerunning the 3B is pointless; the prompt changes we already made are the max cheap lever); and the decision framing (accept review as designed vs upgrade model; mechanical patterns ruled out).\n\nMaybe end with a question: \"What's in ../models? If there's a 7B+ instruct/coder model there, that's the experiment.\" Could use the question tool? Eh — a plain request for `ls ../models` is natural. Actually, could combine: \"run `ls ../models` on the Mac and tell me what's there; if nothing bigger, I'd suggest pulling one (e.g., a 7-14B code-capable instruct GGUF) — the pipeline swaps with `--model`.\"\n\nKeep the answer focused and not too long. The user likes precise, evidence-based framing.\n\nOne more thing: the user said \"but that's a problem for the generator\" — I should validate the part that's true: yes, for the pipeline's overall promise \"prompts → runnable Python\", the stub-filling quality is part of the generator's product. What's NOT fair is blaming the mechanical emitter or asking for more patterns (ban). So the pipeline's \"runnable Python\" claim currently comes with a review asterisk; quantify it.\n\nAlso, I should be careful about the tone: the user might be frustrated. Be direct, honest, constructive. Don't be defensive about the design; acknowledge plainly: \"if we want zero hand fixes, the model is the bottleneck.\"\n\nLet me draft:\n\n---\n\n**Yes — as measured, it's a stub-filling quality problem, and at this model size it's the pipeline's remaining bottleneck.** Here's the honest ledger.\n\nFilled bodies reviewed so far: 15 across 3 runs (library r3, library r4, expenses r3). As-written verdicts: 6 clean, 9 defective (~40%).\n- library r4: 2/5 clean (renew, service delegate); defects: `.author` hallucination, return not persisting, borrow wrong return object.\n- expenses r3: 2/5 clean (both CSV exports); defects: year ignored, undefined `category_id`, `dict_keys[0]`.\n\nDefect classes: crashes (3), silent no-ops (2), contract mismatches (2)... let me recount: library r4: .author crash, return silent, borrow contract + run-3: int.lower crash, return datetime crash, borrow .copy crash; expenses: yearly silent-wrong (ignores year), monthly NameError crash, recurring crash. Yeah ~three families: crash, silent wrong, contract.\n\nWhy it's not a *mechanical* generator problem: gate/emitter/docs contracts all hold; stubs documented; compile + invented-name guards pass; failures are Python-semantics and reasoning errors the emitter never claimed to handle.\n\nCrucially: two of the observed classes were system-addressable and we killed them (datetime storage via A; `.copy` habit via B). The remaining classes (undefined variable, dict-view indexing, ignored parameter, cross-entity imagination) are reasoning failures: prompt nudges have hit diminishing returns, and rerunning the same 3B model is deterministic (temp 0, fixed seed) — it will produce the same bodies.\n\nSo the decision is now explicit:\n1. **Model upgrade** — the only lever that changes reasoning quality. `--model` swaps it; same two programs give a second data point (if defects drop sharply → it was model scale).\n2. **Accept review** — the current design's answer: tagged bodies + manual review + hand fixes for stragglers (what we did).\n3. New mechanical patterns — ruled out (catalog additions).\n\nIf we say \"zero hand fixes\", the honest statement is: not with this 3B model; measure a bigger one.\n\nSuggest: `ls ../models` — if there's a 7B+ instruct/code model, we run the same two programs with it and I review. If not, that's a download decision. Keep the 3B as fast default, or drop the LLM pass to stubs-only if review effort isn't paying off.\n\n---\n\nHmm, should I mention that expenses run with the SAME model probably won't improve much (A+B doesn't target its defect classes)? The expenses rerun is pending; worth one line: \"the pending expenses run matters for the ledger, but with the same model its expected gain is small — the A+B prompt fixes targeted the library classes; the expenses defects are mostly the reasoning classes.\"\n\nWait, actually — careful: the expenses RERUN with the current prompt would give DIFFERENT bodies than run 3 (prompt changed → different input → different output even at temp 0). So there could be change (better or worse). It's worth doing for the record (and it's already queued). Just manage expectations: unknown, but the classes it fails on aren't what A/B targeted.\n\nAlso note: I should double-check whether \"6/15\" is the right denominator. Reviewed filled bodies:\n- Library r3: 5 (renew, search_books, repo search, return, borrow).\n- Library r4: 5 (same methods, new answers) — but only the 3 changed verdicts matter... no, as data points they're 5 bodies each.\n- Expenses r3: 5.\nTotal 15 body-reviews. Clean: library r3 (renew, search_books) = 2; library r4 (renew, search_books) = 2; expenses r3 (2 exports) = 2. Total 6/15.\nDistinct methods: 10 total stubs (5+5); methods clean as-written: 2 library + 2 expenses = 4/10. Both views are worth stating.\n\nHmm, one nuance for library r3: search_books was \"✅ delegates but inherits its dependency's crash\" — as a body it's fine. And repo search in r3 failed differently. OK.\n\nAlso the borrow run-4 — I marked it \"✅ works\" in the verdict table but then hand-fixed it (returns book, 14-day invention). Was it \"clean\"? No — I listed defects: wrong return type + invention. So for the strict count, borrow r4 is a straggler. So my \"2/5\" for r4 (renew, service delegate). Yes as stated.\n\nLet me also reflect: is it fair to say