ct loan, update status, increment copies).\n- `LibraryService.renew_membership: pattern STANDARD_CRUD: no mechanical reading` — same.\n\nSo the stubs exist because the docs describe operations the mechanical emitter has no pattern for. That's by design (no catalog additions). The LLM fills them. The user asks: repair loop?\n\nI think the best answer structure:\n\n**1. Separate two questions:** (a) Does the *design* need a repair loop? (b) Would a repair loop actually fix the defects we saw? Answer: a loop only helps if it has a **verifier**. The pipeline's only verifiers today are: compile, name guard, and the human. Adding a loop means adding a verifier — and for our defects:\n   - Crash defects (3-4 of them): a trivial dynamic verifier (call the method) catches them. Loop-able.\n   - Silent defects (2): need behavior assertions that encode the same knowledge the stub lacked — writing them is as hard as the fix; not loop-able generically.\n   - Contract defects (borrow returns book): checkable if we check return types — maybe.\n   \n   So even a well-built repair loop wouldn't have caught \"return silently didn't persist\" — the most dangerous class. The manual review caught it because the user inspected the DB.\n\n**2. The determinism problem:** with temp=0 + fixed seed, \"retry\" = same answer. A repair loop must feed *specific error feedback* (traceback, violated check) to prompt a change. For semantic slips, tracebacks help (model sees `'Book' object has no attribute 'author'` — maybe realizes). But if the model lacks the knowledge (where to get author name), feedback alone may not suffice. Feedback + richer facts both.\n\n**3. Cost/benefit vs alternatives:** the manual loop costs minutes per straggler; a repair loop adds: execution harness for generated programs (import package, seed store, synthesize arguments per signature, order calls, capture tracebacks, feed back, regenerate, re-verify, cap iterations), plus false-positive handling. That's a substantial subsystem — and by the user's earlier decision they explicitly didn't want review machinery. The pragmatic middle: keep the manual loop; invest in the model (bigger model cuts all classes at once) rather than a verifier that structurally can't see the silent class.\n\nHmm wait, but let me steelman the repair loop more. The user's actual goal: \"drive the pipeline to zero generator blockers\" and now they see hand fixes needed. Repair loop = automated iteration toward correctness. For the specific method classes:\n\n- `search_..._isbn`: verifiable dynamically (create books, search, check results!). Actually this one is *nicely* verifiable: seed known books, call search with a query, check returned set. If the harness could be given such a check... but who writes the check? The doc? No. Human. Hmm.\n- `borrow_book`: seed a book with copies; call; check loan created, copies decremented. Verifiable if check written.\n- `return_book`: seed loan; call; check loan returned_at set, copies incremented. Verifiable if check written.\n- `renew_membership`: check member expiry extended. If check written.\n- `yearly`: seed expenses across years; check output contains only the year. If check written.\n\nSo actually ALL of the five were verifiable with hand-written checks — the checks are easy for a human who knows the domain. Building the harness: medium effort; writing checks: per-method human effort (small, but per program per method). Then the LLM could iterate against failures: call → traceback/assertion message → feed back → regenerate. With a 3B, convergence shaky; with 7B+, plausible.\n\nBut is that the right recommendation for THIS user's constraints? They said no review/persistence machinery (that was about answers review). A test harness is different machinery. They also said generated output is never pushed back; hand fixes live on Mac. Hmm.\n\nI think my recommendation should be: **not yet a repair loop — two cheaper steps first, then decide.** \n1. Finish the measurement: expenses run-4 plus try a bigger model (the pending ls ../models). If a 7B+ cuts defects to near zero, a repair loop is unnecessary; review stays as safety net. If defects persist at model scale, repair loops enter the conversation — and then the honest scope is \"test harness + checks\" which is a bigger project with its own decision.\n2. If we do build automated repair, the minimal viable version that actually pays: dynamic call-and-catch smoke per filled stub (catches crash classes) with one feedback regeneration; explicitly NOT silent-behavior checks (those need per-method assertions). And be clear this is a new subsystem beyond the current \"no machinery\" stance.\n\nAlso address the specific stub lines they pasted: explain again why mechanical refuses each (parameter maps to no field = multi-field/derived query; plain-method needs custom logic; CRUD pattern's reading step unbuildable mechanically). These are the frontier of the emitter — mechanical coverage stops exactly here by design. A \"repair loop\" doesn't change the emitter; it changes what happens after the LLM writes. So they're orthogonal: repair loop is an LLM-side quality process, not a path to mechanical coverage.\n\nAlso worth noting: some of these stubs got *good* bodies (renew_membership passed twice!). So it's not \"these stub classes are un-fillable\"; it's per-instance quality variance across classes — reinforcing that the lever is model capability, not architecture.\n\nOne more consideration: maybe the user means \"repair loop\" as in the classic \"generate → verify → repair\" agentic pattern and is asking whether the architecture needs it. I should present it fairly: what it buys, what it costs, when it pays. And tie to the constraint that the verifier is the scarce resource.\n\nAlso the user pasted stub reasons — maybe they're also implicitly asking: \"could the generator turn these reasons into self-repair prompts?\" I.e., a repair loop internal to generator.py: after LLM fills, run static checks; on failure, re-prompt with the failure text (\"your code references `category_id` which is not defined here are the available names: ...\"), cap N iterations. That's the cheaper, more tractable variant and fits generator.py. Static loop. It catches the crash class where the syntax/name check exists (undefined name is statically detectable). Let me think about false positives for undefined-name detection: body uses builtins (len, str, sum, sorted, isinstance, min, max, any, all, getattr?), imports are handled by `_add_imports` (datetime/timedelta added at module level — the module text we check against includes them? The check needs the post-import module scope). Globals in the file: the generated file has helper functions (`_row_to_...`? `_holds`?) and constants. A conservative checker: flag a name LOAD that is neither a param, a local assignment, a comprehension/target var, a module-level def/class/import, nor a builtin. False positives possible for names injected via `from ... import *`? Not in generated files. Via `__builtins__` dynamic? No. I think a conservative list is decent. But it only catches 1-2 of our classes. The `.keys()[0]` needs a pattern rule (\"subscript of `.keys()`\" — actually `dict_keys` not subscriptable is a *runtime* type error; statically we could flag `.keys()[` but also `.values()[`, `.items()[` — small known-bad pattern list). `.author` needs field knowledge per model type — the module imports `models`; we could AST-resolve `record = models.Book(...)` assignments then check attribute accesses against Book's dataclass fields... but record could come from `self._store.read('Book', id)` — return type unknown statically unless we track. We could build a tiny local type-inference for the common patterns: assignments from `models.X(` and `self._store.read('X'...)`/`read_one_X` helpers? Hmm, this is creeping. And risky (false rejections). The user banned catalog additions, not guards, but guard creep has diminishing returns and rejections push work back to humans anyway (stub). Rejection just keeps the honest stub — acceptable but not a fix.\n\nWait, actually, careful: what would \"repair\" (not rejection) do better? Re-prompt with the specific failure. For `category_id`: feedback \"NameError/undefined name\" might fix. For `.author`: re-prompt \"Book has no field author; author name lives in Author table; see cross-table precedent\" — that's a prompt-content fix, better done globally in the stub prompt (one line about cross-table reads being allowed and how, with precedent) — which we considered. For `.keys()`: feedback \"dict_keys does not support indexing; use list(d.keys())[0]\" — likely fixable. But with a 3B and temp=0... after feedback the distribution shifts because input changed; plausible.\n\nSo the minimal repair loop = static checks + targeted feedback + 1-2 re-asks, all inside generator.py's fill_stubs. That's genuinely modest machinery, same family as the existing name guard. It could raise the pass rate on crash classes from ~60% to maybe ~80-90%? Total guess — can't know without trying. But the user's stated stance earlier: \"answers are checked manually; no review/persistence machinery\" — a self-repair pass is not review/persistence; it's part of the filling step. Hmm, but it IS new machinery. The user is now asking \"do we need it?\" — so they're open. My job: give a clear recommendation with tradeoffs, and propose the experiment/measurement path.\n\nActually, hmm, wait. Let me reconsider scale: 5 stubs per program. Human review caught everything in ~minutes. Repair-loop engineering (checks, feedback templates, iteration caps, test coverage of the loop itself) is likely hours. ROI positive only if many programs / many stubs / repeated regenerations. The user has 3-4 programs (library, expenses, 60, inventory maybe). So economically: no, don't build a repair loop yet; spend the effort on model quality + finishing the two runs; revisit if regeneration becomes frequent. That's the honest cost-benefit answer. Unless their real goal is the research/demo value of the loop (this is a pipeline research project — maybe they want to demonstrate the technique). The conversation is about \"the generator\" as a system they're evaluating. I'll present both readings: engineering ROI says measure first; if the goal is architectural completeness (pipeline should be self-correcting), then the loop's design hinges on the verifier and the honest version is bigger than it looks.\n\nAlso, keep in mind their constraint: \"generator must refuse blocked docs, never invent silently\" and \"no catalog additions\". A repair loop doesn't violate these (it's LLM-side), but if the loop feeds back execution results, the generator starts executing generated code — new surface (speed, sandboxing, args synthesis). All doable locally.\n\nLet me also answer their literal examples to show the taxonomy — why each of those four stubs is mechanically unreachable, and what class of fill those need:\n\n1. `search_books...: parameter 'query' maps to no field` → the doc's query param spans three fields + fuzzy semantics; no mechanical pattern; LLM attempt crashed `.author` (wanted author name). Verifiable by: call with seeded books.\n2. `borrow_book: plain-method: method needs custom logic` → multi-step transaction (select, guard, update two tables, create loan, return loan). LLM: mostly right, wrong return. Verifiable: postconditions.\n3. `return_book: STANDARD_CRUD: no mechanical reading` → read loan + update status + increment copies; LLM missed persistence. Verifiable: postconditions.\n4. `renew_membership: STANDARD_CRUD: no mechanical reading` → read member, extend date, persist; LLM got it right both runs. Verifiable: postconditions.\n\nObserve: 3 of 4 ARE verifiable dynamically with domain checks; 1 (persistence) wasn't caught by \"no exception\" checks. So a *smoke-only* auto-loop (no assertions) would NOT have caught the worst defect (silent return); the user's manual review did. That's the punchline: automated repair without assertions underperforms the human loop; with assertions it needs per-method knowledge; the human provides it cheaper at current scale.\n\nHmm, though — actually let me double-check the silent return defect: would a smoke test catch it? Call return_book(loan_id) → no exception, returns something. A second call return_book(same) should error \"Book already returned\" — a *check*. Without checks, no. Unless the harness calls methods and then verifies *nothing*... right, no. OK.\n\nAnd one more possible catch for return: the LLM body set `return_date` on the loan object but didn't persist — if the smoke harness re-reads the store and compares, it catches. Who writes \"re-read and compare\"? The harness could generically compare *nothing*. So again: checks needed.\n\nAlso note: some automated checks ARE derivable from the doc mechanically — e.g., if the doc says postcondition \"loan.status = returned\", the generator could... no, the docs are architecture descriptions, not formal specs. Not today.\n\nSo my final recommendation structure:\n\n1. **Direct answer:** A repair loop only repairs if it can detect failure. Today's failure detector is you; the pipeline's detectors (compile + name guard) only catch syntax/name misuse. So \"do we need a repair loop\" = \"do we need an automated verifier + feedback cycle\". \n2. **What it would/wouldn't catch** — table of the defect classes from our data: crashes (catchable cheaply), contract slips (catchable with light checks), silent no-ops (need per-method assertions = same knowledge as the writer). Our worst defect (silent return) is in the third bucket → a naive repair loop would have shipped it.\n3. **Determinism:** retry without feedback = same output; the loop must inject failure info (and sometimes facts). With the 3B, convergence is uncertain; with a bigger model it's plausible.\n4. **Cost comparison at current scale:** 5 stubs/program, man review minutes vs. building harness+checks+feedback+iteration caps (new subsystem). Engineering ROI says: finish the measurement first (expenses run-4 + bigger model test). Decide the loop after the data, because if a 7B+ drops defects to ~zero, no loop needed; if not, the loop alone won't fix the silent class anyway.\n5. **If/when we build it, minimal shape:** inside fill_stubs: static checks (undefined names, known-bad patterns like `.keys()[`) → reject or re-ask with the specific complaint once, cap 2 iterations; optionally dynamic: import module, call filled methods with harness-synthesized args... that's the bigger step — flag as separate decision.\n\nHmm, wait — maybe I'm overcomplicating. Let me re-read the user's message once more: \"that means we need a repair loop ? for ex I see stub: BookRepository.search_books_by_title_author_isbn: parameter 'query' maps to no field ...\" \n\nI think they're connecting: \"the generator emits stubs (these reasons) → the LLM fills them → defective → we hand-fix → so do we need a repair loop [to close the gap automatically]?\" They're asking as a design question. They might also be slightly confused about whether the stub reasons themselves are the problem (\"we need a repair loop?\" could mean \"do we need to repair the generator so it handles these instead of stubbing?\").\n\nI should first clarify what a repair loop would be repairing: the LLM's bodies, not the generator's stubs. And that adding mechanics for those stubs = catalog additions (banned). So the options stay: model quality, review, or loop-with-verifier.\n\nI also want to give a crisp recommendation because the user likes decisive, grounded answers. My recommendation: **Not yet.** Finish the pending runs and test a bigger model first — that's the cheapest high-leverage experiment and it directly informs whether a loop is needed. Then, if defects persist at model scale, the first repair-loop increment worth building is the static-feedback variant (undefined names, known-bad idioms, plus a single re-ask with error text), because it's small and stays inside the existing pass. The dynamic/smoke variant is a bigger subsystem and should be its own decision.\n\nActually, hmm, let me reconsider whether to recommend the static variant at all, or be even more skeptical. The guard already does compile + invented-name rejection. What did the four defect classes look like?\n\n- `.author` — attribute on an instance. Static catch needs type inference. The body was `record.author.name` where record came from `read_all_books`? If we track `records = self.read_all_books()` → list[Book], then `record` → Book, then `.author` not in Book fields → flag. Feasible with small inference, but fragile; false positives risk rejecting good bodies. Medium effort, uncertain.\n- `category_id` — undefined local. Feasible, low false positives if done conservatively (assignment/param/builtin/module-level names). Small effort. But wait — would this even fire *before* hand review? Yes, at fill time. Good.\n- `dict_keys[0]` — pattern rule on `.keys()[`/`.values()[`/`.items()[`. Tiny effort, low false positives (these are almost always wrong when subscripted).\n- return not persisting — static: no. Needs dynamic+assertions.\n- borrow wrong return — static: could check `return` statements against declared return type name? The stub prompt includes the signature with types (we added types!). A check: return expression bare name returning a Book local when signature says Loan — needs inference again. Weak.\n\nSo a static loop realistically catches ~1.5-2 of our 6 defects. Meh. Is it worth building? Borderline; maybe after the bigger-model measurement shows what remains. I'll say: modest value, do it only if remaining defect rate justifies. Honestly, the cleanest statement: **the repair loop's ceiling is set by verifier power, and cheap verifiers only see crashes; our worst bugs were silent.** That's the core insight for the user.\n\nOne more alternative I should mention briefly because it's genuinely good for THEIR process: **tighten the manual loop's cost** rather than automate it — e.g., keep dumping raw answers (already done), keep the review table (they do it), and pre-seed the smoke commands. Nah, too small. Skip.\n\nAlso, there's the option: **improve input facts for the observed classes** (we did A+B; candidates: cross-table read precedent for `.author`; \"persist after mutating an object\" fact for return — wait, that's interesting: the silent-return bug was \"mutated the dataclass but didn't call update_loan\". Hmm, actually what did the body do? From checkpoint: \"return_book ❌ silent corruption (loan never updated...)\". The LLM presumably built a new loan/model and returned it without calling `self._repos.loans.update_loan(...)`. A general fact in the stub prompt: \"If you build a modified model instance, you must call the repository's update_<entity> to persist it — constructing an object does not write to the store.\" That's a semantic fact about the architecture (same family as A's \"dates are ISO strings\"). It targets exactly the return/persist class! Similarly, the invent-\"14 days\" class → fact: \"Prefer fields already present; don't invent policy values that the prompt doesn't state (leave what the doc gives)\". Hmm, we already have facts? The checkpoint says B added \"plain dataclasses, no copy(...)\" and \"date fields hold ISO strings\". Adding 1-2 more facts of this kind is cheap and directly targets observed classes: (i) mutation isn't persistence — call update_<entity>; (ii) cross-table reads are allowed (precedent: find_books_by_author reads Book) and relevant field lists: Book has author_id (not author). These are *prompt-input* fixes, not loops. But the user previously capped? I don't recall a cap on facts; there was a \"no catalog additions\" and \"minimal machinery\". Facts in the stub prompt are part of the LLM pass design; the user approved A+B of this kind. So my recommendation can include: before/instead of a loop, one more fact revision targeting the two remaining semantic classes (persistence-call, no-invented-policy) — but honestly, with a 3B, wording effects are hit-and-miss; it's cheap to try though. Hmm — but earlier I told the user \"remaining classes don't yield to wording\". Is that right? Let me not contradict myself too hard: I said prompt nudges hit diminishing returns. A precision fact about persistence is arguably a *knowledge* injection, not a wording nudge — different category, could work (like A's date fact worked). I'll present it as: the input-fix avenue isn't exhausted (persistence fact, cross-table fact), and it's near-zero cost — do it alongside the bigger-model test. A bigger model + better facts > repair loop at this scale.\n\nWait, but also be careful: the user's question is \"that means we need a repair loop?\" — maybe they don't want a menu of five options; they want a straight read. I'll lead with: no, not necessarily — and here's why the loop wouldn't have caught the worst bug, so if you're imagining the loop as the fix, be aware of its blind spot; the cheaper fix is (a) measure a bigger model + (b) one more fact injection targeting the persistence class; the loop becomes worth discussing only if defects persist at model scale, and then only the static+feedback variant first.\n\nAlso address their stub list: those reasons are the emitter correctly refusing — they mark where mechanical derivation ends by design; the shapes there (multi-field search, transactions, custom logic) are exactly the documented-stub boundary. A repair loop doesn't change that; it changes the fill quality above it.\n\nLet me also sanity check the term \"repair loop\" in literature: generate-test-repair / self-repair loops (e.g., Self-Repair, codex repair studies) — mostly rely on test suites or execution feedback. Known result-ish: repair works when feedback is concrete and the bug is shallow; model capability bounds convergence; repair can also introduce regressions. I can paraphrase without citing specific papers (knowledge cutoff issues; no need).\n\nNow, structure the reply:\n\n- Lead: No — not yet; and more importantly, a repair loop as usually imagined (call it, see if it crashes, re-ask) would have missed your worst bug (silent return). Repair needs a verifier; the verifier is the scarce resource.\n- Explain the three verifier tiers with our data:\n  1. Cheap: \"does it run / compile / no undefined names\" — catches `.author` (runtime), `category_id` (static/runtime), `dict_keys` (runtime). These are auto-catchable at fill time by *calling the method with synthesized arguments* or static name checks.\n  2. Medium: return-shape checks — borrow returns Book not Loan; checkable dynamically against the declared return type. Catches 1 more.\n  3. Expensive: silent behavioral checks — return didn't persist; yearly ignores year. Need per-method postconditions written by someone who understands the intent — that someone was you, manually. The loop can't derive them; if you write them, they'd catch it; but writing them per method is the same domain knowledge effort as reviewing, minus iterating the LLM.\n- Determinism caveat: same seed/temp → same body; retry must inject the failure text (and possibly facts) to change output; with 3B convergence is iffy.\n- Cost at scale: 10 stubs total now, review minutes; building harness+checks+feedback+regression caps is hours and a new subsystem. \n- Recommendation: sequence — (1) expenses r4 for the ledger; (2) bigger model A/B on both programs (pending `ls ../models`); (3) meanwhile, one more near-free input fix targeting the observed silent class: a stub-prompt fact \"constructing/modifying a model object does not write; call update_<entity> to persist\" + the cross-table read precedent for author names (maybe list Book's fields have author_id, so use the Author table via store). Then re-decide on any loop from data: if a 7B+ leaves mostly silent slips, a loop wouldn't fix those anyway — that's review territory; if it leaves crash slips, the static/feedback mini-loop is the right first increment (small, inside fill_stubs).\n- Close: their four stub lines are the emitter honestly refusing — by design; loop or not, that boundary stays.\n\nHmm, wait — should I really recommend the extra facts without approval? Earlier decision said prompts/*.txt frozen — that's prompts, not generator stub-prompt facts. The facts live in generator.py's stub_prompt. A+B were approved. I'll propose, not do — server-side generator change needs their go-ahead anyway (and the Mac runs). Actually the last interaction flow: they asked questions; I answer; then they decide. Fine — propose clearly and ask for green light.\n\nAlso, minor: the user wrote \"for ex I see stub: ...\" — maybe they're looking at the generator's stub report output (the \"stub: ...\" lines come when running generator without --model, reporting mechanical refusals). I can acknowledge: that's the mechanical report; those lines are the generator refusing, which is its job. The five that got LLM bodies are exactly the ones that failed review (four of them — borrow, return, search; renew actually passed). So the stub report itself is not evidence of a repair-loop need; it's the design working.\n\nWait, let me double check which one passed: renew_membership ✅ both runs. search_books service delegate ✅. The four they pasted: search repo ❌, borrow ❌(r4 semi), return ❌, renew ✅. Interesting — three of the four are the defective ones. OK.\n\nOne more thing to verify in my head: the phrase in the stub line \"rule plain-method: method needs custom logic\" — for borrow_book. And \"pattern STANDARD_CRUD: no mechanical reading\" for return/renew. I shouldn't assert exact internal meanings beyond what's evidenced; I can say: the emitter has no pattern for multi-step transactions/domain logic, so it refuses transparently — which is the contract.\n\nShould I do any tool work? No. This is a design conversation. Maybe I should