that year — use it.\" The model then must implement filtering; given the API (store select with ge/le on iso strings? or read all and filter), plausible fix.\n6. borrow returns Book — return-shape check; needs type inference on the return expression, or a weaker check: \"the method's declared return type is models.Loan; the body returns `book`\" — resolving `book`'s type needs inference. Weaker still: \"return statement returns a single Name; check where it was bound: if assigned from `models.Book(...)` or a `read_one_book` call → mismatch\"—that's the inference again, but narrow. Actually we do know from the module text: `book = self.read_one(...)`? Hmm.\n\nSo a motley set of syntactic checkers: name existence (weak), idiom rules (keys()[0]), unused declared parameters (easy, catches a real defect), return-name type matching (narrow inference), dataclass field vs attribute-ish access (inference, fragile).\n\nNow the honest engineering assessment: We can build a small family of *conservative* static checks; each catches a known class; feed the complaint back; re-ask once; keep either the fixed body or the original (or stub) — but \"keep better\" needs a criterion; we can at least require the second answer to pass the checks that failed (i.e., accept only if the complaint is resolved AND no new complaint). That's a \"repair loop\" of exactly the static kind I mentioned last turn (\"first increment = static check + one re-ask\"). The user is now proposing precisely that, with the round-trip through the model. My previous message said: \"if tier-1 crashes remain, the first increment is small — static/feedback check inside `fill_stubs`, one re-ask with the complaint.\"\n\nSo is now the time? The user is pushing: \"at the bare minimum...\" They want the mechanism, or at least agreement on it. I should:\n1. Validate the part that's right: yes, static checks + feedback + re-ask is the cheap, real increment; recovery on name/substitution errors is plausible because the prom