          "text": "Now I have full clarity on the current behavior:\n\n- `fill_stubs` iterates stubs; for each: builds prompt, calls model once, cleans body, calls `_body_refusal` (regex checks on self/store/models/errors prefixes), then compiles the candidate module, then splices. On refusal or compile error → stub stays, answer dumped, note recorded. **No re-ask today.**\n- The guard is regex-based, not AST-based, and only checks the four prefixed namespaces. It does NOT check bare names, `self._repos[\"X\"].method`, attribute chains on instances, etc.\n\nSo the user's proposal: AST-check the body, check it refers to existing classes/functions, inform the model, re-ask. Since the API section is in the prompt, recovery should be possible.\n\nLet me now decide the content of my answer. Key points to make:\n\n**1. Detection — what AST actually adds, and what it doesn't.**\n\nCurrent state: the body must compile (module-level `compile`), and the regex guard checks `self.*`, `self._store.*`, `models.*`, `errors.*`. But note: `compile()` does NOT check that names exist (undefined names are runtime errors). AST + scope collection adds:\n- bare names: module defs/classes/imports/constants + params + locals + builtins (conservative; flag loads not bound anywhere in reachable scopes);\n- `self._repos[\"X\"].method(...)` → substring check against repositories.py method names per class (needs repo name→class map; textually available);\n- known-idiom rules are not name-existence per se, but the same checker family: subscripting `.keys()/.values()/.items()`;\n- **unused declared parameters** — easy, pure syntax, and it's exactly the `year` defect class;\n- maybe: dataclass field access when type is locally obvious (`x = models.Book(...)` / service passing a model) — narrow inference, decent for simple bodies, fragile in general.\n\nHonest limits:\n- `category_id` (bound in one branch, used outside) is *not* caught by a simple binding-existence check — it IS bound somewhere; cat