fidence checks (dict_keys indexing, attribute on store-dict) → refuse; heuristic checks (unused param) → could be report-only... no, keep it simple: complaints are complaints; but the dry-run will tell us false-positive rates. Also mention: all of this is stdlib-only (ast is stdlib), no new dependency. Pylint-as-optional note.\n\n**5. Answer the \"bare minimum\" framing:** agree — static feedback + re-ask is exactly the right *first* loop increment; it's the part of \"repair\" that doesn't need a runner or seeded store. But be precise that its ceiling is the checkable classes; the two silent defects stay review's job. And it complements, not replaces, the two input-fact lines (the facts prevent classes; the checker catches slips).\n\nAlso I should present the \"what compile/AST do and don't\" in a compact way, maybe three bullets:\n- compile(): syntax only; undefined names/attributes are runtime.\n- current guard: four prefixed namespaces (regex); it's what refused run-3's invented names.\n- AST + scope/facts: bare names, self._repos method existence, dict/model field misuse (with small local inference), idioms, unused params. Flow cases (category_id) need linter-grade analysis; silent semantics are out of reach.\n\n**6. Recovery mechanics detail** worth stating: the re-ask prompt should include (i) its own previous answer, (ii) the exact complaint(s), (iii) the relevant slice of facts (field list for the flagged type / the module shows the correct method), (iv) \"reply with only the full body\". Accept-only-if-clean + cap 1 (maybe 2), else keep original refusal/body. And dump both answers (dump format gains `answers: [...]`? currently `answer` is one string; keep compat—maybe store the final; or list). Minor, can decide at implementation.\n\nAlso — should I *ask* permission or just propose and start the dry-run? The user is mid-design conversation; the dry-run is read-only, zero-model, cheap. I think doing it now is the best move: it grounds the next decision in da