rness on server? The replay reproduced run 3 — yes, answers embedded in drive_expenses.py presumably). Let me check both harness files for embedded answer text.\n\nLet me read drive_expenses.py (it's the bigger one) — or grep for \"def \" / \"answer\" patterns. Simplest: read the file. 10KB ≈ 250 lines. Fine.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider scope. Writing a full `_body_complaints` draft now = significant code. But a *dry-run prototype* (throwaway, /tmp) with 3-4 candidate rules is much smaller: load module text + answer, AST-walk, collect: (a) subscript-of-keys, (b) unused params, (c) attribute access on names assigned from store reads, (d) bare unknown names (rough scopes). Run over ~10 answers (library r4 5 + expenses r3 5 + maybe library r3 5 — the library r3 answers might not be embedded; run-4 was replayed; run-3 library was also? \"Expenses run-3 replay + smoke harness (reuse for run 4)\" and library replay was for run 4. Library r3 answers may not be on server). So ~10 data points. That's a genuinely useful dry run.\n\nBut — hmm — does answering with \"I ran it\" overcommit? It's read-only experiments in /tmp; nothing touches the repo or the Mac. Previous pattern: I did replicas in /tmp freely. Yes, acceptable and in-style.\n\nCost: I need to reconstruct the answers. Let me look at the harness files. If embedded, extract to /tmp and run prototype checks. Then the answer includes a small results table: method → recorded defect → candidate check firing (complaint text) → recovery likelihood.\n\nLet me read the harnesses now.",
