          "text": "Excellent! The dry run works. Results:\n\n**Library (run 4):**\n1. `search_books_by_title_author_isbn` → fires D: `record.author`: Book has no field `author` (fields listed). ✅ catches the `.author` crash.\n2. `search_books` → clean ✅ (correct — it was fine).\n3. `renew_membership` → clean ✅ (correct).\n4. `return_book` → fires H: returns `updated_book` from `update_book` (Book) but declared `-> models.Loan`. Hmm — this is *a* defect of return_book (wrong return object), but not THE defect (not persisting the loan). It catches a real inconsistency though.\n5. `borrow_book` → fires H: same. ✅ catches the wrong-return-object.\n\n**Expenses (run 3):**\n1. `export_expenses_to_csv` → clean ✅ (correct).\n2. `detect_recurring` → fires A: dict view indexing ✅ catches `dict_keys[0]` crash.\n3. `export_to_csv` → clean ✅.\n4. `get_yearly_summary` → three fires:\n   - unknown name `x`!! The lambda `sorted(top_categories.items(), key=lambda x: x[1], reverse=True)` — the lambda parameter `x` is not handled by my name collector! `x` is bound as a lambda *arg*, which is not a Name Store node — it's in `node.args`. Same for comprehension handling? Comprehension targets ARE Store names. So this is a false positive from the prototype: lambda args aren't collected. In the real implementation I need to collect all binding forms: lambda args, comprehension targets, with/except/global/nonlocal, function args. So `x` is a prototype bug, not a real check. I should fix the prototype to confirm clean, because if the check fired \"unknown name x\" on yearly, that's a false positive that a real checker must not have. Let me fix and rerun to confirm.\n   - `year` unused ✅ catches the \"year ignored\" defect.\n   - G fire for `x` — cascade of the same bug (x never Store'd) — fix with the same collection fix.\n   \n   Note: `year` unused fire is solid.\n5. `get_monthly_report` → fires G: `category_id` read before assigned ✅ catches the NameError class (in this instance). Note: no false \"unknown name\" since category_id is Store'd later.\n\nAlso interesting: the flow check caught `category_id` — but wait, was `category_id` a param of get_monthly_report? Signature: `def get_monthly_report(self, month: str = None)`. So category_id is not a param → correct fire.\n\nSo after fixing the lambda-args collection issue, expected final result: 6 fires on exactly the 6 compromised answers... let me recount which answers are \"compromised\":\n\nLibrary r4 verdicts (reviewed):\n1. search — ❌ crash (`.author`) → fired ✅\n2. search_books — ✅ clean → clean ✅\n3. renew — ✅ clean → clean ✅\n4. return — ❌ (silent persistence + wrong return) → fired (shape only; persistence NOT caught) ⚠️ partial\n5. borrow — ❌ (wrong return) → fired ✅\n\nExpenses r3:\n1. export repo — ✅ → clean ✅\n2. detect — ❌ dict_keys → fired ✅\n3. export service — ✅ → clean ✅\n4. yearly — ❌ year ignored → fired ✅ (year unused)\n5. monthly — ❌ NameError → fired ✅ (use-before-assign)\n\nSo the checker flags 7 of the 10 answers: the 6 defective ones minus... let me recount: defective: search, return, borrow, detect, yearly, monthly = 6; flagged: search ✓, return ✓, borrow ✓, detect ✓, yearly ✓ (after fixing x false positive), monthly ✓ = 6/6 defective flagged, 0/4 clean flagged. Wow — if that holds, it's a clean result: every recorded defect gets at least one precise complaint and no clean body gets noise.\n\nWait, but \"return\" gets flagged for the wrong return shape, not the persistence. Still, from the repair loop's perspective: the body gets a complaint, model re-asks; the re-answer might fix both or just the shape. The complaint doesn't mention persistence — so the fix may remain half-defective. Honest statement needed: H flagged return (good) but its complaint is about the return object; persistence is invisible. Same for borrow — the complaint catches exactly its defect.\n\nAlso note: H's \"update_book returns Book but declared Loan\" is a general contract mismatch check that fired twice. Good ROI (one rule, two hits).\n\nNow fix the lambda-args issue in the prototype to make the measurement clean. In the real implementation, \"bound names\" collection must cover: function args (including lambda), comprehension targets, with-as, except-as, global/nonlocal, match-case patterns (3.10+), walrus. For the prototype, add lambda arg collection + with/except as-names to both `stores`-like sets (bound_anywhere for C; and store_line for G: set store_line for those names to the node lineno).\n\nLet me patch `complaints`:\n\n```python\nbound = set()\nfor node in ast.walk(tree):\n    if isinstance(node, ast.Name) and isinstance(node.ctx, ast.Store):\n        bound.add(node.id)\n    elif isinstance(node, ast.Lambda):\n        for arg in node.args.args + node.args.kwonlyargs + node.args.posonlyargs:\n            bound.add(arg.arg)\n    elif isinstance(node, (ast.With, ast.AsyncWith)):\n        for item in node.items:\n            if isinstance(item.optional_vars, ast.Name):\n                bound.add(item.optional_vars.id)\n    elif isinstance(node, ast.ExceptHandler) and node.name:\n        bound.add(node.name)\n```\nHmm — `ast.arguments` also for nested FunctionDefs? not needed (same scope? nested def names are Store). Comprehension targets are Stores already. `for` targets are Stores. Fine.\n\nFor G's store_line: lambda arg binding should set store_line[arg] = node.lineno. Let me set store_line for those too. Also careful about ExceptHandler.name being a str. And With optional_vars.\n\nActually simpler: in the G loop, also update store_line for lambda args / with-as / except.\n\nLet me patch: define a helper that walks and yields (name, kind, lineno) for all binding occurrences:\n\n```python\ndef bindings(tree):\n    for node in ast.walk(tree):\n        if isinstance(node, ast.Name) and isinstance(node.ctx, ast.Store):\n            yield node.id, node.lineno\n        elif isinstance(node, ast.Lambda):\n            for arg in (*node.args.posonlyargs, *node.args.args, *node.args.kwonlyargs):\n                yield arg.arg, node.lineno\n        elif isinstance(node, (ast.With, ast.AsyncWith)):\n            for item in node.items:\n                if isinstance(item.optional_vars, ast.Name):\n                    yield item.optional_vars.id, node.lineno\n        elif isinstance(node, ast.ExceptHandler) and node.name:\n            yield node.name, node.lineno\n```\n\nThen:\n- bound = {name for name, _ in bindings}\n- store_line: min per name from bindings.\n- loads: separate walk (Name Load). Note: lambda args appear as Name nodes with... no, args are `arg` nodes, not Names. OK.\n- C: known = params | module | builtins | BODY_IMPORTS | bound | {self}.\n- G: skip pre-bound; compare load_line vs store_line from bindings.\n\nRewrite the relevant block. Then rerun. Expected: yearly shows only \"parameter `year` is never used\". \n\nAfter that, compose the answer with a results table. Also address the design points:\n\n1. Correct the user's premise: compile() checks syntax, not names; AST walk + the API facts is what checks names. What we already have: regex guard on the four namespaces (it refused run-3's invented models./errors. names — that's why `models.date_today` never made it). What's new: the checks above + the prompt facts as a contract.\n\n2. Results table (the money shot): 6/6 recorded defects flagged with a precise, actionable complaint; 4/4 clean bodies left alone; zero model calls. Caveats: this is post-hoc on recorded answers; false positives must be watched on new answers (the prototype hit a false positive `x` from lambda args on first run — the real implementation must bind all binding forms and be tested). One defect half-covered: return's silent persistence is not flaggable statically — H only caught its shape; so the loop surfaces it, but the model might \"fix\" only the return statement. And repair power still unproven: complaint → re-ask → does the 3B fix it? That needs the Mac. But note the complaints are highly actionable (fields listed; keys() wrapping; year unused; category_id flow).\n\n3. Design: where it plugs in — `fill_stubs`: extend `_body_refusal` family into `_body_complaints` (list), for each stub: ask → complaints? → build repair prompt: original answer + complaints + relevant facts → one re-ask → accept only if zero complaints; else keep stub + note \"kept (repair failed: ...)\". Cap 1 re-ask (maybe 2). Raw dumps extended to record both attempts. Still tagged llm-written; still counted as stubs. No execution, no dependencies (stdlib ast), deterministic.\n\n4. What each complaint maps to in the API section: field list ↔ D; \"store reads return dicts\" ↔ ...; \"no copy\" ↔ already; \"self._repos\" ↔ method-existence candidate; BODY_IMPORTS ↔ C. Principle: every fact the prompt asserts can be mechanically enforced — the API section becomes a contract, violations get quoted back. That's exactly the user's intuition (\"since I have an api section... should be able to recover\"): the complaint can quote the very fact the prompt states, so recovery is a shallow edit, not new reasoning. That's the strongest recovery argument, and it holds for D/A/B; for G/H the complaint is structural but still concrete.\n\n5. Expected next step: I can implement it in generator.py + tests (73 tests currently; add checker tests using the recorded bodies — e.g., the 10 answers as fixtures, asserting exactly the complaints above; and a fill_stubs test mocking a refusal then a corrected answer). Then Mac runs expenses r4: the notes will show complaints/recoveries empirically.\n\n6. Also honest limits section: flow cases beyond first-order (e.g., possibly-unbound across branches) will need a second pass or a linter (pylint) — note but not now. Runtime/silent semantics (persistence) stays review. And the loop does not change the model's ceiling: if the re-ask keeps failing, stub stays honest.\n\nWait — one more check I should think about: the \"return\" answer H fire. Is `update_bo