at you want is the AST walk *against the API facts* — and that'sexactly checkable, because the API section already names every legal fact: fields per model, `self._repos`, sibling signatures, store methods. Principle: **anything the prompt asserts as a fact, the checker can enforce, and the complaint can quote.**\n\nDry run (`/tmp/esus_llm_check2/dry_check.py`): the ten recorded bodies (library r4 + expenses r3) through candidate checks — model-field misuse, dict-view indexing, unused declared parameter, read-before-assign, declared-return-type vs the sibling's actual return. Result:\n\n| body | recorded verdict | checker |\n|---|---|---|\n| repo search | crash `.author` | fires: \"Book has no field `author` (fields: id, title, isbn, published_year, available_copies, author_id)\" |\n| service search | good | clean |\n| renew | good | clean |\n| return_book | silent (no persist) + wrong return | fires: \"returns `updated_book` from `update_book(...)` (Book) but declared `-> models.Loan`\" |\n| borrow_book | wrong return | same fire |\n| export (repo) | good | clean |\n| detect_recurring | crash `dict_keys[0]` | fires: \"indexes a dict view … wrap with `list(...)`\" |\n| export (service) | good | clean |\n| yearly | ignores year | fires: \"parameter `year` is never used in the body\" |\n| monthly | NameError | fires: \"`category_id` is read (line 2) before it is assigned (line 14)\" |\n\n6/6 defective bodies get a precise complaint; 4/4 good ones untouched; no false positives (I fixed one crude case in the prototype — lambda args weren't counted as bindings; the real checker must enumerate every binding form).\n\nRecovery: for complaints like these, \"should recover\" is well-founded — the model doesn't have to discover anything, it replaces a name/field that the complaint quotes, with the API block in-context. Where recovery is doubtful: `.author` still needs real logic (join) — though the complaint points at `author_id` and the module shows the `find_books_by_author