ast.Lambda):\n            names += [arg.arg for arg in (*node.args.posonlyargs,\n                                          *node.args.args,\n                                          *node.args.kwonlyargs)]\n        elif isinstance(node, (ast.With, ast.AsyncWith)):\n            names += [item.optional_vars.id for item in node.items\n                      if isinstance(item.optional_vars, ast.Name)]\n        elif isinstance(node, ast.ExceptHandler) and node.name:\n            names.append(node.name)\n        for name in names:\n            bound[name] = min(bound.get(name, 10 ** 9), node.lineno)\n```\nNote: also `for` targets are Store ✓. Also assignment expressions (walrus) are NamedExpr → target Name Store? In AST, NamedExpr.target is a Name with Store ctx ✓. Also `global`/`nonlocal` names — not relevant. Also FunctionDef nested defs' params? If the body defines a nested function using its own params — nested FunctionDef node: its args are `ast.arg` in node.args (same as Lambda) — need to include: add `elif isinstance(node, (ast.Lambda, ast.FunctionDef)):` — but careful: nested def body names belong to a different scope — a nested function's *locals* shouldn't leak to outer scope. E.g. body defines `def helper(x): return x + 1` then `helper(2)` — nested param `x` should be known *within* the nested def only; including x in the outer known set could mask a genuine unknown `x` use in the outer body. Edge case; conservative approach: collect nested function params into the known set anyway? That risks masking; but bodies with nested defs are rare, and the emitter style probably avoids; the prototype used only Lambda (needed for the `sorted(..., key=lambda x: ...)` case). Keep Lambda + With + Except (prototype behavior), plus nested FunctionDef args to be safe from false positives? Hmm. A nested def's param used inside nested def — without including, we'd flag it as unknown name (load occurs, not in bound (params are ast.arg, not Store), not in known). That's a false positive → forces repair. Which risk is worse? A false \"unknown name\" pushing a repair ask on a legit body vs masking a real unknown in weird code. False positives hurt (repair churn, possible degradation). Include nested FunctionDef args; and ALSO their Store names are already collected via the shared walk (nested defs' assignments are Name Store nodes → in `bound`!) — hmm, that too can mask outer-scope unknowns (e.g., outer body uses `x` that only the nested def assigns). But the same is true for comprehension/loop scopes... In practice: conservative = fewer false positives; we accept slight over-permissiveness in the unknown-name check (it's a backstop; the other checks + review still run). OK: include nested FunctionDef/Lambda args in known. Let me write:\n\n```python\n        elif isinstance(node, (ast.Lambda, ast.FunctionDef, ast.AsyncFunctionDef)):\n            args = node.args\n            names += [a.arg for a in (*args.posonlyargs, *args.args,\n                                      *args.kwonlyargs)]\n```\nCareful: for FunctionDef, node.args exists ✓.\n\nUnknown names:\n\n```python\n    known = (set(params) | {\"self\"} | _module_bindings(module_text)\n             | set(BODY_IMPORTS) | set(bound) | set(dir(builtins)) | loads?? no)\n```\nHmm: `loads` isn't known; but names that are only loaded and never bound ARE the unknown ones. Wait — module bindings includes `models`/`errors` imports ✓. But class-level names in the same module? e.g. helper functions defined at module level ✓ (FunctionDef). What about names bound in module-level `for` loops or `with`? Rare in generated modules. Fine.\n\nBut careful: `set(dir(__builtins__))` — use `builtins` module: generator doesn't import builtins currently; add `import builtins`. Or use `dir(__builtins__)` — with the module-level `import builtins`, use `dir(builtins)`. Add import.\n\nUnknown complaint wording:\n\n```python\n    unknown = sorted(set(loads) - known)\n    for name in unknown:\n        found.append(f\"uses `{name}`, which neither the module nor the body defines; name only what the prompt lists\")\n```\nHmm, clunky. \"the name `x` is not bound anywhere — use a parameter, a local assignment, a module import or name\". Let me use: f\"uses `{name}`, which nothing in the module or the body binds\". Simple.\n\nUnused params:\n\n```python\n    for param in params:\n        if param not in loads:\n            found.append(f\"declares parameter `{param}`, which the body never uses; the behavior must use it\")\n```\nHmm — the signature is fixed (the emitter wrote it). \"parameter `year` is never used in the body\". Keep close to prototype.\n\nModel-field check:\n\n```python\n    fields = _model_fields_from(files...)  # dict\n```\nWait `_model_fields` returns list of strings — I need a dict; parse helper:\n\n```python\ndef _field_map(text):\n    \"\"\"`{class: {field, ...}}` from the emitted models module.\"\"\"\n    mapping = {}\n    for entry in _model_fields(text):\n        name, _, fields = entry.partition(\"(\")\n        mapping[name.strip()] = {piece.split(\":\")[0].strip()\n                                 for piece in fields.rstrip(\")\").split(\",\")\n                                 if piece.strip()}\n    return mapping\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```python\n    typed = {}\n    for node in ast.walk(tree):\n        if (isinstance(node, ast.Assign) and len(node.targets) == 1\n                and isinstance(node.targets[0], ast.Name)\n                and isinstance(node.value, ast.Call)):\n            func = node.value.func\n            if (isinstance(func, ast.Attribute)\n                    and isinstance(func.value, ast.Name)\n                    and func.value.id == \"models\"\n                    and func.attr in model_fields):\n                typed[node.targets[0].id] = func.attr\n    seen = set()\n    for node in ast.walk(tree):\n        if isinstance(node, ast.Attribute) and isinstance(node.value, ast.Name):\n            cls = typed.get(node.value.id)\n            if cls