ared = _declared_return(info)  # first models.X in the -> annotation\n    if declared:\n        returns_by_method = {}\n        for text_ in (module_text, references_text):\n            for match in re.finditer(r\"def (\\w+)\\([^)]*\\)\\s*->\\s*([^:]+):\", text_ or \"\"):\n                returns_by_method.setdefault(match.group(1), match.group(2))\n        for node in ast.walk(tree):\n            if not isinstance(node, ast.Return) or not isinstance(node.value, ast.Name):\n                continue\n            name = node.value.id\n            for assign in (n for n in ast.walk(tree)\n                           if isinstance(n, ast.Assign)\n                           and len(n.targets) == 1\n                           and isinstance(n.targets[0], ast.Name)\n                           and n.targets[0].id == name\n                           and isinstance(n.value, ast.Call)):\n                func = assign.value.func\n                method = func.attr if isinstance(func, ast.Attribute) else null\n                actual = _first_model(returns_by_method.get(method, \"\"))\n                if actual and actual != declared:\n                    found.append(f\"returns `{name}` from `{method}(...)` (a {actual}) but the method is declared `-> models.{declared}`\")\n                    break\n```\n\n`_declared_return`:\n```python\ndef _declared_return(info):\n    match = re.match(r\"def \\w+\\([^)]*\\)\\s*->\\s*(.+?):?$\", info.get(\"def\") or \"\")\n    if not match: return None\n    return _first_model(match.group(1))\n```\nHmm — `->` regex on def line: `def borrow_book(self, ...) -> models.Loan:` match group 1 = \"models.Loan\". `List[models.Book]` → first models.X → Book ✓. \"Dict[str, Any]\" → None ✓.\n\nWait: in `_body_complaints` I planned `references_text` param; fill_stubs passes files[\"repositories.py\"]. For repos fills, module_text == repositories.py (references same) — dup scanning fine.\n\nDedupe: multiple returns same name → break per return; multiple return statements same issue → duplicates? Different Return nodes with same name → second append duplicates. Add seen set keyed (name, method). Keep simple: track `(name, method, actual)`.\n\nNow — IMPORTANT: the complaints order in output. For yearly: \"unknown x\" (prototype) — after lambda fix, only \"year unused\". ✓.\n\nNow the empty-body case: `body == []` → `_body_complaints([])`? `\"\\n\".join([])` = \"\" → wrapper has only def line → parses; loads empty; params unused → e.g. file_path unused → complaint. But refusal already returns \"the model returned nothing\" → we skip AST when refusal fires (in fill_stubs: `problems = [refusal] if refusal else _body_complaints(...)`). ✓. But if `_body_complaints` were called directly with empty body in tests, returns unused-param complaint — fine, not a path.\n\nWait — hmm, actually one question: should refusal short-circuit AST? A body with invented `self.helper()` might deserv both messages... For repair prompt, the refusal message is precise enough. Keep short-circuit.\n\nNow `stub_prompt` changes + `fill_stubs` rewrite.\n\nLet me also extract `_splice_body`? Current code inline. I'll write a helper:\n\n```python\ndef _spliced_candidate(text, start, end, info, body, model_name):\n    \"\"\"The module text with the stub's raise replaced by the body.\"\"\"\n    lines = text.splitlines()\n    replacement = []\n    docstring = info.get(\"docstring\")\n    if docstring:\n        replacement.append(re.sub(\n            r\"\\[[^\\]]*\\]\\s*(\\\"\\\"\\\")$\",\n            lambda match: f\"[llm-written by {model_name} — review]\"\n            f\"{match.group(1)}\", docstring))\n    replacement.extend(\"        \" + line if line.strip() else \"\"\n                       for line in body)\n    candidate = \"\\n\".join(lines[:start] + replacement + lines[end + 1:])\n    if text.endswith(\"\\n\"):\n        candidate += \"\\n\"\n    return candidate\n```\n\nThen in fill_stubs define attempt():\n\n```python\n            def attempt(raw):\n                \"\"\"(candidate_text, problems, body) for one raw answer.\"\"\"\n                body = _clean_body(raw)\n                refusal = _body_refusal(body, _module_names(text), store_names,\n                                        model_names, error_names)\n                problems = [refusal] if refusal else _body_complaints(\n                    body, info, text, files.get(\"repositories.py\") or \"\",\n                    files)\n                if problems:\n                    return None, problems, body\n                candidate = _spliced_candidate(text, start, end, info, body,\n                                               model_name)\n                try:\n                    compile(candidate, filename, \"exec\")\n                except SyntaxError as error:\n                    culprit = \"\"\n                    culprit_lines = candidate.splitlines()\n                    if error.lineno and error.lineno <= len(culprit_lines):\n                        culprit = culprit_lines[error.lineno - 1][:70]\n                    message = (f\"the body does not compile ({error.msg}\"\n                               + (f\"; line {error.lineno} {culprit!r}\" if culprit else \"\")\n                               + \")\")\n                    return None, [message], body\n                return candidate, [], body\n```\n\nHmm wait — should `_body_complaints` take `files`? It needs models.py fields only. Pass `files` and let it call `_field_map(files.get(\"models.py\") or \"\")`. Or pass `model_fields` prepass to avoid re-parsing per stub — the stub count is small; but `_field_map` runs per attempt per stub; trivial. Pass files.\n\nBut — careful: `attempt` closure uses `text` — but `text` is rebound in the outer loop after acceptance... within the stub's processing before acceptance, text is stable; on acceptance we set `text = candidates...`. The closure would capture the variable `text` — after `text, added = _add_imports(candidate...)`? Wait current code: `text, added = _add_imports(candidate, \"\\n\".join(body))` — so `text` becomes the new module text; the closure isn't used anymore for that stub. ✓. But `attempt(result.text)` calls happen before that. OK. Define `attempt` inside the per-stub scope (after span re-find) — it needs start/end/info; fine.\n\nAlso note: `_add_imports(candidate, \"\\n\".join(body))` — body from the accepted attempt → attempt returns body ✓.\n\nNow the loop body:\n\n```python\n            out(f\"llm: {label} ...\")\n            prompt = stub_prompt(requirements, text, info, references, api)\n            try:\n                result = runner.generate_free(llm, prompt, f\"stub:{label}\",\n                                              temperature=FALLBACK_TEMPERATURE,\n                                              seed=FALLBACK_SEED)\n            except Exception as error:\n                note = f\"the model failed ({error})\"\n                rows.append((label, \"kept\", note))\n                dumped.append({\"label\": label, \"status\": \"kept\",\n                               \"note\": note, \"answer\": None})\n                continue\n            raw = [result.text]\n            candidate, problems, body = attempt(result.text)\n            if problems:\n                out(f\"llm: {label} refused ({problems[0]}) — one repair ask\")\n                repair = stub_prompt(requirements, text, info, references, api,\n                                     previous=result.text, complaints=problems)\n                try:\n                    result = runner.generate_free(\n                        llm, repair, f\"stub:{label}:repair\",\n                        temperature=FALLBACK_TEMPERATURE, seed=FALLBACK_SEED)\n                except Exception as error:\n                    note = f\"the model failed on the repair ask ({error})\"\n                    rows.append((label, \"kept\", note))\n                    dumped.append({\"label\": label, \"status\": \"kept\",\n                                   \"note\": note, \"answer\": raw[0],\n                                   \"complaints\": problems})\n                    continue\n                raw.append(result.text)\n                candidate, second, body = attempt(result.text)\n                if second:\n                    note = \"still refused: \" + \"; \".join(second)\n                    rows.append((label, \"kept\", note))\n                    dumped.append({\"label\": label, \"status\": \"kept\",\n                                   \"note\": note, \"answer\": result.text,\n                                   \"first_answer\": raw[0],\n                                   \"complaints\": problems})\n                    continue\n                note = \"repaired: \" + \"; \".join(problems)\n            else:\n                note = \"\"\n            text, added = _add_imports(candidate, \"\\n\".join(body))\n            if added:\n                note = (\"; \".join(filter(None, [note, \"added import \" + \", \".join(added)])))\n            rows.append((label, \"written\", note))\n            dumped.append({\"label\": label, \"status\": \"written\", \"note\": note,\n                           \"answer\": result.text, **({\"first_answer\": raw[0], \"complaints\": problems} if len(raw) > 1 else {})})\n```\n\nHmm — the `note` composition for repaired + imports: \"repaired: X; added import json\". OK.\n\nWait, one detail: on repair-accepted, `problems` holds FIRST complaints; note = \"repaired: \" + all first problems. Could be long — acceptable. Tests pin one.\n\nAlso dump for repair-then-accept: include first_answer + complaints. Using ** dict expansion — fine but let me write it plainly for clarity.\n\nHmm — also the dump \"answer\" for keep-after-repair = second answer; first_answer = first. ✓. For kept due to model-failure on repair: answer = raw[0] (the only one); complaints = first problems. Hmm — key \"complaints\" without \"first_answer\" — slight schema inconsistency; let me include \"fi