          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The user asks two questions:\n\n1. Shouldn't we raise temperature when retrying?\n2. Is the prompt tight enough to prevent hallucinations (at first try and at retry)?\n\nI need to give an honest, evidence-based answer. Let me think carefully.\n\n## Question 1: Raise temperature on retry?\n\nArguments for:\n- At temp 0, the model is deterministic given the same prompt. But the repair prompt is different (complaints + previous answer). The observed failure: the model echoed the previous body with cosmetic edits. With temp 0, when shown its own answer + complaint, the model's highest-probability continuation is to reproduce the body it sees (copying is a strong attractor for a 3B). Raising temperature *might* escape the copy attractor — sampling a different continuation that actually engages with the complaint.\n\nArguments against / caveats:\n- The user's own earlier insight (recorded in the checkpoint): \"pass is deterministic, so same model+prompt reproduces same bodies; levers are model upgrade or review — not more prompt wording.\" The repair at temp 0 failing is a *model capability* signal: the model, shown its answer and told exactly what's wrong, still reproduces it. The echo is not a sampling artifact necessarily — it's what the model \"believes\" the answer should look like (record.author because it doesn't understand that it can't access author via the Book record; returning updated_book because it doesn't understand the declared return type).\n- Higher temp on a 3B: more diversity but also MORE hallucination risk. We've measured: at temp 0, first-try bodies had 6/10 defective (across the ten recorded). The checker + review are what keep defects out. A higher-temp retry would sample from a lower-probability region — possibly \"creative\" but more likely to have *new* subtle defects (the worst kind: previously the worst defect was silent). The verifier constrains acceptance (the checker catches the listed classes, review catches the rest), but the *review burden* is what increases.\n- What would \"raise temp\" concretely mean? We could parameterize the retry temperature (e.g., 0.3–0.7) and seed differently (seed+1). That's easy to implement: `FALLBACK_RETRY_TEMPERATURE`/seed. But the empirical prior: with temp 0 the echo was verbatim-ish; at temp 0 the model produces its highest-probability answer to \"here's your answer + complaint\" — which is a copy. Raising temp perturbs the distribution; whether the perturbation lands on a *correct* body or a *differently broken* body is an empirical question. Given the 3B's first-try quality (6/10 defective), I'd expect most perturbed samples to be wrong in some way, but non-echo — some might be right.\n\nActually, there's a cleaner way to think about it: the alternative to \"repair at temp 0 = echo\" isn't necessarily \"repair at temp>0 = fixed\". Both are hypotheses. The cheapest test: run ONE retry experiment on the Mac with temp raised on the repair ask, same prompt, see what comes out. We have deterministic infrastructure; we can A/B a handful of retries (e.g., 3 retries at temp 0.3/0.7 with different seeds) — cost: a few minutes of Mac time on the same prompts, no code churn if I write a small script. Hmm, but wait: that means another generator change (temperature for repair) or a standalone experiment script that replays the same prompts/replies. \n\nActually — a standalone experiment is better (no generator churn until we know it helps): take the two library repair cases (search, return), build the exact repair prompts (I can reproduce them on the server with the recorded answers), and ask the Mac's model: same repair prompt at temp 0 (control: expect echo), temp 0.4 seed X, temp 0.8 seed Y, maybe with different seed variants. Review outputs for whether the complaint is addressed. That gives a direct answer: \"does temperature/seed sampling ever produce an engaged repair for this model?\" If yes, wiring a retry temperature is justified; if no, it's model capability.\n\nBut hold on — is there a subtlety about how the model handles \"shown its own answer\"? An alternative experiment axis: *don't show* the refused answer; just append the complaints. The echo may be an artifact of the anchor. That changes prompt wording (the thing the user earlier concluded doesn't help...). Hmm, the earlier conclusion was about *first-try* wording (listed field types etc. — fixes A/B DID change first-try quality? Actually A/B changed the *store* and the *prompt content*; run 4 got 5/5 written but 3 defective. The prompt wording changes did change behavior). Let me not overreach; I'll offer the temperature experiment as the user asks, and note the anchor hypothesis as a second variable we could test in the same script (cheap: two prompt variants × a couple of temps).\n\nWait, but the user asked a direct question: \"shouldn't we raise temp when retrying?\" My answer should be: \"It's a reasonable lever, and here's the honest nuance: at temp 0 we saw a copy attractor; raising temp gives the sampler a chance to escape it, but it also samples worse bodies more often, and every sampled body still has to pass the same gate + human review. The clean way to settle it is a tiny experiment: fix the repair prompt, vary temp/seed, and see if any engaged repair appears. Want it?\" — and I can prepare the script.\n\nAlso important: the seed question. At temp 0, seed is irrelevant (argmax sampling). Wait — is temp 0 pure argmax in llama.cpp? Yes, `temp=0` → greedy. Seed irrelevant. At temp>0, seed matters. So the retry needs: higher temp + a different seed (or at least temp>0 with the same seed is fine — same seed + different temp changes sampling). Actually with seed fixed at 20261012 and temp>0, the RNG is seeded per call, so the first sampled token sequence differs from temp 0 (greedy). Fine.\n\nAnother angle: instead of one retry, \"repair\" could be reframed as \"regenerate with a different seed at temp 0\" — but that does nothing: temp 0 = greedy = same output for same prompt. Right. So a *different* repair requires either prompt change or temp>0. That's exactly why the user asks. Good — their instinct is sound: with a parrot at greedy, sampling is the only way to get a different answer from the same prompt... but the print must be: different ≠ better, and we can measure.\n\n## Question 2: Is the prompt tight enough to prevent hallucinations?\n\nHonest answer: \"prevent\" — no. The evidence base:\n\nFirst try (temp 0, with the current prompt after fixes A+B):\n- Library run 4/5: of the 5 answers, 3 had defects: one was an invention (`record.author` — accessing a field that doesn't exist on Book; Book has author_id; the join requires reading Author — note: NOT a name hallucination: `models.Book(**book)` is right, `record.author` is a *field* invention). return/borrow returned the wrong object (not an invention, a contract miss). Also run-4 borrow invented a 14-day loan period (semantic invention, not statically visible; the prompt says nothing about loan periods — \"no loan period stated\" — so the model *filled a gap*; we accepted it with a comment in the fix).\n- Earlier run 3: `models.date_today`, made-up exception names, etc. — those were *before* the fields/errors facts were added (fix B). After B, no invented `models.`/`errors.` names in run 4? Let me check: the run-4 answers used existing classes (MemberNotFoundError, LoanNotFoundError, BookNotFoundError, InsufficientCopiesError — all real). So the name-invention class was suppressed by the API facts. Good — prompt tightening DID eliminate a class.\n- Expenses run 3 (pre-A+B): `Invalid isoformat string` (store/date handling), dict_keys indexing, use-before-assign, unused year. Those were \"programming errors\", not name inventions.\n\nSo the honest taxonomy of 3B failures:\n1. **Name inventions** (`models.date_today`, fake exception classes): largely handled by B + the refusal regex (post-B: none observed).\n2. **Field inventions** (`record.author`): still happening — the prompt lists Book's fields (author_id present), yet the model wrote `.author`. There's no join guidance; the `author_id → Author.id` foreign key relation is in the models? Is there a hint? `Book(author_id: int)` — the FK semantics (author_id points at Author) may be visible in... the prompt shows models with field types but maybe not the FK relationship. The model must infer that author names live in the Author table. That's a *semantic* leap — tightenable maybe with a fact line (\"cross-table joins read the other entity's repository/store space\", e.g. AuthorRepository.find_books_by_author precedent). We already called this the \"two fact lines for _api_reference\" open proposal (persistence via create_*/update_*; cross-table join precedent)! That's directly relevant to this question. The user asks \"is the prompt tight enough?\" — answer: it can be tightened one notch with exactly these two facts, but the *evidence* says the residual failure classes are not solved by listing more facts:\n3. **Contract misses** (return types, dead guards, discarded create results, use-before-assign, dict view indexing): these are *understanding/programming* failures — a 3B can't reliably do even when told once. No amount of prompt \"tightening\" fixes reading comprehension; it lowers probability per instance. We can pin it: the return-shape defect persisted even when the complaint was explicit in the repair ask. THAT is the strongest evidence for \"prompt is not the binding constraint\".\n\nSo my answer to Q2: The prompt is tight enough that the model no longer invents *API names* (that was B, and the refusal gate is the backstop). It is NOT (and can't be made) tight enough that a 3B won't invent *fields/semantics* or miss *contracts* — first try 3/5 library bodies had defects; the repair ask with an explicit complaint produced the same defect. Levers that remain: (a) two specific fact lines (join precedent, persistence shapes) — cheap, targeted at classes we've seen; (b) the sixth check (discarded create_*); (c) bigger model; (d) review (unchanged).\n\nI should be careful not to sprawl: the user asked two crisp questions. Answer crisply, propose the experiment (temp sweep) with a concrete script offer, and relate the prompt question to evidence.\n\n## Should I build the temp experiment now?\n\nThe user is asking \"shouldn't we raise temp\" — I think they're suggesting a change/experiment. The best move: explain the tradeoff briefly, then propose the concrete cheap test (same repair prompts on the Mac at temp 0.3/0.7, maybe with the refused answer shown vs not) — OR just implement a retry temperature in generator.py and let the expenses run test it? Hmm. If we change the retry to temp>0+seed variation now, then the expenses run r4 becomes the test — but then we conflate \"expenses recovery rate\" with \"retry temp effect\", and worst case we get more diverse garbage that passes the checker and increases review burden. Also the retry temperature is a *generator* change — deserves its own small decision.\n\nBetter: standalone Mac experiment with the two known echoes (or all three classes). Script: use the *server* to generate the exact repair prompts (I can reconstruct: I have the baseline modules + the recorded first answers). Wait — the repair prompt is built from `text` = module at ask time. For search: repositories.py mechanical (unchanged) ✓. For return: services.py with search_books+renew spliced (I can reconstruct via the run's actual dump/replies: search_books & renew answers accepted → splice → then return prompt). I have all the pieces on the server; I can build the exact repair prompts with generator functions and write a Mac-runnable script that:\n  1. loads the model,\n  2. asks the repair prompt at temps [0.0 (control), 0.4, 0.7, 1.0]? and maybe 2 seeds each at >0 temps,\n  3. prints all answers (or writes them to a file),\n  4. optionally runs the checker locally? The Mac has generator.py → script can call `generator._body_complaints` to flag whether each sample addresses the complaint → auto-score! Nice: print per-sample: complaints remaining (or \"clean\").\n  \n  This is a small, self-contained experiment. The user runs it on the Mac, pastes results. Then we know: does sampling ever produce a complaint-clearing answer for these two cases? If yes at some temp → wire retry temp. If never → model capability confirmed, and we move on.\n\nCost: model load once + ~6-8 short generations — a couple of minutes.\n\nBut wait — also consider: maybe the experiment should include `return` and `search` repairs only (the two echoes), plus maybe borrow's defect variant? Keep it to the two where we have the exact repair prompts. Or the expenses three? Those prompts are pre-A+B (run-3 answers) — the *current* expenses prompts differ (post-A+B); building exact repair prompts for expenses would require the run-4 first answers which we don't have yet (run pending). So: library search+return only. Good enough to answer \"can sampling escape the echo?\"\n\nHmm — one more design point: the retry in the real loop uses seed=FALLBACK_SEED (same as first). For the experiment, vary seed per sample too (e.g., seed+1, seed+2) to decorrelate. With temperature>0 and the same seed, successive samples differ anyway (RNG advances within a call? no — per call the RNG is reset by seed... wait: `llm.reset()` and `call[\"seed\"] = seed` per call. If seed is set per call, then two calls with same temp+seed produce IDENTICAL sampling streams! So for diversity we MUST vary the seed per sample. Important detail! So: sample i = (temp, seed + i).\n\nLet me double-check that claim: `_generate_impl`: `llm.reset()`, `call = {...}`; `if seed is not None: call[\"seed\"] = seed`. In llama-cpp-python, `create_completion(seed=...)` sets the RNG seed for that completion → same seed + same temp + same prompt ⇒ same output. Right. So seeds must vary per sample.\n\nAlso — does `generate_free` accept arbitrary seed/temperature? Yes (params). The experiment script can import runner directly.\n\nOK — so my reply plan:\n1. Answer Q1: reasonable instinct; at temp 0 the repair is forced (greedy) and we observed the copy attractor; temp>0 is the only way to sample a different answer from the same prompt. But: diversity ≠ correctness; every sample still must clear the gate + review, and a 3B at higher temp samples worse bodies more often. Cheap decisive test: since we have the exact two repair prompts, run a temp/seed sweep on the Mac and score each sample with the checker; if any temp yields engaged repairs, wire it (retry temperature + varied seed); if none do, it's capability and we stop spending on repair.\n2. Answer Q2: no, \"prevent\" isn't achievable at this scale, but be precise about which classes the prompt has closed and which remain:\n   - Closed: invented API names (fix B + refusal gate; none in run 4).\n   - Open: field/semantic inventions (`record.author`, 14-day loan) and contract misses (return type, discarded create result, use-before-assign). Evidence that tightening can't close them: the repair ask *explicitly* stated the fact (\"Book has no field author\", \"declared -> models.Loan\") and the model reproduced the defect anyway. Prompt text can shift probabilities, not capability.\n   - Levers that remain (for the after-experiment discussion): the two fact lines (join precedent + persistence return shapes), the sixth check (discarded create_*), bigger model, review.\n3. Offer: I can write the sweep script now; they run it on the Mac; we decide with data. Also mention I'd wire it as: retry at temp T with a fresh seed only if the sweep clears at least one of the two cases.\n\nShould I also ask about the pending expenses run? The sweep is orthogonal; they can run expenses too. Order: sweep first (informs whether to change generator before expenses r4?), or expenses first? If the sweep says \"raise temp helps\", we might want expenses r4 to *already* use it... but expenses r4's purpose is data on first-try + repair behavior with the current commit; and we can re-run any time (deterministic). Actually — expenses r4 will also produce fresh repair echoes (likely). If we're going to change retry temp, running expenses r4 before that is still useful (first-try quality with current prompts, and the loop's keep/refuse behavior). Keep both on the menu: sweep + expenses r4. Let the user choose order, or just say: run both, paste both.\n\nHmm — but hold on, should I actually *prepare* the sweep script in this turn? The user asked a question (\"shouldn't we...?\") — likely expecting my judgment, maybe a plan. I think the best response: give the judgment, then build the script (it's quick for me) and hand the command — turning the discussion into the next data point. The user's style so far: they like concrete Mac commands and data. But careful: the script build requires me to reconstruct the exact repair prompts on the server — let me actually do that: write the sweep script now, verify it builds the right prompts (print lengths / check they contain the complaint + previous answer), and give the Mac command.\n\nWait — will the *sweep* run on the Mac need the prompts embedded? Yes: embed the two prompt strings (built on the server, exact bytes). I can generate the script programmatically: build the prompts with the server's generator + the recorded answers, and write a self-contained Mac script embedding them. Better than recomputing on the Mac (avoids version skew — though the Mac has the same commit... still, embedding is exact and simpler; prompts are ~10-20KB each maybe — two prompts, fine).\n\nActually, computing them on the Mac from the repo would also be exact IF the Mac has 9a18c2f (it does) and the baseline files + answer texts — but the baseline on the Mac is only in /tmp/library_repair (the regenerated tree!) — which is exactly the right baseline (post-run). Hmm: /tmp/library_repair/generated/*.py — the regen created mechanical files and then fill_stubs wrote the accepted bodies into them; the *stubs* for search/return are still there (kept). But the module text at the time of each prompt: for search prompt, repositories.py was pure mechanical (stub in place) ✓ = /tmp/library_repair/generated/repositories.py *before* borrow... hmm, borrow was written into services.py later; search's prompt only needs repositories.py (unchanged by any acceptance — search was in repositories, and no other repo stub; borrow/search_books/renew edits touched services.py only). So search prompt = stub_prompt(requirements, repositories_text_mechanical, info, references=(), api) — I can rebuild on the Mac from /tmp/library_repair? repositories.py there is still mechanical (search stub kept) ✓. But requirements text: from /tmp/library_repair/requirements... hmm wait, does /tmp/library_repair contain requirements.txt etc.? It's a copy of out/library ✓.\n\nFor return prompt: services.py with search_books + renew spliced (borrow NOT yet — borrow ask came after return in processing order... wait order: search_books, renew, return, borrow — so return prompt's text had search_books+renew but NOT borrow). The current /tmp/library_repair/generated/services.py has borrow spliced too (borrow accepted later). So I can't directly rebuild the return prompt from the Mac's file without undoing borrow's splice... but I have the borrow answer; I could replace borrow's body back with the stub. Fiddly.\n\nCleanest: build both prompts on the server (exact), embed in the Mac script as data. The server can reconstruct: \n- mechanical repositories.py (never touched by acceptances) + info from find_stub_spans → search prompt via generator.stub_prompt(requirements, repos_text, info, references=(), api=_api_reference(files, \"repositories.py\")).\n- services.py: mechanical base + splice search_books answer + renew answer (need accepted splices with _spliced_candidate + _add_imports? renew/search_books used imports? \"renew\" didn't add imports; search_books neither) → then the return stub info → return prompt with references=_method_signatures(repos_mechanical) + api=_api_reference(files,\"services.py\")... \n\nWait — subtlety: api = _api_reference(files, filename) computed per file — from the *files dict* at the time? `_api_reference(files, filename)` uses models/errors, maybe repositories? It's computed once per file before spans — unaffected by splices (it reads models.py/errors.py and maybe the file itself?). Let me not overthink: rebuilding on the server exactly replicates fill_stubs' local state if I run the same class of code. \n\nBetter approach: run a server-side \"dry\" fill_stubs that *records the prompts* — i.e., mock generate_free to capture prompts AND feed the recorded answers in order (so acceptance state evolves exactly as in the Mac run), then extract prompts[1] (search repair) and prompts[5] (return repair: index? calls: 0 search first, 1 search repair, 2 search_books, 3 renew, 4 return first, 5 return repair, 6 borrow). YES — that's exactly the repair_replay.py setup I already have (it mocks side effects with the recorded answers + reviewed bodies...). Wait — in repair_replay I used REVIEWED bodies as repair replies (so return was accepted → its prompt #5 was the repair prompt ✓ — wait no: with REVIEWED replies, return's repair was ACCEPTED, and borrow's prompt (call 6) followed with return's reviewed body spliced — that differs from the Mac run where return stayed a stub! For extracting the *repair prompt bytes*, what matters is the prompt for call 5, whose text depends on search_books+renew splices (accepted in both scenarios) and NOT on return's own acceptance. And search's repair prompt (call 1) depends only on mechanical repos. And search's repair-reply body does NOT affect services texts. So I can reuse the repair_replay machinery: capture `call_args_list[1].args[1]` (search repair prompt) and `[5]` for return repair prompt. But careful: call indexing in repair_replay was [search_first, search_repair, search_books, renew, return_first, return_repair, borrow] = 8 calls if repair accepted... indexes: 0 search,1 search_repair,2 search_books,3 renew,4 return,5 return_repair,6 borrow — yes [1] and [5] ✓.\n\nThen write the Mac script embedding those two prompt strings (verbatim), which then:\n```python\nllm = runner.load_model(model_path, logits_all=False, quiet=True)\nfor name, prompt in [(\"search\", P_SEARCH), (\"return\", P_RETURN)]:\n    for temp, offset in [(0.0, 0), (0.3, 1), (0.7, 2), (1.0, 3)]:\n        result = runner.generate_free(llm, prompt, f\"{name}@{temp}\", temperature=temp, seed=generator.FALLBACK_SEED + offset)\n        body = generator._clean_body(result.text)\n        problems = <run the checker like the real flow>\n        print(...)\n```\nFor scoring, the script needs module context for refusal+complaints: embed the relevant module texts + model/error/store names? Simpler: reproduce the exact acceptance check used in the run: for search: refusal+complaints with (body, info, repos_text, repos_text, files) where files = {\"models.py\": ..., \"repositories.py\": repos_text} — models.py from the Mac's /tmp/library_repair/generated/models.py (or from out/library/generated/models.py — same). Hmm, embedding module texts is bulky; alternatively the script reads them from /tmp/library_repair/generated/ — those files exist on the Mac! And for return: services.py needed as context — but its current content differs (borrow spliced). Does that matter for scoring return's repair answers? The checker uses module_text for `_module_bindings` (bare names) and the return map scans module_text for method annotations — splicing borrow's body doesn't change imports/classes; borrow's body adds no top-level names. `_module_bindings` only reads top-level statements — unchanged. The returns map gains nothing (borrow body has no def). So using the *current* services.py (with borrow body) for scoring is equivalent for these checks. And `_module_names(text)` for the refusal regex — `_module_names` collects `    (?:def )?(\\w+)` lines — borrow's body lines at 8 spaces could add names like \"member\", \"book\", \"loan\" to module names (harmless for refusal — those are about self.X/store method names; more names = slightly more permissive, negligible). For exactness I could instead embed the mechanical services text... but the Mac has the mechanical text? /tmp/library_repair/generated/services.py is post-run (borrow written). The pure mechanical services is in out/library? No — Mac's out/library is the *patched* tree (not mechanical). Hmm. But as argued, scoring on the post-run file is fine (same imports & top-level); I'll note that.\n\nHonestly, simplest robust approach: the Mac script reads the two module files from /tmp/library_repair/generated/ and models.py, builds `files` dict, takes `info` from find_stub_spans (labels search by method name), and scores. Fine.\n\nWait, actually — hmm, for scoring fidelity, is there any chance the *checker* results differ between scoring contexts? The complaints for search (record.author) need the `fields` map from models.py and nothing else. For return (return shape) need the returns map (update_book annotation from repositories.py mechanical — /tmp/library_repair/generated/repositories.py still mechanical ✓) + declared return from info[\"def\"] ✓. So yes, fine.\n\nAnd output: for each (case, temp, seed): print the complaints (or \"(clean)\") and the answer's first lines? The user pastes output; I want to see whether any sample clears. Print full answer bodies? Could be long ×8 samples × ~15 lines = manageable (~120 lines). Print complaints + a trimmed body (or full). I'd print full answers for the engaged ones only? Can't know until run. Print complaints + first 12 lines? Hmm — the *review* question (does it actually work semantically, not just clear complaints) needs the full text for any clean sample. Let me print per sample: `sample {case} temp={t} seed={s}: {complaints or 'CLEAN'}` then for CLEAN ones print the full body; for non-clean print maybe nothing more (complaints suffice) — but \"engaged but still failing\" is also interesting (echo vs attempt). Print a tiny signature: first non-empty line? Let me print the first 3 non-empty lines for non-clean, full body for clean. Good balance.\n\nHmm — but also, the sweep at temp 0 for search should reproduce the echo (control) — nice sanity.\n\nOne more variable the user asked about implicitly: \"at first try and at retry\" — temp for FIRST try stays 0 (they asked about retry). I'll say first try stays greedy (deterministic, reproducible); the experiment only varies retry sampling. If the sweep shows a better retry temp, I wire `FALLBACK_RETRY_TEMPERATURE` + seed offset.\n\nLet me now also decide: do I write the sweep script in THIS turn? That requires: (a) extract the two prompts via the replay machinery on the server; (b) write the Mac script embedding prompts; (c) test the Mac script on the server? Can't run the model on the server... but I CAN dry-run it with a mock? The script's non-model parts (checker scoring path) could be smoke-tested on the server by replacing generate_free... meh. Simpler: I can verify prompt extraction and scoring logic on the server with the recorded *repair answers* (echoes) — feed the same scoring function and see \"still refused\" outputs as expected. Actually the scoring logic is the same code as the real loop (reuse via a tiny function or call generator._body_refusal/_body_complaints directly). I'll trust it (it's the same call pattern as repair_replay Part A) — but let me at least assemble the script and run it on the server with `--dry` (mock model returning the known echoes) to prove it works end-to-end before handing to the Mac. Hmm, that means the script needs a dry mode — extra complexity. Alternative: I test prompt extraction + scoring inline in a server script; the Mac script is then a thin loop I'm confident in. I'll do a quick server-side dry validation anyway if cheap... Let me think about scope: the user asked a question; a heavy build might be premature. But I think building the sweep is the right \"let's test that\" move. I'll build it and validate.\n\nAlso — the wait: The response should ANSWER the questions first and clearly (they asked why/why-not), then present the test. Let me make sure the reasoning is honest and precise. Actually — I should also mention the *alternative lever* that costs nothing: in the repair prompt, DON'T show the refused answer (remove the anchor). That's a one-line prompt change; the sweep can test BOTH variants (with/without the refused answer) at each temp! That's a 2-variant × temps experiment: 2 (cases) × 4 temps × 2 variants = 16 samples. A bit much but still minutes. Hmm — variant B prompts: same but `previous=None` (complaints only). The echo possibility changes. I think including the variant is genuinely informative: if \"complaints only\" at temp>0 fixes things, the cheaper lever is prompt shape, not sampling. Let me include it: variants \"anchored\" (current) and \"bare\" (complaints, no refused answer). \n\nWait, but hold on: \"bare\" repair prompt — without showing the previous answer, is it still a *repair*? The model gets: same task + \"your previous answer was refused: - complaint\". It doesn't see what it wrote. Same as informed regeneration. Fine.\n\nSo the experiment matrix: 2 cases × 2 variants × temps [0, 0.3, 0.7, 1.0] with seeds [base, base+1, base+2, base+3] respectively = 16 samples. Each ~ up to max tokens of a body (~100-200 tokens) — seconds each after load. OK.\n\nHmm, that might be overkill; maybe temps [0, 0.4, 0.8] × variants... Let me keep 0/0.3/0.7/1.0; 16 samples is fine (they take seconds; the model load dominates).\n\nLet me now actually build:\n\n1. Server: extract prompts. Script `/tmp/esus_llm_check2/build_sweep.py`:\n   - reproduce repair_replay setup; mock captures prompts; replies = [LIB_ANSWERS[0], REVIEWED[\"search\"], LIB_ANSWERS[1], LIB_ANSWERS[2], LIB_ANSWERS[3], REVIEWED[\"return\"], LIB_ANSWERS[4], REVIEWED[\"borrow\"]] — wait, careful: I need the prompts as built in the *Mac run*, where return's repair answer was NOT accepted (stub stayed). Does that change the return-repair prompt? The return repair prompt (call 5) is built BEFORE its answer — its bytes depend on the module text at that time = mechanical services + search_books spliced + renew spliced. The repair reply doesn't alter that prompt. ✓. So reuse repair_replay's setup and capture calls [1] and [5].\n   - Wait — one more check: in the Mac run, search's repair reply was refused; in my replay the search repair reply (REVIEWED[\"search\"]) gets ACCEPTED — does that change the *return* prompt? Search's acceptance touches repositories.py. The return prompt's text = services.py; references = _method_signatures(repositories.py) — computed per file at target time... `references = _method_signatures(files.get(\"repositories.py\"))` — computed ONCE before the spans loop for services.py, using the CURRENT repositories.py (which in the Mac run had... search KEPT as stub → repositories.py mechanical (stub) vs in my replay search ACCEPTED → repositories.py has the reviewed body spliced). `_method_signatures` collects `def` lines at class level — the reviewed search body has no new def; the mechanical stub vs body: both have `def search_books...` signature line (the stub still has its def; the spliced body keeps the def line too). So signatures identical either way ✓. And `api`/`requirements` unchanged. So the return prompt bytes are identical in both scenarios ✓. \n   - The search repair prompt: depends on mechanical repositories.py + search first answer → identical to Mac ✓.\n   \n   So capture [1] and [5]. Then embed into the Mac script.\n\n2. Mac script `/tmp/esus_llm_check2/temp_sweep.py` (written to the *repo*? No — /tmp on the Mac can't be written by me. The user needs the file ON the Mac. Options: (a) commit the script into the repo