from kernel.extraction import runner\n\nSWEEP = \"/tmp/library_sweep\"\nshutil.rmtree(SWEEP, ignore_errors=True)\nshutil.copytree(\"out/library\", SWEEP)\ncode = generator.main([SWEEP])   # mechanical only; no --model\nassert code == 0\nfiles = {name: open(os.path.join(SWEEP, \"generated\", name)).read() for name in (...)}\nrecorded = { ... first answers per label ... }  # hmm — do I need them? The wrapper calls the model for firsts (temp 0) — deterministic → same answers as the run (we saw search/svc answers reproduce byte-identical; but borrow's prompt differs from run 4... doesn't matter: the wrapper's firsts are the SAME prompts as the run's, so they reproduce the run's first answers: search (defect), search_books, renew, return (defect), borrow (the accepted one? borrow's first ask in the run was accepted — in the sweep it'll be called once at temp 0 with the same prompt (modulo... wait, the sweep's fill sequence: search first → repair (sweep sample) → ... → borrow first. Borrow's prompt depends on return's state: in a sweep run, return's repair sample may be ACCEPTED (then borrow prompt gets return's sample spliced) or refused (stub). So borrow first ask varies per run! That's fine — cache by (label, prompt-hash?) — simpler: cache firsts by label on FIRST call and reuse; the first run's borrow prompt = return kept as stub (if sample refused) or... ugh, sequencing effects again. Simplify: don't cache; just let firsts be called per run at temp 0 (deterministic given prompt; costs a few extra seconds per run ×8 runs = fine). Actually caching by label could give a stale borrow answer whose prompt differed — which would then mis-score. Live calls per run are simpler AND honest. Extra cost: 8 runs × 5 firsts = 40 quick generations (each ≤ ~200 tokens) + 8×2 repair samples — on a loaded 3B, quick. Acceptable (~a few minutes).\n\n    Hmm wait — but each fill run regenerates firsts at temp 0; for search/svc the prompts are fixed → same answers → same refusals → the sweep's repair samples get the same complaints. Deterministic ✓. borrow's first may vary run to run (return state) — but borrow is not our measured case; only its row outcome might differ... and its repair (if refused) would consume a sweep sample from the wrapper! Wait — if borrow's first gets refused in some run, the wrapper would apply the sweep choice to borrow's repair too, mixing variables. Hmm — to keep it clean, the wrapper should apply sweep sampling ONLY to the two measured labels (search, return) and return a FIXED answer for other repair asks: e.g., re-ask at temp 0 (control) or reuse first answer (echo). If borrow's repair happens, give it the \"still echo\" (call temp 0 with the repair prompt) — deterministic, mimics current behavior. Fine: wrapper rule: if label in measured repair labels → sweep sample; else real call temp 0 (first asks) or temp 0 repair (other repairs).\n\n    But hmm — which label is \"measured\"? \"stub:BookRepository.search_books_by_title_author_isbn:repair\" and \"stub:LibraryService.return_book:repair\". Hardcode the two label prefixes ✓ (script for this specific experiment).\n\n    Also scoring: fill_stubs' rows. Print each run's rows. Also the wrapper prints the chosen sample's raw answer (maybe truncated? full is better; search answers ~15 lines, return ~22). 8 runs × 2 samples × ~20 lines = 320 lines of output — heavy but pasteable; maybe print only complaints? The *script* can score the sample itself... but only fill does refusal+complaints with full context. Alternative: wrapper computes complaints for logging: it has (prompt,label) but not context... For logging, just print the raw answers of the samples; the user pastes output; I read. Or print first 6 lines of each sample to keep it readable, and the fill rows give the verdicts. Then, if some sample cleared, we rerun THAT combo printing full text? Two-pass annoyance. Let me print full sample text — honesty over brevity; the user pipes to a file anyway? Suggest: `python3.12