          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The user asks: \"can I run it against the current copy of the library prompt ? /tmp/library_repair ?\"\n\nThey want to point the sweep at /tmp/library_repair instead of re-copying out/library. Let me think about what the script does and whether /tmp/library_repair works.\n\nThe script currently:\n1. `shutil.rmtree(SWEEP)`, `shutil.copytree(\"out/library\", SWEEP)` — copies out/library (Mac's patched tree) to /tmp/library_sweep\n2. `generator.main([SWEEP])` — mechanical regen → pristine stub tree\n3. runs cells\n\nThe user asks if they can run it against `/tmp/library_repair`. What is /tmp/library_repair? From the checkpoint: \"Mac live library repair test (`/tmp/library_repair` scratch copy, patched `out/library` untouched): 3 written (search_books, renew, borrow), 2 kept (search, return); repair attempts 0/2 (echoes).\"\n\nSo /tmp/library_repair is the post-run tree: mechanical files with 3 bodies written (search_books, renew, borrow) and stubs for search + return. The user is asking whether the sweep can run against that tree.\n\nKey considerations:\n\n1. If we run the sweep against /tmp/library_repair as-is (post-run state), the tree has only 2 stubs (search, return). The fill would ask only those 2 firsts and then the repairs. Actually that's attractive: fewer wasted first asks (no search_books, renew, borrow regeneration). But there's a critical problem: the returned bodies in that tree are the run's answers, which were written into the module text. The fill_stubs would re-run from that state — but wait, would the first answers reproduce?\n\nLet me think carefully:\n\n- The source tree for the sweep should ideally be the *state at the time of the repair prompt*. For search: repositories.py was mechanical (search stub) — the search repair prompt was built from that exact text. If we run fill_stubs on /tmp/library_repair's current files:\n  - repositories.py: search is still a stub (kept) → text = mechanical ✓ (search_books_by_title_author_isbn stub present)\n  - services.py: has search_books, renew, borrow bodies spliced; return still a stub.\n\n- fill_stubs processes spans in reversed order within each file. For services.py, the current text has ONE stub (return). So it would ask the return first question — will it reproduce the same first answer as the live run? The return first prompt in the live run was built from services text with search_books + renew spliced (borrow not yet). In /tmp/library_repair, services.py has search_books + renew + borrow spliced. Borrow's body is extra text in the prompt → different prompt → possibly different first answer. Hmm. But wait — would the return first answer even matter? The sweep's measured repair asks need the first answer to be refused (which it will be) and the repair prompt to be built with the same complaints. If the first answer differs because the prompt differs (borrow body present), the complaints list may differ → the repair prompt differs from the live one. That muddies the comparison a bit, but the sweep is still testing \"can sampling engage on a refusal\" — the specific body may differ.\n\nActually wait — more important: for search, the repair prompt depends only on repositories.py (mechanical, unchanged) + search's first answer. If search's first answer reproduces (it should, since the prompt is built from repositories.py which is identical to mechanical), then the search repair prompt is byte-identical to the live run. Good.\n\nFor return: the first ask prompt is built from services.py text = mechanical + search_books + renew + borrow bodies. In the live run, the return first prompt had only search_books + renew (borrow was after return in processing order — processing order is reversed spans across files: repositories.py first? Let me recall: STUB_FILES order. From the repair replay: \"processing order (repositories first, then services bottom-up = reversed spans)\". Services spans reversed: borrow, return, renew, search_books? Reversed span order = bottom-up: borrow was processed last... hmm.\n\nWait, from the checkpoint: \"Mac live library repair test: 3 written (search_books, renew, borrow), 2 kept (search, return); repair attempts 0/2 (echoes).\" And the processing order noted: \"repositories first, then services bottom-up = reversed spans\". Reversed spans means from the bottom of the file upward: in services.py, methods order is... search_books, renew, return, borrow? Actually quoted: \"borrow drift explained: run 4 had spliced return's defective body into services.py before the borrow prompt (3B imitates visible bodies); this run return stayed a stub → different prompt → passing-if-wrong answer.\"\n\n\"this run\" = the run where return stayed a stub (the live library repair test?). Hmm wait: \"run 4 had spliced return's defective body into services.py before the borrow prompt\" — run 4 was the earlier library run (4 reviewed hand fixes?). \"this run return stayed a stub\" — the live repair test run (/tmp/library_repair run): 2 kept (search, return); the repair attempts were echoes; borrow was written. So in the live repair-test run, borrow's prompt had return... as a stub? \"this run return stayed a stub → different prompt → passing-if-wrong answer\" — yes, borrow's prompt differed from run 4.\n\nProcessing order: reversed spans within services.py means bottom-up: borrow first? No wait. Let me re-derive: \"services bottom-up = reversed spans\". The spans in a file are in file order (top to bottom). Reversed = bottom to top. So services methods bottom-up: if the file order is search_books, renew_membership, return_book, borrow_book, then reversed = borrow, return, renew, search_books. But the checkpoint says \"Mac live library repair test: 3 written (search_books, renew, borrow), 2 kept (search, return)\". And \"borrow drift explained: run 4 had spliced return's defective body into services.py before the borrow prompt\" — that implies borrow's prompt comes AFTER return's splice, i.e., order: search_books, renew, return, borrow (top to bottom). Hmm, that contradicts \"reversed spans = bottom-up\".\n\nHmm wait, maybe the file order is different. Let me check the actual services.py method order in out/library/generated/services.py. Actually, from the repair_replay description: \"mock runner.load_model/generate_free in processing order (repositories first, then services bottom-up = reversed spans)\". And the calls captured: index 0 search first, 1 search repair, 2 search_books, 3 renew, 4 return first, 5 return repair, 6 borrow. So services order = search_books, renew, return, borrow — top to bottom. That's \"reversed spans\" if the spans list was... hmm, find_stub_spans returns spans in file order; reversed(spans) gives borrow, return, renew, search_books. But observed order is search_books, renew, return, borrow. So maybe the method order in the file is borrow, return, renew, search_books? Unlikely. Or maybe \"reversed\" is wrong in my memory and it's just span order (top to bottom). Whatever — the observed call order from repair_replay (which I built and validated against the live run) was: search(repo), search_books, renew, return, borrow. That's the ground truth for the live run sequence.\n\nHmm wait, actually no: let me recheck from the checkpoint: \"mac live run: 3 written (search_books, renew, borrow), 2 kept (search, return)\". And repair_replay had 8 calls: 0 search, 1 search_repair, 2 search_books, 3 renew, 4 return, 5 return_repair, 6 borrow... that's 7 calls listed; with borrow repair maybe 8. Scan the checkpoint: \"`/tmp/esus_llm_check2/repair_replay.py`: ten-answer check + library repair replay\" and \"repair replay with reviewed bodies → 5/5 written, 3 repaired\". The call sequence listed in the summary: [search first, search repair, search_books, renew, return first, return repair, borrow] — 7 calls, because with reviewed bodies, borrow's first was accepted. OK.\n\nSo in the live run: order = search (repo), search_books, renew, return, borrow. Borrow came last. \"run 4 had spliced return's defective body into services.py before the borrow prompt\" — that was run 4, not the repair test. In the repair test, return stayed a stub.\n\nAnyway — the key question: can the sweep run against /tmp/library_repair?\n\nOptions:\n\n**Option A: run the sweep on /tmp/library_repair directly (no rmtree/copytree, no mechanical regen).**\n- The tree has 2 stubs: search (repositories.py), return (services.py).\n- fill_stubs would ask: search first → refused → search repair (measured cell ✓); return first → refused → return repair (measured cell ✓). Only 2 firsts + 2 repairs per cell instead of 5+firsts. Faster (good).\n- BUT: the first answers would be generated fresh at temp 0 within the sweep. Would they reproduce the live run's first answers?\n  - Search first: prompt built from repositories.py (mechanical) → identical prompt → reproduces the record.author answer → complaint `record.author` → repair prompt identical to live ✓.\n  - Return first: prompt built from services.py = mechanical + search_books + renew + borrow bodies (borrow's body now present!). In the live run, the return prompt was built when only search_books + renew were spliced (return was asked before borrow... wait, observed order: renew, then return, then borrow. So return's prompt had search_books + renew, NOT borrow). Now the tree has borrow's body too → different prompt → the return first answer may differ (maybe the same, maybe not — borrow's body shows `create_loan` usage and returning `loan`... interesting: the borrow body was the defective one that returns the pre-save local; it might anchor return's answer differently).\n  - Also: search's repair accept in the sweep cells — wait, if a cell's search repair is ACCEPTED, it splices into repositories.py; then return's references (method signatures from repositories) unchanged; return prompt unchanged in that respect. Fine.\n  - Hmm, but also: does fill_stubs process files in STUB_FILES order — repositories.py then services.py? If search's repair is accepted, repositories.py gets the body; doesn't affect return's prompt except references signatures (no new defs) ✓.\n- Another consideration: /tmp/library_repair — is it in a state where generator.main can be skipped? The sweep doesn't need mechanical regen; it just loads the files and calls fill_stubs. So the script would need a small change: skip the copy and the mechanical main, read files from /tmp/library_repair/generated, and get requirements from /tmp/library_repair.\n\n**Option B: copy /tmp/library_repair and re-run mechanical main on the copy** — main would regenerate mechanically from the documents, wiping? Wait, `generator.main([SWEEP])` regenerates generated/*.py from the documents — it overwrites the files, losing the accepted bodies (search_books, renew, borrow) and leaving all 5 stubs again. That's effectively the same as copying out/library. So re-running main on the copy gives the full 5-stub tree (like my original script). That's not \"against the current copy\" in the user's sense.\n\n**Option C: copy /tmp/library_repair as-is into /tmp/library_sweep without main** — run fill_stubs on the 2-stub state.\n\nWhat does the user actually want? They ask \"can I run it against the current copy of the library prompt ? /tmp/library_repair ?\" — I think they mean: use the tree that the live repair test ran against, i.e., skip the out/library copy + regen step, and run the sweep on /tmp/library_repair's state so that (a) it matches the live run more closely, and (b) it's faster/simpler.\n\nImportant: the current script would rmtree/rebuild /tmp/library_sweep — and /tmp/library_repair is a different path; also note the script does NOT touch out/library. But if the user wants to use /tmp/library_repair, we must be careful NOT to let fill_stubs write into it? fill_stubs doesn't write to disk — it mutates the in-memory `files` dict. Wait, does it? fill_stubs(files, ...) returns rows; it mutates the dict? Let me check: the docstring says it splices bodies into module text and tags docstrings — but does it write files back to disk? In the normal generator flow, main writes files after fill. fill_stubs itself: does it write via dump_path? dump_path is optional and writes the JSON dump. In my sweep script I call `generator.fill_stubs(mechanical(), MODEL, requirements, out=lambda _line: None)` with no dump_path → no disk writes from fill_stubs (only the model load reads). Let me verify fill_stubs doesn't write files — from what I read (lines 3432-3511), it works on `files` dict; I saw `dumped` list. There's probably a dump written only if dump_path. Also `_spliced_candidate` returns a string. So no disk writes except dump_path. Good — but to be safe, the sweep should still read from /tmp/library_repair without writing. Actually wait — one more: if I read files from /tmp/library_repair and pass fresh dicts per cell, the disk tree is untouched ✓.\n\nHmm, but hold on: is /tmp/library_repair the right state? Let me think about what state gives the cleanest measurement:\n\nThe ideal: for each measured case, the populate-prompt (first ask) should reproduce the live first answer exactly, so the repair prompt is byte-identical to the live one (including the complaint text the model saw). \n\n- Search: any tree where repositories.py is mechanical (search stub present, nothing else spliced into repositories.py) reproduces it. Both out/library-regen and /tmp/library_repair qualify (search stayed kept, so repositories.py = mechanical; nothing else in repositories.py was ever written... wait, is search the only stub in repositories.py? The regen showed 1 repo stub: search_books_by_title_author_isbn. Yes.)\n- Return: to reproduce the live return first prompt, services.py must be mechanical + search_books + renew only. /tmp/library_repair has + borrow. So the return prompt differs → different first answer possibly → different complaints → different repair prompt. The sweep would still test sampling on a return refusal, but not byte-identical to the live one.\n\nIs that a problem? Not necessarily — but it changes what we're measuring slightly. Hmm. But actually, wait: there's a subtlety. If the sweep uses /tmp/library_repair as-is, the return first ask happens with borrow's body visible — and per the \"borrow drift\" analysis, visible bodies anchor the 3B. The borrow body was the one that returns the pre-save local... Actually the borrow body in /tmp/library_repair is the accepted defective one (\"return loan\" where loan = models.Loan(**record)? Let me not deep-dive; the point stands: prompt differs).\n\nAlternatively: **the user might want to run it against /tmp/library_repair to avoid touching out/library and to reuse the exact run state**. I should answer clearly:\n\n- Yes — the sweep can run on /tmp/library_repair, with one script tweak (point SWEEP at it, skip copytree + main, read requirements from there... wait, requirements: generator.load_requirements(out_dir) reads from the out dir — /tmp/library_repair is a full out-dir copy (it has the requirements documents). ✓\n- But flag the difference: the return first ask will see borrow's body (which the live return ask didn't) → its first answer may differ → the return repair prompt may differ from the live one. Search is byte-identical. So if the goal is \"replay the live repair prompts\", /tmp/library_repair's services.py is not the live state for return; the out/library-regen path (current script) IS the live state for both (mechanical + splice order search_books, renew, return, borrow — wait, does the regen path reproduce the live return prompt? In the regen path, fill_stubs processes all 5 stubs in order search, search_books, renew, return, borrow. The return first prompt = services text at that moment = mechanical + search_books + renew (borrow not yet processed). ✓ Exactly the live state. So the current script IS the faithful replay. Good.)\n\nHmm wait, but one more check on the current script vs the live run: In the live run, was `renew`'s answer accepted before return? Yes (3 written: search_books, renew, borrow). And search_books accepted. So mechanical + search_books + renew ✓. And in the sweep, per cell, if search's repair is accepted, repositories.py has search's body — doesn't affect services prompt (except maybe _api_reference? which reads models/errors, and references = signatures from repositories — search's body adds no defs) ✓.\n\nAlso in the sweep, per cell, the return first answer at temp 0: reproduced from the same prompt as live ✓ regardless of cell. So the measured repair prompts are byte-identical to the live ones in the current script — that's the design (validated in repair_replay: search/svc answers reproduce).\n\nHmm, but actually let me double check one thing: the live run's return first repair prompt — the complaint list. In the current script, the return first answer at temp 0 = live answer → same complaints → same repair prompt (previous=the answer, complaints=same) ✓. And in the live run the repair was asked with the same. So byte-identical ✓.\n\nSo what do I tell the user?\n\n1. Yes, you can point it at /tmp/library_repair — I'll give the tweak (a `--tree` style modification or just edit the top constants). Actually simplest: give them a one-line edit: replace the copy+main block with reading from /tmp/library_repair. But I should provide the modified script? Or explain the edit?\n\nWait — but actually, is /tmp/library_repair even the right thing to want? Let me reconsider what the user means by \"the current copy of the library prompt\". Hmm. \"run it against the current copy of the library prompt ? /tmp/library_repair ?\" — maybe they mean: the sweep currently does `copytree(\"out/library\", SWEEP)` + `generator.main([SWEEP])`. They ask: can we instead run against /tmp/library_repair (which is already a fresh mechanical run's output dir)? \n\nPossibly their motivation: /tmp/library_repair is the *exact* tree from the live test, so results would be directly comparable to the live echoes. Or they just don't want to re-copy/regenerate.\n\nActually — hmm, wait. Let me reconsider: maybe they're asking because they want to avoid the `generator.main` step since that rewrites generated files? Or because out/library is patched and they worry? Let me think about what main does to the SWEEP copy: it regenerates generated/*.py mechanically AND writes other outputs (reports?), possibly writing surface_decisions/method_slots (persisted decisions). On SWEEP (a copy), that's fine. out/library untouched ✓ (copytree first).\n\nSo the honest engineering answer:\n\n- **Yes, it can run against /tmp/library_repair**, and it's actually a bit faster (2 stubs instead of 5 → fewer first asks per cell). Two caveats:\n  1. The tree has borrow's accepted body in services.py, which the live *return* ask did not see → the return first answer (and hence its complaint/repair prompt) may not match the live echo. Search would be byte-identical. If you want the exact live replay, keep the out/library + regen path (current script) — I verified via repair_replay that it reproduces both live repair prompts.\n  2. fill_stubs doesn't write to the tree (only reads; dump_path not set), so running it on /tmp/library_repair is safe — /tmp/library_repair stays as-is. Actually wait, I must double-check that fill_stubs doesn't touch the disk in some other way... The model load reads the gguf. The `files` dict is mutated in memory. dump_path=None → no dump. Let me verify quickly by grepping fill_stubs for \"open(\" / \"dump\" / write operations. Also generator.main writes — we'd skip it.\n\n  3. Also: run it on a *copy*? If some future fill writes... To be extra safe, the tweaked script could copytree /tmp/library_repair → /tmp/library_sweep and read from there. That keeps /tmp/library_repair pristine AND avoids re-running main. Actually that's elegant: replace the two lines (copytree from out/library + main) with copytree from /tmp/library_repair, no main call, requirements from the copy.\n\n  Hmm wait, but requirements: load_requirements(SWEEP) — after copying /tmp/library_repair, its documents are there ✓.\n\n- But which should we recommend? The design goal was a faithful replay of the live repair prompts. Let me weigh:\n  - Current script (out/library → regen): full 5-stub sequence, measured prompts byte-identical to live (search AND return). Costs: 5 firsts + 2 repairs per cell (~7 asks).\n  - /tmp/library_repair variant: 2 stubs, prompts: search ✓ byte-identical; return ✗ (borrow body visible in prompt → first answer at temp 0 might differ; if it differs, complaint set may differ; repair prompt differs from live).\n  \n  Wait, actually — hold on. Will return's first answer actually differ? The prompt differs (extra borrow body), so the model's greedy continuation could differ. Maybe it produces the same defect (returning wrong object) — likely similar, but not guaranteed byte-identical. We could just try: the sweep will tell us (the [ask] lines don't print the answers though — only measured sweeps print). Hmm.\n\n  Actually — WAIT. Bigger issue for the /tmp/library_repair variant: the borrow body being visible in services.py at return-ask time... no wait, that's fine mechanically. But also the file order of asks: fill_stubs processes stubs in file order? In the live run order was search_books, renew, return, borrow (top to bottom in services.py? need to confirm the actual method order in the file — borrow is last? From the sweep script's CASES dict order I listed search, return, borrow — irrelevant).\n\n  Hmm, but here's the thing: with only 2 stubs (search, return), fill_stubs will ask search first, then return. The return prompt includes borrow's spliced body. In the LIVE run, return's prompt had no borrow (return was asked before borrow). So a temp-0 return first could differ → the complaint quoted in the repair differs → the repair prompt differs. That weakens the \"byte-identical replay\"