ritten (search_books, renew, borrow)\" in the live repair test → yes, borrow body written to services.py.\n\nHmm OK wait, actually — maybe I should reconsider once more what the user is really after. \"can I run it against the current copy of the library prompt ? /tmp/library_repair ?\" — Another reading: they want the sweep to run on the same *state* the live test used, because the current script rebuilds from out/library — and maybe they're worried out/library (patched, with reviewed fixes) differs from the tree the live test ran on (/tmp/library_repair was copied from... what? It was \"a scratch copy\" of out/library? From checkpoint: \"Mac live library repair test (`/tmp/library_repair` scratch copy, patched `out/library` untouched)\". So /tmp/library_repair was a copy of out/library (patched?) — then mechanically regenerated and run. Its generated files after the run differ from out/library's generated files (which contain the 4 reviewed hand fixes... hmm, actually out/library has \"already holds the reviewed fix\" for borrow).\n\nImportant subtlety: out/library on the Mac = patched tree with reviewed fixes in generated/ (borrow fix among them?). Wait: \"delivered `out/library` already holds the reviewed fix\" (borrow). And \"mac_patch_library4.py validated but run on Mac still unconfirmed\". Hmm, there are two things: library4 patch script (return/search fixes?) and the borrow fix \"delivered out/library already holds\"... Confusing. The checkpoint says out/library holds the reviewed borrow fix (delivered via commit? \"delivered\" — maybe via a git commit to out/library). And mac_patch_library4.py was a separate patch (for the 4 reviewed fixes from an earlier run) which may or may not have been run.\n\nRegardless: running `generator.main` on a copy of out/library regenerates generated/*.py from documents, so any patched generated content is irrelevant — the sweep starts mechanical. ✓ So the current script's baseline is the mechanical tree, which equals the live test's starting point (the live test: scratch copy of out/library → regen → LLM pass). Wait — did the live test regen mechanically first? Yes — every fill run starts from the mechanical tree: `generator.py out/library --model ...` runs the mechanical generation then fill_stubs. And /tmp/library_repair was a copy of out/library (documents + generated?), then run with --model → mechanical regen + fill. The mechanical regen is deterministic → /tmp/library_repair's starting mechanical state == the sweep's starting mechanical state ✓. The only difference between the two options is *where the sweep starts feeding fill_stubs*: from the full 5-stub mechanical state (current script) vs. from the 2-stub post-run state (/tmp/library_repair).\n\nGiven the goal — produce the same refusal, then sample repairs — starting from the 2-stub state is *fine* except for the return-prompt drift. Actually hmm, wait — is it? Let me think about the return prompt drift more carefully, because there's a chance it matters a lot: the live return refusal was an echo. If the sweep's return first answer (with borrow visible) differs and is *accepted*, then there'd be no return repair ask in the sweep at all (return row: written). Then the return measurement evaporates. Hmm. But borrow's body... does it help return? Borrow's body (the accepted one) — what did it look like? It was the defect (returns pre-save local). Would seeing it change return's answer? Maybe. The live borrow answer returned `loan` (pre-save local) — and the live return answer returned the wrong object too. Plausibly return still fails.\n\nAlternatively — and maybe THIS is the cleanest suggestion — the user could run the sweep against /tmp/library_repair *but restoring return's first-ask context*... no, too fiddly.\n\nLet me step back and think about what to actually answer:\n\n**Direct answer: Yes — with two small caveats, and I'd tweak the script.** Then offer the tweak. Which tree should the tweak use?\n\nOption 1: Keep curre