ly produce correct repairs; removing the anchor makes the model try, but the tries are wrong at a rate ~100% (5 engaged repairs, 0 correct reviewed... well, anchored 0.8 return needs review before judgment; but everything else is defective).** Let me carefully assess each engaged repair for correctness:\n\na) anchored 0.8 return (\"repaired\"):\n```python\nloan = read_one_loan(loan_id) → None check\nloan.return_date = datetime.datetime.now(); loan.status = \"returned\"\nbook = read_one_book(loan.book_id) → None check\nbook.available_copies = book.available_copies + 1\nupdated_book = update_book(loan.book_id, book)\nupdated_loan = update_loan(loan_id, loan)\nreturn updated_loan\n```\nQuestions: (1) `read_one_loan` returns models.Loan? If so, `loan.return_date = ...` sets attribute; `update_loan(loan_id, loan)` — does update_loan take a models.Loan? Need store semantics. (2) `update_book(loan.book_id, book)` — correct book id ✓ (loan.book_id). (3) returns updated_loan ✓ declared type. (4) available_copies increment persists via update_book ✓. (5) Does `update_loan` persist return_date/status? Need check. Also does Loan model allow mutation (it's a dataclass? models are dataclasses/namedtuples?). If models are frozen dataclasses, mutation fails. Need to check models.py — are they plain classes? The reviewed borrow fix returned the stored row; the store's add returns dict copy. Let me check the store template semantics: `read_one_loan` returns \"models.Loan(**row)\"? and `update_loan(loan_id, loan)` writes fields of the loan back? If `update_*` takes a model and merges, then this body is CORRECT — it's exactly the canonical pattern. Actually wait — is it? The canonical pattern for return: update loan (status/return_date) + increment book copies. This body does both via updates and returns the updated loan ✓. It might genuinely be correct! Compare with the previously reviewed-and-delivered out/library return fix — what did IT look like? From the checkpoint: \"return/borr