e experiment's verdict on \"raise temp on retry?\":**\n- Raising temp on the *anchored* repair: 0.4 = still echo (same output as 0.0); 0.8/1.0 = escapes the echo for return but produces either wrong (1.0) or maybe-right (0.8) bodies; search still echoes (0.8/1.0 search: still record.author! anchored search kept in every cell — the search echo persisted even at 1.0). Interesting: anchored search at 0.8/1.0 changed to `read_all_books()` but STILL `record.author.name` → same complaint.\n- Removing the anchor (bare): escapes the echo even at greedy 0.0 — the model genuinely attempts. But the attempts are mostly wrong: 4 of 6 engaged repairs have defects (2 don't exist... let me count engaged-with-written bodies: search: bare0.0 (?) , bare0.4 (crash), anchored 0.8/1.0 return (1 wrong 1 maybe-right), bare0.0 return (missing increment). So 1 maybe-right out of 5, and 1 unknown (read_one_author). \n- The honest conclusion: **sampling doesn't buy correctness — it buys variation; the anchor change buys engagement; but engagement is at the same quality level as first tries, and passing the gate is not being correct.** The dangerous part: more repairs \"succeed\" in gate terms → more subtly wrong bodies get tagged and shipped unless reviewed. The current behavior (echo → keep stub → NotImplementedError) is the honest failure mode; the gate-passing repair is the risky one.\n\nSo my recommendation to the user: \n- Do NOT wire temperature-based repair, and do NOT (yet) change the prompt to bare. The data shows the model can be made to *attempt* but not to be *right*, and the loop's refusal/keep/dump design is exactly right for this model.\n- Exception: review anchored 0.8's return repair — if it's genuinely correct, it's evidence that at 0.8 the model CAN produce a correct engaged repair on this case... but n=1 and another cell with same temp (1.0) produced wrong. And bare 0.0 produced an engaged but incomplete return (missed increment). Hmm — so even the \"correct\" one is likely