          "text": "It's a raw completion call (`llm(prompt=...)`), no chat template. So Qwen3-Instruct is being used as a raw completion model with no chat format. The model is nonetheless emitting verbose \"reasoning\" — which is what a chat-tuned Qwen does when continued in raw text? Hmm. Actually, Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507 is *non-thinking*, but its training includes \"thinking\" traces? Wait, I need to double check. Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507 (released July 2025) — per Qwen's blog, the 2507 update \"removes the thinking mode\" (Instruct-2507 is non-thinking; Thinking-2507 is thinking). Both are updated on the base. The model card says: \"This model has no thinking mode. It does not generate ` thinking` blocks.\" So why the reasoning?\n\nPossibility: the user downloaded `Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507-Q4_K_M.gguf` from a repo that actually packaged... no. Let's check the real behavior: raw completion without the chat template means the model is not in \"assistant\" mode; it's text continuation. Qwen models respond to raw prompts by continuing the pattern — with a prompt that ends mid-code-fence, maybe it treats the \"Now write the body...\" instruction as part of a conversation and starts... The reasoning text \"We are to implement...\" is how the *base* model would continue a textbook-style prompt. The instruction-tuned model might also produce such traces when not chat-templated. This is plausible: the model has been trained on lots of math/code reasoning data, and without the chat control tokens, it falls into \"reasoning continuation\" rather than \"assistant answer\". The 3B (Ministral) was more compliant under the same raw format, perhaps because its instruct tuning generalizes better.\n\nWould applying the chat template fix it? likely: a chat template with the user message and an assistant prefix would put the model in non-thinking answer mode. But then the harness's open-fence prompt is a \"user message\" ending with \"```python\" — the model would answer bare code, lik