eld with default None, redundant but okay; if `id` isn't a field, TypeError. `_body_complaints` checks `models.Loan(...)` fields only via assignments (`typed`) and attribute access — it maps `loan = models.Loan(...)` to `Loan` and then checks `loan.<attr>`; it does not check the constructor kwargs! Hmm, that's a hole: kwargs to a model constructor aren't checked against fields. But the module compiles; runtime would TypeError if `id` isn't a field. Need to check models.py.\n   - `updated_book` unused (harmless; checkpoint mentions a parked \"create/update-result-discard check\" candidate).\n   - `InsufficientCopiesError` exists (errors.py line 29). Good.\n\nSo the chat framing yields a direct, reviewable body in ~220-250 tokens vs the raw path's 3584+ tokens of rambling. That's the decisive result: **use the chat template for the stub pass** (and maybe the whole LLM pass, but that's a bigger question since the document extraction is grammar-constrained and raw by design; the fill/fallback free-text calls are the candidates).\n\nKey numbers from the probe:\n- borrow prompt: 9,486 chars = 2,223 tokens; room 5,905.\n- repair prompt with the dumped first answer: 25,937 chars = 5,851 tokens; room **2,277** tokens. Hmm — not 16! So my \"16-token floor\" claim was wrong for this stub; the repair prompt fits with 2,277 tokens of room. But the first repair answer was 10,208 chars / ~2,300+ tokens and got truncated at `book.available_copies -= ` — consistent with a ~2,277-token budget, hit. So the repair answers for services were truncated at the available room. For the repo stub, the repair prompt would be bigger; let me compute: repo first answer 13,674 chars; the prompt would be ~? The probe only did borrow. On the server I estimated 34,259 chars; the Qwen tokenizer is ~4.44 chars/token (25,937/5,851 = 4.43). So the repo repair prompt 34,259 chars ≈ 7,730 tokens; room = 8192 - 7730 - 64 ≈ 398 tokens. The observed 41-char answer (~10 tokens) is shorter than 398; maybe the model just emitted a def line and stopped (it was at temp 0, an odd degenerate response), or the estimate is off. Either way, the repairs were crippled: services ran out at ~2.3k tokens, repo probably had almost no room.\n\nSo the repair-prompt overflow is real but less extreme than I said for services; the model's repairs were truncated at the room left (~2,277 tokens), and the model needs more (it rambles). The 41-char repo repair remains suspicious but not a clean 16-token clamp.\n\nThe bigger conclusion: the raw path is unusable for Qwen regardless of room; chat framing fixes it. Given that, the recommended path:\n\n**Primary fix: chat framing for the stub pass (and fallback?).** The runner's `generate_free` is used by:\n- `fill_stubs` (stub bodies),\n- `generate_fallback` (whole tiny program),\n- tests.\nThe document pipeline (`generate`) uses grammars; chat framing there is a different question (structured output with grammar + chat could work but changes everything; out of scope).\n\nFor `fill_stubs`, add a chat-mode call. Options:\n(a) In `runner`, add `generate_chat(llm, prompt, label, ...)` that calls `llm.create_chat_completion(messages=[{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":prompt}], ...)` and wraps into StepResult. `StepResult` needs finish_reason; chat's finish_reason values are \"stop\"/\"length\" (same TRUNCATED check).\n(b) Or in `fill_stubs`, choose `runner.generate_chat` when the model supports chat. Detect chat support how? Every GGUF has a template (mostly). `llama_cpp`'s `create_chat_completion` raises if no chat template. Could try chat first and fall back to raw on failure? That's messy. Maybe a `chat=True` parameter with a sensible default: try chat; if it raises, raw. For the A/B, both models (Ministral-3 and Qwen3) have chat templates (Ministral too). Would chat framing change the 3B's behavior? Probably for the better (it was obeying raw already; chat should also obey). But it could change the *bodies* (different encoding), invalidating prior comparisons; for the A/B we want the same framing for both models, so applying chat to both is fair.\n\n(c) Note the chat answer includes prose-free code but no fences; extraction handles it (bare text). The body has an indentation issue: the chat answer's lines are indented 4 spaces (the model wrote the body at the module's 8-space style? The probe shows `|     book = ...` (4 spaces) and nested 8; `_clean_body` dedents by min indent → correct. Good.\n\n(d) The `finish_reason` \"stop\" → not truncated → accepted path works.\n\nAlso, the chat answer for borrow uses `datetime.datetime.now()` and `datetime.timedelta(...)` — proper module attribute use. Better than the raw pick.\n\nNow, the defect in chat user-only: `book.__dict__.copy().update(...)` returns None; `updated_book = ...` receives None. The system+user answer avoids that and is cleaner: `book.available_copies -= 1; update_book(book_id, book)`. Which framing to use? The system message is a change to the ask; the user has frozen prompts (the prompt text), but a system role line is an interface addition. For the A/B fairness, I'd use the plain user message first (no system), because the prompt already contains all instructions; but the system+user answer was cleaner. Hmm. One sample each; can't conclude. For the experiment, maybe run the fill with chat user-only on the Mac and compare guard results for all 5 stubs. The bodies will be reviewed manually anyway.\n\nLet me think about what the user should do next:\n\nOption 1: Wire chat framing into `fill_stubs` (server patch), then run the full Qwen fill on the Mac (5 stubs, ~1-2 min each at chat speeds = fast), get a dump with direct bodies, and run the harness guards + review. Also run Ministral-3B with the same chat framing for the A/B (control). This is the path to the model A/B.\n\nOption 2: Before patching, run the chat probe on one repo stub to confirm chat works there too (the prompt is bigger; the repo search). The borrow worked; the repo should too. Probably unnecessary, but cheap. The user could run the probe with the repo label while I patch. The current probe defaults to borrow; they can run `... BookRepository.search_books_by_title_author_isbn`.\n\nAlso, the probe's finding about the chat answer's defect (`book.__dict__.copy().update(...)`): the *guard* accepted it? Let me check: `updated_book = self._repos[...].update_book(book_id, book.__dict__.copy().update({...}))` — `_body_complaints` walks for `models.Loan(...)` and checks `loan.<attr>`; no type check on the update arg. It compiles. `_body_refusal` checks self names; `book.__dict__` is fine. So the harness would accept a silently broken body. That's the known \"engagement ≠ correctness\" theme; the parked create/update-discard check candidate would catch `updated_book` unused? The candidate checks discarding create/update results; here the result IS assigned but unused (`updated_book` unused). Hmm, the parked check is about discarding the result (not assigning). Either way, human review remains the only truth. Not the main point now.\n\nAlso, the raw full-room answer at 5,905 tokens ended \"Therefore, we must not use it.\\n\\nWe are forced to\" — still stuck. So no budget fixes it. This is the strongest argument for chat: raw can't be saved with more tokens.\n\nNow, the harness patch plan changes slightly:\n1. **Add a chat call for the stub pass** — the primary fix. `runner.generate_chat` wrapping `create_chat_completion`. Keep `generate_free` raw for the fallback? The fallback has the same issue potentially; the fallback prompt ends with \"your entire response must be Python code\"; raw completion may ramble too. For consistency, use chat for both? The fallback prompt isn't the stub shape; changing it is out of scope. The stub pass is the current goal.\n   - How to select chat vs raw? A new parameter or just switch `fill_stubs` to chat unconditionally. If the model's GGUF has a chat template, `create_chat_completion` applies it. If not, it raises (ValueError \"chat template not found\"); fall back to `generate_free`. The fallback keeps raw behavior. I'd implement `generate_chat` with a fallback in `fill_stubs`: try chat; on a chat-unsupported error, raw. But silent fallbacks can hide failures; log it.\n   - For the A/B fairness, both models get chat.\n2. **Continuation-aware `extract_code`** — still needed? Chat answers are bare bodies (no fences, no echo). The parser bug only trips on raw rambling; with chat, it won't. But keep the fix? It's a latent bug; however, with chat framing the answer is direct, and the existing `extract_code` handles it (no fences → bare text). The continuation fix can be deferred, but it's small and protects against models that fence a full method. Hmm — scope: for now, the chat switch + truncation check + dump telemetry might be enough. The extraction bug was triggered by the raw path; if we keep raw as a fallback for models without chat templates, the bug remains. I'd still fix it, but it's secondary. Maybe defer to keep the patch focused? The user will want the extraction fixed since it's a genuine bug (and tests). But the priority is chat framing.\n3. **Truncation check**: with chat, the model stops in ~220 tokens; if a chat answer hits `finish_reason='length'`, treat as a refusal (and maybe retry with the full room — but chat already had room; if it rambles, no point). Keep it: record `truncated` and keep the stub with a clear note.\n4. **Repair prompt with cleaned body + fit check**: with chat, if a body is refused (e.g., borrow's `timedelta` complaint under chat? The chat answer used `datetime.timedelta`, so no), the repair ask embeds the cleaned body (small). This remains useful.\n5. **Dump telemetry**: record `finish_reason`, `tokens`, `truncated`, and the first complaints.\n\nNow, how much to implement now? The user's probe output strongly suggests chat is the way. I should present the findings and propose the patch. Since the user said \"want me to start on that patch now, or wait\" — no wait, they just pasted the probe output without an explicit \"yes\". But the answer to my question is implicitly: the probe worked; now patch. I think I should proceed with the implementation. The user tends to want me to drive.\n\nLet me implement:\n\n### A. runner.generate_chat\n```python\ndef generate_chat(llm, prompt, label, temperature=0.0, max_new_tokens=None, seed=None, system=None):\n    \"\"\"One generation through the model's chat template (no grammar mask).\n\n    Raw completion leaves an instruct model continuing the document instead\n    of answering it: Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507 spent whole budgets of visible\n    reasoning (probe: 5,905/5,905 tokens 'length') where its chat template\n    stopped at 220 tokens of code. Same budget rules as `generate_free`.\n    \"\"\"\n    llm.reset()\n    budget = generation_budget(llm, prompt, max_new_tokens)  # hmm, prompt tokens differ under template\n    messages = ([{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": system}] if system else []) + [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}]\n    call = dict(messages=messages, max_tokens=budget, temperature=temperature)\n    if seed is not None: call[\"seed\"] = seed\n    started = time.time()\n    response = llm.create_chat_completion(**call)\n    choice = (response.get(\"choices\") or [{}])[0]\n    text = (choice.get(\"message\") or {}).get(\"content\") or \"\"\n    finish_reason = choice.get(\"finish_reason\")\n    usage = response.get(\"usage\") or {}\n    tokens = usage.get(\"completion_tokens\")\n    if not isinstance(tokens, int): tokens = token_count(llm, text)\n    return StepResult(label, text, tokens, time.time()-started, finish_reason, budget)\n```\nNote `generation_budget` uses `available_tokens(llm, prompt)`, which tokenizes the raw prompt; the chat template adds ~10-20 tokens (probe: 2231 vs raw 2223 = +8). The reserve of 64 covers it. Good.\n`llm.reset()`? For chat completions, llama.cpp handles the context internally; `reset()` clears the KV cache. The non-