nerate_chat`; if the model has no template, `generate_chat` raises with a clear message and the per-stub try/except keeps the stub, noting the failure. But that would break models without templates. The project's models (Ministral, Qwen) have templates. I'd rather add the explicit probe:\n```python\ndef supports_chat(llm):\n    template = (llm.metadata or {}).get(\"tokenizer.chat_template\")\n    return bool(template) or getattr(llm, \"chat_format\", None) is not None\n```\nThen `fill_stubs` picks `runner.generate_chat` or `runner.generate_free`, and says which in the `out` line. That's clean.\n\nHmm, but `Llama.chat_format` may default to \"chatml\" when no template? In llama-cpp-python, `chat_format` is resolved from metadata `tokenizer.chat_template` or the `--chat-format` arg; defaults to \"chatml\"? The constructor's `chat_format=None` means infer from metadata; if none, `llm.chat_format` may be None. A metadata check is reliable enough.\n\n### C. truncation check in fill_stubs\nAfter each generation:\n- If `result.truncated`: \n  - For the first attempt: try once more with `max_new_tokens=runner.available_tokens(llm, prompt)`. With chat, if the model rambled, the retry may just ramble more; but it's the pipeline's pattern. If the retry is still truncated → keep stub, note \"the answer was cut off at the N-token ceiling\", dump with truncated flags. No repair ask.\n  - Hmm, for chat framing the budget is already the full available room for the *first* attempt? No: `generation_budget` uses `min(MAX_NEW_TOKENS=3584, available)`. The probe used the default for the first raw call (3584) and the full room for the second. For chat, the first call had `budget = min(4096, room-512) = 4096`? The probe passed `budget` explicitly. In the real fill, the default is 3584, and room is ~5900; a chat answer needs ~250. Fine. If the chat answer truncates at 3584, a retry with 5905 might finish. Keep the retry.\n  - The retry uses the same prompt; for chat, same messages.\n- Record on the dump: `\"tokens\": result.tokens, \"budget\": result.budget, \"finish_reason\": result.finish_reason, \"truncated\": result.truncated`. For both attempts when repaired.\n\n### D. repair previous = cleaned body\nIn `attempt`, we have `body`; when `problems` and the first answer will be repaired, pass `previous=\"\\n\".join(body)` (or None if empty) instead of the raw `first_answer`. The dump still stores the raw `first_answer` for the record. Add a test asserting the prompt quotes the body, not the raw transcript (e.g., a raw answer with a fence + prose where the extracted body is the code; the repair prompt should contain the code and not the prose). Wait — with the extraction fixed, the body is code; but the current extraction isn't fixed. For the chat path, the raw answer is the body (bare), so previous=body == raw. The change matters for models that wrap/ramble. Since we may not ship the extraction fix in this patch, the repair quote change still helps: if the raw is prose (extraction failure), the body is prose too... hmm, then quoting the body doesn't fix the overflow because the body is the prose. With chat framing, bodies are clean. So the repair quote change is secondary.\n\nLet me decide the patch scope. The most valuable, minimal change to unblock the A/B:\n1. `runner.generate_chat` + `fill_stubs` uses it when the model has a template; prints which framing.\n2. Truncation handling: treat a truncated answer as a refusal (keep stub, note, dump telemetry); one full-room retry first.\n3. Dump telemetry (`finish_reason`, `tokens`, `budget`, `truncated`).\n4. Repair previous = cleaned body (small; keeps the ask within context once bodies are clean).\n5. The extraction continuation fix: needed if raw framing is used (fallback for no-template models) or if a chat model fences a full method. Given the chat answers are bare and the 3B's are bare, maybe defer. But it's a genuine bug we diagnosed; leaving it feels wrong. It's ~30 lines + tests. I'll include it if time permits. Hmm.\n\nActually, wait. With chat framing, the model's answer may still start with the def line (some models do) — `_clean_body` handles that. May fence the method — `extract_code` with a complete ```python block works. The continuation bug only bites when the model *closes the prompt's open fence* before writing code, which is a raw-completion echo behavior. With chat framing, the prompt is a user message, not an open continuation; the model won't see an open fence to close (the fence is inside the user message, but the assistant starts fresh). So the bug is much less likely. I'd defer the continuation fix but note it. However, the user may want it fixed since we found it. Let me include it — it's low-risk and improves robustness. But each addition needs tests and time. Let me first check with the user? No — I'll implement the core (chat + truncation + dump + repair body). The extraction fix can be a follow-up if any model still trips it.\n\nHmm, but if I don't fix `extract_code`, the *tests* for chat framing won't exercise it. Fine.\n\nWait — there's another important consideration: the *document* extraction pipeline (prompts → JSON docs) uses `generate` with grammars, raw. The Qwen model for the *whole pipeline* might also suffer (but grammar-constrained JSON can't ramble incoherently; the grammar forces JSON; the model might emit reasoning tokens? With a grammar mask, only JSON tokens are allowed, so no reasoning — the mask prevents it. Good.) The fallback `generate_fallback` uses raw free text; the Qwen fallback might ramble too. Should the fallback also use chat? The fallback is for empty-architecture programs; it's a \"degenerate fallback\" verified by running. If Qwen is used, chat framing would help there too. But out of scope; maybe note it. Actually, `generate_fallback` calls `runner.generate_free` with `fallback_prompt`; if Qwen rambles, the fallback fails. For consistency, could switch to chat too. The fallback prompt ends with \"your entire response must be Python code and nothing else\" — chat framing would make the model comply. I'd switch both call sites to `generate_chat` with the same supports-chat probe. But the fallback is not in the current A/B; keep the change minimal? The same helper can serve.\n\nLet me now look at how `fill_stubs` is called and whether `llm` is passed around; the supports-chat probe can live in `runner`:\n```python\ndef chat_framing_available(llm):\n    \"\"\"True when the GGUF carries a chat template `create_chat_completion` can use.\"\"\"\n    try:\n        metadata = llm.metadata or {}\n    except Exception:\n        return False\n    if metadata.get(\"tokenizer.chat_template\"):\n        return True\n    return getattr(llm, \"chat_format\", None) is not None\n```\nHmm, `chat_format` default? In llama-cpp-python `Llama.__init__`, `chat_format` argument if None → `self.chat_format = chat_format` (None). And `create_chat_completion` calls `_grammar_for...`? It uses `llama_chat_format.get_chat_completion_handler(self.chat_format)`; if chat_format is None, it raises ValueError(\"Chat format not specified...\"). Actually newer versions read the template from metadata when `chat_format` is None? Let me recall: llama-cpp-python supports Jinja templates? As of 0.2.7x, `create_chat_completion` with `chat_format=None` tries `llama_chat_format.get_chat_completion_handler(self.chat_format)` which raises \"chat_format not specified\". There's `chat_template` support via `self.metadata[\"tokenizer.chat_template\"]` and the `minja`/`jinja` package? Hmm — important! The probe *worked* on the Mac: `llm.create_chat_completion` returned a proper answer with the Qwen template (token counts +8). So the installed llama-cpp-python does handle the GGUF template. Good. The user's version supports it (likely via `llama_chat_format` auto from metadata). So `chat_framing_available` can just try metadata or rely on `create_chat_completion` succeeding. Since the probe proves it works for the