          "text": "Good patterns. Now let me check the top of `fill_stubs` and the exact structure to patch. I have it from before. Let me also check `_stub_method`, `_stub_params`, and `_spliced_candidate` briefly if needed. Not needed.\n\nLet me now write the patch. Steps:\n1. runner.py: add `generate_chat` and `has_chat_template`.\n2. generator.py:\n   - `extract_code` continuation support (+ helper `_fenced_regions`?).\n   - `_clean_body` calls `extract_code(..., open_fence=True)`.\n   - `fill_stubs`: framing choice, truncation handling, repair previous=cleaned body, dump telemetry.\n3. tests: update mocks to StepResult; add tests for chat framing, truncation keep, repair previous, continuation extraction, `has_chat_template`.\n\nLet me start with the runner. Read the end of runner.py to place the new function after `generate_free`. Already read lines 220-260. I'll add after `generate_free` and before `_generate_impl`.\n\n`generate_chat` shares the timing/response extraction with `_generate_impl`. To avoid duplication, refactor `_generate_impl` to accept a callable? Let me write a small shared `_step_result(label, call, started, budget, llm)`:\n\nActually simpler: implement `generate_chat` with its own response parsing (it differs: message.content vs text). Duplication is ~15 lines. Or extend `_generate_impl` with a `chat` flag:\n\n```python\ndef _generate_impl(llm, prompt, grammar, label, temperature, max_new_tokens, seed, chat=False):\n    llm.reset()\n    budget = generation_budget(llm, prompt, max_new_tokens)\n    started = time.time()\n    if chat:\n        call = dict(messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}],\n                    max_tokens=budget, temperature=temperature)\n        if seed is not None:\n            call[\"seed\"] = seed\n        response = llm.create_chat_completion(**call)\n        choice = (response.get(\"choices\") or [{}])[0]\n        message = choice.get(\"message\") or {}\n        text = message.get(\"content\") or \"\"\n    else:\n        call = dict(prompt=prompt, max_tokens=budget, temperature=temperature)\n        if grammar is not None:\n            call[\"grammar\"] = grammar\n        if seed is not None:\n            call[\"seed\"] = seed\n        response = llm(**call)\n        choice = (response.get(\"choices\") or [{}])[0]\n        text = choice.get(\"text\") or \"\"\n    finish_reason = choice.get(\"finish_reason\")\n    usage = response.get(\"usage\") or {}\n    tokens = usage.get(\"completion_tokens\")\n    if not isinstance(tokens, int):\n        tokens = token_count(llm, text)\n    return StepResult(...)\n```\nThen `generate_free` calls `_generate_impl(..., chat=False)` and `generate_chat(..., chat=True)`. Clean.\n\nSignature: `_generate_impl(llm, prompt, grammar, label, temperature, max_new_tokens, seed, chat=False)`.\n\nAdd:\n```python\ndef generate_chat(llm, prompt, label, temperature=0.0, max_new_tokens=None, seed=None):\n    \"\"\"One generation through the model's chat template, returning a StepResult.\n\n    Raw completion is what the document steps need: a grammar mask makes the\n    output machine-readable, and there is nothing to converse about. A\n    *free* ask is different — an instruct model continued as raw text does\n    not answer it. Measured on Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507, `LibraryService.\n    borrow_book`: raw filled the whole context (finish_reason 'length' at\n    3584 and again at 5905 tokens, still mid-reasoning), while the same\n    prompt through the GGUF's chat template stopped at 220 tokens of code.\n    The template is what selects the assistant mode; `has_chat_template`\n    lets the caller pick between the two.\n    \"\"\"\n    return _generate_impl(llm, prompt, None, label, temperature,\n                          max_new_tokens, seed, chat=True)\n```\nAnd:\n```python\ndef has_chat_template(llm):\n    \"\"\"True when the GGUF carries a chat template `create_chat_completion` uses.\n\n    Every model the work has measured does; the check keeps