on\\nx\\n```\" (starts with tagged) → outside → block x ✓.\nBody then \"```python\\nexample\\n```\" → first fence tagged → outside → blocks = [(\"python\", body), (\"python\", example)] → last python block = example (wrong!). Hmm: a bare body followed by a fenced example. Starting outside, the first block is the body (treated as a fenced region?), wait: start outside; the first line \"body\" is outside (skipped!) until fence #1 (tagged) opens → body is lost, block = example. Bad.\n\nUgh. The completion is not self-contained; its fence structure depends on the prompt. The fundamental ambiguity: a tagged first fence might open the model's own block *or*... in Shape B, the prose before it belongs to the prompt's fence; in \"body then fenced example\", the body before it is the prompt's fence content (the real answer) and the fenced block is an example. Both have prose/code before a tagged fence. The difference is what's before the fence: the answer or narration. Can't tell syntactically.\n\nPractical approach: choose based on what compiles. Candidates:\n- the prompt region (everything before the first bare-or-any fence?), \n- the last python-tagged fenced block,\n- maybe the first python block.\nEvaluate compile/non-empty and prefer... but `extract_code` has no business compiling. Alternatively, return the prompt-region candidate if it looks like code, else the fenced candidate. \"Looks like code\" heuristic: any line matching `^\\s*(return|raise|if|for|while|with|try|[A-Za-z_]\\w*\\s*=|[A-Za-z_]\\w*\\.\\w+\\()`? Prose can contain colons/lists but rarely `=` or `return`. The Qwen echo region is `def ... raise NotImplementedError(...)` — which *looks like code* but is an echo. Hmm, echo detection: starts with def or contains NotImplementedError → treat as an echo, prefer fenced. Prose \"Sure:\" doesn't look like code → prefer fenced. Body \"rows = self._store.all(...)\" looks like code → prefer region.\n\nSo rule:\n1. Collect the prompt region (text before the first fence line) and all answer-opened blocks (re-parse from outside after the first fence, whichever kind).\n2. If the prompt region is an echo → use the last python answer block (or last answer block).\n3. Else if the prompt region looks like code (heuristic) → use it.\n4. Else if there are non-empty answer blocks → use the last python one.\n5. Else → the prompt region (whatever it is).\n\nLet me define \"looks like code\": lines contain one of:\n- `^\\s*(return|raise|yield|pass|import|from|def|class|if|for|while|with|try|except|elif|else|assert|del|global|nonlocal|lambda)\\b`\n- an assignment `^\\s*[\\w.\\[\\]'\"]+\\s*[-+*/|&^%]?=\\s*\\S` (excluding `==`)\n- or the text is *only* a dotted call etc.\nSimpler: try compiling the region as a wrapped body (`def _wrapped(self): ...`)! If it compiles, it's very likely code. That's deterministic and robust. Prose rarely compiles; but \"Sure:\" doesn't (syntax error). The echo compiles (def...raise) — check the echo first. A prose region like \"We need to implement this.\" doesn't compile. A list region \"1. ... 2. ...\" doesn't. Good.\n\nBut `_continuation_block` lives in generator which already imports `ast`/`compile`; compiling in extraction is a side effect but acceptable for a heuristic. However, `extract_code` is used for the fallback (open_fence=False) too; the heuristic only in the continuation path.\n\nWait — simpler alternative: move candidate selection to `_clean_body`/`attempt`. `extract_code` could return the *prompt region* and the *fenced candidates*, but the API returns a string. Changing the API is more invasive. Let me implement the compile heuristic inside `_continuation_block`:\n```python\ndef _continuation_block(raw):\n    lines... collect: prompt region (`first_region`) and answer blocks.\n    if first_region is an echo: candidates = fenced\n    elif _compiles_as_body(first_region): return first_region\n    elif fenced: candidates = fenced\n    else: return first_region or None\n```\n`_compiles_as_body(text)