ock is extra. Actually that's arguably right: for an open-fence continuation, the content before the first closing fence is the answer to the ask. The Qwen echo is the exception (it re-emitted the stub) — hence the echo check.\n- \"```python\\nx\\n```\" (starts with a tagged fence): first region empty → not an echo (empty text; my current code treats empty as \"no body\" → fenced candidates → x). With the simplified rule, first region empty → fall through to fenced → x ✓.\n- \"body then fenced example\": first region is a body (not an echo) → body ✓ (better than the last rule!).\n\nSo the simplified rule:\n1. Parse regions: first region = text before the first fence line (starting inside).\n2. If the first region has non-empty content and is NOT an echo → return it.\n3. Else, if there are answer-opened python blocks → return the last one (or last fenced if no python).\n4. Else → return the first region's text (may be the echo) or None.\n\nWhere \"answer-opened blocks\" are collected by re-parsing from outside *after* the first fence; but with the first region being an echo, the fenced blocks after it: the first fence closes the prompt; subsequent fences alternate; a fenced block between fence #2 and fence #3 is \"outside\" in my state machine if fence #2 is a closing... Let's trace Shape A again with \"collect from outside after the first fence\": after the first bare close, state outside; the prose; fence #2 (```python) opens; code; fence #3 closes → block code. So blocks = [code, ...]. Implementation: skip lines up to and including the first fence, then parse with inside=False... but the line after the first fence could itself be a fence (Shape: \"```\\n```python...\"). Parsing with inside=False handles it.\n\nActually, simplest implementation:\n```python\ndef _continuation_block(raw):\n    lines = raw.splitlines()\n    first_fence = next((i for i, line in enumerate(lines) if _FENCE_LINE.match(line)), None)\n    if first_fence is None:\n        return None  # no f