          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "Interesting — the pipeline's `_generate` has its own log messages (\"no room left to raise\"). My `_answered` helper in fill_stubs doesn't log a \"no room\" line; it just doesn't retry (if `room <= budget`). That's acceptable — actually, should it log? The fill stubs note says \"the answer was cut off at the N-token ceiling\", which is enough.\n\nWait, but in my `_answered`, when the retry has no more room (`room <= budget`), the result stays truncated → the kept note is \"the answer was cut off at the {budget}-token ceiling\". Good.\n\nNow append a test class to test_truncation_retry.py for the chat framing + `has_chat_template` + `generate_chat` + `available_tokens` clamp for chat. Actually `generate_chat` belongs with the free calls; but test_truncation_retry.py is about budgets. Maybe a new file `tests/test_free_calls.py` is cleaner: \"the free (maskless) calls: raw continuation for documents, chat for asks\". Let me create it with:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"The maskless calls: raw continuation for documents, chat for free asks.\n\nWhy this has a test at all: the stub filler asked an instruct model to\ncomplete a document that ended inside a code fence, and Qwen3-4B-Instruct-\n2507 answered by continuing the *document* — it echoed the stub, closed the\nfence, and reasoned for the whole context (finish_reason 'length' at\n3584/3584, and again at 5905/5905). Through the GGUF's own chat template the\nsame ask stopped at 220 tokens of code. The template is the framing lever;\nthese tests pin which call each case takes and what it records.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport sys\nimport unittest\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom unittest import mock\n\nsys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent))\n\nfrom kernel.extraction import runner  # noqa: E402\n\n\nclass ChatModel:\n    \"\"\"Just enough of a Llama to answer `generate_chat`.\"\"\"\n\n    metadata = {\"tokenizer.chat_template\": \"{{ prompt }}\"}\n\n    def __init__(self, content=\"return 0\", finish_reason=\"stop\",\n                 completion_tokens=7, context=8192, prompt_tokens=100):\n        self.content = content\n        self.finish_reason = finish_reason\n        self.completion_tokens = completion_tokens\n        self._context = context\n        self._prompt_tokens = prompt_tokens\n        self.calls = []\n\n    def reset(self):\n        self.calls.append((\"reset\",))\n\n    def n_ctx(self):\n        return self._context\n\n    def tokenize(self, data):\n        return list(range(self._prompt_tokens))\n\n    def create_chat_completion(self, **kwargs):\n        self.calls.append(kwargs)\n        return {\n            \"choices\": [{\"message\": {\"content\": self.content},\n                         \"finish_reason\": self.finish_reason}],\n            \"usage\": {\"completion_tokens\": self.completion_tokens},\n        }\n\n\nclass GenerateChatTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_the_prompt_goes_through_the_chat_template(self):\n        model = ChatModel(content=\"return 0\")\n        result = runner.generate_chat(model, \"write the body\", \"stub:x\",\n                                      temperature=0.0, seed=20261012)\n        call = model.calls[-1]\n        self.assertEqual(call[\"messages\"], [{\"role\": \"user\",\n                                             \"content\": \"write the body\"}])\n        self.assertEqual(call[\"temperature\"], 0.0)\n        self.assertEqual(call[\"seed\"], 20261012)\n        self.assertEqual(call[\"max_tokens\"], 3584)\n        self.assertEqual(result.text, \"return 0\")\n        self.assertEqual(result.finish_reason, \"stop\")\n        self.assertEqual(result.tokens, 7)\n        self.assertEqual(result.budget, 3584)\n        self.assertFalse(result.truncated)\n\n    def test_a_length_finish_is_truncated_and_says_so(self):\n        model = ChatModel(finish_reason=\"length\")\n        result = runner.generate_chat(model, \"write the body\", \"stub:x\")\n        self.assertTrue(result.truncated)\n        self.assertEqual(result.budget, 3584)\n        self.assertIn(\"TRUNCATED at the 3584-token ceiling\", result.describe())\n\n    def test_the_budget_respects_the_room_the_prompt_left(self):\n        model = ChatModel(context=8192, prompt_tokens=8000)\n        result = runner.generate_chat(model, \"write the body\", \"stub:x\")\n        # 8192 - 8000 - 64 = 128, under the default ceiling.\n        self.assertEqual(model.calls[-1][\"max_tokens\"], 128)\n        self.assertEqual(result.budget, 128)\n\n    def test_an_overridden_ceiling_is_still_clamped_to_the_room(self):\n        model = ChatModel(context=8192, prompt_tokens=8000)\n        runner.generate_chat(model, \"write the body\", \"stub:x\",\n                             max_new_tokens=4096)\n        self.assertEqual(model.calls[-1][\"max_tokens\"], 128)\n\n\nclass ChatTemplateProbeTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_a_template_in_the_metadata_means_chat(self):\n        model = mock.Mock(metadata={\"tokenizer.chat_template\": \"{{ ... }}\"})\n        self.assertTrue(runner.has_chat_template(model))\n\n    def test_the_resolved_chat_format_also_counts(self):\n        model = mock.Mock(metadata={}, chat_format=\"chatml\")\n        self.assertTrue(runner.has_chat_template(model))\n\n    def test_a_model_without_either_stays_on_raw_continuation(self):\n        self.assertFalse(runner.has_chat_template(object()))\n        self.assertFalse(runner.has_chat_template(mock.Mock(metadata={},\n                                                            chat_format=None)))\n\n\nclass RawGenerationStaysAvailableTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    \"\"\"`generate_free` is the document-style raw call; it must not move.\"\"\"\n\n    def test_generate_free_still_sends_a_raw_prompt(self):\n        model = ChatModel()\n\n        def llm_call(**kwargs):\n            model.calls.append(kwargs)\n            return {\"choices\": [{\"text\": \"prose\", \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"}],\n                    \"usage\": {\"completion_tokens\": 3}}\n\n        model.__class__ = type(\"Raw\", (ChatModel,), {\"__call__\": lambda self, **kw: llm_call(**kw)})\n```\nHmm, that's getting awkward. Simpler: define a `RawModel` class with `__call__` and `reset`, `n_ctx`, `tokenize`:\n\n```python\nclass RawModel:\n    def __init__(self, context=8192, prompt_tokens=100):\n        ...\n    def reset(self): ...\n    def n_ctx(self): ...\n    def tokenize(self, data): ...\n    def __call__(self, **kwargs):\n        self.calls.append(kwargs)\n        return {\"choices\": [{\"text\": \"prose\", \"finish_reason\": \"stop\"}],\n                \"usage\": {\"completion_tokens\": 3}}\n\n\nclass RawGenerationStaysRaw(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_generate_free_still_sends_a_raw_prompt(self):\n        model = RawModel()\n        result = runner.generate_free(model, \"a document\", \"step\")\n        self.assertEqual(model.calls[-1][\"prompt\"], \"a document\")\n        self.assertNotIn(\"messages\", model.calls[-1])\n        self.assertEqual(result.text, \"prose\")\n```\nGood.\n\nAlso add a test for `has_chat_template` with a Mock whose `metadata` raises? `mock.Mock()` returns a Mock for `.metadata` (truthy, `.get` returns a Mock which is truthy) — hmm: `mock.Mock().metadata or {}` → a Mock (truthy) → `metadata.get(\"tokenizer.chat_template\")` → a Mock (truthy) → `if ...: return True`. So `runner.has_chat_template(mock.Mock())` → True! That could affect tests where `load_model` returns a bare Mock. In test_generator, `load_model` returns `object()` in stub tests → False. In test_generator at line 269-272, `generate_fallback` tests return `mock.Mock()` but those don't call `has_chat_template`. OK.\n\nBut for safety in the `fill` tests, `load_model` returns `object()`. Fine.\n\nNow, one more concern: `has_chat_template(mock.Mock(metadata={}))`: `metadata={}` → `.get` → None → `getattr(mock, \"chat_format\", None)` → a Mock (truthy!) → True. That would break the \"no template\" expectation for a bare Mock. For `mock.Mock(metadata={}, chat_format=None)` → chat_format None → False. But a plain `mock.Mock()` used as a model in some test would read as \"has chat\". In test_generator, `test_unparseable_code_is_refused_not_written` uses `return_value=mock.Mock()` but only for `generate_fallback` (no has_chat_template). Any other site calling fill_stubs with a bare Mock? `test_nothing_to_fill_never_loads_the_model` patches load_model but shouldn't load (no stubs). Let me grep for `fill_stubs` invocations with `load_model` return_value.\n\nActually, in `test_generator.py` line ~1544: `mock.patch.object(generator.runner, \"load_model\")` — not called. Others use `object()`. Good.\n\nBut robustness: `getattr(llm, \"chat_format\", None)` on a Mock returns a Mock — truthy. Should I harden `has_chat_template` to only accept a string? \n```python\nchat_format = getattr(llm, \"chat_format\", None)\nreturn bool(metadata.get(\"tokenizer.chat_template\")) or isinstance(chat_format, str)\n```\nThat's cleaner: a Mock's `.chat_format` is not a str → False. And a real llama_cpp `chat_format` is a str (\"chatml\" etc.). Let me update the runner. Also `metadata.get(...)`: if metadata is a Mock (not a dict), `.get` returns a Mock → `bool(Mock)` = True → still True. Hmm. Should I require the template value to be a str too? `isinstance(template, str)`:\n```python\ntemplate = metadata.get(\"tokenizer.chat_template\") if isinstance(metadata, dict) else None\n```\nBut real llama_cpp `llm.metadata` is a dict? In llama-cpp-python, `Llama.metadata` is a dict property (returns `self._metadata`?). Actually `llm.metadata` returns a dict from the GGUF. The probe printed `general.name` etc., so it's dict-like. Use:\n```python\nmetadata = getattr(llm, \"metadata\", None) or {}\ntemplate = metadata.get(\"tokenizer.chat_template\") if hasattr(metadata, \"get\") else None\nreturn isinstance(template, str) and template.strip() != \"\" or isinstance(getattr(llm, \"chat_format\", None), str)\n```\nHmm, `isinstance(template, str) and template` then `or isinstance(chat_format, str)`. Let me write:\n```python\ndef has_chat_template(llm):\n    \"\"\"True when the GGUF carries a chat template `create_chat_completion` uses.\n\n    Every model measured so far does; the check keeps a template-less GGUF\n    (and a test mock) on `generate_free` instead of failing body by body.\n    \"\"\"\n    try:\n        metadata = llm.metadata or {}\n        template = metadata.get(\"tokenizer.chat_template\")\n    except Exception:  # a mock or a binding without the property\n        template = None\n    if isinstance(template, str) and template.strip():\n        return True\n    return isinstance(getattr(llm, \"chat_format\", None), str)\n```\nWith a bare Mock: `llm.metadata` → Mock (truthy) → `.get` → Mock → not a str → then `chat_format` Mock → not a str → False. \n\nWith `object()`: AttributeError → template None; chat_format → AttributeError on getattr? `getattr(object(), \"chat_format\", None)` → None → False. Good.\n\nWith the real model: metadata dict, template str → True.\n\nUpdate the runner accordingly.\n\nNow, also think about `_answered` when `runner.available_tokens` raises (e.g., a model whose `tokenize` misbehaves): the outer try in fill_stubs catches → \"the model failed\". Acceptable.\n\nNow let me also double check `_answered` retry in the *repair* path with the `Model` fake in tests: `tokenize` returns [] → available 8128 > budget 3584 → retry. Good.\n\nLet me now write the edits:\n1. runner `has_chat_template` hardening.\n2. test_generator new tests.\n3. new tests/test_free_calls.py.\n\nFirst check current runner has_chat_template text.",
