rget method name is in the module at ~line 110, close to the ask.\n- Does the data support \"dilution\"? The model used exact repo method names and error names — the API/references were read. It failed on the *requirements* details (ordering, OR) and invented a constant. That is consistent with the intent being less salient than the API and local code patterns. But it's also consistent with pattern priors.\n- The prompt is actually modest; \"attention dilution\" in the strict sense (long-context recall) is less likely than **instruction competition**: 34 signatures + API facts + module snippets vs one bullet of intent. The model follows the strongest/local patterns.\n- Note the model repeated the stub's docstring in the answer? The chat answers didn't. Fine.\n\nWhat to recommend concretely:\n\nA. **Fix the prompt contradictions** (these are harness defects, independent of the model):\n   1. Make the reference honest: show `read_one_book` as raising `BookNotFoundError` (or state \"read_one_* raise NotFoundError when missing; the `| None` is the dataclass-style signature lie\"). Better: change the displayed reference to what the mechanical body does, and/or remove `| None` and add one line: \"read_one_* raise their NotFoundError; they do not return None.\"\n   2. Resolve the date convention: state exactly what to pass. The store accepts datetime/date objects and normalizes to ISO (the template), so the cleanest line: \"Date fields are stored as ISO strings; pass `datetime.date`/`datetime.datetime` values (the store normalizes) or ISO strings; never format with `strftime`/space-separated timestamps.\" Alternatively mandate `datetime.datetime.now()` and `.isoformat()`.\n   3. Error semantics: list each error with its one-line meaning in the API section, or only list the errors relevant to the current method (the contract's raises). For return_book, the contract (fabricated) says InvalidReturnDateError; if we include its meaning (\"returned before its due date\"), the model would implement a date comparison, still not the requirement. The real fix is the document (frozen), but the prompt can at least not invite misuse: list names *and* descriptions. Note: the errors module docstrings aren't shown; adding one line per error is cheap (8 errors).\n   4. Delegation: when the stub reason says \"no same-name repository method\", add the closest *shape*-matching repo method if one exists: \"`BookRepository.search_books_by_title_author_isbn` searches the same fields; delegate to it if it matches.\" Or fix the module comment in the *emitter* (the services module docstring) to say \"a method that a repository already provides should delegate; check the list\". This is generator text, allowed.\n   5. The requirements' Book model omission of author_id is a document issue (frozen). The API covers it; no action.\n   6. Due date underspecification: no period in requirements. Options: leave the model to invent (bad) or make the prompt require refusal on unspecified behavior. But refusing the whole body because of one constant is also bad. Better: the prompt could say \"use only values the requirements state; if a detail is unstated, choose the simplest defensible default and note it in a comment\" — but that invites invention. Or \"raise NotImplementedError for the undetermined sub-detail\"? Can't — the method needs a value. The honest answer: this is an intent gap; the right fix is in the requirements (not frozen? The original user prompt is the requirements.txt — frozen as part of the run? The user said `prompts/*.txt` frozen; requirements come from the user project, not our prompts. For the library experiment, the requirements are the input. We can't change the input for the A/B; we can record the finding.)\n\nB. **Run a prompt ablation before the A/B** (same Qwen, same 5 stubs, temp 0):\n   - V0 baseline.\n   - V1 salience: repeat the method's one-line requirement immediately above the ask (\"The requirement for this method: ...\"). Only chang