d_one_member, update_member). It clearly read the list. The service search ignored `search_books_by_title_author_isbn` — but the stub reason said no same-name method. So the nudge is the likely cause, not losing a line in 34.\n\nLet me also double-check the stub docstring for service search in the *prompt*: \"Searches books by title, author, or ISBN. [no same-name repository method to delegate to]\". Yes. The \"[no same-name...]\" is the emitter's refusal reason. The model might read \"no same-name repository method to delegate to\" as \"do not delegate\". That's a contradiction with the available differently-named method.\n\nAlso check the borrow stub's docstring: \"Borrow a book by member and book IDs, checks availability, creates loan, and decrements copies. [rule plain-method: method needs custom logic]\". So the intent is right there. And the model still ordered create first. Strong evidence against dilution for that error.\n\nSo the answer: \n- Attention dilution: the prompt is modest (2.2k tokens); intent appears twice (requirements at 53%, docstring at ~65%, ask at 98%). The specific failures don't line up with distance: borrow's intent is in the docstring directly above the stub, and it still inverted the order. So dilution is not the main explanation here.\n- Contradictions: yes, several real ones. They explain specific failure shapes: dead None checks (signature says `| None`), invented InvalidReturnDateError guard (error list without semantics), service-search reimplementation (delegation nudge vs available method), date format drift (ISO rule vs datetime annotations), invented 14 days (unstated period — not a contradiction but a gap). These are worth fixing regardless.\n- The rest is local reasoning/prior: ISBN `elif` instead of `or`, create-before-check even though the docstring says check first. No prompt fix addresses those except a reviewer/checker/stronger model.\n\nThen propose the experiment: fix the contradictions first (they're independent defects), then run the salience variant if we want to measure dilution; and keep the A/B after. Or run V1/V2 ablation on Qwen to quantify. The user's question is analytic; a crisp answer with the receipts and a recommended next step is best.\n\nOne more factual check: is the stub docstring emitted from the requirements? Yes — the docstring text mirrors the requirement line (the emitter uses the method's description). So the model had it twice.\n\nLet me also mention the phrase \"The data the sibling modules hand you\" section: `self._repos` etc. The API facts are long but useful.\n\nAlso note: the prompt's \"Rules\" say \"Reply with ONLY the replacement...\" and the model complied. Good.\n\nI think the answer should be:\n1. Both are real possibilities in general; in this run the receipts point more to contradictions + priors than dilution.\n2. Contradictions list (with mechanism and which observed error it explains).\n3. Dilution assessment with numbers and the docstring rebuttal.\n4. Cheap ablation to settle it (if they want to spend a run): V1 repeat intent above the ask; V2 contradiction fixes; V3 minimal prompt. Same model, same 5 stubs.\n5. Recommendation: fix contradictions (harness correctness), don't chase dilution with a bigger prompt; the A/B can proceed after, or run the ablation first on Qwen since it's cheap.\n\nLength: moderate. Use a table for contradictions → observed failure. Avoid over-hedging but be honest about attribution limits.\n\nLet me draft the concrete fixes for contradictions:\n- `read_one_book -> models.Book | None`: replace displayed `| None` with the actual behavior, or add a line \"read_one_* raise NotFoundError on a missing record; the `| None` in the generated annotation is not the runtime contract.\" Ideally fix the emitted signature? The models are generated mechanically; changing the signature would affect docs. Prompt-side line is cheapest.\n- Error list: add meanings from the class docstrings (one line each), or list only the error