          "text": "Both are real risks, but this run's receipts point more at **contradictory/misleading prompt facts and local priors** than at long-prompt dilution. Concretely:\n\n**Contradictions that map onto observed failures**\n\n| prompt fact | what the prompt shows | what the model did | observed failure |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `read_one_book` reference | `-> models.Book \\| None` | checked `if book is None` | dead checks — the mechanical body actually raises `BookNotFoundError` |\n| error list | `errors.py defines only these: … InvalidReturnDateError …` (names only, no semantics) | invented a `status != \"active\"` guard with it | fabricated return-book rule |\n| service stub reason | `[no same-name repository method to delegate to]` | reimplemented search by hand | crashes; ignored `search_books_by_title_author_isbn`, which **is** in the reference list |\n| date rule | \"Date fields hold ISO strings (`\"2026-10-05\"`)\" vs `loan_date: datetime.datetime` | `datetime.now().strftime(\"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S\")` | non-ISO, space-separated timestamps |\n| requirements | no loan period stated | invented 14 days | silent gap-filling |\n\nThe `| None` and the error-name list are not dilution — they are the prompt telling the model something false or semantically empty. The delegation line actively nudges away from the right repository method.\n\n**On dilution:** the prompt is 9,486 chars but only ~2,223 tokens (27% of the 8k context). The intent is not just at line 88 of 167 — it is repeated in the stub's own docstring, directly above the hole: *\"checks availability, creates loan, and decrements copies.\"* The model still created the loan before checking, and still wrote `elif` instead of an OR. Those two errors are local reasoning/priors, not lost-in-the-middle: the instruction was ~40 lines from the ask, in the target method itself. So \"the prompt is too big\" is not a good explanation for them.\n\n**What is worth testing cheaply, on Qwen, before the A/B** (5 stubs