eate_\", \"update_\")):\n                read_names.add(name)\n        if cls:\n            typed[name] = cls\n    seen = set()\n    ... attribute check ...\n```\nThen add read-modify after the attribute check:\n```python\n    mutations = {}\n    for node in ast.walk(tree):\n        if isinstance(node, ast.Assign):\n            targets = node.targets\n        elif isinstance(node, ast.AugAssign):\n            targets = [node.target]\n        else:\n            continue\n        for target in targets:\n            if (isinstance(target, ast.Attribute)\n                    and isinstance(target.value, ast.Name)\n                    and target.value.id in typed):\n                mutations.setdefault(target.value.id, target.attr)\n    updated = set()\n    for node in ast.walk(tree):\n        if not (isinstance(node, ast.Call)\n                and isinstance(node.func, ast.Attribute)\n                and node.func.attr.startswith(\"update_\")):\n            continue\n        for arg in list(node.args) + [kw.value for kw in node.keywords]:\n            for inner in ast.walk(arg):\n                if isinstance(inner, ast.Name) and inner.id in mutations:\n                    updated.add(inner.id)\n    for name in sorted(mutations):\n        if name in read_names and name not in updated:\n            field_name = mutations[name]\n            found.append(\n                f\"`{name}.{field_name}` is changed, but `{name}` is never \"\n                \"passed to an update_* method; a dataclass is a copy, so \"\n                \"the change is lost\")\n```\nPlace before the read-before-assign loop or after? The attribute check; then read-modify; then read-before. The order in `found`: dict view, unknown names, unused params, typed-field, read-modify, read-before, return-type. The existing tests with no read-modify unaffected. The new tests exact lists include the read-modify after typed; if only read-modify, fine.\n- The `mutations` `targets` for `ast.Assign` can include `as