          "type": "text",
          "text": "Yes — partly. We added explicit **library-specific** contracts, but they live in `generator.py`, not in:\n\n- `prompts/*.txt`\n- `out/library/requirements.txt`\n- the catalog\n- any prompt-hash–stamped human answer\n\nThey are assembled at runtime into the exact fill prompt.\n\n## What was added\n\n| Rule | Where | Trigger | Is it new? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `return_date` is now, not `due_date` | `_target_contract` | requirement contains `sets return_date` | **Yes — new semantic fact** |\n| Return increments `available_copies`, read by `loan.book_id`, save with `update_book` | `_target_contract` | requirement contains `increments copies` | Mostly a restatement of “increments copies” |\n| Borrow order: check book → create loan → decrement/update book | `_target_contract` | requirement contains `checks availability` + `creates loan` | Restatement of “checks availability, creates loan, decrements copies” |\n| A copies-changing requirement must write a `copies` field and call `update_<class>` | `_body_complaints` | requirement contains `increments/decrements copies` | Checker, partly derived from the emitted models |\n| Refuse `return_date` assigned from `due_date` | `_body_complaints` | hardcoded field names | Library-specific checker |\n| Allow inline imports only from `BODY_IMPORTS` | `_body_refusal` | any body-level import | Generic |\n\nThe return-date/update-book facts are emitted only in the **exact** context. The complaint checks run in every fill context. Both models see the same rules at the same commit, so the A/B is still same-harness — but the exact context is now visibly **library-tuned**.\n\n## The important distinction\n\nThe requirements say:\n\n```text\nreturn_book(loan_id): sets return_date, updates status, increments copies\n```\n\nThey do **not** say “return_date is now, not due_date.” We added that semantic in the generator. That is the one rule that is a real new fact rather than a restatement, and it is not human-owned or prompt-hash stamped. The same applies, to a lesser extent, to hardcoding the field names `return_date`/`due_date` in the checker.\n\nSo: the return success came partly from the model and partly from the harness spelling out the library contract. That is fair for a same-harness Qwen/Ministral comparison, but it is not a pure test of model generalization.\n\n## Recommendation\n\nIf the contract is meant to be evidence, move these facts into the human-owned library requirements/architecture facts, with prompt-hash persistence, and have the generator read them instead of carrying `return_date = now` and the `return_date`/`due_date` field check in code. The copy/update and borrow-order rules are closer to direct restatements and can stay as checker constraints derived from the requirement and models.\n\nThe double-return behavior — no active-loan check — is another candidate human fact. It is not stated in the requirements and the generator currently accepts it."
