          "text": "We need answer the user. Let's think through the architecture and what to recommend.\n\nThe user's critique:\n1. \"I agree with you that this should not appear in code.\" → Move library-specific facts out of generator.\n2. \"if I'm forced to have this kind of checks for this prompt in order to get a correct generated code that means the system won't generalize: what if I need something specific for say a factory and stock management?\"\n\nThe core issue: The generator is carrying domain-specific semantics (return_date, available_copies, borrow order). That is overfitting. The system should generalize via a domain-agnostic contract/fact layer. The facts will always be domain-specific; the *mechanism* must not be. The model needs the facts for any domain; \"generalize\" means the pipeline can accept and enforce facts from arbitrary domains, not that the 4B model can infer unstated behavior.\n\nWe should not merely say \"we'll add more checks.\" We should propose a data model and refactor. Let's design carefully, considering the existing repo structures:\n\nExisting artifacts per app:\n- requirements.txt (source prompt-ish)\n- components.json\n- data_models.json\n- errors.json\n- service_contracts.json (signatures/descriptions/raises)\n- method_slots.json (pattern decisions, decided_by user, requirements_sha)\n- invariants.json (text rules, kind schema/rule)\n- pattern_matches.json\n- persistence_ports.json\n- interaction_adapters.json\n- surface_decisions.json\n- intentions.json\n- field_aliases.json\n- generated/\n- run.log\n\nThe generator uses `service_contracts.json`? It fills method stubs. It has `_requirements_line`, `_target_contract`, `_body_complaints`. The human answer persistence uses method_slots/surface_decisions with requirements_sha. So the natural place for a general behavior contract is a new artifact, e.g. `method_contracts.json` or extend `service_contracts.json` with a `behavior`/`contract` entry. But the service_contracts likely