          "text": "You’re right. That patch is the library’s specification moved into Python. It should not be there, and it would not help a factory/stock system: `stock_qty`, `restock_product`, or `consume_material` would never match those library phrases and field names.\n\nThe important distinction is:\n\n- **The engine** must be domain-neutral.\n- **The domain facts** are unavoidably specific.\n\nThe goal is not “the model knows library, factory, and stock.” The goal is “the engine can consume any app’s confirmed facts, and refuses when they are missing.” Every domain needs its own facts; they should not become a growing pile of regexes in `generator.py`.\n\n## What is genuinely general — keep it\n\n- typed reads and read-modify-write\n- return-type checks\n- unresolved-name and import checks\n- create-before-raise\n- update-before-create\n- exact prompt: models as code, signature, return dataclass, requirement line\n- human decisions stamped with `requirements_sha`, like `method_slots.json`\n\nThose guards caught real failures and apply to any domain.\n\n## What should move out\n\n- `return_date = now, not due_date`\n- `available_copies` / `update_book`\n- the numbered borrow order\n- the “copies” field-name heuristic\n- the `return_date`/`due_date` field-name check\n\nThe newly added domain facts are the problem, not the generic guards.\n\n## The design: a behavior contract layer\n\nAdd a human-owned file per app, for example `out/<app>/method_contracts.json`, following the `method_slots.json` convention:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"holder\": \"LibraryService\",\n  \"method\": \"return_book\",\n  \"decided_by\": \"user\",\n  \"requirements_sha\": \"...\",\n  \"preconditions\": [\n    {\"check\": \"loan.status == 'active'\", \"error\": \"InvalidReturnDateError\"}\n  ],\n  \"effects\": [\n    {\"target\": \"loan.return_date\", \"op\": \"set\", \"value\": \"now\",\n     \"write\": \"LoanRepository.update_loan\"},\n    {\"target\": \"loan.status\", \"op\": \"set\", \"value\": \"returned\",\n     \"write\": \"LoanRepository.update_loan\"},\n    {\"target\": \"book.available_copies\", \"op\": \"increment\", \"by\": 1,\n     \"write\": \"BookRepository.update_book\"}\n  ],\n  \"forbid\": [\"return_date = due_date\"]\n}\n```\n\nThe same schema for stock:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"holder\": \"InventoryService\",\n  \"method\": \"restock_product\",\n  \"decided_by\": \"user\",\n  \"requirements_sha\": \"...\",\n  \"effects\": [\n    {\"target\": \"product.stock_qty\", \"op\": \"increment\", \"by\": \"qty\",\n     \"write\": \"ProductRepository.update_product\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nAnd for a factory:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"holder\": \"FactoryService\",\n  \"method\": \"consume_material\",\n  \"preconditions\": [\n    {\"check\": \"stock.quantity >= qty\", \"error\": \"InsufficientStockError\"}\n  ],\n  \"creates\": [\n    {\"class\": \"Consumption\",\n     \"call\": \"ConsumptionRepository.create_consumption\"}\n  ],\n  \"effects\": [\n    {\"target\": \"stock.quantity\", \"op\": \"decrement\", \"by\": \"qty\",\n     \"write\": \"StockRepository.update_stock\"}\n  ],\n  \"order\": [\"preconditions\", \"create_consumption\", \"update_stock\"]\n}\n```\n\nThe generator then knows only contract concepts: effects, writes, creates, order, forbids. It does not know “book,” “copies,” “stock,” or “return date.”\n\nThe checks become generic:\n\n- effect: the target field is assigned/augmented, and the named write call exists\n- create: the named create call exists\n- order: create precedes update; validation precedes create\n- forbid: the named relation does not appear\n- precondition: a guard/raise exists, or the fact is left to smoke tests initially\n\nThe existing create-before-raise and update-before-create guards are just special cases of the generic order/dominance checks.\n\n## Where the facts come from\n\n`extract_arch.py` proposes them and asks the human when needed:\n\n- “`return_book` sets `return_date`. Is it the current time, the due date, or a parameter?”\n- “`borrow_book` creates a loan and decrements copies. Must the create precede the decrement?”\n- “`restock_product` changes `stock_qty`. Must quantity be positive?”\n- “`consume_material` creates a consumption and decrements stock. Must the create happen first? Reject negative stock?”\n\nThose questions are built from the operations and fields in the app, not from hardcoded library names. Answers are persisted with `decided_by: user` and `requirements_sha`. If a contract is missing or stale, the generator leaves the stub and reports `[needs_contract]`; it does not invent facts.\n\nA stronger model may need fewer obvious facts spelled out, but it cannot infer a factory’s inventory policy from a paragraph. Correctness still needs a human-owned specification. That is not a failure of generalization; that is where generalization actually comes from.\n\n## Immediate consequence\n\nThe current Qwen 4/5 run is **library-tuned**, not a clean baseline. I would not compare Ministral against it. The return body also exposes why the facts need human ownership: `invariants.json` says `return_date` should be after `due_date`, `service_contracts.json` declares `InvalidReturnDateError`, the generated return sets `return_date = now`, and a second return increments copies again. Those artifacts disagree; a hardcoded generator rule cannot resolve that correctly.\n\nI recommend:\n\n1. Remove the library-specific rules added in `8fa0eb0`/`d4ef10b`, keeping the generic guards.\n2. Add `method_contracts.json` plus a contract compiler and generic checker.\n3. Migrate the library facts into that file.\n4. Add an inventory/factory-stock test fixture so the generic path is exercised without library terms.\n5. Re-run Qwen exact, then Ministral on the same fact-driven harness.\n\nShall I start with steps 1–3 and the stock fixture?"
