h imports runner) — so llama_cpp must be installed in the test env (it is, since generator imports fine in tests).\\n\\nFake runner setup:\\n```python\\nfrom unittest import mock\\nfake_llm = object()\\nwith mock.patch.object(generator.runner, \\\"load_model\\\", return_value=fake_llm), \\\\\\n     mock.patch.object(generator.runner, \\\"generate_free\\\") as gen:\\n    gen.return_value = types.SimpleNamespace(text=\\\"...body...\\\", tokens=42, seconds=0.1)\\n    rows = generator.fill_stubs(files, ctx, \\\"Ministral-3-3B-Instruct-2512-Q4_K_M.gguf\\\", \\\"requirements text\\\")\\n```\\nWait — mock.patch.object(generate_free) replaces the function; fill_stubs calls runner.generate_free ✓. But `generator.runner` is the SAME module object as `kernel.extraction.runner` ✓ patching affects both.\\n\\nTest cases:\\n1. `test_the_model_fills_a_stub_and_the_body_lands_marked`: body returns e.g.\\n```\\nvalues = self._store.all(self.ENTITY)\\nreturn [models.Product(**record) for record in values]\\n```\\n→ module contains the body (indented 8), docstring rewritten `[llm-written by Ministar...]`, compile-ok, rows == [(\\\"ProductRepository.export_products\\\", \\\"written\\\", \\\"\\\")]. Hmm — wait, export_products's real stub: let me check the shop fixture: ProductRepository.export_products(file_path: str) -> None refused NO_PATTERN_IN_DOMAIN → stub in repositories.py. In my hand ctx I should mirror a similar stub. Let me build the ctx minimal (like the service composition ctx) and add a custom method with an entry that refuses: e.g. `{\\\"source\\\": \\\"contract:WidgetRepository.export_widgets\\\", pattern_id NO_PATTERN_IN_DOMAIN, decision \\\"undefined\\\"}`.\\n\\nHmm, careful: the ctx for render_program also needs \\\"routes\\\"? render_program → emit_cli → _leaf_scan → ctx.get(\\\"routes\\\") ✓ optional; components {} → invocation {} → no leaves ✓; adapters [] ✓. match_index needed for planning ✓. Let me use the ServiceCompositionTests.ctx() minus services? It has repositories with read_one/all + a \\\"delete_everything\\\" custom service method (no pattern entry) → services.py stub for delete_everything! And repositories all mechanical. I need a repo stub too: add to WidgetRepository a method \\\"export_widgets\\\" with an entry in match_index decision undefined (NO_PATTERN_IN_DOMAIN). \\n\\nThe reason text for undefined: plan_method → kind \\\"llm-gap\\\"? For `NO_PATTERN_IN_DOMAIN` with decision \\\"undefined\\\", tie_rules.effective_directive → kind \\\"llm-gap\\\" probably (\\\"refused claim\\\") → plan: `_gap_reason(...)` maybe \\\"READ_ALL returns collection...\\\"; if `_shape_crud` gives read_one for unique params... To force a clean stub: use a method with an entity/collection shape that stays stub, or no match entry at all + filter-ish name with no params? plan_method(None entry): crud from name; \\\"export_widgets\\\" → classify None → shape None → returns (\\\"stub\\\", \\\"no pattern entry and the signature matches no pattern\\\")? Need to see the tail of plan_method after `shape = _shape_crud(...)`. Whatever it returns, it's a stub ✓.\\n\\nSimplest: adapt the shop-like ctx: repositories = WidgetRepository with methods read_widget_by_id, read_all_widgets, export_widgets(file_path str) -> None with NO entry; services = [] ... but render_program emits services from ctx[\\\"services\\\"] ✓ empty fine. Then repositories.py has exactly one stub (export_widgets) ✓.\\n\\nLet me confirm plan_method's fallback returns a stub for export_widgets with no entry: classify_crud(\\\"export_widgets\\\") → None (no markers); `crud = None` → skip filter check; `if crud is not None:` no; `shape = _shape_crud(method, entity_names)` → return None (returns \\\"None\\\") → then the tail (which I haven't seen) → presumably (\\\"stub\\\", ...). Let me view the tail of plan_method to be sure.\\n\\n2. `test_a_body_that_does_not_compile_keeps_the_stub`: fake returns \\\"return (((\\\" → rows kept; files text still has the raise; stats/rows note contains \\\"does not compile\\\".\\n\\n3. `test_a_body_calling_an_undefined_self_name_keeps_the_stub`: \\\"return self.missing_helper()\\\" → kept.\\n\\n4. `test_a_body_using_a_module_without_its_import_gains_one`: body \\\"return json.dumps(...)\\\"? export_widgets returns None... make the body `import`-free but using json: \\\"return json.dumps(self._store.all(self.ENTITY))\\\"? Compile ok; stores imported json added ✓ assert \\\"import json\\\" in module text and note \\\"+import json\\\"? My note format: let me define note \\\"\\\" or \\\"added import json\\\". Rows (label, \\\"written\\\", \\\"added import json\\\").\\n\\nAlso test the driver-through-main? Simpler to test fill_stubs directly + one main-level test that --model triggers the pass? The main wiring is 3 lines; a direct fill_stubs test + the argparse help is enough. Hmm — but the wiring order (before writing files) matters: if I called fill_stubs AFTER writing, the file on disk wouldn't have bodies. Let me add a tiny end-to-end main test with patched runner on a temp dir: generate shop-ish → assert written cli? Actually a full-out-dir fixture is heavy; the shop `fixture()` has stubs (export_products, price_report). Plan: `with tempfile...: out = write_out(..., fixture()); patch runner; code = generator.main([out, \\\"--model\\\", \\\"fake.gguf\\\"]); assert text of repositories.py contains the fake body`. Feasible! The gate must pass (as GenerationTests shows) ✓. The generation writes files; then I read out/generated/repositories.py and check the model's body present + `[llm-written by fake.gguf` marker. This covers: --model flag wiring, pass before write, marker rewrite, stats print.\\n\\nBut careful: `fill_stubs` model load happens once; patched ✓.\\n\\nOne more: `price_report` in services.py → stub → also filled with the same fake body (mock returns the same text for every call) — fine.\\n\\n5. keep-stub when generate_free raises? e.g. model runtime error mid-run — wrap per-stub generate_free in try/except (Exception) → \\\"kept stub — the model failed: ...\\\"? Good robustness: yes, wrap the call.\\n\\nNow, `_module_names(text)`: scanning `^    (?:def )?(\\\\w+)` on the module text — for services.py, `_holds` module-level helper is at indent 0 → not captured; but bodies call `_holds(...)` as a plain name ✓ no self. So irrelevant. For repos module-level helpers likewise. Good.\\n\\nOne more risk: `self._store.select(...)` etc. — store_names parsed from files[\\\"store.py\\\"] `^    def (\\\\w+)` — includes `__init__` not (dunder has `    def __init__`) → captured; fine.\\n\\nAlso, IMPORTANT: `_body_refusal` must be called with `module_names` from the CURRENT text (before replacement) ✓; and store_names parsed once ✓.\\n\\nNow stub_prompt: the module text should be the ELIGIBLE context. For repositories.py, the whole module; for services.py, the whole module (54 lines). Also references: services → repo signatures ✓.\\n\\nPrompt length check for library repos (15.7KB ≈ 4k tokens): requirements 1.5KB + prompt overhead ~1KB + references (skip for repos) + stub def... total ~18.5KB ≈ 4.7k tokens. Context 8192: prompt 4.7k + output up to 3.5k = 8.2k > 8192! generation_budget clamps max_tokens to the room left, so it's safe (output gets the remainder ~3.4k) ✓. Good: no overflow; budget handles it.\\n\\nWait — also `_clip_module` budget 24000 chars. For library repos 15.7KB < 24k → full module. Fine.\\n\\nAlright — also need to double check `runner.load_model` prints; brief look at load_model (96-135) — and generation_budget. Let me peek, then write the code.\\n\\nOne more thing: `fill_stubs(files, ctx, model_path, requirements)` — ctx unused? I planned references from files, requirements passed. ctx not needed... drop ctx param. But is ctx needed to know errors list? Module text shows errors usage ✓. Drop ctx. Hmm — but `_method_signatures(files.get(\\\"repositories.py\\\"))` for services — right. OK: `fill_stubs(files, model_path, requirements)`.\\n\\nWait — one more consideration: should the pass ALSO fill libraries' `LibraryService.search_books` which calls `BookRepository.search_books_by_title_author_isbn` (repo stub)? The services prompt references repo signatures (which exist regardless of fill). If the repo stub got filled, its signature is unchanged ✓. Fine.\\n\\nLet me look at the last piece: plan_method tail (for the test's stub reason), load_model prints.\",\n./session-ddc973e9-db8b-46d7-9024-ee8ace1d1575.json:146307:              \"newString\": \"def strip_code_fences(text):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Backward-compatible wrapper over `extract_code` (same contract).\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    return extract_code(text)\\n\\n\\n# --- the model pass: bodies for what the emitter refused -------------------\\n\\n\\nSTUB_FILES = (\\\"repositories.py\\\", \\\"services.py\\\")\\nSTUB_PROMPT_BUDGET = 24000  # chars of module text one prompt may carry\\n# Modules a filled body may reach for. The ones the file already imports are\\n# left alone; the rest are added when the body's text actually uses them,\\n# so an accepted body never meets a NameError at its first call.\\nBODY_IMPORTS = (\\\"csv\\\", \\\"json\\\", \\\"os\\\", \\\"re\\\", \\\"math\\\", \\\"time\\\", \\\"io\\\", \\\"random\\\",\\n                \\\"string\\\", \\\"collections\\\", \\\"itertools\\\", \\\"functools\\\", \\\"decimal\\\",\\n                \\\"uuid\\\", \\\"datetime\\\", \\\"pathlib\\\", \\\"sqlite3\\\")\\n\\n\\ndef find_stub_spans(text):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"`[(start, end, info)]` for every `raise NotImplementedError` stub.\\n\\n    `start` is the docstring line when the emitted method has one — the\\n    filler rewrites its `[...]` work order — and `end` the closing line of\\n    the raise. `info` carries the class, the method's `def` line and the\\n    reason the emitter refused, so the prompt can state the exact hole.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    lines = text.splitlines()\\n    spans = []\\n    cls = None\\n    def_line = None\\n    for index, line in enumerate(lines):\\n        if re.match(r\\\"^class (\\\\w+)\\\", line):\\n            cls = line.split()[1].rstrip(\\\":\\\")\\n            def_line = None\\n            continue\\n        if re.match(r\\\"^    def (\\\\w+)\\\", line):\\n            def_line = line.strip()\\n            continue\\n        if line != \\\"        raise NotImplementedError(\\\" or def_line is None:\\n            continue\\n        end = None\\n        reason = \\\"\\\"\\n        for offset in (1, 2):\\n            probe = lines[index + offset] if index + offset < len(lines) else \\\"\\\"\\n            if probe.strip().startswith('\\\"') and probe.strip().endswith('\\\")'):\\n                reason = probe.strip()[1:-2]\\n                end = index + offset\\n                break\\n        if end is None:\\n            continue\\n        start = index\\n        docstring = None\\n        if index and lines[index - 1].startswith('        \\\"\\\"\\\"'):\\n            start = index - 1\\n            docstring = lines[index - 1]\\n        spans.append((start, end, {\\\"cls\\\": cls, \\\"def\\\": def_line,\\n                                   \\\"reason\\\": reason, \\\"docstring\\\": docstring}))\\n    return spans\\n\\n\\ndef _stub_method(info):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"The method name a stub span belongs to.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    match = re.match(r\\\"def (\\\\w+)\\\", info.get(\\\"def\\\") or \\\"\\\")\\n    return match.group(1) if match else \\\"?\\\"\\n\\n\\ndef _clip_module(text, cls, budget):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"The module text, or its header plus the stub's own class when huge.\\n\\n    The module is the API reference; trimming it in the middle would show\\n    a half-finished body as if it were the pattern to imitate. One class\\n    over budget at least keeps the stub's own class whole.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    if len(text) <= budget:\\n        return text\\n    lines = text.splitlines()\\n    header = []\\n    for line in lines:\\n        if re.match(r\\\"^class \\\", line):\\n            break\\n        header.append(line)\\n    block, inside = [], False\\n    for line in lines:\\n        if re.match(r\\\"^class \\\", line):\\n            inside = line.split()[1].rstrip(\\\":\\\") == cls\\n        if inside:\\n            block.append(line)\\n    return \\\"\\\\n\\\".join(header + [\\\"\\\"] + block)[:budget]\\n\\n\\ndef stub_prompt(requirements, module_text, info, references=(),\\n                budget=STUB_PROMPT_BUDGET):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"One ask: the emitted module is the API reference; fill one hole.\\n\\n    Nothing is rewritten into a summary — the module shows the real store\\n    calls, model constructors and error raises the body must imitate, and\\n    a model told a simplified API invents one that does not exist. When\\n    the text would not fit `budget` it is clipped to the module header and\\n    the stub's own class; `references` lists signatures a service body\\n    may need from the repositories module.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    method = _stub_method(info)\\n    lines = [\\n        \\\"A Python module was generated mechanically from the architecture\\\",\\n        \\\"documents below. Exactly one method could not be derived\\\",\\n        \\\"mechanically and raises NotImplementedError; write its body.\\\",\\n        \\\"\\\",\\n        \\\"Rules:\\\",\\n        \\\"- Reply with ONLY the replacement for the raise statement: the\\\",\\n        \\\"  body's lines, indented with 8 spaces. No `def` line, no\\\",\\n        \\\"  docstring, no markdown fences, no explanation.\\\",\\n        f\\\"- The method is `{info['cls']}.{method}`; the emitter refused it\\\"\\n        \\\" with:\\\",\\n        f\\\"  {info['reason']}\\\",\\n        \\\"- Call only what the module shows: `self._store` methods used in\\\",\\n        \\\"  sibling bodies, `self._repos['Name']` repositories, `models.*`,\\\",\\n        \\\"  `errors.*`, module helpers. `_store.select` criteria support\\\",\\n        \\\"  only `eq`, `ge` and `le` — anything else (substring search,\\\",\\n        \\\"  several fields) reads `self._store.all(self.ENTITY)` and\\\",\\n        \\\"  filters in Python.\\\",\\n        \\\"- If the requirements do not determine the behavior, reply with\\\",\\n        '  `raise NotImplementedError(\\\"...\\\")` and nothing else.',\\n    ]\\n    if references:\\n        lines += [\\\"\\\", \\\"Methods this module may lean on:\\\"]\\n        lines += [f\\\"  {signature}\\\" for signature in references]\\n    lines += [\\\"\\\", \\\"requirements:\\\", (requirements or \\\"\\\").strip(), \\\"\\\",\\n              f\\\"module ({info.get('file', 'module')}):\\\",\\n              \\\"```python\\\",\\n              _clip_module(module_text, info[\\\"cls\\\"], budget).rstrip(),\\n              \\\"```\\\",\\n              \\\"\\\",\\n              f\\\"Now write the body of `{info['cls']}.{method}`, replacing\\\",\\n              \\\"the `raise NotImplementedError(...)` statement:\\\",\\n              \\\"```python\\\"]\\n    return \\\"\\\\n\\\".join(lines) + \\\"\\\\n\\\"\\n\\n\\ndef _clean_body(text):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"The model's answer as unindented body lines: fences and `def` gone.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    lines = [line.rstrip() for line in extract_code(text).splitlines()]\\n    while lines and not lines[0].strip():\\n        lines.pop(0)\\n    while lines and not lines[-1].strip():\\n        lines.pop()\\n    if lines and re.match(r\\\"^\\\\s*def \\\\w+\\\", lines[0]):\\n        lines.pop(0)  # the whole method came back; only its body goes in\\n    while lines and not lines[0].strip():\\n        lines.pop(0)\\n    indents = [len(line) - len(line.lstrip(\\\" \\\")) for line in lines\\n               if line.strip()]\\n    if not indents:\\n        return []\\n    trim = min(indents)\\n    return [line[trim:] if line.strip() else \\\"\\\" for line in lines]\\n\\n\\ndef _module_names(text):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Class attributes and methods: the `self.<name>` a body may touch.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    names = {\\\"_store\\\", \\\"_repos\\\"}\\n    for line in text.splitlines():\\n        match = re.match(r\\\"^    (?:def )?(\\\\w+)\\\", line)\\n        if match:\\n            names.add(match.group(1))\\n    return names\\n\\n\\ndef _body_refusal(body, module_names, store_names):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Why this answer may not replace a stub, or None when it may.\\n\\n    Compilation is checked by the caller; this catches the two ways a\\n    compiling body still cannot work: the model kept the refusal, or it\\n    invented an API surface the module and store never defined.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    if not body or all(not line.strip() for line in body):\\n        return \\\"the model returned nothing\\\"\\n    text = \\\"\\\\n\\\".join(body)\\n    if \\\"NotImplementedError\\\" in text:\\n        return \\\"the model kept it unimplemented\\\"\\n    unknown = sorted({name for name in re.findall(r\\\"self\\\\.(\\\\w+)\\\", text)\\n                      if name not in module_names})\\n    if unknown:\\n        return f\\\"calls self.{unknown[0]}, which the module does not define\\\"\\n    invented = sorted({name for name in re.findall(r\\\"self\\\\._store\\\\.(\\\\w+)\\\", text)\\n                       if name not in store_names})\\n    if invented:\\n        return (f\\\"calls self._store.{invented[0]}, which the store does not \\\"\\n                \\\"define\\\")\\n    return None\\n\\n\\ndef _add_imports(text, body_text):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Add the imports a filled body needs; return `(text, added)`.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    added = []\\n    for module in BODY_IMPORTS:\\n        if not re.search(rf\\\"\\\\b{re.escape(module)}\\\\.\\\", body_text):\\n            continue\\n        if re.search(rf\\\"^import {re.escape(module)}\\\\b\\\", text, re.M) \\\\\\n                or re.search(rf\\\"^from {re.escape(module)}\\\\b\\\", text, re.M):\\n            continue\\n        lines = text.splitlines()\\n        anchor = max((index for index, line in enumerate(lines)\\n                      if re.match(r\\\"^(?:import|from) \\\\w\\\", line)), default=0)\\n        lines.insert(anchor + 1, f\\\"import {module}\\\")\\n        text = \\\"\\\\n\\\".join(lines) + (\\\"\\\\n\\\" if text.endswith(\\\"\\\\n\\\") else \\\"\\\")\\n        added.append(module)\\n    return text, added\\n\\n\\ndef _method_signatures(text):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"`Class.method(...) -> returns` lines: what a service may delegate to.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    signatures = []\\n    cls = None\\n    for line in text.splitlines():\\n        match = re.match(r\\\"^class (\\\\w+)\\\", line)\\n        if match:\\n            cls = match.group(1)\\n            continue\\n        match = re.match(r\\\"^    def (\\\\w+\\\\(.*\\\\)(?:\\\\s*->\\\\s*[^:]+)?):$\\\", line)\\n        if match and cls:\\n            signatures.append(f\\\"{cls}.{match.group(1)}\\\")\\n    return signatures\\n\\n\\ndef fill_stubs(files, model_path, requirements, out=print):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Ask the model for the bodies the mechanical emitter refused.\\n\\n    Best-effort by design: the mechanical set is already final and honest,\\n    so a missing model, a refused answer or a body that does not compile\\n    leaves the stub in place and says why. Every accepted body passes the\\n    module's `compile`, may only touch `self.<name>` and `self._store.<name>`\\n    the module actually defines, gains any import it uses, and is tagged in\\n    its docstring as LLM-written — nothing here verifies behavior, and only\\n    reading the body (and running it) can. Returns `[(label, status,\\n    note)]`, one row per refused stub, `status` `written` or `kept`.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    targets = [(name, find_stub_spans(files[name])) for name in STUB_FILES\\n               if isinstance(files.get(name), str)]\\n    todo = sum(len(spans) for _name, spans in targets)\\n    if not todo:\\n        return []\\n    model_name = os.path.basename(model_path)\\n    try:\\n        llm = runner.load_model(model_path, logits_all=False, quiet=True)\\n    except Exception as error:  # missing file or missing runtime: stubs stand\\n        return [(f\\\"{todo} refused stub(s)\\\", \\\"kept\\\",\\n                 f\\\"the model would not load ({error})\\\")]\\n    store_names = {match.group(1) for match in\\n                   re.finditer(r\\\"^    def (\\\\w+)\\\", files.get(\\\"store.py\\\") or \\\"\\\")}\\n    out(f\\\"llm: {todo} refused stub(s) — {model_name} writes them body by body\\\")\\n    rows = []\\n    for filename, spans in targets:\\n        text = files[filename]\\n        references = ()\\n        if filename == \\\"services.py\\\":\\n            references = _method_signatures(files.get(\\\"repositories.py\\\") or \\\"\\\")\\n        for start, end, info in reversed(spans):\\n            info[\\\"file\\\"] = filename\\n            label = f\\\"{info['cls']}.{_stub_method(info)}\\\"\\n            out(f\\\"llm: {label} ...\\\")\\n            prompt = stub_prompt(requirements, text, info, references)\\n            try:\\n                result = runner.generate_free(\\n                    llm, prompt, f\\\"stub:{label}\\\",\\n                    temperature=FALLBACK_TEMPERATURE, seed=FALLBACK_SEED)\\n            except Exception as error:  # one failed call keeps one stub\\n                rows.append((label, \\\"kept\\\", f\\\"the model failed ({error})\\\"))\\n                continue\\n            body = _clean_body(result.text)\\n            refusal = _body_refusal(body, _module_names(text), store_names)\\n            if refusal is not None:\\n                rows.append((label, \\\"kept\\\", refusal))\\n                continue\\n            lines = text.splitlines()\\n            replacement = []\\n            docstring = info.get(\\\"docstring\\\")\\n            if docstring:\\n                replacement.append(re.sub(\\n                    r\\\"\\\\[[^\\\\]]*\\\\]\\\\s*(\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\")$\\\",\\n                    lambda match: f\\\"[llm-written by {model_name} — review]\\\"\\n                    f\\\"{match.group(1)}\