                "text": "y_title_author_isbn` (`parameter 'query' maps to no field`); expenses exports/reports/`detect_recurring` (`export_expenses_to_csv` refuses with `AGGREGATE_BY_FK_AND_DATE returns mapping/scalar, but this method returns none`).\\n- `out/60` and `out/inventory` BLOCKED (`[uncovered_field]` adapter blockers); runs stale (Oct 12) — re-run decision pending.\\n- `book list -author` unfixable (doc contradiction); `expense report -yearly` ambiguous (required flags; shape rule blocks dict-returning alternates).\\n- Inventory `calculate_stock_value_by_category` not expressible with current slots (no group-by).\\n- GUI bespoke screens (forms, cellar map, over-capacity warnings) not started.\\n\\n## Next Move\\n1. Finish the option-selected-actions refactor in `generator.py` (BINDINGS choices shape, runtime `_choose`/`_needs_met`/`_incomplete`, `_call`, dispatch, parser loop from stored plans, stale call removal); add tests in `tests/test_generator.py` (mechanical alternate routes when flags passed; incomplete-flag error; mis-targeted alternate not wired; route dispatch exec; existing dead-option test re-verified).\\n2. Extend `review_surface.py` with route questions; record `member list -active-only` → `MemberRepository.find_active_members` (drive review with piped `input_fn`, mirroring slot-record flow).\\n3. Regenerate `out/library` + `out/expenses`; smoke `budget list -category 1 -month 2026-10` (routes), `member list -active-only` (routes), `budget list -category 1` (errors naming -month); run full suite; commit.\\n4. Run the LLM pass for the 10 custom-logic stubs (user-decided next block).\\n5. Decide whether to re-run extraction for `out/60` + `out/inventory`.\\n\\n## Relevant Files\\n- `generator.py`: slot machinery + routes loading + emit_cli (mid-refactor: choices phase, BINDINGS, dead options) — primary work file.\\n- `review_surface.py`: surface decisions owner; extend with route questions (`SURFACE_DECISIONS_NAME` to import from generator).\\n- `review_slots.py`: slot review CLI (`_slot_options`, `_resolve_deferred`, `run_review(ctx|dir, input_fn=...)`, records static `decided_by: \\\"user\\\"`).\\n- `tests/test_generator.py`: CLI emission tests incl. `test_a_declared_option_no_field_reads_is_reported` (ServiceCompositionTests).\\n- `tests/test_slots.py`: criterion tests (`CriterionTests`, `criterion_ctx`, WIDGET fixtures).\\n- `out/library/method_slots.json`: recorded threshold + criterion answers; `out/library/surface_decisions.json` (to hold the member route).\\n- `kernel/codegen.py`: `bind_field`, `action_argv`, `plan_method`, `bind_commands` (twin rules with store template).\\n- `kernel/codegen_store_template.py`: runtime `match_criterion`/`select`/`_holds` (criteria triplets `(field, op, value)`).\\n- `out/library/generated/cli.py`, `out/expenses/generated/cli.py`: emitted dispatch (BINDINGS/ADAPTERS/_call/dispatch) — shape changes here.\\n- `meter_coverage.py`: coverage scoreboard + stub ledger.\\n- `extract_arch.py`: extraction/review wiring.\\n- `out/60/`, `out/inventory/`: blocked runs, stale docs.\\n\\n## Important Context\\n- Latest commits: `a575253` (criteria + dead flags), `d09c8a4` (mapping fixes); working tree has uncommitted mid-refactor `generator.py` (option-selected actions) that likely fails tests until BINDINGS/dispatch pieces land; pycache churn unstaged (87 tracked `__pycache__` files; offered to untrack + add `.gitignore` — awaiting user word).\\n- Coverage: library 29/34 (85%), expenses 31/36 (86%), total 60/70 (86%); 608 tests green at last full run (before refactor).\\n- Emitted BINDINGS new plan: `(command, sub) -> [(action, score, need)]` with need items `(dest, kind, flag)`; `kind` = \\\"truthy\\\" (bool) or \\\"value\\\"; fallback entry has empty need and is last; dispatch picks first satisfied, partial flags of an unsatisfied choice error (`also needs -month`), unless the partial dests are consumed by the chosen choice.\\n- Route records: `{\\\"kind\\\": \\\"route\\\", \\\"command\\\", \\\"subcommand\\\", \\\"flag\\\", \\\"service\\\", \\\"method\\\", \\\"decided_by\\\": \\\"user\\\", \\\"requirements_sha\\\"}` in `surface_decisions.json`; `apply_surface_decisions` ignores them (no \\\"keep\\\").\\n- Dead-flag detection is generation-time only; flags stay emitted. `member list -active-only` currently returns all members (flag dead until routed).\\n- `book list --available-only` (prompt line `library book list [--author] [--available-only]`) never reached `components.json`; components has only `-author` (bool).\\n- `budget list` binding: `('budget','list') -> ('read_all_budgets', 4)`; alternate target `read_budget_by_category_and_month` (fields category_id+month, both required) exists as an unbound action.\\n- Review-driving pattern: `input_fn` receives `f\\\"{slot} [{candidates}] or skip: \\\"`; track current method via `out_fn` lines starting `\\\"SLOTS for \\\"`.\\n- Library prompt criteria context: `Loan.status` vocabulary comes from invariants (`must be one of ['active','returned','overdue']`); `ctx[\\\"invariants\\\"]` now loaded.\\n- `ISO_DATE` transform validates with `datetime.date.fromisoformat`; `budget add -month 2026-10` works (DIRECT); `out/expenses/generated/expenses.db` is an untracked artifact.\\n- Match_param (kernel) and match_criterion (store template) are twins — changes must land in both; `_criterion_values`/`_field_enum` live in generator and are used by `review_slots`.\\n- `_report_dead_options` old 6-arg call in emit_cli's parser loop and the still-tuple BINDINGS emission are known inconsistencies to fix first; `_emit_options` would be re-planned in the parser loop until the stored-plans render lands.\\n- Book `filter_books_by_available_copies` return is `List[Book]` so shape alone doesn't block it — the param-name consistency check does.\",\n./session-ddc973e9-db8b-46d7-9024-ee8ace1d1575.json:144922:          \"text\": \"Committed `86ffecc`. \\n\\nCurrent state:\\n- Option-selected actions complete: routes + alternates + honest refusals, reviewed, tested, live-verified.\\n- Next planned block (user-chosen): LLM pass for the 10 custom-logic stubs.\\n- Pending question from earlier: untrack `__pycache__` (87 tracked files churning) — awaiting user word.\\n- out/60 + out/inventory re-run decision pending.\\n\\nWait — checkpoint says \\\"Next Move: 4. Run the LLM pass for the 10 custom-logic stubs (user-decided next block).\\\" The user had chosen that as the next work block. But before diving in, let me reconsider: should I continue immediately into the LLM pass, or report and confirm? The checkpoint's Next Move lists the LLM pass as step 4 after the option-selected-actions work (steps 1-3 = what I just finished). The user chose that next block in a prior message. So I should proceed with the LLM pass now... but it's a big block: \\\"LLM pass for the 10 custom-logic stubs.\\\"\\n\\nHmm, but wait — the summary says \\\"Blocked: 10 custom-logic stubs await the LLM pass (user-chosen next block)\\\". The user's decisions: \\\"Next work block (user-chosen): LLM pass for the 10 custom-logic stubs.\\\"\\n\\nHowever — this is a long session; the immediate work (option-selected actions) is now complete and committed. Before launching into the LLM pass (which requires Ministral via ../models with caffeinate, temp=0, seed), I should probably report the completion of the current block with the summary of findings (including the ISO_DATE doc inconsistency I found in expenses) and then continue.\\n\\nActually, the instructions here: the user set the next block explicitly. Since there's no pending question, I should continue working on the LLM pass. But hold on — the LLM pass design: \\\"10 custom-logic stubs await the LLM pass (user-chosen next block)\\\". What is the LLM pass exactly? From the objective: \\\"no catalog additions (uncovered patterns → documented stubs / LLM pass); generator must refuse blocked docs, never invent silently.\\\" So the LLM pass = for stubs whose custom logic can't be mechanically derived, call the LLM (Ministral from ../models) — presumably per-method, with the method's doc text + context — to GENERATE candidate bodies that are then verified? Or to produce a patch/report?\\n\\nHmm — I don't have the full details of what was planned for the LLM pass (the summary says \\\"the 10 custom-logic stubs await the LLM pass\\\"). The stubs:\\n- library: borrow_book, return_book, renew_membership, search_books, BookRepository.search_books_by_title_author_isbn (parameter 'query' maps to no field)\\n- expenses: export_expenses_to_csv, reports, detect_recurring (AGGREGATE_BY_FK_AND_DATE returns mapping/scalar...)\\n\\nThe LLM pass presumably: hand each stub's prompt/context to Ministral and have it write the method body, then store as an \\\"llm_body\\\" artifact with human review? There's a `--model` flag on generator for the degenerate fallback... and \\\"caffeinate for long Mac runs\\\" implies the LLM runs take a while.\\n\\nWait — actually, maybe the LLM pass refers to re-running the extraction (extract_arch.py) with different prompts for those methods? No — \\\"temp=0, seed 20261012\\\" refers to extraction LLM calls.\\n\\nHmm. The prompt files are frozen (prompts/*.txt). The extraction pipeline (extract_arch.py) drives prompts → JSON docs. The \\\"LLM pass for the 10 custom-logic stubs\\\" could mean: a NEW probe/pass where the LLM is asked to express the custom logic per stub, writing e.g. `custom_logic.json` docs that generator consumes to emit real bodies (with review). But no catalog additions allowed.\\n\\nThis is a significant new feature. Before building it, I should check the repo for any existing scaffolding/notes about the LLM pass. Let me search for hints: grep for \\\"llm\\\" in generator.py/kernel, look for custom_logic or llm_pass files, check prompts dir, maybe a plan doc.\\n\\nActually — the session summary says \\\"Next Move 1-3\\\" was the option-selected actions (done now), and \\\"4. Run the LLM pass for the 10 custom-logic stubs (user-decided next block)\\\". Given the size and ambiguity, and that this checkpoint reached me mid-task, the right move is to complete the current block report to the user... but the conversation flow: I'm an agent in a long session; the user last chose the LLM pass as the next block. I should continue autonomously into it, but first do a quick discovery of what the LLM pass machinery is (maybe it already exists in extract_arch.py).\\n\\nLet me grep the repo for existing LLM-pass infrastructure.\",\n./session-ddc973e9-db8b-46d7-9024-ee8ace1d1575.json:145216:          \"text\": \"This is a general doc, not the LLM-pass plan. The LLM pass plan details aren't in the repo explicitly. From my earlier conversation (summarized): \\\"Run the LLM pass for the 10 custom-logic stubs (user-decided next block)\\\". The nature of the pass: presumably, for the custom-logic stubs (methods whose business logic can't be mechanically derived), ask the local LLM (Ministral, temp=0, seed) to write the method body given the requirement text + signature + documents, then store the result as an artifact (llm_bodies.json?) that generator consumes when present — with human review. But is that the design? \\\"no catalog additions (uncovered patterns → documented stubs / LLM pass)\\\" — hmm, \\\"no catalog additions\\\" means don't add new mechanical patterns; instead the LLM pass handles them.\\n\\nActually — wait. Maybe the \\\"LLM pass\\\" refers to something more specific in the extraction pipeline: e.g., re-running step F (pattern classification) or a pass that fills method *slots* via LLM rather than asking the human? The 10 stubs are custom-logic methods; the LLM could be asked to pick catalog patterns/slots... but \\\"no catalog additions\\\" and these are CUSTOM logic — they don't fit patterns.\\n\\nAlternatively — maybe the plan: for stubs like `search_books_by_title_author_isbn`, the LLM extracts the *criteria mapping* (query → fields title/author/isbn with substring semantics) as a new kind of document (`method_bodies.json` written by an LLM pass with review_generation?). Hmm.\\n\\nSince the checkpoint explicitly says the stubs are \\\"Blocked ... await the LLM pass (user-chosen next block)\\\", and lists them, I think the intended design (discussed earlier, now summarized) is: an LLM pass — a separate pipeline stage (perhaps `llm_bodies.py` or integrated in extract_arch) — where the local model writes implementations for the custom-logic methods, stored as reviewed artifacts, and generator emits them instead of NotImplementedError when present.\\n\\nBut given uncertainty, the honest move is to ask the user a clarifying question about the LLM pass scope BEFORE building it — no. Wait, the user chose this block; but the exact design of the pass might have been specified in the earlier conversation detail that I don't have (the summary only says \\\"LLM pass for the 10 custom-logic stubs\\\"). Hmm.\\n\\nActually — let me re-read the summary: \\\"Every mechanical codegen step must be finished; unmet patterns stay NotImplementedError\\\" and \\\"Blocked: 10 custom-logic stubs await the LLM pass (user-chosen next block)\\\". And requirement: \\\"no catalog additions (uncovered patterns → documented stubs / LLM pass); generator must refuse blocked docs, never invent silently.\\\"\\n\\nSo the LLM pass is a planned pipeline stage: for methods that remain stubs after mechanical generation, an LLM writes bodies (from requirements + docs), and those bodies are... presumably saved with human review, then generator emits them. The \\\"run the LLM pass\\\" steps: implement + run it with Ministral over the 10 stubs.\\n\\nHowever, there's real design ambiguity:\\n1. Where do LLM-written bodies live? (e.g. `out/library/llm_bodies.json`, prompt-hash stamped, with per-method source text and review status)\\n2. Who reviews them? (human review flow like review_slots? or emitted as-is with \\\"LLM-written\\\" markers?)\\n3. Do we run the model on this Mac (llama-cpp-python, model path ../models/Ministral-*.gguf)? Long run → caffeinate.\\n\\nGiven the user explicitly chose this block, I'll design it minimally and consistently with existing patterns:\\n- New script `llm_bodies.py` (or `llm_pass.py`): builds per-stub prompts (requirements excerpt + docstrings + signature + documents context), calls the local Ministral model (kernel.extraction.runner presumably has the model loading), writes `llm_bodies.json` next to the documents (like method_slots.json): {bodies: [{holder, method, body, decided_by: \\\"llm\\\", model, temperature, seed, requirements_sha}]}.\\n- Review: `review_bodies.py`? Hmm, \\\"human-in-the-loop reviews\\\" requirement: \\\"Drive the LLM architecture-extraction pipeline ... with human-in-the-loop reviews\\\". For LLM-written bodies, a review step showing the body and accepting/rejecting seems necessary.\\n- generator.py: when emitting a stub method, if `llm_bodies.json` has an accepted body for (holder, method) matching the shas (prompt + signature?), emit that body verbatim (wrapped in a docstring note \\\"LLM-written, reviewed\\\") instead of NotImplementedError. Refuse/ignore stale (sha mismatch, signature mismatch) with a finding.\\n\\nThat's a lot. Given the amount of remaining uncertainty, asking the user ONE focused question about the pass's expected shape is justified — but the user isn't necessarily available... Actually in this interactive setting the user IS the one reading. But they said \\\"Next work block: LLM pass for the 10 custom-logic stubs\\\" — they expect me to proceed.\\n\\nHmm, wait. Let me look at the stubs' actual nature once more. Maybe the \\\"LLM pass\\\" is much simpler and already discussed in detail:\\n\\nStub reasons:\\n- library: `borrow_book` — custom logic (decrement available copies, create loan, error cases).\\n- `return_book`, `renew_membership`, `search_books` (multi-field substring search), `search_books_by_title_author_isbn` (parameter 'query' maps to no field).\\n- expenses: `export_expenses_to_csv` (write a file), `report` methods (aggregate), `detect_recurring` (grouped aggregate).\\n\\nAn LLM writing these bodies in a local file, reviewed once by the human, and emitted — that matches \\\"Some supported patterns can be completed after a person supplies the required details\\\" (doc) and the general philosophy: model makes small reviewable decisions; code generates.\\n\\nAlternatively the LLM pass might write the bodies DIRECTLY into `generated/` (a one-shot augmentation), not into a persisted doc... but \\\"same prompt+temp 0+seed reproduces output\\\" implies determinism matters, and persisting artifacts with shas matches all previous patterns.\\n\\nDecision: I'll implement the pass following the established pattern:\\n1. `kernel/extraction` model runner reuse to load Ministral and run completions with temperature 0 / seed 20261012.\\n2. New module `llm_bodies.py` (CLI): for each stub in a generated run, gather context, ask the model for a body, save `llm_bodies.json` (per method: body text, model, sha of requirements + method signature, status \\\"proposed\\\").\\n3. `review_bodies.py`: human review of proposed bodies (accept/reject), recording decided_by: \\\"user\\\"/rejected.\\n4. generator.py: load accepted bodies; emit them for matching methods (exact signature/imports constraints), verify compile; still report as \\\"llm\\\" in stats; stale/unaccepted → stub as before.\\n\\nHmm — but hold on: how do emitted bodies get their required imports (errors, store)? The body is a method body inside the class; imports are at file top per emitted module. LLM body could only use what's already imported... To keep it verifiable: emit the body inside a wrapper that's compile-checked; allow the body to raise NotImplementedError if unsure? The verification must be mechanical: compile + (maybe) restricted imports? \\\"custom-logic\\\" bodies may need `json`, `csv`, `datetime`, `os` — the emitted modules import a fixed set. Let me check what the emitted repositories/services import: models, errors, store, typing?, maybe `from typing import List`? Let me check out/expenses/generated/services.py header.\\n\\nHonestly — this is getting deep, and there's a real risk of building the wrong thing. This is exactly the situation where the harness says \\\"The user may also invoke a skill...\\\" no. Let me reconsider: perhaps in the original conversation the user and I had a detailed discussion of the LLM pass design (\\\"next work block (user-chosen): LLM pass for the 10 custom-logic stubs\\\") — the summary compresses it, so the design was likely discussed. Without it, I should ask a brief clarifying question.\\n\\nGiven the ambiguity and the cost of building the wrong machinery, I'll ask the user one question with sensible options. Actually — the question tool! That's appropriate here: \\\"before starting the LLM pass, confirm shape\\\". Let me ask a focused question:\\n\\nOptions:\\na) (Recommended) Bodies as reviewed artifacts: llm_bodies.py proposes method bodies via Ministral (temp 0), review_bodies.py lets you accept/reject, generator emits accepted bodies instead of stubs (sha-stamped, stale → stub + finding).\\nb) LLM pass writes only into the generated app once (no persistence/review machinery).\\nc) LLM pass = only for the extraction-stage stubs (re-run specific extraction steps via LLM with the frozen prompts), not code bodies.\\n\\nHmm — wait. Let me re-read one checkpoint line once more: \\\"**Every mechanical codegen step must be finished; unmet patterns stay `NotImplementedError`; meter mechanical coverage.**\\\" and \\\"**Blocked**: 10 custom-logic stubs await the LLM pass (user-chosen next block): library `borrow_book`/`return_book`/...\\\".\\n\\nAlso: \\\"**Next Move** ... 4. Run the LLM pass for the 10 custom-logic stubs (user-decided next block).\\\" — the phrasing \\\"Run the LLM pass\\\" (not \\\"build\\\") suggests the pass MACHINERY might already be expected/exists... but I grepped and found no llm machinery. Maybe it's in kernel/extraction/runner.py as a generic model runner, and the \\\"pass\\\" is simply: use `--model` somewhere? Let me check generator.py's `--model` handling: \\\"degenerate case → LLM fallback only with `--model`\\\". And extract_arch.py `--model`. Maybe \\\"the LLM pass\\\" = extend generator so that stubs can be LLM-written when `--model` is passed? Hmm — \\\"generator must refuse blocked docs, never invent silently\\\" — LLM writing stubs at generation time WOULD be inventing (not reviewed) unless reviewed.\\n\\nLet me look at the actual code: how does generator's fallback work (write_app_fallback?), where model loading lives, and how review_slots records decisions — to ground the design. Then decide whether to ask.\\n\\nLet me look at extract_arch.py's model loading + generator's --model fallback.\",\n./session-ddc973e9-db8b-46d7-9024-ee8ace1d1575.json:145293:          \"text\": \"The runner exists: `load_model`, `generate` (with grammar), `generate_free`. The extraction pipeline uses these with temp 0 / seed.\\n\\nThe \\\"LLM pass\\\" design question remains. Given the strong pattern of this project — deterministic artifacts + human review + prompt-hash stamps, and the checkpoint's explicit \\\"Run the LLM pass for the 10 custom-logic stubs (user-decided next block)\\\" — I believe the intended design is:\\n\\nFor each custom-logic stub, ask the local LLM to write the method BODY (free generation, temp 0, seed), store the proposal in a doc, let a human review (accept/reject), then generator emits accepted bodies (with stale detection). That matches \\\"human-in-the-loop reviews\\\" and \\\"some supported patterns can be completed after a person supplies the required details\\\".\\n\\nBut \\\"some supported patterns can be completed after a person supplies the required details\\\" refers to slots, already built.\\n\\nHmm, let me search the earlier session's thinking more concretely. The checkpoint summary is my only record. It says in \\\"Blocked\\\": \\\"10 custom-logic stubs await the LLM pass (user-chosen next block): library borrow_book/return_book/renew_membership/search_books/BookRepository.search_books_by_title_author_isbn (parameter 'query' maps to no field); expenses exports/reports/detect_recurring (export_expenses_to_csv refuses with AGGREGATE_BY_FK_AND_DATE returns mapping/scalar, but this method returns none).\\\"\\n\\nThe phrase \\\"parameter 'query' maps to no field\\\" suggests a mechanical attempt was made to express search via fields and failed. The LLM pass would then, e.g., ask the LLM which fields the query should search (a decision, not a body)? For search_books: \\\"query maps to no field\\\" — the mechanical failure is about MAPPING the parameter. An LLM pass could propose the criteria spec (fields searchable, substring ops) as a small reviewable decision — consistent with \\\"use the model to produce small, reviewable architecture decisions\\\". For export: the mechanical failure is about the return shape/target — an LLM could propose... hmm, an export body really is custom code (open file, write csv).\\n\\nMixed: some stubs are pattern-mapping decisions (search criteria), some are outright custom code (borrow_book: create loan + decrement copies + errors).\\n\\nGiven this genuine fork, I think a short clarifying question is the right move (the harness encourages gathering user preferences; and building the wrong machinery is expensive). I'll ask one question with clear options, defaulting to the most consistent design.\\n\\nActually — wait. Let me reconsider the phrase \\\"the LLM pass\\\" once more. In the original conversation (pre-checkpoint) the user chose \\\"Next work block: LLM pass for the 10 custom-logic stubs\\\". For the user to choose it as a work BLOCK, the design must have been discussed. The summary's \\\"Decisions\\\" section doesn't include a design for it though — if a design had been agreed, the summary would likely list it. So the design is open. Asking one question is appropriate and cheap.\\n\\nOptions for the question:\\n1. \\\"Model proposes bodies, human accepts, generator emits them\\\" (artifacts in llm_bodies.json + review_bodies.py + generator consumption; stale → stub + finding). This is the most complete, consistent design.\\n2. \\\"Model proposes the missing facts only where they are facts\\\" (e.g., for query→fields mappings, propose criteria slots like FILTER_WITH_SEMANTICS?; for genuinely custom code, leave stub). Smaller scope.\\n3. \\\"One-off: model writes bodies straight into the run's generated files (with a header note), no persistence/review\\\".\\n\\nHmm — but option 3 conflicts with determinism. Option 1 is the natural reading of \\\"human-in-the-loop\\\" + \\\"mechanical means actually works\\\".\\n\\nAlso which model: \\\"Use Ministral from ../models\\\" — the path: /Users/gillesstenger/Documents/models (sibling of esus dir? esus is /Users/gillesstenger/Documents/esus, so ../models = /Users/gillesstenger/Documents/models). And long runs need caffeinate.\\n\\nLet me formulate the question concisely, recommending option 1. Also ask about scope of targets: all 10 stubs vs only library+expenses runs (both).\\n\\nActually, maybe I should also ask whether the LLM pass should attempt the two blocked runs (60/inventory) later — no, that's a separate pending decision. Keep the question to the pass design. One question, 3 options.\\n\\nHmm, actually — let me think about whether I can just infer and proceed. The cost of asking: one round-trip. The cost of building wrong: large. Also the user's instruction style (\\\"Run the LLM pass\\\") suggests they'd like it run; the artifact+review shape is strongly implied by everything in this project (no silent invention; persisted decisions; prompt-hash stamps; review with skip). I could ALSO design it myself and show it. But there's one more consideration: maybe \\\"LLM pass\\\" was already discussed as: \\\"for stubs, ask the LLM to fill method_slots-like *logic parameters*\\\" — e.g. for search methods, a document `method_bodies.json`... \\n\\nOK let me just ask, briefly, while stating what I found and the recommended design. This is exactly what the question tool is for.\",\n./session-ddc973e9-db8b-46d7-9024-ee8ace1d1575.json:145396:          \"text\": \"The user clarified: they want the LLM pass added to the generator, not extract_arch.py. The generator, when it has stubs left unimplemented, uses the LLM to generate what is left unimplemented. For now, we check the resulting code manually (no review machinery needed in the pipeline for now).\\n\\nSo: `generator.py` gets an LLM pass — presumably gated behind `--model` (the generator already has a `--model` flag for the degenerate fallback). For stubs remaining after mechanical generation, call the local Ministral model to write those method bodies; emit them into the generated code; report which methods were LLM-generated; manual review of the resulting code by the user for now.\\n\\nDesign decisions to make:\\n1. When does the LLM pass run? Options: (a) automatically whenever `--model` is passed; (b) automatically always (requires model path — the generator doesn't know the model path by default). Since loading a model is expensive and the default generator run must stay fast/deterministic-ish, gate on `--model` (or maybe on `--model` + stubs exist). The degenerate fallback already uses `--model`. So: `generator.py out/library --model ../models/<ministral>.gguf` → after mechanical emission, the pass asks the model for each stub method body.\\n\\n2. What exactly does the model get? Per stub method: the requirements text (or a relevant excerpt), the method's holder (repo/service), name, params, return type, description, docstring/reason, plus the related documents context (models/fields, other methods, errors) — and the target file (repositories.py or services.py) + maybe the current stub text. Ask for a Python method body only (no signature), via free generation (generate_free) with a grammar? A Python grammar isn't available; generate_free returns text. Then we must mechanically validate: extract the code, ensure it compiles (compile the module with the body inserted), strip markdown fences, refuse on anything non-compiling (report + keep stub).\\n\\n3. Determinism: temp 0, seed 20261012 per the project rules. Persist results? User said \\\"for now we check manually the resulting code\\\" — so no persistence/review machinery now. But rerunning would call the model again (slow). Hmm — the project rule \\\"same prompt+temp 0+seed reproduces output\\\" is for extraction. For the generator pass, determinism with the same model+seed should hold (llama.cpp sampling with fixed seed, temp 0 → greedy). Still, to make reruns cheap and outputs stable, could cache bodies in the out dir... but the user explicitly said no review for now; caching is not review. The checkpoint's earlier decision framework: \\\"Human answers persist ... re-applied on reruns\\\" — but LLM bodies aren't human answers. The user says \\\"llm generate what is left unimplemented. for now we check manually the resulting code.\\\" — I read this as: run the generator with the model, the remaining stubs get LLM bodies, and we (the user + me) manually inspect the resulting generated code for correctness. No persistence needed NOW; keep it simple: generate at generation time when --model is given.\\n\\nBut wait — cost: generating bodies for each stub on every run; with ~10 stubs per run and a local 8B model this might be minutes. Acceptable for now (caffeinate for long runs). Hmm, but also: if the model path isn't passed → stubs stay as before (backward compatible). Good.\\n\\n4. Which stubs are eligible? All NotImplementedError stubs in repositories.py and services.py (the method bodies that the mechanical emitter stubbed). But some stubs are \\\"unmappable param\\\" cases or \\\"no pattern\\\" — the LLM can attempt