, load the model ONCE, loop methods with generate_free(temp 0, seed), assemble replacement bodies, compile-check the whole module (with the real store template importable?), accept only compiling bodies; report per method: `llm: <holder>.<method> — written (N lines)` or `llm: <holder>.<method> — refused: does not compile` plus keep-old-stub.\\n\\nHow to know which methods are stubs in the emitted text? render_program computes stats[\\\"stub_reasons\\\"]. I need a structured list of stub locations: method name, holder class, file. Where are stubs emitted? In emit_repositories/emit_services; `stats[\\\"stub_reasons\\\"]` accumulates human-readable reasons. Let me grep how stub bodies are written — maybe a helper `_stub_body(reason)` used by both emitters; and stats[\\\"stubs\\\"] incremented. I need the (file, class, method, reason, signature) info. Simplest robust approach: after render, scan the emitted text for `raise NotImplementedError(` lines — but mapping back to method/class from text is doable (track current class/def while scanning lines). That's mechanical and robust. Then to replace, I need the exact stub line(s) span to replace: the stub body is presumably:\\n```python\\n        raise NotImplementedError(\\n            \\\"reason\\\"\\n        )\\n```\\nor single-line. Let me look at the actual emitted stub text first.\\n\\nAlso imports: LLM body may use os/csv/json/datetime — the emitted module's imports are fixed (models, errors, store; maybe `json`? let me check). If an LLM body needs an import that's missing, compile still succeeds but runtime NameError! To be safe: pre-scan the body for import needed? Better: give the model an allowed-imports preamble and mechanically verify the body uses only names available? That's hard. Practical approach: the prompt states available imports and forbids new imports; bodies are manually checked anyway (user said manual check). But \\\"mechanical means actually works\\\" principle suggests at least ensure imports: I could add a fixed set of common stdlib imports to ALL emitted repository/services files when the LLM pass runs? Changing module imports changes deterministic output though. Alternatively: for the LLM pass, if the body references `csv|json|os|datetime|re|collections|math|time`, prepend the needed import lines to the module (module-level), determined mechanically by scanning the body tokens for `module.` usage. That's a bounded, verifiable tweak: add `import csv` etc. if used and not already imported. Also `from datetime import date`? Patterns vary. Simpler: our emitted modules might already import datetime (models use it?). Let me check the current emitted headers for repositories.py and services.py.\\n\\nLet me also check how the CLI-visible errors (registered error classes in errors.py) are referenced in stub bodies — e.g. borrow_book must raise BookNotAvailableError etc. The prompt should include errors list.\\n\\n7. Model loading: reuse kernel.extraction.runner.load_model + generate_free. Model path: the user said \\\"Use Ministral from ../models\\\" — path /Users/gillesstenger/Documents/models. Let me list that dir to find the file name.\\n\\n8. `--model` flag semantics: currently used only for the degenerate fallback. Now also for stub filling. Update --help text and the module docstring. Also `--quiet` mode: the pass should print progress; with `--quiet`, per-step progress + verdict.\\n\\n9. Output dir: generated/ files; stats: count \\\"llm_filled\\\"; coverage line \\\"methods: X implemented, Y stubs\\\" — where LLM-filled count as implemented? They're not mechanical. Report separately: `methods: 29 implemented, 5 stubs (85% mechanical)` + `  llm: 5 of 5 stubs written by <model> (check by reading)`? And meter_coverage counts mechanical only — fine, don't touch meter_coverage now.\\n\\n10. Verification bounds (mechanical, honest):\\n- The assembled module must compile (compile(module_text)).\\n- The replaced body must not redefine the signature / not contain