hese facts:\\n- grep stub emission;\\n- read an emitted repositories.py + services.py stub region (library);\\n- check imports headers;\\n- store template public API;\\n- model dir listing.\\n\\nThen design + implement in generator.py:\\n- `--model PATH` flag exists? Check argparse in main().\\n- new function `llm_fill_stubs(files, ctx, stats, model_path, quiet)`:\\n  - find stubs in files[\\\"repositories.py\\\"], files[\\\"services.py\\\"], files[\\\"gui.py\\\"]? no gui.\\n  - load model once (runner.load_model with quiet=True), generate per stub via generate_free.\\n  - replace, compile-check, count.\\n- main(): after render_program, if args.model and not degenerate-fallback-already-used: run the pass. Careful: the degenerate fallback path (clean-but-empty docs) writes app.py and exits — that path shouldn't run stub filling.\\n- Reporting + exit code stays 0.\\n\\nAlso handle: no stubs → pass skipped with note (\\\"no stubs to fill\\\"), no model load at all (cheap).\\n\\nSequence: main() currently: readiness checks → render_program → gui handling → write files. I'll insert after render_program (before writing) so only validated text is written.\\n\\nLet me look at all these now.\",\n./session-ddc973e9-db8b-46d7-9024-ee8ace1d1575.json:145623:          \"text\": \"Now I have the full picture. Design of the LLM pass in generator.py:\\n\\n**Entry**: `--model PATH` now does double duty: degenerate fallback (as before) AND stub filling. Update the help text and module docstring.\\n\\n**Flow in main()**: after `render_program(ctx, stats)` and gui emission, before writing files:\\n```python\\n    if args.model:\\n        filled = fill_stubs_with_llm(files, ctx, stats, args.model)\\n```\\n`fill_stubs_with_llm`:\\n1. Find stub regions in files[\\\"repositories.py\\\"] and files[\\\"services.py\\\"] (both share the pattern below).\\n2. If none → print nothing special (or a line only when verbose) and return 0 — skip model loading entirely.\\n3. Load the model once (`runner.load_model(path, quiet=True)`); generate per stub.\\n\\n**Stub detection in emitted text**: the stub is always:\\n```\\n        raise NotImplementedError(\\n            \\\"<cls>.<fname>: <strategy>: <detail>\\\")\\n```\\nfor repos, and\\n```\\n        raise NotImplementedError(\\n            \\\"<cls>.<fname>: <detail>\\\")\\n```\\nfor services. Both have the pattern: a line `        raise NotImplementedError(` followed by exactly one line `            \\\"...\\\"` followed by `)`? Let me verify against the real emitted files. Also the service stub for `borrow_book` might be a guard-related form. And there might be other raise NotImplementedError shapes (e.g. `_actors` template with custom message?). Also `_repo_body` fallback returns `[\\\"raise NotImplementedError(\\\", '    \\\"...\\\"']` unindented — then prefixed with 8 spaces.\\n\\nLet me grep the emitted files for the exact shapes.\\n\\nFor the replacement, I need for each stub: the enclosing class name, method name, def line (signature), the docstring line (contains strategy/reason), and the raise lines to replace. I can parse the file text line by line: track current `class X:` and `    def name(...)`; when I see the exact raise-start line at indent 8, record the span [i, j] where j is the closing line `            \\\"...\\\")` — looking for the line ending `\\\")` at indent 12? Careful multi-line reasons? The reason strings come from f-strings without newlines ✓ single line.\\n\\nThe docstring gives me the reason text: `\\\"\\\"\\\"<description> [strategy: detail]\\\"\\\"\\\"` — but for services the shape is `\\\"\\\"\\\"<desc><where>\\\"\\\"\\\"`. To build the prompt I have: class, method name, def line (with params and return), docstring text, reason (from the raise payload). That's enough.\\n\\n**Prompt**: build a single user prompt (the runner takes a prompt string; `generate_free(llm, prompt, label, temperature=0.0, max_new_tokens=None, seed=None)`). Include:\\n- The requirements text (`load_requirements`? For a run without requirements.txt? main has out_dir; requirements.txt read via kernel loader — `from kernel.consistency.loader import load_requirements`? generator doesn't import it currently... it's fine to import).\\n- The class + file (repositories.py or services.py), the entity, model fields (name + type).\\n- The store API (for repo methods).\\n- Sibling method signatures in the same file (so it may call them; esp. services delegating to self._repos['Repo'].method).\\n- The errors available (names + descriptions).\\n- The target method's full signature + description + reason.\\n- Instructions: return ONLY the method body (the lines that replace `raise NotImplementedError(...)`), 8-space indentation, no def line, no markdown fences; use only these names; raise the registered errors where the rules say so; SQLite store API usage: `self._store` methods...; if unsure, `raise NotImplementedError(\\\"...\\\")`.\\n\\nHmm — the model may not know the store template API. Better: include the ENTIRE current generated module text (repositories.py / services.py) as context so it can imitate sibling methods (that's the strongest context: the file shows exactly how other methods query the store, wrap models, raise errors). The files are maybe 200-400 lines; with 8192 context (runner default context_size=8192) — prompt could be ~3-4k tokens for big files. Check the sizes: out/library/generated/services.py lines? Let me check. If too big, fall back to a distilled context. Actually, with a small model, showing the whole module + the specific method to fill is much more reliable than describing the API in prose. Let me check file sizes first (wc -l and rough chars).\\n\\nAlso determinism: seed=20261012, temperature=0.0. The runner.generate_free signature: (llm, prompt, label, temperature=0.0, max_new_tokens=None, seed=None). Let me check how extraction calls it with seed — look at kernel/extraction/pipeline.py for the seed constant (20261012).\\n\\n**Validation after body**: \\n- Strip fences; strip trailing whitespace; drop empty first/last lines.\\n- Re-indent: compute min indent of non-empty lines, remove it, then prepend 8 spaces to each non-empty line.\\n- Insert into the file text (replacing the raise span).\\n- After all stubs in a module replaced: `compile(text, filename, \\\"exec\\\")` — if it fails, revert THIS method to the stub and record refusal. Actually better per-method: compile the whole module after each replacement; on failure revert that method and continue. (Compile of the whole file per stub is cheap.)\\n- Also refuse if body is empty or is exactly `pass` or contains `NotImplementedError` (no gain? Keep stub and report \\\"kept stub\\\"). Hmm — if the model returns `raise NotImplementedError(\\\"...\\\")`, that's a refusal signal; keep the old stub and count as stub. Also if the body defines a nested `def` — allow (custom logic may need helpers? keep it simple: allow; compile passes; manual review anyway).\\n- Imports: check body for module usage of common stdlib (json, csv, os, datetime, re, math, collections, itertools, pathlib, time, io). If used (regex `\\\\b(?:json|csv|...)\\\\.`) and the import isn't already in the module, add `import X` after the existing import block. That keeps the code runnable rather than NameError. Also `datetime` is probably already imported in some modules? Check headers. This mechanical import addition is honest and reported: \\\"llm: added import json for ...\\\". Let me include this.\\n\\nActually — the store template's `_transform` uses datetime? In cli.py yes. repositories.py imports? Let me check.\\n\\n**stats/reporting**:\\n- `stats[\\\"llm\\\"] = [(holder.method, \\\"filled\\\"/\\\"kept\\\", note)]`\\n- print lines: `  llm: BookRepository.search_books_by_title_author_isbn — written by ministral (review the body)` and `  llm: ... — kept stub: body does not compile`.\\n- Coverage line: keep mechanical coverage as-is; add `llm: N stub(s) written by <model> — review the generated bodies` before stub list. Stubs count unchanged (mechanical), but the emitted file no longer raises for filled ones. The docstring? The replaced method loses its work-order marker; add a marker in the emitted code so manual review can find them: insert a line right after the docstring: `        # LLM-written by <model> (<date>?) — review; seed 20261012`? Determinism: a date would break reproducibility... skip date; use model filename. Actually to keep exactly reproducible + identifiable: `        # llm: <model basename>, temperature 0.0, seed 20261012 — review me`. Hmm, the user said \\\"for now we check manually the resulting code\\\" — a marker comment helps find them. And also the `_record` stats row: strategy \\\"llm\\\" for the meter? meter_coverage counts stats[\\\"methods\\\"] rows... let me check `_record` + meter to not break it. Keep `_record` rows unchanged (mechanical accounting), add a separate stats list.\\n\\nAlso: should the LLM fill stubs when the run REFUSES readiness? No — gate refuses before. Fine.\\n\\n**Which stubs to attempt**: ALL stubs in repositories.py and services.py? Or only custom-logic ones? The user said \\\"llm generate what is left unimplemented\\\" — all of them. The prompt includes the reason so the model can handle \\\"parameter maps to no field\\\" etc. OK.\\n\\n**Clipping**: model context 8192; prompt should stay under ~6000 tokens. If the module text is too big, truncate the module context? Compute rough token estimate (chars/4). If over budget, use a distilled context (signatures only). Let me check actual file sizes first.\\n\\n**--quiet mode**: \\\"plan + per-step progress + verdict on terminal; full detail in run.log\\\" — generator's main doesn't currently print plan/quiet? The --quiet flag: is it on generator.py? The checkpoint says \\\"--quiet mode: plan + per-step progress + verdict on terminal; full detail in run.log\\\" — that's extract_arch.py's --quiet, likely. generator.py has no --quiet. Fine — generator prints lines as usual; LLM steps print per method.\\n\\n**caffeinate**: the user uses `caffeinate` around long runs; not built into the code.\\n\\nNow let me look at:\\n1. out/library/generated/repositories.py and services.py stub exact text + file sizes + imports.\\n2. runner.generate_free + load_model signature + how pipeline picks seed/temp.\\n3. kernel/extraction/pipeline.py seed usage.\\n4. --models dir listing.\",\n./session-ddc973e9-db8b-46d7-9024-ee8ace1d1575.json:145795:                \"text\": \"Ministral-3-3B-Instruct-2512-Q2_K_L.gguf\\nMinistral-3-3B-Instruct-2512-Q4_K_M.gguf\\nQwen3-4B-Instruct-2507-Q4_K_M.gguf\\nQwen3-Reranker-0.6B-Q4_K_M.gguf\\ndef generate_fallback(out_dir, model_path):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Write `generated/app.py` from the requirements; return the exit code.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    requirements = load_requirements(out_dir)\\n    if not requirements:\\n        print(f\\\"error: {out_dir} has no requirements.txt to generate from\\\")\\n        return 1\\n    ctx = load_context(out_dir)\\n    prompt = fallback_prompt(requirements, ctx[\\\"errors\\\"])\\n    llm = runner.load_model(model_path, logits_all=False)\\n    result = runner.generate_free(llm, prompt, \\\"fallback\\\",\\n                                  temperature=FALLBACK_TEMPERATURE,\\n                                  seed=FALLBACK_SEED)\\n    code = strip_code_fences(result.text)\\n    if not code:\\n        print(\\\"error: the model returned no code for the fallback program\\\")\\n        return 1\\n    try:\\n        compile(code, \\\"app.py\\\", \\\"exec\\\")\\n    except SyntaxError as error:\\n        print(f\\\"error: the fallback program does not parse ({error}) — \\\"\\n              f\\\"not writing it\\\")\\n        return 1\\n    header = ('\\\"\\\"\\\"LLM-generated fallback: the extracted architecture is empty (no models,\\\\n'\\n              'services or commands), so there is nothing mechanical to be faithful to.\\\\n'\\n              f'Generated from {out_dir}/requirements.txt by `generator.py --model`; '\\n              'verify by running it.\\\\n'\\n              '\\\"\\\"\\\"\\\\n\\\\n')\\n    target = os.path.join(out_dir, GENERATED_DIR)\\n    os.makedirs(target, exist_ok=True)\\n    path = os.path.join(target, \\\"app.py\\\")\\n    with open(path, \\\"w\\\", encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\") as handle:\\n        handle.write(header + code + \\\"\\\\n\\\")\\n    print(f\\\"degenerate architecture (no models, services or commands) — LLM fallback\\\")\\n    print(f\\\"[write] {path} ({result.tokens} tokens in {result.seconds:.1f}s)\\\")\\n    print(f\\\"verify by running it: python3 {path}\\\")\\n    return 0\\n\\n\\ndef main(argv=None):\\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__.splitlines()[0])\\n    parser.add_argument(\\\"out_dir\\\", help=\\\"Extraction output directory (e.g. out/expenses)\\\")\\n    parser.add_argument(\\\"--model\\\", default=None,\\n                        help=\\\"GGUF model for the degenerate fallback: when the \\\"\\n                             \\\"documents are clean but empty (no models, services \\\"\\n                             \\\"or commands), the whole tiny program is written \\\"\\n                             \\\"from requirements.txt instead of an empty shell\\\")\\n    parser.add_argument(\\\"--gui-tech\\\", default=None,\\n                        help=\\\"Override the declared GUI technology (default: \\\"\\n                             \\\"the components facade declaration)\\\")\\n    parser.add_argument(\\\"--verbose\\\", action=\\\"store_true\\\",\\n                        help=\\\"Also print harmless diagnostics (skipped \\\"\\n                             \\\"argparse-owned options, adapter fields with no \\\"\\n                             \\\"CLI flag). Anything actionable is already a \\\"\\n                             \\\"probe blocker, which refuses generation.\\\")\\n    args = parser.parse_args(argv)\\n\\n    ok, message = gate(args.out_dir)\\n    print(message)\\